    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Adjunct-Faculty---Architecture_R140256": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Architecture",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Adjunct Faculty will teach introductory courses in architectural drawing, sketching, studio design, computer modeling, architectural history, technology, or project case studies; as well as other undergraduate and graduate courses depending on expertise, enrollment, funding, and departmental needs. Qualifications: Master of Architecture, PhD or equivalent preferred. Please note: applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,484.48"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Teaching-Professor--Biology_R142908": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor, Biology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity: The Department of Biology at Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts invites applications from qualified candidates for a full-time, non-tenure track (NTT) faculty position at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor with significant opportunity for career advancement. Responsibilities: The primary responsibilities of this role are to teach multiple lecture sections of undergraduate courses, including Integrated Human Anatomy & Physiology and Inquiries in the Biological Sciences , and to design and implement research-based revisions to assigned courses. Additional duties may involve coordination with other instructors, organization of laboratory curricula, and additional teaching and course development. NTT faculty with appointments in Biology are expected to teach five courses per year, some of which may occur in summer, and provide one equivalent unit of service to the Department/College/University. Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Qualifications: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in the biological sciences or other closely related discipline prior to the start of the appointment Experience teaching undergraduate lecture courses Excellent communication, collaboration, and leadership skills Experience in teaching Anatomy and Physiology, and course and/or laboratory curriculum development is preferred The position requires strong interpersonal and collaboration skills, with the ability to work both independently and as part of a team while engaging effectively with faculty, students, and administrative staff Documents to Submit: Cover letter Curriculum vitae (CV), that includes a list of publications Teaching philosophy statement (1 page) Leadership statement (1 page) List of three references  Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts.  Teaching statement (1 page) : Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the College of Science (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring global student populations. Leadership Statement (1 page) :   Please describe your past experiences and activities and/or your future plans detailing your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice.   Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. Inquiries about the position may be directed to Associate Teaching Professor Ana Otero ( a.otero@northeastern.edu ), Search Committee Chair. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Teaching Professor: $75,000.00 - $90,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Arlington-VA/International-Student---Scholar-Advisor_R142784": {
      "title": "International Student & Scholar Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: Northeastern University provides immigration advising and support to over 24,000 F-1 and J-1 nonimmigrant international students, scholars, and alumni from 149 countries, making it one of the most globally diverse institutions in the United States. The Office of Global Services (OGS) plays a critical role in supporting this population through immigration advising, compliance, and student-centered services across the university's campuses. Reporting to an Associate Director, Director, or Senior Director within OGS, the International Student & Scholar Advisor (ISSA) serves as a Designated School Official (DSO) and an Alternate Responsible Officer (ARO), providing comprehensive nonimmigrant advising to F-1 and J-1 international students and scholars, and their dependents, at Northeastern University. This role delivers accurate and timely information to a diverse range of students and scholars and facilitates support through internal and external departments and staff. The ISSA uses the Student and Exchange Visitor Information System (SEVIS) to perform functions required by federal regulations, including but not limited to creating Forms I-20/DS-2019, authorizing employment, reporting on student and scholar maintenance of status, and processing changes to student and scholar information. As a member of the OGS team, this ISSA provides support both to their assigned campus location and to other U.S. campuses. In partnership with OGS leadership, the ISSA supports the development of international student and scholar services at the assigned campus location, including advising, orientations, information sessions, and support services grounded in NAFSA best practices, in service of the goal to retain and engage international students in the university community and to create an environment that contributes to student success. The ISSA communicates and coordinates with departments and campus partners at other Northeastern campus locations on cross-campus coordination and general inquiries. Key Responsibilities: Advise, counsel, and assist F-1 students, J-1 students, and J-1 scholars to ensure maintenance of nonimmigrant status, employment authorization, and successful acculturation, academic, social, and personal adjustment. Create, update, and maintain student and scholar records in SEVIS, including reportable events, as required by federal regulations. Maintain up-to-date knowledge of federal regulations, SEVIS procedures, Northeastern academic policies, and OGS policies and procedures to provide timely and comprehensive advising. Provide guidance to Northeastern academic departments and key contacts on regulatory requirements, institutional policies, and OGS policies and procedures. Advise students on logistical matters, including obtaining a Social Security number and applying for a U.S. driver's license or state ID. Liaise with relevant government agencies, including the Department of Homeland Security (DHS), the Student and Exchange Visitor Program (SEVP), the Department of State (DOS), U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP), and U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS), regarding student requests, data corrections, and travel. Design and/or deliver regulatory training for students, scholars, and colleagues. Communicate with academic and co-op/career advisors to support comprehensive international student advising. Collaborate with the OGS student support and programming team on orientation and other programming, including the initial review of F and J regulations with new students. Contribute to developing, designing, and implementing programs and systems that support and enrich the international student and scholar experience at Northeastern. Provide on-ground advising support and represent OGS at a U.S. Global Network campus, including attending campus-wide meetings and assisting with on-campus events. Support initiatives tied to the overall health of the campus landscape, spearheaded by campus leadership or in partnership with campus staff. Provide awareness and context to campus stakeholders regarding the international student lifecycle. Serve within the DSO on-call rotation, providing after-hours support for time-sensitive compliance matters on a scheduled basis. Perform other duties as assigned. Occasional evening and weekend work may be required, including for on-call coverage, orientations, and campus events. Minimum Qualifications: U.S. citizenship or lawful permanent residency is required for all DSO/ARO-designated roles, per federal regulations. Bachelor's degree. One to three years of formal experience as a DSO/ARO working directly with SEVIS to process documentation, maintain records, and comply with regulatory reporting requirements. Professional experience working with international student and/or scholar populations across the international student experience (pre-arrival, enrollment, post-completion employment). Ability and willingness to multitask and develop systems of organization for managing a high-volume caseload. High level of interpersonal and communication skills (verbal and written), with demonstrated intercultural competence. Ability to work independently in a fast-paced environment as a contributing member of a high-functioning team. Knowledge of privacy laws and the ability to exercise discretion and confidentiality when advising students and scholars. Proficiency with Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Outlook) and other web-based applications. Preferred Qualifications: Familiarity with international student/scholar case management systems (e.g., Sunapsis). Prior study, work, or living experience outside of the U.S. Knowledge of a second or third language. Prior experience with academic advising. Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106S Expected Hiring Range: $48,610.00 - $66,838.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Open-Rank--Assistant--Associate--or-Full--Tenure-Track-Professor-in-AI-mediated-Telepresence_R142875": {
      "title": "Open Rank (Assistant, Associate, or Full) Tenure-Track Professor in AI-mediated Telepresence",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Northeastern: Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Boston, Arlington, Charlotte, London, Miami, New York City, Oakland, Online, Portland, Seattle, Silicon Valley, Toronto, and Vancouver—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. The Bouvé College of Health Sciences (BCHS) is the leading national model for education and research in the health, psychosocial and biomedical sciences, and supports the University’s mission of educating students for a life of fulfillment and accomplishment and creating and translating knowledge to meet global and societal needs. BCHS is home to the Center for Community Health Education, Research, and Services; the Institute for Cognitive and Brain Health; the Brain Game Center for Mental Fitness and Well-Being; and collaborates with Northeastern’s Colleges in the many Center and Institute opportunities available. The College of Arts, Media and Design (CAMD) is a collaborative community within the university that acts as a catalyst for research, inspiration, and discovery. It brings together a unique combination of disciplines, including Architecture, Art+Design, Communication Studies, Journalism, Media and Screen Studies, Music, and Theatre. CAMD is also home to the Center for the Arts; the newly launched Center for Design and the Center for Communication, Media Innovation, and Social Change; and the CAMD Media Studios Organization (MSO). About the Opportunity: The College of Arts, Media and Design (CAMD) at Northeastern University is seeking interdisciplinary candidates for an academic year, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, with a record of excellence in critically-engaged research and/or creative practice that advances the concept of AI-mediated telepresence, where technology is used to enhance connection across distance in areas such as telemedicine, telehealth, immersive/AI-mediated shared environments, and human information networks beginning in academic year 2027-2028. A successful candidate could focus on research areas pertaining to design, development, management, and deployment of telepresence and AI in real-world contexts and practices, including (but not limited to): immersive, AI-powered, and digital health technologies; embodied intelligence; immersive social presence; human-agent teaming; the relationship between telepresence and AI; AI-mediated remote teaching and learning; distributed simulation and training, including clinical, professional, and educational training; and/or multi-modal experience design. A program or demonstrated potential for externally funded research and/or industry partnerships is required. Areas of application may encompass health system impact, community impact, rural health, simulation, remote and/or immersive learning, brain and physical health, virtual reality therapy platforms, virtual art therapy, AI/smart devices, wearable devices including exoskeletons, robotics, rehabilitation, advanced manufacturing and design, and collaborative design practices. We are particularly interested in candidates qualified for joint appointment between two or more of the College of Arts, Media and Design, the Bouvé College of Health Sciences, Khoury College of Computer Sciences, and the College of Engineering. This position is at Northeastern University's Boston campus. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Belonging, the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to further develop and support a thriving faculty. Responsibilities: Responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in area of expertise, including courses in the core curriculum and our interdisciplinary programs; mentoring students at undergraduate, masters, and doctoral levels in area of expertise; maintaining a strong program of interdisciplinary research that attracts external funding and/or recognition; and participating in departmental, college and university service. Qualifications: The successful candidate will have a Ph.D. degree, or foreign equivalent, in Design, Media Studies, Architecture, Communication Studies, Computer Science, Health Science, Public Health, Informatics, Psychology, Neuroscience, Bio/Biomedical Engineering, or other terminal degree in a closely related field such as an MFA or M.Arch by the employment start date; and demonstrated potential to attract external funding for research and/or creative practice. Appointments at the Associate Professor rank require a minimum of 5 years of related experience. Appointments at the Full Professor rank require a minimum of 10 years of related experience.   Additional Information: Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, a cover letter, statements addressing the prompts below, and a list of at least three references. Applications will be reviewed beginning on November 16th.   Successful faculty at Northeastern are dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering a sense of belonging among all members of the Northeastern community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the experiences, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your experience, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts:   Research statement: Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, the funding you have received to support the work (if applicable), the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students, with the external community, and/or with individuals from marginalized groups. Finally, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing in the College of Arts, Media and Design. Please limit the research statement to 3 pages.   Teaching statement: Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the College of Arts, Media and Design (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a diverse cohort of undergraduate and graduate students.  Please limit the teaching statement to 2 pages.   All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by the law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $94,000.00 - $124,000.00 | Associate Professor: $104,000.00 - $145,000.00 | Professor: $117,000.00 - $200,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director-Compliance-and-Enrollment-Management_R142922": {
      "title": "Assistant Director Compliance and Enrollment Management",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Assistant Director of Compliance and Enrollment Management promotes education, monitoring and tracking of NCAA compliance and assists in enrollment services. The position plays an integral role in promoting a culture of integrity at Northeastern through education, communication and positive relationships. Primary responsibilities include: creating and delivering rules education to coaches and staff; collecting and monitoring documentation related to playing and practice seasons, recruiting and other pertinent areas; maintenance of required reports, forms and databases; providing NCAA rules interpretations and assisting the Associate AD in working with campus partners (admissions, financial aid, housing, registrar, colleges). This role provides a pathway to engage coaches, student-athletes and staff in embracing NCAA, University and department policies and procedures. Key Accountabilities & Responsibilities: Compliance Monitoring & Rules Education Review required documentation for compliance with NCAA rules. Types of documentation reviewed includes: playing and practice seasons, recruiting (i.e. complimentary ticket requests), awards & benefits (i.e. donation requests), financial aid, eligibility, squad lists, amateurism. Develop and implement a comprehensive rules education program for student-athletes, coaches, staff, alumni, and external constituents. Assist Associate AD in conducting beginning and end of the year team meetings as well as coaches on-boarding meetings. Process waivers and violations as assigned. Assist with staff rules interpretation requests and review of proposed and adopted NCAA legislation. Compliance Record Maintenance Regularly update the data in Compliance Assistant and Teamworks Compliance (formerly ARMS). Maintain and submit forms required by the institution, conference and NCAA including: Squad lists, medical/personal hardship petitions, NCAA Transfer Portal, NIL, academic year rollover in LSDBi and Teamworks Compliance, assists with the official visit approval process, collect end of the year participation reports, distribute Initial Eligibility Progress reports, conduct compliance audits as assigned, conduct recruiting exams, and monitor continuing eligibility certification. Assist with Athletic Scholarship program, collection of NCAA Academic Performance Program information and annual update of department compliance handbook. Enrollment Services Assists the Associate AD with processes as needed. Annually review and revise the department student-athlete handbook. Act as secondary liaison with institutional constituents including Admissions, Financial Aid, Registrar. General Administration Contribute to the overall advancement of the athletics department by promoting an atmosphere of compliance and, thus, maintaining institutional control. Assist the Associate AD for Compliance/ES with continued development and implementation of a successful compliance program. Ensure the integrity of University and departmental policies and procedures with the timely submission of accurate expense reconciliation and time off from work requests. Assist in updating and/or revising sections of the Compliance policy and procedure materials as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Knowledge of NCAA rules interpretations and presenting education sessions, Teamworks Compliance (formerly ARMS) recruiting software system, Banner, Compliance Assistant, NCAA, LSDBi and RSRO systems. Strong verbal and written communication skills. Ability to prepare well written correspondence, letters. Proficient with Microsoft Word, Excel, Access, Outlook and Publisher. Knowledge and skills typically acquired through a bachelor's degree (Master's degree or J.D. preferred), and a minimum of 2 years of full-time, grad or intern experience working with NCAA compliance (at an NCAA member institution, conference office, or the NCAA national office). Position requires occasional travel; nights and weekends required as necessary. Position Type Legal and Regulatory Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Human-Services-Program_R142910": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Human Services Program",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.    Responsibilities: The Human Services Program in the College of Social Sciences and Humanities seeks applicants to teach Human Services undergraduate courses in one or more of the following fields: expressive arts, art therapy, and advocacy/activism.   Positions are available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and departmental need.   Qualifications: A master’s degree in Social Work or a related field is required and teaching experience in Human Services or Social Work is preferred. Academic preparation and work experience relating to the Human Services field required. Evidence of effective teaching experience strongly preferred.   Documents to Submit: Cover letter and CV. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,085."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Philosophy-and-Religion_R142911": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Philosophy and Religion",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe. Responsibilities: The Department of Philosophy and Religion in the College of Social Sciences and Humanities seeks a part-time instructor to teach introductory level undergraduate courses in philosophy, religious studies, and ethics. Positions are available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and departmental need. Qualifications: Ph.D. required. Documents to Submit: Cover letter and CV. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,085."
