    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Cultures--Societies--and-Global-Studies_R142907": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Cultures, Societies, and Global Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.  Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate courses in Africana Studies, Global Asian Studies, and Latinx, Latin American and Caribbean Studies in the Department of Cultures, Societies and Global Studies. Responsibilities beyond teaching-related duties include participating in departmental meetings and holding regular office hours. Positions are available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and departmental need. Qualifications: Master’s degree required, Ph.D. degree preferred with college level teaching experience. Documents to Submit: CV and Cover Letter required. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,085."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Research-Data-Analyst--DIBI_R142888": {
      "title": "Research Data Analyst, DIBI",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY The Defense Industrial Base Institute at Northeastern University (NU) – a rapidly growing defense focused institute that conducts cutting-edge applied R&D – is seeking a highly motivated, experienced and enthusiastic  Jr. Data Researcher with expertise in applying methods/frameworks/systems that apply ML&AI. The Jr. Data Researcher is expected to work as part of a multi-disciplinary team (academia, industry and government) and contribute to the successful execution of federally funded projects/activities.   Responsibilities include providing technical support to Principal investigators or co-principal investigators as a Jr. Data Researcher for a wide range of projects and activities involving machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI), including autonomy, sensing and communication, and decision support systems, among others. The Jr. Data Researcher will work collaboratively with multi-disciplinary teams across the DIBI Higher Education Consortium, consisting of academic, government, industry partners, to recommend solutions or support institute leadership on prototypes for projects in application areas, including autonomous systems, robotics, and machine learning systems, among others. Specifically, the Jr. Data Researcher will help to create and modify on the back end the existing Navigator methodology; working with other data scientists and leadership across the institute.   Successful candidates will be responsible team players and passionate about machine learning technologies, as well as possess a deep understanding of machine learning technology and experience in turning machine learning systems into practical, state-of-the-art systems that can be enhanced for projects or activities as per federal agency SOW.. A close working relationship with and support of thinking creatively on application of Navigator frame work for government and industry contracts will be required.   The primary office for this position is located at NU’s Boston Campus Through NU, the institute offers an impressive benefits package, including multiple retirement plan options with extremely generous matching, as well as tuition waiver for classes and advanced degree programs. A full description of available benefits can be found on the NU website . MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Required Bachelor’s or Master’s degree in Data science, Computer Engineering, Computer Science, Applied Mathematics, or a closely related field. 2–4 years of professional experience in software engineering, data science, or applied R&D, with exposure to machine learning and AI system development in research, prototype, or production environments. Preferred Master’s degree with a focus on ML/AI, data ‑ intensive systems, network science, optimization, or related areas. Experience contributing to government, defense, or security ‑ related R&D programs (internships, fellowships, or full ‑ time roles). Familiarity with simulation ‑ based models (e.g., physics ‑ based, network ‑ based, agent ‑ based, or stochastic simulations) for analysis, experimentation, or decision support.    Desired Skills & Attributes Required Proficiency in Python and familiarity with modern ML/AI development workflows. Exposure to C++ and/or Java for performance‑critical components is a plus Experience contributing to the design, implementation, testing, or evaluation of ML/AI‑enabled or simulation‑driven software systems. Hands‑on experience with machine learning frameworks (e.g., PyTorch), including model training, evaluation, and experimentation. Familiarity with distributed or accelerated computing environments (e.g., GPU‑enabled systems, shared compute clusters ). Working knowledge of database systems, including: Relational databases (e.g., PostgreSQL / SQL) Exposure to graph databases (e.g., Neo4j, Memgraph , or similar) Familiarity with cloud computing environments (e.g., Azure, AWS, or GovCloud equivalents), including containerized or scalable ML workflows. Solid software engineering fundamentals, including version control, modular code design, testing, documentation, and reproducibility. Ability to rapidly prototype solutions and iterate toward more robust implementations with guidance from senior engineers. Self‑motivated team member able to work independently on well‑defined tasks while contributing to broader project objectives. U.S. Citizenship with the ability to obtain and maintain a security clearance. Desired:  Exposure to Retrieval‑Augmented Generation (RAG), vector databases, embedding pipelines, or LLM‑enabled systems. Familiarity with network science or graph analytics concepts, including: Graph modeling and analysis using tools such as NetworkX Introductory experience with graph‑based ML or GNNs is a plus Monte Carlo or stochastic simulation methods Synthetic data generation or simulation‑in‑the‑loop workflows Exposure to geospatial data, spatiotemporal datasets, or PostgreSQL/PostGIS is a plus. Interest in UI or frontend development for technical or analyst‑facing tools (e.g., Svelte , React, or similar frameworks). Familiarity with MLOps concepts, experiment tracking, or reproducible research pipelines. Experience working with multidisciplinary teams across research, engineering, and applied R&D environments. Key Responsibilities: Support Research and Development efforts to scale Navigator Methodology applied to defense contracts (SIB),to include, development and implementation, prototype modeling & simulation, design, and experimentation. This supports the defense funding received that supports this role. (40%) Test and validation of soAI/ML Tools for sprints, tasks,  projects and deliverables; to include scaling of projects by suggesting tools and systems that enhance efficiency and support humans in their work. (40%) Provide subject matter expertise across a diverse set of application areas and ad hoc requests that may come in from the sponsor. This may include attending meetings, visits, conferences and engagements with US Government, industry and academic partners. Research and support PI or CO-PIs with scoping project budgets as appropriate (Systems, tools) (10%) Special Projects as assigned: Working closely with Managing Director, Founding Director and other core leadership members, help to scope proposals and opportunities. Working closely with Managing Director and Sr. Director, Strategy and Communications to make recommendations on systems build out for internal operations (10%) Position Type Data Planning and Analysis Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Data-Scientist--DIBI_R142889": {
      "title": "Associate Data Scientist, DIBI",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY The Defense Industrial Base Institute at Northeastern University (NU) – a rapidly growing defense focused institute that conducts cutting-edge applied R&D – is seeking an experienced and highly capable Jr. Data Scientist with expertise in applying methods/frameworks/systems that apply ML&AI. The Jr. Data Scientist will operate as part of a multi-disciplinary team  (academia, industry and government) and will contribute directly to the successful execution of federally funded projects/activities,   Responsibilities include providing technical expertise and analytical support to Principal investigators or co-principal investigators as a Jr. Data Scientist for a wide range of projects and activities involving machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI), including autonomy, sensing and communication, and decision support systems, among others. The Jr, Data Scientist will work collaboratively with and help guide, multi-disciplinary teams across the DIBI Higher Education Consortium — spanning academic, government, and industry partners — to design, validate, and maintain datasets and analytical products that inform institute leadership and sponsor decision-making. Specifically, the Jr. Data Scientist will take a role in building and maintaining the data pipelines and analytical components underlying the existing Navigator methodology, working closely with — and providing guidance to — data researchers, engineers, and leadership across the institute. Successful candidates will be responsible team players and passionate about machine learning technologies, as well as possess a deep understanding of machine learning technology and experience in turning machine learning systems into practical, state-of-the-art systems that can be enhanced for projects or activities as per federal agency. A close working relationship with the team, along with creative thinking on the application of the Navigator framework for government and industry contracts, will be required.   The primary office for this position is located at NU’s Boston Campus Through NU, the institute offers an impressive benefits package, including multiple retirement plan options with extremely generous matching, as well as tuition waiver for classes and advanced degree programs. A full description of available benefits can be found on the NU website . MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Education & Experience Required Master’s degree in Data Science, Statistics, Computer Science Computer Science, Applied Mathematics, or a closely related field  2–4 years of professional experience in software engineering, data science, or applied R&D, with exposure to machine learning and AI system development in research, prototype, or production environments. Preferred Master’s degree or Ph.D with a focus on data science, data ‑ intensive systems, network science, optimization, or related areas. Experience contributing to government, defense, or security ‑ related R&D programs (internships, fellowships, or full ‑ time roles). Familiarity with simulation ‑ based models (e.g., physics ‑ based, network ‑ based, agent ‑ based, or stochastic simulations) for analysis, experimentation, or decision support. undefined   Skills & Attributes Required Advanced proficiency in Python and/or R studio and familiarity with modern ML/AI development workflows. Exposure to C++ and/or Java for performance‑critical components Experience contributing to the design, implementation, testing, or evaluation of ML/AI-enabled or simulation-drive software systems. Demonstrated experience with data visualization and reporting tools (e.g., Tableau, Power BI, matplotlib, Plotly), including designing dashboards for executive or sponsor audiences. Proven experience independently building, validating, and interpreting statistical or machine learning models, and communicating tradeoffs to non-technical stakeholders. Strong command of data cleaning, ETL processes, and data quality assurance practices, including for messy or incomplete real-world datasets. Solid software engineering fundamentals, including version control (e.g., Git/GitHub), documentation, and reproducibility. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with experience presenting analytical findings directly to leadership or sponsors. U.S. Citizenship with the ability to obtain and maintain a security clearance Desired:  Exposure to Retrieval‑Augmented Generation (RAG), vector databases, embedding pipelines, or LLM‑enabled systems. Familiarity with cloud computing environments (e.g., Azure, AWS or GovCloud equivalents). Introductory experience with graph‑based ML or GNNs is a plus Monte Carlo or stochastic simulation methods Synthetic data generation or simulation‑in‑the‑loop workflows Familiarity with  geospatial data, spatiotemporal datasets, or PostgreSQL/PostGIS is a plus. Interest in UI or frontend development for technical or analyst‑facing tools (e.g., Svelte , React, or similar frameworks). Exposure to dashboarding or BI tools for analyst-facing or leadership-facing reporting Familiarity with MLOps concepts, experiment tracking, or reproducible research pipelines. Experience working with multidisciplinary teams across research, engineering, and applied R&D environments. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Support Research and Development efforts to scale Navigator methodology applied to defense contracts (SIB), to include, development and implementation, prototype modeling & simulation, design, and experimentation. This supports the defense funding received that supports this role (40%) Test and validation of soAI/ML Tools for sprints, tasks, projects and deliverables; to include scaling of projects by suggesting tools and systems that enhance efficiency and support humans in their work (40%) Provide subject matter expertise across a diverse set of application areas and ad hoc requests that may come in from the sponsor.  Present information and answer questions form government sponsors and stakeholders.Research and support PI or CO-PIs with scoping project budgets as appropriate (Systems, tools) (10%) Special Projects as assigned: Working closely with Managing Director, Founding Director and other core leadership members, help to scope proposals and opportunities. Working closely with DIBI Leadership Team and Sr. Director, Strategy and Communications to make recommendations on systems build out for internal operations (10%) Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $76,335.00 - $107,823.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142878-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Northeastern University seeks a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join a fixed-term, mixed-methods research initiative investigating the validity the International Big Picture Learning Credential (IBPLC) as a rigorous, equitable pathway into college admissions. The project — a partnership between Northeastern, Big Picture Learning U.S. (BPL U.S.), Big Picture Learning Australia (BPL Australia), and the National Association for College Admission Counseling (NACAC), and supported by the YTL Fund — examines whether the IBPLC predicts postsecondary success, whether advisors apply its assessment frames reliably and consistently, whether the credential measures what it claims to measure, and how admissions professionals come to trust it. The Postdoctoral Associate will work closely with the Principal Investigator(s) and partner organizations, with substantial input across all components of the study. Responsibilities Design and manage the longitudinal tracking system for enrolled IBPLC students, coordinating consent, data collection, and case-study protocols with BPL U.S. sites Conduct quantitative analysis of predictive validity, linking IBPLC scores and assessment data to first- and second-year college outcomes (grades, persistence, survey measures) Coordinate the reliability study: design observation protocols for IBPLC authorization training and school-based advisory case studies, and analyze consistency of advisor scoring across sites Support the construct validity aim by coordinating the subject matter expert panel reviewing IBPLC assessment frames across all six Learning Goals, and by analyzing alignment between frames and student performance data Design, field, and analyze annual admissions-leader focus groups, pilot application readings, and advisor and student surveys; lead qualitative coding of interview and focus group data Maintain IRB protocols, data use agreements, and study documentation Lead drafting of annual research summaries, the final comprehensive report, and dissemination products, including peer-reviewed manuscripts, practitioner-facing briefs, and conference presentations Assist in administrative tasks of the study Qualifications Required: PhD or EdD in education, educational measurement/psychometrics, sociology, higher education, or a related field, completed by the start date Demonstrated mixed-methods research skills, including experience with longitudinal, predictive validity, or reliability designs and proficiency in R, SPSS, or comparable statistical software Experience with qualitative data collection and coding (e.g., interviews, focus groups, case studies) and familiarity with tools such as NVivo, Dedoose, or Atlas.ti Experience with survey design and administration (Qualtrics or equivalent) and rigorous data management practices Record of scholarly writing and demonstrated ability to communicate findings to practitioner and policy audiences Preferred: Background in competency-based education, alternative credentials, or college admissions research Experience with quantitative and psychometric analyses Familiarity with National Student Clearinghouse data or other national postsecondary outcome datasets Experience facilitating focus groups or expert panels with professional stakeholders Experience managing multi-site IRB protocols and data use agreements Experience in US high schools Position Details Grant-funded appointment, contingent on funding and satisfactory performance and renewable based on available funding Salary commensurate with experience, consistent with Northeastern postdoctoral scales, plus standard benefits Boston campus, hybrid schedule; some domestic travel expected Reports to the Principal Investigator(s) of the IBPLC Validity & Implementation Study Applications without a Cover Letter will not be considered Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Global-Director--Global-Service-Experience_R142915": {
      "title": "Global Director, Global Service Experience",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: Northeastern University’s Office of the Registrar operates a federated network service model that serves 66,000 students - degree, non-degree, and continuing education - across 14 campuses in three countries. The Global Director of Global Service Experience is the network-wide leader responsible for the service model, delivery, and outcomes of the academic and registrar service experience.  Those served include current, former, and prospective students; parents; advisors; faculty; college administration; peer student support units; regulators, among others. This role does not exist at Northeastern today and is being created to affirm, within the Provost's academic and student services portfolio, that student experience is not a function — it is the center of everything we do and how we do it. It brings a singular focus on service excellence across the academic and student service portfolio in the global network, amplifying and advancing work in ServiceNow, including continuing to expand the AI chatbot and related functionality, service assessment, and SLA frameworks.   Students at Charlotte, Silicon Valley, Vancouver, and London, for example, have different needs, different compliance contexts, and different campus cultures. This leader owns the design, consistency, and continuous improvement of the academic and student services portfolio. The Global Director of Global Service Experience leads the four cluster Sr. Associate Registrars (Boston, NYC, Oakland, London) who are the front-facing registrar presence for students and other populations they serve, as well as the network-wide service team that supports former and prospective students as well as other stakeholders. This is a leadership role for someone who believes that excellent service is built by design proactively, not delivered by heroics reactively.  Role Responsibilities: Own the student service experience across the global network — setting standards, measuring outcomes, and driving continuous improvement network-wide. Lead and develop the four cluster Sr. Associate Registrars, providing strategic direction, operational support, and professional development. Design and implement service standards that are consistent across the network while remaining responsive to the unique needs of each campus population. Champion the student lens in every operational and policy decision — ensuring that what the student experiences is always the test of whether something is working Build the operational infrastructure for cluster-based service delivery— including adjusting ServiceNow case management standards, escalation pathways, SLA frameworks, and cross-cluster knowledge sharing. Partner with the Registrar for Global Operations to ensure cluster teams have the operational knowledge, tools, and support they need to serve students effectively — including an embedded rotation model in which cluster service staff are periodically placed within central specialist teams as an experiential approach to building deep institutional knowledge. Build bench depth across the cluster network — developing generalist registrar capability that grows into future leadership roles across the institution and the field Identify and escalate structural barriers to student service — including cross-office coordination failures, policy gaps, and technology limitations — with solutions, both short and longer term. Represent the student service function in senior leadership conversations, contributing a clear and data-informed point of view on what students need and what the network is and isn’t delivering. Minimum Qualifications: Master's degree required in any field; equivalent combination of advanced education and demonstrated leadership in service design and excellence, customer/student experience, or operational management, or related. 7+ years of progressive leadership experience in service design, delivery, and assessment aimed at continuous service enhancements, or related, preferably in a global and multi-point of service context. Demonstrated experience leading geographically distributed teams, with a track record of building cohesive culture and shared standards across locations Experience working with case management systems, service analytics, or similar operational platforms. Exceptional communication skills — with students, staff, faculty, and senior leadership — including the ability to represent frontline realities at the executive level and work across the institution to solve escalated cases. A genuine commitment to the student experience as the primary measure of success, combined with the operational discipline to build systems that deliver it consistently A leadership philosophy that values people, presence, and relationships as the foundation of institutional trust. Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 114S Expected Hiring Range: $130,945.00 - $189,868.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142905": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The successful candidate is expected to perform high-level theoretical research at the interface of theoretical physics and artificial intelligence (AI). The individual will work primarily with Jim Halverson and Fabian Ruehle and will become a Junior Investigator at the National Science Foundation (NSF) AI Institute for Artificial Intelligence and Fundamental Interactions (IAIFI), where they will participate in IAIFI activities and interact with scientists from Harvard, Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), and Tufts. Minimum Qualifications Required PhD in Physics or an adjacent field, such as Mathematics, completed by the start of the appointment Knowledge of High Energy Theory and Machine Learning, as usually acquired through a PhD in Physics or a related field Preferred Experience in string theory Experience in artificial intelligence (AI) Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Conduct academic research at the highest level in accordance with the IAIFI mission Disseminate research results through research papers, conference presentations, and related scholarly communications Required Documents Resume Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Burlington-MA/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142904": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The DeLTA Lab, led by Professor Alberto de la Torre, studies how light can be used to control and engineer new phases of matter in quantum materials and has an opening for a Postdoctoral Research Associate. The position is funded by Professor de la Torre’s National Science Foundation (NSF) CAREER award, “Engineering order in frustrated quantum materials via strong light-matter coupling.” The postdoctoral researcher will design and fabricate photonic cavities for strong light-matter coupling at infrared (IR) and terahertz (THz) frequencies and develop on-chip ultrafast transport platforms. Both platforms will be integrated with exfoliated flakes and thin films of frustrated quantum materials and characterized with the lab’s time-resolved optical, THz, and second-harmonic-generation spectroscopies, including a 7 T, 1.5 K optical cryostat fitted with radio frequency (RF) leads. The postdoctoral researcher will work closely with collaborators in the College of Engineering, fabricate devices in the Kostas cleanroom, and help mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers, including co-op researchers. The appointment is for one year and is renewable for a second year contingent on satisfactory performance and continued funding. Minimum Qualifications Required PhD or foreign equivalent in Physics, Applied Physics, Electrical Engineering, Materials Science, or a closely related field, completed by the start date Knowledge of electromagnetic and photonic device design, micro- and nanofabrication, and optical or electrical characterization of materials, as normally acquired through a PhD in the field Ability to design, fabricate, and test optical cavities and/or high-frequency electronic devices Ability to perform electromagnetic simulations, such as finite-difference time-domain (FDTD) or finite-element methods Ability to independently plan experiments, analyze data, and communicate results in peer-reviewed publications Preferred Hands-on experience with cleanroom nanofabrication, such as lithography, thin-film deposition, and etching Experience designing photonic, THz, or microwave cavities Experience with ultrafast optics, THz or on-chip picosecond electronics, superconducting devices, or cryogenic measurements Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Photonic Cavity Design and Fabrication (30%): Design, simulate, and fabricate photonic cavities, including IR/mid-IR distributed Bragg reflector (DBR) microcavities and quartz/vacuum THz cavities, resonant with collective excitations of target quantum materials using FDTD and inverse-design methods. Integrate exfoliated flakes and thin films into the cavities and characterize the cavity response and light-matter coupling with optical and THz spectroscopy On-Chip Ultrafast Transport Platforms (30%): Design and fabricate on-chip ultrafast transport platforms based on photoconductive Auston switches and coplanar waveguides. Perform device processing at the Kostas cleanroom, including lithography, deposition, etching, and wire bonding Device Testing and Measurement (20%): Test and benchmark the devices using time-resolved optical and electrical measurements, including pump-probe photoconductive sampling, THz time-domain spectroscopy, and second-harmonic generation, at room temperature and in the 7 T, 1.5 K optical cryostat. Use these platforms to measure light-induced changes in the transport and optical properties of frustrated quantum materials Research Communication (10%): Analyze data, prepare manuscripts for peer-reviewed journals, present results at group meetings and international conferences, including the American Physical Society (APS) March Meeting and the Conference on Lasers and Electro-Optics (CLEO), and contribute to project reports and proposals Mentoring and Laboratory Practices (10%): Mentor and train graduate, undergraduate, and co-op researchers in device design, cleanroom fabrication, and ultrafast measurement techniques, and help maintain safe laboratory and cleanroom practices. The position carries no formal teaching duties Required Documents Resume Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Business-Relationship-Manager_R133113": {
      "title": "Business Relationship Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY The Business Relationship Manager (BRM) serves as a pivotal partner and advisor to business units, focusing on aligning IT solutions with the university's strategic objectives to deliver maximum value, bridging the gap between business partners and technology solution providers, to ensure IT initiatives are closely aligned with business strategies. The role requires flexibility in performing duties outside of the primary responsibilities to support evolving business needs. The ideal candidate must be adaptable and willing to take on additional tasks or projects as required, ensuring smooth operations across the organization. This may include stepping in to assist with cross-functional teams, handling unexpected challenges, or contributing to initiatives that support business growth and success. A proactive mindset and the ability to pivot quickly are essential for thriving in this dynamic environment. The role is hybrid and in the office a minimum of three days a week to facilitate collaboration and teamwork. In-office presence is an essential part of our on-campus culture and allows for engaging directly with staff and students, sharing ideas, and contributing to a dynamic work environment. Being on-site allows for stronger connections, more effective problem-solving, and enhanced team synergy, all of which are key to achieving our collective goals and driving success. Occasional availability outside of traditional working hours is required to address urgent business needs as they arise, including, but not limited to, responding to security incidents, supporting software deployments, resolving software issues or system breaks, and addressing other critical operational requirements. The ideal candidate must be proactive and adaptable, ensuring minimal disruption to business operations by promptly addressing any issues, regardless of time or day. Flexibility and a strong sense of urgency are essential for success in this position. Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The University is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Knowledge and skills required for this position are normally obtained through a Bachelor’s degree in computer science, management information systems, or a related field and six to eight years of project and relationship management-related experience focusing on strategic business engagement, preferred. Additional skills and experience: Analytical and Creative Thinking: Strong analytical skills to critically evaluate information and contribute innovative ideas that add business value and enhance solution creation. Stakeholder Management and Conflict Resolution: Ability to manage stakeholder expectations, resolve conflicts, and influence decision-making processes to align IT services with business needs. Effective Communication: Excellent communication skills to articulate IT capabilities and solutions to business constituents, ensuring they understand available services and self-service options. Broad Business and IT Perspective: A comprehensive understanding of both business and IT landscapes, with the ability to bridge the gap and ensure alignment between the two. Leadership and Mentorship: Strong leadership skills to guide and mentor a team of BRMs, fostering a collaborative and strategic approach to relationship management. Technical Knowledge and Orchestration: Familiarity with technical areas such as risk management, architecture, and testing, with the ability to orchestrate the delivery of this knowledge to business areas. Product Management Understanding: Knowledge of product management concepts and the ability to collaborate effectively with product/service managers to align solutions with business needs. Adaptability and Flexibility: A flexible and adaptive style to navigate diverse organizational environments and evolving business needs, ensuring the delivery of optimal value. Negotiation and Influence: Strong negotiation skills to influence stakeholders and secure necessary resources and support for IT initiatives. Emotional Intelligence: High emotional intelligence to navigate complex interpersonal dynamics and build strong, trusting relationships with business partners. Strategic Visioning: Ability to develop and communicate a clear strategic vision that aligns IT initiatives with long-term business goals. Project Management Expertise: Advanced project management skills to oversee complex IT projects, ensuring they are delivered on time, within scope, and on budget. Cultural Awareness: Sensitivity to cultural differences and the ability to work effectively in diverse, global environments. Innovation and Creativity: A strong inclination towards innovation, encouraging the exploration of new technologies and approaches to solve business challenges. Crisis Management: Ability to manage and resolve crises effectively, maintaining business continuity and minimizing impact on operations. Networking and Relationship Building: Proficiency in building and maintaining a network of contacts within the industry to stay informed about trends and opportunities. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES AND ACCOUNTABILITIES 1) Strategic Relationship Building, 40% Develop and nurture strong, strategic relationships with business partners to develop comprehensive understanding of and help define objectives, strategies, and challenges. Serve as a trusted advisor and liaison between business units and IT. 2) Advisory Role in Technology Strategy, 30% Provide strategic advice to business partners on technology trends and opportunities, helping shape their technology strategy and ensuring alignment with IT service offerings and capabilities. 3) Promotion of Business Value, 15% Advocate for business projects and their value within the IT community, ensuring that IT leaders understand the strategic importance of these initiatives. 4) Collaboration with Product/Service Managers, 15% Work closely with product/service managers to provide insights into business needs, enabling them to prioritize and develop plans for solutions that align with business strategies. Position Type Information Technology Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 113S Expected Hiring Range: $99,010.00 - $143,567.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer--School-of-Nursing--Clinical_R142895": {
      "title": "Part Time Lecturer, School of Nursing, Clinical",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description The School of Nursing is seeking part-time faculty to teach clinical courses in the following areas: Adult Medical/Surgical, Public Community Health, Maternal Child/Women's Heath, Pediatrics, and Psychiatric/Mental Health, and Senior Practicum. Part-time instructor positions are available as determined by the department’s curricular and institutional needs. A nursing clinical instructor plays a crucial role in the education and training of nursing students. Their primary responsibility is to facilitate the clinical learning experiences of nursing students and ensure that they develop the knowledge, skills, and competencies required to become competent and safe nurses. Responsibilities include educating, evaluating, supervising, mentoring, and professional role modeling.    Requirements to teach a clinical course include a minimum of a Master’s Degree in Nursing, a minimum of two years’ relevant professional nursing experience, and a current Massachusetts RN license. Previous teaching experience is highly desirable. Start dates: May, September, and January. Seeking applicants with significant career accomplishments within subject area and college teaching experience in a large, global, higher education institution. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2085"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/METS-Program-Assistant_R142880-1": {
      "title": "METS Program Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity 1.    Job Summary Assist in providing comprehensive academic advising workshops to program participants. In collaboration with METS team plan and supervise all METS related activities, events, field trips and college tours. Collaborate with other college access partners and school personnel to promote college awareness for all students. In collaboration with METS team, assist with coordination and implementation of all facets of the METS Program. Participate in METS staff meetings.   2.    Minimum Qualifications Experience and ability to effectively work with students with diverse academic, socioeconomic, cultural, and ethnic backgrounds similar to METS Program participants. Mission-driven and passionate about programs designed to identify and provide services for individuals from disadvantaged backgrounds. Ability to coordinate and facilitate academic support programs including advising, workshops, fieldtrips, assessment, follow-up and resource referral. Excellent written, oral and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to take direction, prioritize, and work independently. Demonstrated experience-solving problems including conflict resolution. Demonstrated ability to work well under pressure or on deadline, maintaining a professional composure when dealing with common problems that occur in events and classroom settings.     3.    Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Serve as a METS Representative at the assigned target school(s). Program Assistants work with the Academic Advisor to provide assistance in college advising services to assigned cohorts at target schools. Responsible for dissemination of college resources & information throughout the school sites. Responsible for co-facilitating college workshops through monthly seminars & in-class workshops. Topics addressed through the workshops include: career exploration, college preparation, college admissions information, financial aid, cultural awareness, hands-on activities. Recruitment & publicity of program services. Distribute and collect METS student applications, share METS informational flyers and program event calendars with current and prospective participants and families. Maintenance of METS space in College Centers at school sites. Increase college awareness and post current informational flyers for upcoming community events and deadlines for college applications, financial aid applications, and scholarships. Attendance at School Events: Collaborative meetings & events, parent meetings, back to school nights, registration drives, orientations, college events, etc. Attend weekly METS staff meetings and 1-1 meetings with supervisor. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $25/hour"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Dean-of-Administration---Finance---College-of-Science_R142887": {
      "title": "Associate Dean of Administration & Finance - College of Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the College of Science The College of Science (COS) offers instructional and research programs at the forefront of discovery, invention, and innovation across the physical sciences, life sciences, mathematics, and linguistics. The college comprises six academic departments and is home to 15 research centers and institutes, with faculty and students engaged in collaborative, cross-disciplinary, and often AI-linked research spanning fields such as biotechnology, neuroscience, network science, marine and environmental science, and nanoscience, with locations expanding across the campus network. Job Summary The Associate Dean of Administration and Finance (ADAF) is the principal administrative and budget officer and strategist for the college, reporting to the Dean. A member of the college's senior leadership team, the Associate Dean helps formulate and implement the college's strategic plan in a dynamic and fast-moving university. As the lead administrator for the college, the Associate Dean is responsible for oversight, implementation, and planning of processes, policies, and projects in budgeting, human resources, sponsored research administration, space management, and college-specific information technology. The Associate Dean leads a team of professionals responsible for all financial and budget operations, business modeling and planning, space management, personnel actions, and grants and contracts administration originating in the college. The role oversees operating, restricted, development, start-up, renovation, and cost-share accounts and provides strategic analysis and guidance to enhance revenue, support budget projections, and inform decisions related to growth and innovation. The Associate Dean advises the Dean on annual revenue and expense budgets, develops multi-year financial scenarios, and collaborates with associate deans, department chairs, and staff to support initiatives aligned with college and university priorities. The Associate Dean leads staffing allocation decisions across the college, develops strategies to address evolving academic and operational needs, and applies change management practices to guide organizational and staffing transitions. The role provides analytical reporting on enrollment, staffing, financial, and space trends to support resource allocation decisions and works closely with Human Resources and Faculty Affairs on recruitment, retention, performance reviews, compensation actions, faculty offer packages, space planning, and merit review processes. Working closely with the Associate Dean of Research, the Associate Dean oversees sponsored research administration, including pre-award and post-award grant management, and ensures compliance with federal, state, and sponsor requirements. The Associate Dean also manages the college's space portfolio and coordinates planning, site evaluation, facilities upgrades, renovations, and capital projects across the college's Boston campus and additional locations in Burlington, Oakland, Nahant, New York City, Portland, and Toronto. The Associate Dean provides financial and space reporting to the Provost's Office and other university-wide units and represents the Dean and the college on committees, working groups, and external engagements. Qualifications Master's degree required; MBA preferred Minimum of 10 years of progressively responsible leadership experience managing all administrative functions of a large, multi-million-dollar operation, preferably in higher education Minimum of 5 years of managerial and supervisory experience Experience in grant and contract administration Experience in project management, change management, and resource and staff allocation Experience using financial databases and developing analytical reports Strong analytical, communication, negotiation, and facilitation skills Demonstrated ability to manage multiple concurrent projects and priorities Commitment to professionalism and a strong service orientation Demonstrated ability to work effectively with faculty, students, and staff Key Responsibilities and Accountabilities Financial Management & Budget Administration Develop and manage the college's annual operating budget and multi-year financial models under the university's hybrid budget model Oversee operating, restricted, development, start-up, renovation, and cost-share accounts Prepare quarterly budget-to-actual reporting and advise the Dean on revenue planning, expense forecasting, and business operations Oversee pre-award and post-award grants and contracts administration and ensure compliance with federal, state, and sponsor regulations Collaborate with central budget, the Office of the Provost, and administrative leaders across colleges on finance and administration matters Strategic Planning & Analytics Serve as a member of the college's senior leadership team and support development and implementation of the college's strategic plan Develop analytical reports on enrollment, staffing, financial, and space trends to guide resource allocation decisions Support business proposals and strategic initiatives through data analysis and forecasting Identify opportunities for operational improvement, service optimization, and organizational effectiveness Proactively identify challenges and recommend solutions to support college and university priorities People Operations & Management Partner with Human Resources and Faculty Affairs on recruitment, retention, training, employee engagement, performance management, promotion, position changes, compensation actions, and faculty offer packages Support faculty space planning and merit review processes Lead college-wide staffing allocation decisions and organizational change initiatives using change management best practices Directly supervise, coach, and develop finance, budget, and research administration staff Operations, Space & Representation Manage the college's space portfolio, including space planning, site evaluation, capital projects, and facilities renovations Collaborate with university offices on facilities planning and college-specific IT initiatives Lead college-wide projects and initiatives as assigned by the Dean Represent the college on standing and ad hoc committees related to administration, finance, business processes, and institutional reporting Applicants should include the following documents with their application: Resume/CV Cover letter Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 115S Expected Hiring Range: $157,130.00 - $227,840.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Miami-FL/Operations-Assistant--Miami_R142872": {
      "title": "Operations Assistant- Miami",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Northeastern Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and a world leader in experiential learning. The same commitment to connecting with the world drives our use-inspired research enterprise. The university offers a comprehensive range of undergraduate and graduate programs leading to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Our campuses in Charlotte, N.C., San Francisco, Seattle, Miami, London, Toronto and Vancouver, BC are platforms for undergraduate and graduate learning and collaborative research. Northeastern pursues advanced research in security and materials at the Innovation Campus in Burlington, Massachusetts, and in coastal sustainability at the Marine Science Center in Nahant, Massachusetts. Position Summary The Campus Operations Assistant serves as a strategic leader responsible for fostering community, designing engagement programming, and supporting operational excellence across Northeastern University's Miami campus. Reporting to the Associate Director of Operations, this position combines campus operations support with student, faculty, and staff engagement programming. The Campus Operations Assistant serves as the primary campus liaison for internal and external events, provides on-site training and guidance to work-study students (when applicable), creates internal marketing materials for campus events and programming, and provides hands-on technology support during events. As a key member of the campus operations team, this role works closely with campus leadership and staff, responds to requests from a variety of stakeholders, and follows up to ensure satisfaction — all while approaching each encounter in a gracious, attentive, courteous, and service-oriented manner. Qualifications Bachelor's degree and two to five years of progressively responsible administrative support experience in a fast-paced and service-oriented environment, or a relative combination of education and experience required. Experience in higher education preferred. Ability to work in a highly flexible and innovative environment with an expanding role and shifting responsibilities. Strong interpersonal and written communication skills required. High degree of diplomacy, customer service, and organizational skills required. Demonstrated ability to handle multiple tasks simultaneously, respond to last-minute requests, solve problems, act with discretion, adapt to change, and maintain a professional demeanor at all times. Willingness to be proactive, take initiative and ownership, and build collaborative relationships. Sound judgment and ability to deal effectively with highly visible and confidential matters is essential. Must enjoy meeting with people and handling the needs of a wide array of students, staff, faculty, and visitors. Must be able to handle sensitive material, set priorities, and work independently or as part of a team. High degree of proficiency with MS Office Suite and Outlook required. Experience with 25Live, Canvas, Workday, and event management platforms preferred. Familiarity with audio/video equipment and classroom technology required. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Student, Faculty, and Staff Engagement Programming ·       Design and implement engagement initiatives fostering community building, professional development, and interprofessional collaboration across health sciences programs ·       Develop faculty and staff recognition programs, team-building activities, mentorship initiatives, and networking events that strengthen campus culture ·       Track participation metrics, assess outcomes, and use data-driven insights to refine strategies and demonstrate impact to stakeholders ·       Coordinate event logistics including vendor relationships, promotional materials, space reservations, and day-of execution Campus Liaison & Strategic Event Coordination ·       Serve as primary point of contact for all internal and external campus events, providing expertise on space utilization, resource allocation, logistics planning, and institutional protocol ·       Coordinate comprehensive event logistics and provide day-of management, troubleshooting, and real-time problem-solving throughout events ·       Develop relationships with external organizations, vendors, and community partners while creating standard operating procedures to ensure consistency and quality ·       Ensure alignment with institutional standards, safety protocols, and accessibility requirements General Support to Campus Operations ·       Serve as a primary point of contact, internally and externally, for all activities related to campus, triaging and redirecting inquiries as appropriate to the campus leadership team ·       Serve as a collaborative partner to other campus functions (e.g., Operations, Academic & Student Affairs, Marketing & Recruiting), coordinating communications among separate campus functions as needed to ensure smooth and efficient operations ·       Act as a liaison and provide hospitality to incoming visitors including students and staff from other locations, business partners, and prospective students ·       Oversee daily campus operations to ensure a welcoming, professional, and well-maintained environment including reception coverage during high-traffic periods ·       Coordinate mail services, package deliveries, property access, and room scheduling for classes, meetings, and events ·       Maintain inventory of campus supplies and event materials Work-Study Student Training & On-Site Guidance ·       Train and provide on-site guidance to operations work-study students supporting campus operations and event execution ·       Develop training programs, standard operating procedures, and delegation frameworks for administrative tasks ·       Provide ongoing mentorship and professional development coaching to enhance transferable skills Marketing & Communications for Events and Engagement ·       Create internal marketing materials for campus events and engagement programming including flyers, digital signage, email communications, and a campus events newsletter — complementing (not duplicating) the work of the campus marketing team ·       Design visual marketing materials aligned with institutional brand standards for event promotion Classroom & Technology Support ·       Provide hands-on technology support during events, troubleshooting AV equipment and making real-time adjustments to ensure seamless presentations and programming in campus spaces ·       Assist with annual computer inventory ·       Perform basic troubleshooting for staff and faculty in classroom and conference room spaces Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 105H Expected Hiring Range: $18.69 - $25.70 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---International-Affairs-Program_R142906": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - International Affairs Program",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.      Responsibilities: Teach undergraduates related to one or more of the following areas: Globalization and International Affairs; International Conflict and Negotiation; Middle East Studies; Arab-Israeli Conflict; Research Methods; Climate and Development; Race and Global Human Mobility; Revolution, Civil War  and Insurrection; International Affairs Senior Capstone; Security, Culture and Power in the International Affairs Program. Responsibilities beyond teaching-related duties include participating in departmental meetings and holding regular office hours. Positions are available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and departmental need. Qualifications: Master’s degree is required. A Ph.D. degree is preferred with college-level teaching experience. Documents to Submit: CV and Cover Letter required. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,085."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Temp-Non-Student-Purchasing-Specialist_R142565-1": {
      "title": "Temp Non-Student Purchasing Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description: The Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering (MIE) seeks a Purchasing Specialist to serve as a department buyer, managing procure-to-pay and contract processes for research and department purchases. This position negotiates with vendors, processes purchase orders and contracts from initiation to completion, and partners with the post-award management team, NU-RES, and the Office of General Counsel on compliance and contract tracking. The role also provides financial operations support and student/research staff employment support for the department. Reporting to the Business Manager, this role requires strong attention to detail, discretion in handling sensitive financial and personnel information, and the ability to work independently within established university procurement and financial policies Minimum Qualifications: Education & Experience: Bachelor's degree required (Associate's degree considered with significant relevant experience). Minimum of two years of financial operations and purchasing experience; strong knowledge of procurement processes and grant compliance preferred. Demonstrated experience serving as a primary point of contact in a multi-constituent environment (students, faculty, visitors, vendors). Skills & Competencies: Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams) required. Experience with Workday Finance, IntelliBUY+, Concur, Smartsheet, and Airtable desired. Experience with purchasing card (p-card) programs and reconciliation processes desired. Demonstrated ability to negotiate with vendors, manage quotes, and coordinate contract processes Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $30/hr."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer--MLS-OLLM-Program--School-of-Law_R142883": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer, MLS/OLLM Program, School of Law",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description Northeastern University School of Law Department of Online and Hybrid Programs seeks part-time lecturers to teach and/or develop asynchronous online courses in our graduate law programs. The Master of Legal Studies (MLS) Program is designed for non-lawyer professionals working in highly regulated industries. Our focus areas include, but are not limited to, Legal Studies, Employment Law, Health Law, Business Law, Public Policy, and Intellectual Property. Courses in this program are 7 to 8 weeks long (half a semester). The Online Master of Laws (LLM) Program ( Program Website ) offers courses in general LLM curriculum (including bar preparation), International Business Law, and Intellectual Property Law. This program is designed for foreign-trained attorneys seeking to practice law in the United States, as well as U.S. JD holders interested in specialized legal fields. Courses in this program run for a full semester, 14 weeks. Instructors may be engaged in course development, teaching, or both. Those who develop courses typically teach them upon completion. Candidates should indicate in their cover letter which program(s) they are interested in teaching for (MLS, LLM, or both), as well as any relevant teaching experience (both on-ground and online). Applicants will also be required to select their program preference within the application form. This is a pooled posting; applications will be reviewed on an as-needed basis. Questions? Please reach out to Dan Urman at d.urman@northeastern.edu. Responsibilities: Courses are asynchronous (not live), but instructors must be available for virtual office hours and student meetings via phone or video conferencing. Faculty are to prepare course content prior to the assigned teaching term, this could mean updating case studies, recording short introductory videos, updating due dates and more. Instructors who develop courses are typically expected to teach them upon completion. Faculty are to provide detailed feedback on all student assignments each week, and engage students through announcements, discussion boards and live video check-ins. Faculty are required to enter final grades by the final grade deadline outlined by the Office of the University Registrar. Qualifications: A Juris Doctor (JD) is required; however, candidates with a PhD will also be considered. Previous experience in online teaching and course development is preferred. A strong record of academic and professional achievement, including expertise and experience in the field of law for which you are applying (MLS/Online LLM), is required. Documents to Submit: To apply, please submit the following documents: Resume/CV Cover letter (must specify interest in MLS, LLM, or both, as well as any relevant teaching experience, both on-ground and online) List of references   Pay Transparency: $2,654 - $3,261 per credit for online courses Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,654.00 - $3,261.00."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Athletic-Equipment-Manager_R142847": {
      "title": "Assistant Athletic Equipment Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Reporting to the Head Equipment Manager, the Assistant Equipment Manager supports the daily operations of the athletic equipment department. Responsibilities include laundry services, uniform distribution and collection, equipment inventory and maintenance, game-day setup and breakdown, storage organization, and transportation of athletic equipment. The position works closely with coaches, student-athletes, student workers, and athletics staff to support practices, competitions, team travel, and other department activities. Responsibilities: Assist with the distribution, collection, fitting, organization, maintenance, and storage of athletic equipment and apparel for assigned athletic programs. Maintain accurate equipment inventories and assist with periodic inventory counts. Inspect equipment and apparel for condition, cleanliness, and functionality; report damaged, missing, or unsafe equipment to the Head Equipment Manager. Assist with basic equipment fitting, adjustment, maintenance, repair, and modification. Prepare and organize equipment for practices, competitions, team travel, and other athletic events. Assist with game-day setup and breakdown, including equipment preparation, placement, collection, and storage. Operate department laundry equipment and process uniforms, practice gear, towels, and other athletic apparel. Maintain clean, organized, and secure equipment rooms, storage areas, and laundry facilities. Assist with loading, unloading, and transportation of equipment and supplies between university facilities and other locations as assigned. Operate university vehicles, as authorized, in accordance with university policies and applicable requirements. Provide limited direction and oversight to student workers, as assigned. Communicate equipment needs, shortages, damages, and operational concerns to the Head Equipment Manager. Perform other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to follow established procedures and maintain accurate records. Effective communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to work effectively with coaches, student-athletes, student workers, and athletics staff. Ability to safely handle and transport athletic equipment and supplies. Ability to work a variable schedule, including evenings, weekends, holidays, and athletic competitions. Valid driver's license and ability to meet university requirements for operation of university vehicles, as applicable. Preferred Qualifications Previous experience in athletic equipment management, collegiate athletics, recreation, or a related field. Experience with athletic equipment, uniforms, laundry operations, inventory control, or equipment transportation. Experience with basic equipment fitting, adjustment, maintenance, or repair.   Work Schedule This is a part-time position with a variable schedule based on departmental, team, and competition needs. The position may require evenings, weekends, holidays, and other non-traditional work hours, particularly during athletic seasons, competitions, team travel, and special events. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $25/hr"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Temporary-Partnership-Development-Coordinator_R142298": {
      "title": "Partnership Development Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: Northeastern University, Education Innovation team is looking for a Partnership Development Coordinator to work alongside Experiential Partnership Managers in the creation, retention, and management of new and existing industry relationships and the scoping of a variety of project-based experiential learning formats for Northeastern University students. Key Accountabilities and Responsibilities: Establish, develop, nurture, and sustain/retain relationships with companies and organizations which facilitate sponsorship of project-based experiential opportunities embedded in various ways across the university. Develop and implement strategies for recruiting and retaining industry partners (sponsors), identify and strategize company recruiting priorities based on the evolving needs of academic programs and students, and identify and prioritize strategic activities around company engagement to increase the number and variety of experiential project opportunities for undergraduate, graduate and professional studies students. Projects recruited will be handed off to team members who consult with faculty and staff and align projects with academic courses or other academic houses for engagement across the university. All team members contribute to evolving program operations. Effectively document, triage, and evolve database management systems (Salesforce, etc.) and processes to optimize relationship management for university partners Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree required along with 3-4 years of professional work experience in employer/company relationships, managing, recruiting or equivalent. Knowledge of Microsoft Suite (including Teams), Salesforce, database programs and data management and analysis highly preferred. Experience working with diverse constituents in higher education (i.e. various employers, disciplines, students, faculty and administration) preferred. Work effectively with people of varying cultures and diversity. Excellent skills in relationship building, marketing, public relations, and data base management highly preferred. This position requires an individual who is goal oriented, possesses excellent presentation skills, clearly communicates through writing, is effective in building partnerships, extremely organized, professional and has a high level of customer service. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $27-$30"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor---College-of-Engineering---Civil-and-Environmental-Engineering---Intelligent-Infrastructure---Boston_R142709": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - College of Engineering - Civil and Environmental Engineering - Intelligent Infrastructure - Boston",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Arlington; Boston; Charlotte, North Carolina; London; Miami; New York City; Oakland; Portland, Maine; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. About CEE and COE at Northeastern University:  The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at Northeastern University has a faculty of more than 40 members (several with joint appointments with other colleges) pursuing use-inspired research in structures, urban coastal systems, urban transportation and mobility, construction management, air quality, wind energy, water quality, environmental health, resilience, sustainability and climate change, life cycle assessment, green infrastructure, urban informatics, and robotics. CEE sits within Northeastern's  College of Engineering (COE) , a leader in experiential education and interdisciplinary research focused on innovating for global impact, anchored by Northeastern's signature cooperative education program and R1 research rating. The college enrolls more than 10,000 students pursuing BS, MS, and PhD degrees and includes more than 450 faculty. COE leads four university-wide research institutes while partnering in ten more and brings together researchers from a global network of campuses and partners. About the Opportunity:   As part of a strategic initiative, coordinated across the university under the broad theme of Physical AI, Robotics & Autonomous Systems , the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering Department at Northeastern University seeks faculty candidates for tenured or tenure-track appointments at the assistant, associate, or full professor ranks. The university-wide Physical AI, Robotics & Autonomous Systems theme brings together researchers working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, robotics, and the physical world to build systems that sense, reason, and act in real-world environments. The theme spans intelligent infrastructure and adaptive construction and manufacturing; embodied and wearable AI and human-robot collaboration; automated laboratories for chemistry, materials, and biomanufacturing; AI-enabled telepresence; and physical AI applications in health. Within this theme, the position focuses on Intelligent infrastructure, construction and manufacturing; adaptive robotics & digital twins. The position draws on cross-college collaboration among the College of Engineering, Khoury College of Computer Sciences, the College of Arts, Media and Design, and the College of Science. It is further strengthened by Northeastern's recently launched Physical AI Research (PAIR) Initiative, by the Institute for Experiential Robotics (IER), and by the Institute for Experiential AI (EAI). Appointments will be primarily or fully in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) within Northeastern University’s College of Engineering, with opportunities for joint cross-college appointments with the College of Arts, Media and Design, the Khoury College of Computer Sciences, or the College of Science. The position will be based primarily on Northeastern University’s Boston campus.   Candidates with interest in joint appointments with other departments or colleges should indicate their intent in their application materials in the cover letter. For this position we seek candidates who will leverage advances in AI, automation, digital twins, and sensing to transform construction and construction management; innovate in infrastructure systems adaptation, operations, control, and inspection; and advance human-robot collaboration. The term infrastructure systems refers to all aspects of the built environment (such as dams and reservoirs, buildings and bridges, transportation systems and water distribution networks, energy systems). Candidates working broadly in these fields are encouraged to apply, but example areas of interest include: Construction automation and construction management: AI-enabled construction management for planning, resource allocation, and on-site decision support; infrastructure robotic fabrication and assembly, automated inspection, and site-scale sensing and control; autonomy in complex and dynamic construction environments under uncertainty; AI-enabled digital twins for construction; human-robot interaction for construction and infrastructure focusing on safe and adaptive teaming between people and autonomous systems; human-in-the-loop control; multimodal and wearable sensing; transforming the design and construction methods that are required to effectively reuse structures and/or materials. Physical AI for infrastructure materials: Discovery and/or characterization of novel materials for construction and infrastructure maintenance; repurposing of materials to improve the sustainability and resilience of infrastructure systems; transforming the design and construction methods that are required to effectively use new materials. Autonomous mobility: Autonomous vehicle control and trajectory planning; mixed traffic with fleets of autonomous vehicles; AI-enabled planning, operations, and control of urban mobility systems for sustainability and resilience; transportation of people and goods through connected vehicles, intelligent infrastructure, and new types of services. Our recruitment initiatives at Northeastern are dedicated to fostering education and research across disciplinary boundaries. We aim to create a transformative signature effort at the interface of infrastructure systems, AI, automation, and robotics. Candidates are expected to demonstrate the potential to sustain a research program with a strong emphasis on interdisciplinary and translational research and teach both undergraduate and graduate classes. They should also exhibit active and recognized leadership in their respective fields. The ideal candidate will be able to leverage Northeastern’s expertise and facilities in high-performance computing, machine learning and artificial intelligence, human-system interaction, network science, sensing, large-scale structural testing, materials manufacturing, materials characterization, as well as opportunities enabled by Northeastern’s global network while bringing new strengths in one or more of the above areas. We encourage applicants from a wide range of backgrounds, including academia and industry. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Belonging, the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to further develop and support a thriving faculty. Responsibilities:   Tenure-track faculty in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering are responsible for: teaching courses at the graduate and undergraduate level; developing vigorous research programs that attract external funding; advising, mentoring, and overseeing students and post-doctoral associates in their area of research; and participating in service to the department, university, and discipline. Candidates are encouraged to collaborate with other departments and colleges across the university on interdisciplinary research. Candidates should be committed to promoting experiential learning, which is central to a Northeastern University education and be prepared to work with global student populations. Qualifications:   A Ph.D., or foreign equivalent, in Civil Engineering, Environmental Engineering, Computer Science, or a closely related field by the employment start date. Senior-level candidates at the Associate and Full Professor levels should have a demonstrated record of developing transformative solutions to global challenges, sustaining a research program with a strong emphasis on interdisciplinary and translational research, teaching both undergraduate and graduate classes, and being an active, recognized leader nationally and internationally in their discipline. Applicants at the Associate level should have at least 4 years of academic or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research) and at the Full Professor level at least 8 years or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research). How to apply: Interested candidates should apply using the Northeastern University website. In the cover letter applicants should indicate their theme of interest as Intelligent infrastructure, construction and manufacturing; adaptive robotics & digital twins . Applicants may also identify a department of interest for a joint secondary appointment within the College of Engineering or other colleges. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. The review is expected to start immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must include the following: Cover letter Full curriculum vita Statement of Research Interests, Objectives, & Vision Statement of Teaching Interests & Philosophy A representative journal paper Statement of Leadership Names and contact information for at least three professional references Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts: Statement of Research Interests, Objectives, Vision, and Service: Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, any funding you have received to support the work, the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students and/or the external community. Please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing as a faculty member, including any cross-disciplinary and collaborative aspects. Finally, discuss your involvement with the scientific community in your area and your service to the profession and your future plans of engaging with the profession. Statement of Teaching Interests & Philosophy: Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a diverse cohort of undergraduate and graduate students. Statement of Leadership (1 page): Please describe your past experiences and activities and/or your future plans detailing your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice. Northeastern University is an equal opportunity employer, seeking to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. Northeastern values and celebrates diversity in all its forms and strives to foster an inclusive culture built on respect that affirms inter-group relations and builds cohesion. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.   Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $106,000.00 - $121,000.00 | Associate Professor: $123,000.00 - $141,000.00 | Professor: $138,000.00 - $207,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director--Fraternity---Sorority-Life_R142871": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Fraternity & Sorority Life",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Reporting to the Associate Director of Fraternity and Sorority Life within the Center for Student Involvement (CSI), the Assistant Director of Fraternity and Sorority Life builds the infrastructure needed to support a healthy, safe, and meaningful fraternity and sorority experience on Northeastern University’s Boston campus. The position is one of three full-time staff members supporting more than 40 social Greek-letter organizations and over 3,750 affiliated students, representing approximately 16% of the undergraduate student population. The Assistant Director supports students and chapters within four governing councils: the Interfraternity Council (IFC), Multicultural Greek Council (MGC), National Pan-Hellenic Council (NPHC), and Panhellenic Council. The Assistant Director provides leadership for student safety, well-being, harm prevention, and leadership development initiatives within the Fraternity and Sorority Life (FSL) community. The role oversees off-campus event notifications, reviews new member education and membership intake processes, develops educational programming, manages the FSL communications and marketing strategy, and leads CSI’s student organization accountability committee in collaboration with the Office of Student Conduct and Conflict Resolution (OSCCR). At the divisional and institutional levels, the Assistant Director coordinates hazing-prevention efforts, including policy development, investigative protocols, and tailored education for members of the Northeastern community. The Assistant Director supervises, trains, and evaluates the FSL Specialist, who is responsible for chapter services, volunteer engagement, recognition requirements, and signature community-building events such as Take It to the Floor, Greek Sing, the MGC BBQ, New England Stepfest, FSL Apex Awards, and Greek Week. As a member of the collaborative CSI team, the Assistant Director also supports department-wide programs and events, including Fall Fest, Homecoming, Winter Fest, and the Student Life Awards. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities FSL Community Advisement Review chapter new member education and membership intake plans, providing guidance to support safe and healthy membership experiences Advise governing councils and individual chapters on short- and long-term goals that advance organizational wellness, success, and sustainability Provide event-planning and risk-management guidance by reviewing logistics for on- and off-campus events, including events with or without alcohol, overnight events, and events outside Boston Guide chapters in complying with university policies and the requirements of their membership organizations Serve as a liaison to membership organizations and their support networks, including headquarters staff and local, state, and regional volunteers and chapter advisors FSL Team Management Supervise, train, and evaluate the Fraternity and Sorority Life Specialist Supervise, train, and evaluate practicum students, graduate interns, and designated CSI student employees, as applicable FSL Educational and Wellness Strategy Evaluate, revise, and deliver educational programs and experiences for affiliated students, including FSL Discovery for new members, RISE for emerging leaders, Scout event monitor training, Equip for chapter officers, the Basecamp council officer transition workshop, and the Summit off-campus overnight leadership retreat Partner with the Office of Prevention and Education at Northeastern (OPEN) to lead FSL organizational wellness and prevention initiatives, including the chapter goal-setting process Collaborate with campus partners to identify and deliver educational content and resources tailored to the needs of fraternities and sororities Organizational Accountability Strategy and Support Lead CSI’s student organization accountability committee and administer the warning and letter-only process to reinforce compliance with university policies, protocols, and expectations across more than 500 student organizations Lead the institution’s approach to hazing prevention, including policy revisions, investigative interview logistics for organizational cases, and tailored education for members of the Northeastern community Address off-campus behavior associated with fraternities and sororities in collaboration with key stakeholders Collaborate with OSCCR and membership organization staff and volunteers to provide guidance on timely and relevant sanctions and outcomes related to organizational misconduct Communications, Programs, and Department Support Manage the end-to-end production of the biweekly FSL newsletter and oversee the FSL social media and website presence, ensuring digital content aligns with the FSL marketing strategy Support programs, events, initiatives, services, and committees originating within CSI Represent CSI on divisional committees and projects and at major campus events and programs Perform other duties as assigned that support team success Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor’s degree One to three years of full-time experience in fraternity and sorority life advising, health promotion or wellness, public health or prevention, student conduct, student activities, major event management, or a related area Awareness of trends and issues affecting fraternities and sororities at the inter/national level Ability to identify and apply industry-wide best practices in fraternity and sorority advising to strengthen the FSL community Desire and ability to work in a fast-paced, global university environment Excellent problem-solving skills Exceptional interpersonal, organizational, written, and verbal communication skills Ability to align work with the established mission, vision, and literacies of the Division of Student Life Preferred Master’s degree Direct experience advising fraternal organizations Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Operations-Manager---Department-of-Public-Safety_R137807": {
      "title": "Operations Manager - Department of Public Safety",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity This job description is intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to this classification. It is not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties and skills required of personnel so classified. JOB SUMMARY Reporting to the Associate Director of the NU Oakland Department of Public Safety (DPS), the Operations Manager is responsible for the oversight of general DPS operations as well as other duties and projects as assigned This position will be based out of the Oakland Campus and is primarily a second/third shift position (Evening/Overnight hours). MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Education, & Training High School Diploma or GED in addition to at least 3 years of proven leadership of a police, security, or public safety function Proven successful leadership of multiple personnel on a campus or multi-use site that is comparable to the Northeastern University Oakland Campus Flexibility to work all shifts, weekends, and extra hours as needed Proficiency with oral and written communication, power point presentations, Microsoft Word, Outlook, and video conferencing platforms. Previous training experience within the police, public safety, or security experience, such as defensive tactics, report writing, active threat, emergency medical response, and other related topics. Valid U.S. Driver’s License Must have CPR and First Aid certification within 6 months of hire  Will be required to pass a comprehensive background check, drug screening, and psychological Read, write, and speak English fluently Proven strong attendance and punctuality record Efficient time management techniques & strong organizational skills Strong interpersonal and communication skills Ability to multi-task and prioritize responsibilities Ability to express oneself clearly and concisely, orally and in writing. Strong computer skills, typing skills, and ability to learn new software Team and goal oriented Ability to exercise discretion in handling confidential information Knowledge of (Supervisory) Supervisory principles and practices Clery Act, Title IX, and the National Incident Management System (NIMS) Current public safety methods and procedures Current criminal codes and laws, and effective investigative techniques and procedures. Safety policies, practices, procedures and requirements of the department, including the Illness & Injury Prevention Plan. Skills/Abilities (Supervisory) React in an emergency and make sound, rational decisions, demonstrating good judgment Interpret, apply and explain rules, regulations, policies and procedures Plan, direct and supervise the work of others effectively Meet schedules and timelines Mediate conflict Work with diverse academic, cultural and ethnic backgrounds of college students, faculty and staff Work Environment & Physical Demands This position is an essential personnel position which may require working in a variety of situations including during holidays, weekends, day/evening/night hours, and in a state of emergency Must be able to: sit or stand for long periods of time; walk, run, climb, bend, stoop and lift weights proportionate with need to perform medical assistance; and operate a motor vehicle Prolonged periods of sitting, standing, walking Bending at the waist, kneeling or crouching Must be able to traverse all types of terrain including up and down hills, and on off road uneven surfaces   Environment: Indoor and outdoor environment. Seasonal heat and cold or adverse weather conditions May be exposed to uncomfortable work spaces such as darkness or poor lighting, dirt/dust, fumes/odors, isolation, mechanical or traffic hazards. May be exposed to unpleasant social situations or hostile community response Must remain calm and be able to deescalate volatile situations KEY RESPONSBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES The primary responsibility will be to lead the day-to-day shift operations of the Oakland Department of Public Safety (DPS), to include officers, sergeants, administrative, communications, and contracted personnel. This position will ensure adequate shift coverage and scheduling, supervise and evaluate the performance of assigned staff, & ensure all public safety activities are appropriately and timely documented. The Operations Manager will monitor and ensure staff compliance with departmental policies, procedures, and applicable regulatory requirements.   The Manager position is expected to understand and perform the same officer responsibilities as needed, to include: patrolling campus, investigating offenses, submitting reports, liaising with local law enforcement, responding to incidents to provide guidance/leadership as needed, responding to community complaints, and conducting shift briefings as needed.  The manager position will also monitor the off-campus shuttle schedule and liaise with the shuttle contractor for quality control, complaints, schedule changes, and incidents.     This position is expected to promote Public Safety programs within the campus community; develop and maintain internal and external relationships with campus stakeholders, including various departments, student organizations, community groups, and local businesses. The manager will also assist in the development of the department budget and evaluate and recommend new equipment, supplies or special materials to improve security enforcement.     The Operations Manager is responsible for assisting with (1) assessing all campus events to determine appropriate security protocols (2) recommending and implementing appropriate protocols and (3) on-site security supervision of special events as needed. This position will be responsible for assisting with the development and implementation of a strategic event security vision & plan for the NU Oakland Campus. Communication Expectations: Availability via phone during off-hours for incident notifications and guidance- individual may be expected to respond to campus in the event of a critical incident or emergency.   Work Environment & Physical Demands This position is an essential personnel position which may require working in a variety of situations including during holidays, weekends, day/evening/night hours, and in a state of emergency Must be able to: sit or stand for long periods of time; walk, run, climb, bend, stoop and lift weights proportionate with need to perform medical assistance; and operate a motor vehicle Prolonged periods of sitting, standing, walking Bending at the waist, kneeling or crouching Must be able to traverse all types of terrain including up and down hills, and on off road uneven surfaces Environment: Indoor and outdoor environment. Seasonal heat and cold or adverse weather conditions May be exposed to uncomfortable work spaces such as darkness or poor lighting, dirt/dust, fumes/odors, isolation, mechanical or traffic hazards. May be exposed to unpleasant social situations or hostile community response Must remain calm and be able to deescalate volatile situations Position Type Safety and Security Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $83,970.00 - $118,608.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Program-Coordinator_R142874": {
      "title": "Program Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: Northeastern University's Galante Engineering Business Program offers a progressive opportunity for engineering students to complement their technical education with business skills by earning a graduate certificate in engineering business. The Program is founded on the values of student engagement and leadership to strengthen interpersonal and professional skills. The Program Coordinator provides elevated, strategic-level support for the Galante Engineering Business Program, working alongside the Program Director to oversee a community of up to 50 Galante Engineering Business Fellows. This role expands on core program coordination duties to include ownership of program marketing and communications, stewardship of stakeholder relationships, and oversight of Fellows' academic and co-curricular progress. The Program Coordinator serves as the primary program marketing owner, driving visibility and engagement through a full range of channels including tabling events, information sessions, town halls, social media, newsletters, and targeted outreach. They lead effective communication with a diverse set of stakeholders including Fellows, alumni, benefactors, university administration, faculty, and industry partners to foster a strong, enduring program community. Additionally, this role carries responsibility for monitoring and supporting Fellows' academic progression, ensuring individual course plans align with program requirements and milestones. The ideal candidate is a proactive, relationship-driven professional with strong organizational, analytical, and creative capabilities, who thrives at the intersection of academic program management, communications, and student success. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree required; Master's degree or equivalent experience preferred. Minimum 4 to 5 years of relevant experience in program coordination, communications, or a related field, preferably in higher education. Demonstrated proficiency in design and marketing tools, including website design, flyer and brochure creation, social media content creation, and engaging slide decks. Experience designing and managing advanced email marketing campaigns (e.g., MailChimp or equivalent platforms). Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite (Word, PowerPoint, Excel); ability to analyze data, generate charts, and create summary reports. Strong experience managing multi-channel marketing and communications strategies, including social media, events, and digital outreach. Proven ability to communicate professionally and effectively with diverse stakeholders, including students, alumni, donors and benefactors, faculty, and university administration. Familiarity with academic advising processes, degree audit systems, or course planning tools is strongly preferred. Superior written, verbal, and analytical communication skills. Excellent organizational, planning, and interpersonal skills with the ability to manage competing priorities. General familiarity with budgeting and purchasing systems. Some technical background or familiarity with basic engineering concepts is helpful. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES: Program Marketing Ownership & Student Engagement: Serve as the primary owner of the Galante Program's marketing strategy and execution, developing and implementing a comprehensive, multi-channel plan in collaboration with the Program Director and COE marketing. Drive program visibility and student recruitment through tabling events, information sessions, town halls, open houses, and campus-wide outreach, acting as the face of the program at these touchpoints. Manage and grow the program's social media presence, newsletters, and digital content calendar to consistently engage prospective and current Fellows, alumni, and university stakeholders. Build and maintain partnerships with COE student chapters, DMSB student organizations, and other campus entities to amplify program reach and engagement. Design promotional materials including flyers, signage, social graphics, brochures, and presentation decks that reflect the program's brand and values. Event Coordination and Support: Collaborate with ARC budget personnel to manage accounts, budgets, expenses, invoices, and payment processes for all program events. Act as the primary point of contact and project leader during all Galante Engineering Business events, overseeing catering, setup, hosting, facilitation, and technical and logistical support. Coordinate with the Program Director to schedule, promote, execute, and finalize records for all events, including speaker coordination, space reservations, and materials production. Supervise event scheduling alongside internal meetings, presentations, and outreach events throughout the academic year Stakeholder Communications & Relationship Stewardship: Lead effective, professional communications with all key program stakeholders including Galante Fellows, program alumni, industry benefactors, university administrators, faculty, and INC Board members to foster engagement, trust, and a strong program community. Serve as the author and curator of the weekly Galante newsletter, tailoring content to inform, engage, and connect the broader program community. Support the Program Director in cultivating relationships with donors and benefactors, including preparing correspondence, event invitations, thank-you communications, and relevant program updates. Coordinate alumni engagement initiatives, including tracking career trajectories post graduation and facilitating opportunities for alumni to contribute to the program. Academic Curriculum Oversight & Fellows' Records Management: Monitor and support Fellows' academic progress, ensuring individual course plans align with program requirements, certificate milestones, and graduation timelines. Liaise with academic advisors and graduate program coordinators to resolve academic status issues, confirm enrollment in required courses, and ensure Fellows remain on track across both their engineering and Galante program pathways. Manage and maintain comprehensive applicant and Fellows' records internally within the Program and externally across the College of Engineering (undergraduate and graduate), including tracking academic milestones such as Commencement participation. Proactively identify Fellows at risk of falling off track and coordinate appropriate interventions with the Program Director and relevant advising staff. Administrative Support for the Program Director: Provide general administrative support to the Program Director, including scheduling, correspondence preparation, reporting, and ad hoc program needs. DOCUMENTS REQUIRED: Resume Cover Lette r Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Department-of-History_R142594-1": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Department of History",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.   Responsibilities: Teach European, World, or American history survey courses. Teach upper level undergraduate or graduate courses. Start dates in September, January, May and July.   Positions are available contingent upon enrollment, funding and departmental needs.   Qualifications: Ph.D.in  History required   Documents to Submit: CV, Cover Letter, and evidence of teaching required. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,085."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer-in-Spanish-and-Brazilian-Portuguese---Department-of-World-Languages-and-Cultures_R142612-1": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Spanish and Brazilian Portuguese - Department of World Languages and Cultures",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe. Responsibilities: The Department of World Languages and Cultures regularly seeks instructors to teach elementary, intermediate, and advanced courses in Spanish and in Brazilian Portuguese, as well as culture-focused classes taught in English. Appointments are on a per-term basis.  Positions are available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and departmental need. Qualifications: Master’s degree and previous teaching experience required, Ph.D. preferred. Documents to Submit: CV and Cover Letter Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate for Customized Language Instruction (CLI) is $1,185 per student for a standard 4-credit course. A one credit course at the CLI rate is prorated to $296.26 per student."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Adjunct-Faculty---Department-of-Economics---Oakland--CA_R142630": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Department of Economics - Oakland, CA",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.     Responsibilities: The Department of Economics seeks applicants for part-time teaching in its undergraduate program on the Northeastern University Oakland campus. Applicants qualified to teach economics classes in any field are encouraged to apply to be considered for openings that arise. Adjunct Faculty are responsible for the preparation and administration of assigned classes, including administering exams, holding appropriate office hours, and grading. Classes are taught on the Oakland campus.   Positions available contingent upon enrollment, funding, and programmatic need. Qualifications: Ph.D. degree in Economics preferred; Master’s degree required for undergraduate teaching. College-level teaching experience preferred. Documents to Submit:   CV and Cover Letter required. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,484.48."
