    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director-of-Faculty-Led-Programs_R142850": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Faculty-Led Programs",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Assistant Director of Faculty-Led Programs maintains expertise in faculty-led program design and implementation, resource stewardship, internal database systems, and promotion of faculty-led study abroad. The AD is responsible for the development and implementation of an assigned portfolio of programs and provides a full range of advising services to students. The Assistant Director must be able to initiate, develop, and sustain positive relationships with faculty, staff, students, and external stakeholders. Program development duties include developing and maintaining all aspects of faculty-led programs including budgeting, negotiating with third-party vendors, and assessing overall program quality. Student advising duties include identifying academically appropriate programs, knowledge of financial and withdrawal policies, building cross-cultural awareness, and health and safety support. This position may be required to travel to conduct periodic site visits and to attend, facilitate, and/or present at meetings or conferences domestically and internationally. Some evening and weekend hours to lead events will be required to accommodate Northeastern students and faculty. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities 1.) Faculty-Led Program Development: Provide end-to-end support for an assigned portfolio of faculty in the proposal, design, and implementation stages; Communicate professionally and effectively with internal stakeholders, external partners, and with cross-functional teams to arrive at mutually agreeable and productive solutions; Assist faculty in creating effective marketing and recruitment strategies; Maintain accountability for budgeting with the Finance & Operations team. 2.) Student Advising: Maintain regional expertise, cultural proficiency, and programmatic knowledge in an assigned portfolio of faculty-led global experiences; Handle a high volume of student communication and be able to provide direction and answers, as necessary; Maintain general knowledge of financial policies and processes to educate and advise students on how to finance their global experience; Advise students on student application process, monitor progress toward completing applications, changing in status, withdrawals, etc.; Conduct pre-departure orientations and re-entry programming. Connect outgoing students with current or past exchange students to create meaningful connections prior to and while abroad. 3.) Administrative Duties: Assist in the management of the Terra Dotta application system, troubleshooting student and faculty issues as they arise; Provide on-campus customer service representation on a rotating basis; Serve in the on-call emergency response rotation. Minimum Qualifications Knowledge in advising techniques for study abroad programs in a higher education setting. Understanding of the Forum on Education Abroad’s standards and ethics. Effective oral and written communication to positively engage people from diverse cultural and linguistic backgrounds. Effective management and prioritization skills. Effective organizational, project management, administrative, and computer skills.   Excellent problem-solving and strong analytical skills with proven ability to organize, prioritize, and multi-task to meet goals and deadlines.  Ability to manage multiple projects and responsibilities in a fast-paced environment. Ability to interact with global program representatives, faculty, and students in a highly professional manner with an understanding of protocol and confidentiality. Ability to effectively represent the University to outside institutions and universities.  Knowledge and skills required for this position are typically acquired through completion of a bachelor's degree required (Master’s degree preferred) with previous experience studying or living abroad. Proficiency in Excel and other Microsoft Office software required. History of creativity in finding solutions to programmatic challenges while proposing initiatives to move GEO toward its goals and objective. Familiarity with Salesforce, Terra Dotta, and student record systems (e.g., Banner) preferred.   Ability and willingness to work some evening and/or weekend events. Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Burlington-MA/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142815": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Postdoctoral Associate will conduct integrated experimental and computational research on informatics-driven mechanical characterization of solid and porous materials, supported by an NSF CMMI CDS&E award and industry-sponsored projects. The primary function of the position is to develop and cross-validate the experimental and numerical platforms of the mechanics informatics framework. On the experimental side, this involves designing and fabricating heterogeneous specimens, conducting multiaxial mechanical tests on a dual-actuator loading frame, and acquiring full-field deformation data using three-dimensional digital image correlation (DIC). On the numerical side, this involves building automated finite element and inverse-identification workflows that learn constitutive parameters from a single informative test through Bayesian optimization, together with quantification of measurement and model-form uncertainty. Approximately half of the effort will extend this framework to the constitutive modeling of porous and cellular materials, such as polymer and metal foams, and to industry-sponsored projects on the mechanical testing and qualification of battery components. The role combines hands-on experimental mechanics with rigorous simulation and data analysis and benefits from complementary expertise in scientific machine learning and constitutive modeling of anisotropic and compressible materials. Minimum Qualifications Required PhD in mechanical engineering, aerospace engineering, materials science and engineering, civil engineering, or a closely related field Zero to three years of relevant research experience in experimental and/or computational solid mechanics Knowledge of solid mechanics and continuum constitutive modeling, including anisotropic elasticity, plasticity, and the mechanics of porous or cellular materials Ability to conduct mechanical testing on electromechanical or servo-hydraulic load frames and apply full-field optical measurement techniques such as digital image correlation Ability to design and execute experiments on heterogeneous specimen geometries, including specimen fabrication, instrumentation, and test documentation Knowledge of nonlinear finite element analysis and experience with commercial or open-source finite element codes, such as Abaqus, including user-defined material subroutines Ability to program in Python and develop automated simulation, data-processing, and inverse-identification workflows Familiarity with optimization, Bayesian inference, uncertainty quantification, or machine learning methods applied to engineering problems Ability to analyze and interpret experimental and simulation data, prepare peer-reviewed publications, and present results to academic and industrial audiences Preferred Experience with the mechanical characterization of battery components, foams, or other porous and layered material systems Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Develop and operate the experimental platform for informatics-driven characterization by designing and fabricating heterogeneous specimens, conducting multiaxial mechanical tests on the dual-actuator loading frame, and acquiring and processing full-field deformation data using three-dimensional digital image correlation Develop the numerical platform by building automated finite element and inverse-identification workflows that learn constitutive parameters from global and full-field test data and performing systematic cross-validation between measured and simulated responses, including uncertainty quantification Extend the modeling framework to porous and cellular materials, such as polymer and metal foams, including compressible constitutive formulations, calibration against experimental data, and assessment of the framework’s transferability across material classes Support industry-sponsored projects on the mechanical testing and characterization of battery components and related material systems, including test planning, execution, data analysis, and preparation of technical deliverables for sponsors Document and disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, project reports, and conference presentations; mentor graduate and undergraduate students; and contribute to proposal preparation and collaborative research efforts Required Documents Resume Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Events-Coordinator_R142851": {
      "title": "Events Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Position Summary The Events Coordinator supports the planning, coordination, and execution of the university’s global signature events and other high-profile institutional programs, including Commencement, Convocation, the Global Leadership Summit, and related special events. This role works closely with internal stakeholders, external vendors, and event partners to ensure seamless event delivery and exceptional participant experiences. The position supports all phases of event planning and execution, from concept development through on-site management and post-event evaluation. The Events Coordinator reports to the Director, Event Operations and Experiential Design Producer and is a member of the Interactive Experience team under the leadership of the Executive Creative Director, Interactive Experience. Key Responsibilities ·       Assist in the planning and management of global signature events and institutional programs from concept through execution. ·       Coordinate event logistics, including venue preparation, registration support, transportation, catering, audiovisual services, and production requirements. ·       Serve as a liaison between university departments, vendors, performers, sponsors, and external partners. ·       Develop and maintain event timelines, production schedules, project plans, and operational documents. ·       Manage event materials, signage, supplies, and equipment necessary for successful event delivery. ·       Assist with vendor communications, contract administration, purchasing processes, and invoice tracking. ·       Provide hands-on event support, including setup, execution, troubleshooting, and post-event breakdown. ·       Maintain accurate event documentation, reports, databases, and project records. ·       Support event evaluation efforts and recommend opportunities for continuous improvement. Qualifications & Requirements ·       Strong organizational and project management skills with exceptional attention to detail. ·       Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. ·       Demonstrated proficiency in Microsoft Excel is required. The successful candidate must be able to independently create and manage complex spreadsheets, track event budgets and expenses, maintain registration and attendance data, develop production schedules, and generate reports using formulas, filters, and pivot tables. ·       Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, including Outlook, Word, PowerPoint, Teams, and SharePoint. ·       Experience with event management software and customer relationship management (CRM) platforms, including Cvent, Salesforce, or comparable systems, is preferred. Demonstrated ability to quickly learn and effectively utilize new technology platforms is highly desirable. ·       Ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment while maintaining a collaborative and solutions-oriented approach. ·       Experience coordinating events, conferences, hospitality programs, or related activities preferred. ·       Ability to lift up to 50 pounds. ·       Ability to stand and walk for extended periods during event setup and execution. ·       Availability to work evenings, nights, and weekends as needed to support event operations. ·       Other duties as assigned. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $30.22"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow--Department-of-Physical-Therapy--Movement--and-Rehabilitation-Sciences_R142860": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Department of Physical Therapy, Movement, and Rehabilitation Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will support a program of research at the intersection of physical activity, disability, rehabilitation, and health equity in the TRIBE Lab, Department of Physical Therapy, Movement, and Rehabilitation Sciences. The position contributes to developing and testing educational interventions for rehabilitation professionals and to evaluating community-based physical activity programs for people with disabilities. Working under the direction of the faculty principal investigator, the postdoc will coordinate studies, lead qualitative and mixed-methods analyses, contribute to manuscripts and grant applications, and mentor student researchers while developing toward an independent research career. Start date January 2027. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Knowledge of qualitative and mixed-method research design and analysis, research ethics and IRB procedures, and disability, rehabilitation, or physical activity research. Ability to manage multiple concurrent studies, work collaboratively with community partners, clinicians, and students, and write for peer-reviewed publication. As normally acquired through a doctoral degree (PhD or equivalent) in kinesiology, rehabilitation science, public health, health professions education, or a related field, completed by the start date, and 0–3 years of postdoctoral research experience. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Coordinates day-to-day implementation of research studies, including IRB submissions, participant recruitment, data collection, and meetings with stakeholder and community partners. Develops and maintains study protocols, data management systems, and project timelines. Ensures studies are conducted ethically, on schedule, and in accordance with approved protocols. Co-leads qualitative and mixed-methods data analysis, including codebook development, coder training and reconciliation, and thematic analysis. Designs evaluation frameworks, logic models, and outcome measures for community-based physical activity programs for people with disabilities. Manages and analyzes survey and feasibility data and prepares reports for research and community partners. Leads and co-authors peer-reviewed manuscripts, conference abstracts, and presentations. Translates research findings into accessible materials for community partners, clinicians, and participants. Contributes to the lab’s visibility in disability and rehabilitation research. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Foundation-Year--General-Chemistry--Boston-_R142861": {
      "title": "Part Time Lecturer - Foundation Year, General Chemistry (Boston)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a non-tenure track part time faculty lecturer in Foundation Year on the Boston campus. Foundation Year is a first-year college program for first generation college students from the City of Boston. Students in Foundation Year take college credited classes with dedicated faculty who deliver a rigorous college curriculum while infusing college success skills into the learning space. The program is currently seeking an enthusiastic and student-centered part-time instructor to teach one section of an introductory Chemistry lecture course. The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Instructors in the Foundation Year program are responsible for teaching foundational courses to a diverse student body including science majors, non-science majors, and health science majors. By breaking complex concepts into engaging digestible lessons, faculty are responsible for helping student build content knowledge and analytical skills needed to success in higher-level STEM coursework. The key areas of responsibility include: Instructional Delivery Student Assessment Academic Support Collaboration Course Management Qualifications Advanced degree in Chemistry, Biochemistry or closely related field. Demonstrated familiarity with undergraduate-level teaching Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with a proven ability to explain complex scientific methodologies to beginners Experience navigating digital teaching tools and Learning Management Systems like Canvas. A documented track record of utilizing active learning strategies to engage diverse or traditionally underrepresented student groups in STEM. Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter. Please direct questions to Amy Lantinga at a.lantinga@northeastern.edu. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $1,962.00."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Toronto-ON-Canada/Part-Time-Lecturer---Applied-Logistics--Toronto-_R142854": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Applied Logistics (Toronto)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a part-time faculty position to teach on-campus graduate courses in Applied Logistics at the Toronto, Ontario campus. The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.  Applicant must be legally entitled to work in Canada for the duration of the appointment without employer sponsorship. Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Teach in-person graduate courses within the Master of Professional Studies in Applied Logistics program. Courses include, but are not limited to: Foundations of Applied Logistics, Capacity Planning and Demand Management, Warehouse and Inventory Management Systems, Freight Management, Supplier and Order Management, Network Design and Emerging Technolgies in Logistics, International Logistics and Sustainable Practices, and the Applied Logistics Capstone. Qualifications PhD in Logistics, Supply Chain Management, Operations Management, Industrial Engineering, or a closely related field. 10+ years of industry experience at the logistics and supply chain management level. A history of successful teaching at the graduate level is strongly preferred. Experience teaching in an in-person classroom setting is required. Experience teaching online is a plus. Experience working with international students is desirable. Legal authorization to work in Canada is required. This is a non-tenure track renewable position. Positions are available contingent upon enrollment, funding, and departmental needs. Stipend rates per course. Position is not benefits eligible. Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter. Please direct questions to Ammar Aamer at a.aamer@northeastern.edu. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,797.00."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Campus-Planner_R142842": {
      "title": "Assistant Campus Planner",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: Northeastern University Planning, Real Estate & Facilities is seeking an Assistant Campus Planner to join a team of talented planners to join the Campus Planning & Design (CP&D) department. The role will support the planning group in drafting innovative solutions to space challenges and capital projects to position Northeastern for continued growth and success as a top-tier R1 research institution and global university. The mission of Campus Planning and Design (CP&D) is to connect people, space, and resources to prepare the university to thrive in the future. This team of planners are responsible for the development of physical campus growth strategies for the growing global network of campuses. Under direction from the Associate Vice President, the Assistant Campus Planner will report directly to the Director of Research & Academic Campus Planning while providing broader support to the CP&D team. The role critically supports CP&D’s responsibility for leading the broad range of strategic planning initiatives, space planning studies, and capital project scenario development, that align and maximize the utilization of space and capital investment with Northeastern’s mission.  The Assistant Campus Planner will support the team in developing communication materials that distill complex ideas into concise options and tradeoffs.  Additionally, they will help collect and interpret data, conduct planning research and analysis, and collaborate on active planning initiatives led by the CP&D team. RESPONSIBILITIES: ·   Create strong visual communication materials including maps, presentations, and annotated floor plans to support departmental initiatives and project planning ·   Conduct data gathering/coordination and research support, including precedents ·   Create tools and generate materials to support and communicate multi-step space planning scenarios ·   Accurately survey and document field conditions for specific projects and planning initiatives ·   Conduct research on urban and campus planning initiatives including, but not limited to, mobility, housing, accessibility, best practices, as well as University space and capital project requests ·   Generally, support campus planning staff on specific projects and planning initiatives as needed (note taking, project and meeting coordination, etc.) ·   Prepare clear, well-organized written materials such as reports, meeting notes, memos, and project summaries to effectively communicate findings, action items, and recommendations ·   Leverage AI tools and other emerging technologies to enhance research, data analysis, and workflow efficiency across planning initiatives   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: ·   Bachelor’s degree in Architecture, Urban Planning, Landscape Architecture or a related field ·   At least two years of experience preferred, or an equivalent combination of education and experience ·   Strong graphic illustration skills (Adobe Illustrator, InDesign) and experience distilling complex ideas into simple diagrams ·   Strong Microsoft Office Suite skills (PowerPoint, Excel) ·   Strong ability to communicate in written, verbal, and graphic form ·   Ability to switch between competing tasks and assignments ·   Experience working in a fast-paced environment and managing projects with internal and external stakeholders. ·   Ability, willingness, and comfort working with people from various backgrounds, cultures, nationalities, linguistic styles, and socio-economic status.   Position Type Facilities Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Fall-River-MA/Part-Time-Lecturer--School-of-Nursing--Fall-River--MA_R142862": {
      "title": "Part Time Lecturer, School of Nursing, Fall River, MA",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description The School of Nursing, Fall River campus, is seeking part-time faculty to teach theoretical courses including didactic and/or skills laboratory courses in the following areas: Adult Medical/Surgical, Public Community Health, Maternal Child/Women's Heath, Pediatrics, and Psychiatric/Mental Health. Part-time instructor positions are available as determined by the department’s curricular and institutional needs. Responsibilities include preparation of lectures, course materials, examinations, and evaluation of student performance in the course. Part- time faculty may work under the direction of the lead faculty for the course and will hold office hours as appropriate. Requirements to teach a didactic course include: a Master’s Degree in Nursing, a minimum of two years’ relevant professional nursing experience within the last five years, competence appropriate to teaching responsibilities, a current Massachusetts RN license Previous teaching experience is highly desirable. Start dates: May, July, September, and January. Seeking applicants with significant career accomplishments within subject area and college teaching experience in a large higher education institution. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2085"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Product-Manager---Workday_R142848": {
      "title": "Product Manager - Workday",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Product Manager owns the Workday platform end-to-end as a single, coherent enterprise product spanning Finance, HCM, Payroll, and Procurement. This role sits at the intersection of the business, functional teams, and technical delivery — translating business strategy into a unified Workday roadmap and ensuring that decisions are made in one module. The Product Lead is the accountable owner for the health, roadmap, governance, and value realization of the Workday ecosystem.   24/7 business continuity: This role requires occasional availability outside of traditional working hours to address urgent business needs as they arise, including, but not limited to, responding to security incidents, supporting software deployments, resolving software issues or system breaks, and addressing other critical operational requirements. The ideal candidate must be proactive and adaptable, ensuring minimal disruption to business operations by promptly addressing any issues, regardless of time or day. Flexibility and a strong sense of urgency are essential for success in this position.   Other duties as required: This role requires flexibility in performing duties outside of the primary responsibilities to support evolving business needs. The ideal candidate must be adaptable and willing to take on additional tasks or projects as required, ensuring smooth operations across the organization. This may include stepping in to assist with cross-functional teams, handling unexpected challenges, or contributing to initiatives that support business growth and success. A proactive mindset and the ability to pivot quickly are essential for thriving in this dynamic environment.   Hybrid work schedule: This role is hybrid and in the office a minimum of three days a week to facilitate collaboration and teamwork. In-office presence is an essential part of our on-campus culture and allows for engaging directly with staff and students, sharing ideas, and contributing to a dynamic work environment. Being on-site allows for stronger connections, more effective problem-solving, and enhanced team synergy, all of which are key to achieving our collective goals and driving success.   Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The University is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future. Minimum Qualifications   Bachelor’s degree in business, Information Systems, HR, Finance, or related field (or equivalent experience). 5+ years of experience supporting or leading Workday (or comparable ERP/HCM platform) implementations or operations, with at least 3+ years spanning multiple modules (Finance, HCM, Payroll, and/or Procurement). Demonstrated experience owning a product roadmap or acting as a product/platform owner in an enterprise systems context. Strong understanding of Workday's data model, business process framework, security architecture, and integration methods (EIBs, Core Connectors, Workday Studio/Orchestrate, APIs). Experience coordinating cross-functional teams spanning business, functional, and technical disciplines. Track record managing Workday releases, testing cycles, and change management at an enterprise scale. Excellent stakeholder management and communication skills, with the ability to translate between business language and technical detail. Workday certifications in one or more pillars (Financial Management, HCM, Payroll, Procurement, Integrations, or Reporting). Experience with Workday Extend, Workday Orchestrate, or Adaptive Planning. Prior experience in a product management or platform owner role (e.g., Certified Scrum Product Owner, SAFe POPM). Experience operating in a multi-entity, multi-country Workday deployment. Familiarity with adjacent systems commonly integrated with Workday (procurement/supplier networks, tax engines, benefits carriers, treasury/banking systems, expense management).     Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Product & Roadmap Management Own the overall Workday product strategy and multi-year roadmap across Finance, HCM, Payroll, and Procurement modules. Define and prioritize the enterprise backlog, balancing competing demands from business stakeholders, compliance requirements, and technical debt. Maintain a single source of truth for the Workday product vision, ensuring module-level roadmaps (Finance, HCM, Payroll, Procurement) are integrated rather than siloed. Own the business case, ROI tracking, and value-realization metrics for the Workday investment Cross-Functional Coordination Serve as the primary liaison between business process owners (HR, Finance, Payroll, Procurement leadership), functional analysts/configuration teams, and technical/integration teams. Run cross-module governance forums (e.g., Change Advisory Board, Design Council) to review proposed configuration changes, tenant updates, and integrations for cross-functional impact. Coordinate release and update cycles (including Workday's biannual releases) across all modules, ensuring testing, communication, and training are synchronized. Partner with business stakeholders to gather, document, and prioritize requirements, and translate them into functional/technical specifications. Platform & Technical Stewardship Own the overall system architecture philosophy for Workday, including integration patterns, security/role-based access model, and data governance standards. Partner with technical teams (integrations, EIBs, Workday Studio/Orchestrate, reporting/BIRT, PECI, etc.) to ensure the technical roadmap supports business needs. Oversee tenant configuration standards, environment strategy (sandbox/preview/production), and change management processes to protect system integrity. Monitor system performance, data quality, and integration health across connected systems (e.g., banks, benefits providers, tax engines, procurement/supplier networks). Vendor & Ecosystem Management Serve as the primary business owner of the relationship with Workday (account team, support, and partner/AMS resources). Manage third-party implementation or managed services partners, ensuring delivery quality and contract accountability. Track Workday product updates, new features, and community/Brainstorm ideas, and assess their relevance to the organization's roadmap.   Governance, Risk & Compliance Ensure Workday configurations and integrations comply with SOX, payroll/tax regulations, labor law, and data privacy requirements across all supported geographies. Own change control and audit-readiness for the Workday tenant, including security role reviews and segregation-of-duties (SoD) controls. Partner with Internal Audit and Security teams on periodic control reviews and remediation. Team Leadership Provide functional leadership (direct or matrixed) to module leads/analysts across Finance, HCM, Payroll, and Procurement. Set performance goals, mentor team members, and build organizational Workday expertise/capability. Foster a product mindset across the team — outcomes and business value over ticket-closing. Position Type Information Technology Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 115S Expected Hiring Range: $157,130.00 - $227,840.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Portland-ME/Front-Desk-Assistant---Temporary_R142827": {
      "title": "Front Desk Assistant - Temporary",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description The Front Desk Assistant role centers on campus and community experience: Coordinates the entry area experience: Provides a welcoming, inclusive, and informative experience for visitors to campus - leads people through the check-in process and connects them to their host or necessary resources. Communicates effectively with other operations team members (verbally and through documentation) and takes a team-oriented approach to their work. Handles conflicts or the unexpected with diplomacy, tact, and common sense. Can handle confidential information with discretion. Addresses day-to-day troubleshooting questions brought up by campus users.  Works with IT/AV to ensure technical support for equipment, especially in regard to supporting evening classrooms. Coordinates with campus security guard for campus safety. Administrative and clerical tasks: Co-manages shared email account. Receives/sends mail and packages. Supports students or employees learning how to access campus or digital University resources, offers general directions of campus/class schedules. Occasionally, setting up meetings or booking rooms. Escalates specific questions to the appropriate team members.  Creates IDs cards and completes other repeating or ad hoc administrative tasks. Maintains campus amenities Maintains a high standard of aesthetic organization of campus fixtures, furniture, and equipment. Takes initiative to notice any faults and escalates to CET for addressing. Light resetting of spaces as needed for the next day's opening. Restocks communal spaces, like coffee machine/supplies in the kitchen or office supplies around campus.  Tends to basic cleaning and tidying of kitchen spaces. Occasionally assists in setting up, arranging, or breaking down meetings or events. Operations support In coordination with the Campus Operations Manager, facilitates operational projects by providing status updates and communicating with vendors. Receives campus feedback and fields questions and concerns to the Director of Operations in a detailed manner. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Demonstrate a customer-first, can-do, anything-is-possible attitude. The successful candidate is often the first face of the Roux Institute and therefore must demonstrate our values of a welcoming, inclusion, and dynamism to our community members. Demonstrate maturity, diplomacy, common sense, close attention to detail, professionalism and ability to prioritize and adapt in a fast-paced environment. Effectively troubleshoot and resolve office technology issues and has the ability to organize, manage, and conceptualize projects. Excellent writing and analytical skills as well as strong interpersonal and communications skills are a must. Excellent organizational and time-keeping skills. Excellent computer skills - effectively utilizing Microsoft Office applications such as Word, Excel and PowerPoint, and ease in learning new applications. This role is scheduled for the Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday evening shifts, from 5–10pm. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $22/hour"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Director-of-Technology-Ventures_R142609": {
      "title": "Director of Technology Ventures",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Summary: The Center for Research Innovation (CRI) at Northeastern University is seeking a dynamic and entrepreneurial Director of Technology Ventures to lead the development and acceleration of Northeastern's spinout portfolio. This role carries two closely linked mandates. First, the Director will directly oversee a portfolio of life sciences and physical sciences spinouts, working hands-on with founding teams to sharpen product-market fit, define go-to-market strategy, and move ventures from formation through launch. Second, the Director will design and run scalable programming, internal and external, that extends CRI's support across the full portfolio of roughly 50 spinouts at varying stages, including entrepreneurial training and education, pitch training, grant writing support, and structured connections to venture capital and other investors. The Director will act as a hands-on venture builder and a program architect, balancing individualized company support with repeatable infrastructure that allows CRI's impact to scale beyond what one-on-one engagement alone could achieve. This position reports to the Executive Director of CRI and works in close coordination with the Commercialization, Marketing, and Contracts teams to ensure a holistic, cross-functional approach to spinout success. The ideal candidate combines venture-building instincts (evaluating markets, stress-testing business models, and coaching founding teams) with the discipline to build programs, curricula, and investor networks that serve many companies at once. A deep understanding of the startup life cycle in science-based ventures, strong relationship-management skills across faculty, investors, and industry, and a genuine passion for developing the next generation of entrepreneurial researchers are essential. **Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The University is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future. This position follows a hybrid schedule, and is based out of our Main Campus in Boston, MA. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities 1. Venture Development & Acceleration (Portfolio Management) – 40% Lead venture development activities across a portfolio of life sciences and physical sciences spinouts, partnering with founding teams from early formation through launch and initial growth, and overseeing the execution of portfolio-level initiatives and priorities. Venture Formation and Launch: Lead the design and execution of a scalable, repeatable venture creation process spanning opportunity identification, venture assessment, founding team formation, company launch, and transition to independent operation. Partner closely with CRI's Commercialization team to align venture formation activities with intellectual property strategy, licensing negotiations, and commercialization objectives. Build and maintain a robust pipeline of future spinouts by working with faculty and the Commercialization team to identify high-potential research and invention disclosures, evaluate venture readiness, and advance promising opportunities through company formation and launch. Portfolio Oversight:  Directly oversee a portfolio of life sciences and physical sciences spinouts, managing company-level progress, milestones, and support needs while establishing strategic priorities, resource allocation frameworks, and operating processes for the broader venture portfolio. Lead and direct staff responsible for portfolio monitoring and execution to ensure consistent, data-driven oversight across companies. Build and maintain the pipeline of future spinouts by partnering with the Commercialization team and faculty to identify high-potential research and invention disclosures, assess venture readiness, and advance promising opportunities toward company formation. Product-market Fit: Work directly with faculty founders and early management teams to test and refine value propositions, target markets, and customer/end-user assumptions before and after formation.Go-to-market strategy: Guide spinouts in developing commercialization pathways and go-to-market plans, including regulatory strategy considerations for life sciences ventures and market entry planning for physical sciences ventures. Founder and Team Support: Provide direct coaching to academic founders and startup leadership on business model development, competitive positioning, and de-risking technical and commercial milestones. Talent Network: Set the strategic direction for how the Venture team builds out and maintains CRI's talent network of advisors, executives-in-residence, and potential management team members, drawing on relevant relationships to help seed it. 2. Scalable Programming – 35% Provide strategic leadership for the design, positioning, and continuous evolution of CRI's venture support ecosystem to maximize the quality, readiness, and long-term success of Northeastern spinouts. Develop scalable programs, resources, and investor engagement strategies that systematically strengthen venture formation, accelerate company growth, and extend high-impact support across the full portfolio. Design and operate programming that extends CRI's venture support across the full spinout portfolio through the following areas: Training and Education: Develop and deliver structured entrepreneurial training and education for faculty, researchers, and founding teams across the stages of the venture life cycle, both within Northeastern and, where relevant, for external audiences. Venture Resources: Develop and oversee a comprehensive venture support ecosystem that equips spinouts for successful formation, growth, and fundraising. Build, scale, and continuously enhance a portfolio of internal and external resources, programs, and service providers to address the evolving needs of ventures across stages of development. Manage and expand key support offerings, including I-Corps programming, grant writing assistance, pitch coaching, marketing and branding support, Entrepreneur-in-Residence (EIR) matching, build-space resources, spinout co-op programs, and investor engagement initiatives such as venture capital office hours, while identifying and establishing new resources to accelerate venture success. Investor Connections: Design structured mechanisms, such as investor days, curated introductions, and stage-based funding pathways, to connect the portfolio's ~50 spinouts with venture capital, corporate, and angel investors appropriate to each company's stage. Program Measurement and Improvement: Track program participation and outcomes across the portfolio, and continuously refine programming based on founder feedback and portfolio performance. 3. Ecosystem and External Engagement – 25% VC and Investor Relationships: Direct CRI's investor-relationship strategy and cultivate CRI's institutional relationships with venture capital firms, corporate investors, and angel networks from the ground up, while setting the framework within which portfolio-specific investor engagement is carried out, serving as a trusted connector between investors and CRI-affiliated ventures. Internal Partnerships: Coordinate closely with CRI's Commercialization, Marketing, and Contracts teams, and with Northeastern's broader entrepreneurial ecosystem (e.g., venture mentoring, hub-based entrepreneurship programs, I-Corps), to ensure spinouts have coordinated access to resources. Thought Leadership: Represent CRI externally at conferences, peer-institution forums, and industry events, and share best practices for university venture development. Qualifications: The minimum education, experience, and skills required to perform the job successfully. Advanced degree required ; STEM background (life sciences, physical sciences, or engineering) strongly preferred given the composition of the portfolio. 8–10 years of experience in venture development, technology commercialization, or startup building, including direct experience evaluating product-market fit and go-to-market strategy for early-stage, technology-based companies. Demonstrated experience designing and running scalable programs, such as accelerators, training curricula, or structured investor-engagement initiatives, that serve a portfolio of companies rather than single engagements. Track record of building and maintaining relationships with venture capital firms, corporate investors, and angel networks, with the ability to match investors to companies at the appropriate stage. Experience with non-dilutive funding pathways for early-stage science and technology companies (e.g., SBIR/STTR, foundation grants) and the ability to build support programming around them. Direct experience in an academic technology transfer or research commercialization setting, working with faculty inventors and understanding the constraints of university-affiliated venture formation. Strong financial and business acumen, including familiarity with cap tables, equity structures, and the financial mechanics of early-stage fundraising. Excellent relationship-management, coaching, and communication skills across a range of stakeholders, including faculty, founders, investors, and university leadership. Comfort operating in ambiguity and building new infrastructure in a fast-evolving field. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 113S Expected Hiring Range: $113,865.00 - $165,105.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-time-Research-Coordinator_R142714": {
      "title": "Part-time Research Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Exercise Psychology Laboratory (Director, Dr. Neha P. Gothe) at the Institute for Cognitive & Brain Health is seeking a part-time Research Coordinator. The Research Coordinator will support a wide range of research projects focused on promoting healthy lifestyle interventions across the lifespan. These include a focus on mind-body interventions of physical activity and nutrition incorporating mindfulness and yoga and their impact on physiological and psychological health outcomes. Visit https://eprl.sites.northeastern.edu/ for more information about the lab. This position may serve as a launching pad to graduate and professional (e.g. medical school) studies Minimum Qualifications: • Experience conducting human subjects research Collecting and organizing data, conducting statistical analyses, and coding in computer languages (SPSS, R, etc.) • Familiar with Microsoft Office suite and research databases such as RedCap. • Have excellent oral and written communication skills. • Highly organized and dependable. • Have an ability to work and troubleshoot independently and in a team environment. • Knowledge and skills are normally acquired through the completion of a Bachelor’s degree (BA/BS in a Health-related field) and at least one year experience in a lab environment. • A master's degree (MA/MS) is desirable but not required. • A yoga teacher training certification is desirable but not required. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: Research activities (60%): • Composing and modifying review board protocols. • Developing and posting advertisements (flyers, writing ads) for research participant recruitment. • Contacting, screening, and scheduling potential research participants. • Maintaining a secure recruitment database (RedCap, etc.). • Conducting research study visits. • Computer coding tasks and analyses (R, MatLab, Python, etc.). • Literature review and scientific products. Outreach and education (40%): • Developing relationships within the greater Boston communit to recruit participants. • Developing outreach activities and workshops to recruit study participants. • Explaining complex scientific constructs to relevant audiences. • Summarizing empirical research papers for the public for online publication (e.g., blog posts , newsletters). • Leveraging social media for the lab projects and dissemination. • Design and lead group discussion and physical activity classes for participants to build community Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106H Expected Hiring Range: $23.37 - $32.14 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Director-of-International---Graduate-Admissions_R142725": {
      "title": "Director of International & Graduate Admissions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Director of Graduate Enrollment and Admissions reports to the Associate Dean for Strategic Initiatives, Admissions and Financial Aid and develops recruitment and yield strategies to grow the School of Law's graduate programs, with a primary focus on non-JD programs (LL.M. and MLS), by increasing the number of applicants, admits, and matriculants. The Director creates, updates, and manages application and admissions processes for the LL.M. and MLS programs and develops marketing/recruiting strategy for prospective students, including international applicants. In consultation with the Dean and Associate Dean, the Director may support the launch of new graduate program initiatives as they arise, such as online or hybrid degrees and certificates. The Director also oversees and manages third-party partners with regard to admissions and recruitment functions. Experience in the operation of an academic program is highly recommended. Effective oral and written communication skills, cultural sensitivity, and the ability to work with a culturally diverse constituency and community are required. Must be highly proficient in data management. Must be proficient in word processing and in the use of social media as a marketing and communication tool. Experience working with international students is highly recommended. Experience working with law students is a plus. Facility with project management databases is a plus. Creativity and self-motivation are a plus. Minimum Qualifications: Knowledge and skills required for this position are normally obtained through a Bachelor's degree (JD or related law degree preferred) and experience working in student recruitment, admissions, and marketing academic programs. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: Enrollment (40%) Manages LL.M. and MLS recruitment, application, and admissions processes, including tracking applications, analyzing enrollment data, and maintaining admissions records Communicates with applicants and admitted students Reviews LL.M. and MLS applications for admissibility, rendering final admission decisions and scholarship awards Develops scholarship strategy for LL.M. and MLS programs Reviews JD advanced standing applications for foreign attorneys Develops international and domestic recruitment strategy for the LL.M. and MLS programs Travels as needed to promote the program, both internationally and domestically, including to new or key markets to support recruitment efforts Provides periodic support to the JD program (online and traditional), such as reading application files as needed and participating in Admissions-wide events Oversees enrollment and marketing efforts of third-party partners for graduate and online programs (e.g., MLS and Online LLM).   Online LL.M./LL.M. and MLS Communications & Marketing (30%) Creates/revises print and web-based marketing materials Develops and executes online marketing strategy Sources and manages all print and online marketing ads with outside vendors Creates and manages prospective, admitted, and enrolled student communication streams Primary responsibility for maintaining Online LL.M./LL.M. and MLS web pages Program Development (20%) Supports the Associate Dean and senior leadership in evaluating market opportunities and provides input that informs the direction of new and existing graduate programs and initiatives. Cultivates and manages day-to-day international partnership relationships with institutions abroad, in support of the Associate Dean's strategic partnership initiatives, to build the graduate enrollment pipeline and extend the School of Law's outreach internationally. Tracks and reports on enrollment performance and market trends for graduate programs to inform the Associate Dean's enrollment strategy and planning.   Budget Management (10%) Manages the marketing budget for the LL.M. and MLS programs, including recruitment, advertising, and publications Position Type Enrollment Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $76,335.00 - $107,823.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director-of-Honors-Community-Development_R142820": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Honors Community Development",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: The John Martinson Honors Program is comprised of approximately 3,000 academically gifted students. The Assistant Director of Honors Community Development is responsible for directing, implementing, and assessing the Honors Living-Learning Community initiatives and overseeing student-driven co-curricular programming. The Assistant Director plays an integral role in the John Martinson Honors Program and supports initiatives such as Orientation, Welcome Week activities, graduation recognition programming, and other initiatives as needed. The Assistant Director also provides developmental advising to Honors students on issues such as Honors course selection, academic support, and co-curricular opportunities. There are some weekend and evening hours required in this position to support programming. RESPONSIBILITIES INCLUDE: Selecting, training, and supervising 16 upperclass Honors students serving as Honors Living Learning Assistants Supporting and serving as consultant to 16+ faculty/ staff Honors LLC and Internal Admit Community Experience Coordinators as they design and offer high quality learning experiences that engage students in the community Planning and implementing the annual LLC Fall and Spring Launch, PAWS points Initiative, and LLC Showcase events to engage students in their communities and highlight their experiences to the Honors community and our partners. Recruiting, selecting, training and supervising 8-12 student leaders to co-design, facilitate, and assess initiatives designed to promote the engagement of upperclass students (e.g., Honors Events Council). Coordinating and supporting student-led learning initiatives (e.g., HEIST = the Honors Exchange of Ideas, Skills, and Talents) Designing and implementing assessment instruments to track student outcomes over time. Soliciting feedback, draft, edit and finalize reports for submission. Providing limited developmental advising and guidance to Honors students, with special attention to supporting Associate Directors in assessment. Collaborating with Honors team members, campus, and community partners to develop and realize the Northeastern University Honors program vision. Contributing to the planning and implementation of both first-year and upperclass Honors programming, (e.g. HONR 1102, academic, community-building, and leadership development programming). MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Master's Degree in higher education or related field required and two years of previous work experience in higher education required; advising experience desirable. Strong interpersonal, organizational and communication skills. Computer skills, experience working with Canvas or other Learning Management Systems desirable. Ability to prioritize tasks and work collaboratively. Experience working directly in or with Residential Life, Housing, including hiring and supervising student staff and creating high-impact student programming opportunities highly desired. Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Regulatory-Affairs--Boston-_R142817": {
      "title": "Part Time Lecturer - Regulatory Affairs (Boston)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a non-tenure track part time faculty lecturer in Regulatory Affairs on the Boston campus. Northeastern University's College of Professional Studies is seeking part-time faculty to teach graduate-level, on-ground courses in Boston for the Master of Science in Regulatory Affairs program and its associated graduate certificates. The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa . Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Instructional areas include, but are not limited to, drug and biologics development, medical device regulations, clinical trial management, commercialization of medical products/technology, law and intellectual property, international regulatory affairs, and regulatory writing. Qualifications Candidates must hold a terminal degree (e.g., a doctorate), OR a Master's degree in a scientific discipline with a minimum of five years of relevant industry or government experience. A history of successful teaching at the graduate level is strongly preferred. Candidates must be able to teach on campus in Boston at least one night a week for a 7- or 15-week course. RAC certification is strongly preferred. Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter. Please direct questions to Karen Zhou at k.zhou@northeastern.edu. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,086.44."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Co-op-Coordinator_R142799": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Co-op Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity: Northeastern University is a national leader in cooperative education in which students alternate between academic study and work experiences. The Office of the Chancellor is seeking a Co-op Faculty Coordinator to work with students, with an initial focus in Bouve College of Health Sciences. This position is a full-time, non-tenure track, 12-month faculty position. You will join a team of highly motivated, professional, experienced, collaborative individuals who provide an outstanding cooperative education program in the health sciences and public health. This position may be reallocated to other college co-op team(s) aligned with university-wide enrollment needs. Responsibilities: Education of Students : The Co-op Coordinator educates students through their career development. They educate, advise, and coach students, developing and delivering effective teaching and coaching programs that enable students to be successful in today’s global business environment. This includes, but is not limited to, working with students one-on-one, in workshops and in the classroom. The Co-op Coordinator teaches a career education course each semester, and coaches students through group and individual meetings. Classroom preparation includes teaching interviewing skills, professional behavior and communication, workplace ethics, and as well as leadership skills and self-awareness and emotional intelligence development. The Co-op Coordinator also maintains contact with students while engaged in their co-op experience and works with students through their post-co-op reflection. Integrated Learning : Cooperative education is an integral part of the academic experience at Northeastern and our philosophy is that learning in the classroom is enriched by practical experience, and practical experience enriches classroom learning. The Coordinator collaborates with academic faculty, administration, and the co-op community to guide students in the integration of their co-op and academic experiences. The Co-op Coordinator shares knowledge of industry with academic faculty to integrate employment-based learning with curriculum-based learning. Employer Relations/Job Development:  The Co-op Coordinator develops new partnerships and expands current employer programs by engaging with managers, human resources contacts, and alumni. They engage in lifelong learning to develop and maintain a current understanding of the industry, employers, and positions. Activities to support this endeavor include site visits to new and existing employers, outreach to potential new co-op employer partners, and active participation in relevant professional organizations. High-level customer-orientation, strong presentation skills, and experience in building and maintaining client relationships is essential. Prior professional experience in industry is preferred. Service : The Co-op Coordinator also engages in service to the Cooperative Education department, Bouve College of Health Sciences, and to Northeastern University. Service includes making presentations to prospective students and newly admitted students; participation on committees and task forces; and serving as an advisor to student organizations. Qualifications: A master’s degree in college student development, counseling, education, psychology or a health science/public health related field is required by the appointment start date, as well as a minimum of three years work experience. Excellent written and oral communication skills, and comfort presenting to diverse audiences. Experience working with a varied staff and/or student population. Proficiency in computer/technology and database maintenance, with excellent time management and organizational skills. Knowledge and appreciation of cooperative education and/or experiential learning and its impact on an individual’s career development. Ability to effectively engage a diverse network of global stakeholders, across time zones. Preferred Qualifications: An academic degree in a related health profession, or experience in career development and counseling of students. Professional experience within the health industry is a plus Experience as a career coach, certification preferable, but not required Experience working with an international student population Teaching experience in a classroom environment About The Bouvé College of Health Sciences: The Bouvé College of Health Sciences has over 250 faculty members, with approximately 2,000 undergraduate and 2,400 graduate students. It is the leading national model for education and research in the health, psychosocial and biomedical sciences and supports the University’s mission of educating students for a life of fulfillment and accomplishment and creating and translating knowledge to meet global and societal needs.  Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Coop Coordinator: $66,000.00 - $80,000.00 | Associate Coop Coordinator: $74,000.00 - $94,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142800": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity This job description is intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to this classification. It is not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties and skills required of personnel so classified. JOB SUMMAR Y The Skinner Lab at Northeastern University seeks an enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher to start in February 2026. The primary responsibility of the postdoc is to perform basic or applied research of a limited scope, primarily using existing theories and methods, ensuring that the research is complete. The candidate will assist the supervisor in the interpretation and publication of results and grants, assist in maintaining the laboratory, and may exercise functional supervision over supporting research staff. The appointment is for two years and may be extended for an additional 1-2 years, depending on continuation of funding and regular progress evaluation. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS The candidate should have strong problem-solving skills and be highly motivated. The candidate must have a Ph.D. in chemistry, biochemistry, molecular biology, structural biology or a closely-related field. Previous experience with molecular biology techniques (Western blots, qPCR/RNAseq), experience with gene editing(CRISPR), and DNA manipulation, are all preferred. The candidate should have hands-on experience with metabolomics samples preparation and measurement by LC/MS or GC/MS. A demonstrated proficiency in protein chemistry and protein biophysical analysis are also preferred. Prior experience with native or intact mass spectrometry is not required but would be valuable should it exist. A willingness to learn advanced metabolomics and proteomics techniques to uncover cellular metabolic regulation will be needed. Technical expertise in modern chromatography methodologies and techniques, good computer skills and data analysis experience are all desirable. The best candidates will have excellent communication, organization, and time management skills. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES The level and mixture of the duties may vary by position but typically include a combination of the following responsibilities. Assist in initiating, executing, and completing research and experiments. Prepare and write research papers, presentations, grant proposals, etc. Assist in laboratory management. Help train students. HOW TO APPLY Applications should contain a cover letter briefly describing candidate’s background and specific research interests, a full CV with a publication list, Ph.D. completion date, and the names and contact information for three references who might be contacted for recommendations. Review of applications will begin immediately. The initial appointment is for two years, with the potential for extension depending on regular progress evaluation and continuation of funding. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142801": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Lopez Group at Northeastern University is seeking a postdoctoral researcher with experience in machine learning and organic chemistry to join a collaborative project focused on sustainable polymer synthesis from CO₂. The researcher will develop and apply computational and AI-based methods, including high-throughput quantum chemistry and machine learning, to guide the design of metal-free cyclopropenimine catalysts, substrates, and reaction conditions for the synthesis of polyurethanes and polycarbonates. Qualifications : Candidates should have a Ph.D. in chemistry or a related field, strong programming and machine-learning skills, and a solid foundation in organic or physical organic chemistry. Experience in computational chemistry, cheminformatics, catalyst design, or polymer chemistry is highly desirable.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Administrative-Access-Assistant_R142765": {
      "title": "Administrative Access Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: Northeastern University's Electrical & Computer Engineering Department seeks a proactive and organized Administrative Access Assistant to support Dr. Kevin Fu, a Professor of Electrical & Computer Engineering who has a dexterity impairment affecting his ability to use a mouse and perform precise pointer-based tasks. This role is crucial for coordinating Professor Fu’s daily activities and ensuring his work environment is accessible. Primary activities include managing correspondence, schedules, and an accessible work environment. Occasional travel may be required.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Qualifications : Associate's Degree Required; Bachelor’s degree preferred. High standards for accuracy and thoroughness in areas of responsibility. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Good communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to multitask and problem-solve. Basic financial tracking and bookkeeping skills are a plus. Ability to work independently and in a team. Adaptability to work in a dynamic environment. Knowledge of assistive technology or keyboard-based tools to support users with dexterity impairments is preferred. Additional Competencies: Deadline Management Demonstrated ability to set realistic deadlines and consistently meet them. Experience in prioritizing tasks to ensure timely completion of all projects. Reliability and Accountability A track record of following through on commitments and responsibilities. Ability to communicate proactively in case of any delays or issues. Organizational Skills: Proven ability in organizing workspaces, digital files, and schedules to enhance workflow efficiency. Skill in managing multiple tasks and projects simultaneously without compromising quality. Project Coordination Experience in keeping projects on track and smoothly running. Ability to identify potential issues or bottlenecks in projects and address them proactively. Behavioral Attributes Detail-Oriented and Proactive Strong attention to detail with a proactive approach to problem-solving and task management. Anticipates needs and takes initiative to address them without waiting for direction. Flexibility and Adaptability Ability to both work alone when required to meet deadlines and to interact effectively with the team Excellent Communication Skills Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to provide clear and regular updates on project statuses and any challenges encountered.   KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES: Administrative Assist with communications and correspondence. Manage and coordinate Professor Fu’s calendar. Organize digital documents logically and accessibly. Maintain an accessible and efficient office environment. Student Staff Coordination Assist in hiring and onboarding student workers. Help coordinate and supervise student staff activities. Finances Support handling basic financial tasks like processing bills and managing expense reports. Tracking office expenditures. Project Coordination Provide support in organizing and tracking projects. Assist Professor Fu in preparing materials and project reports Dexterity and Computer Interaction Support Assist Professor Fu with computer tasks requiring precise mouse use or clicking, such as navigating interfaces, selecting items, and interacting with software. Support setup and use of assistive technology and keyboard-based tools to reduce reliance on mouse-dependent workflows   DOCUMENTS REQUIRED : Resume Cover letter Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106H Expected Hiring Range: $23.37 - $32.14 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Portland-ME/Director-of-Facilities---Portland_R142796": {
      "title": "Director of Facilities - Portland",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity This job description is intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to this classification. It is not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties and skills required of personnel so classified. Northeastern University is seeking a Director of Facilities to lead the maintenance and operations of its Portland, Maine campus. Under the direction of the Associate Vice-President of Facilities Operations, the Director of Facilities – Portland is responsible for ensuring the effective planning, coordination, delivery, and daily management of the maintenance and operations of the Portland campus, delivered primarily through a contracted Integrated Facilities Management (IFM) provider. The Director is responsible for defining the scope of work, confirming the proposed solution meets the need, and ensuring the final action plan meets facility requirements and stays within budget. The Director oversees operating procedures, financial requirements and objectives, and maintains optimal operating levels for building systems (HVAC, electrical, plumbing, cleaning, security, life safety) and campus infrastructure.    The Director is directly responsible for grounds maintenance (landscaping, snow/ice removal, exterior upkeep) and general labor functions (moving furniture and equipment, event setup/breakdown, minor repairs, and other physical tasks not requiring a licensed trade), whether these are performed personally, by campus staff, or coordinated through contracted services. The Director also develops budgets, resource allocation plans, and tracking/reporting mechanisms for facilities operations and preventive maintenance.    The Director is responsible for developing and maintaining a customer relations philosophy focused on response time, facilities stewardship, and consistent service delivery regardless of whether work is performed by contracted or in-house personnel. This includes managing the campus operations team comprised of both full and part-time staff to ensure that standard of service is met. This role is expected to scale in scope and structure as the organization transitions to a significantly larger facility in approximately early 2028. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Facilities Management, Engineering, or related field.   Ten or more (10+) years of related experience, including direct experience managing an outsourced or Integrated Facilities Management (IFM) contract.    Working knowledge of building trade systems (HVAC, electrical, plumbing, life safety) sufficient to evaluate the quality of contracted work.    Demonstrated experience developing budgets and preparing financial reports.   Experience interpreting and applying applicable codes, regulations, and university policy in a facilities context as well as developing operating procedures.   Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple projects and vendor relationships simultaneously.   Ability to interact effectively at all levels, including with vendor executives, on-site contracted staff, faculty, and University leadership.   Proficiency with computerized maintenance management systems (Archibus preferred), vendor performance dashboards, and comfort adopting new facilities technology.   Demonstrated commitment to regulatory compliance, safety, and environmental standards.   Comfortable operating as an individual contributor day-to-day while also planning for future team growth.     Preferred   Experience helping scale a facilities function including strategic planning for growth.    APPA Certified Educational Facilities Professional (CEFP) or similar facilities management certification is a plus.       Position Type Facilities Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 114S Expected Hiring Range: $130,945.00 - $189,868.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---English_R142716": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - English",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.  Responsibilities: The Department of English in the College of Social Sciences and Humanities seeks applications for part-time writing instructors. Lecturers teach required writing courses that include First-Year Writing, as well as a variety of Advanced Writing courses that focus on disciplinary writing practices, including specialized courses in technical writing, business writing, and science writing. First-Year and Advanced Writing courses are also available in sections for multilingual writers. For a general introduction to the Writing Programs, please see http://www.northeastern.edu/cssh/english/writing-program/ . Positions available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and department need.   Qualifications: M.A. in English or equivalent required. Documents to Submit: Cover letter and CV. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,085."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Portland-ME/Part-Time-Lecturer---Non-Degree-Learning--Portland-_R141023": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Non-Degree Learning (Portland)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a non-tenure track part time faculty lecturer in Non-Degree Learning on the Portland campus. The successful candidate will teach on-campus and online Custom Learning courses. This position is located at the Roux Institute, Portland campus. The mission of the Custom Learning program is to foster industry best practices among working professionals and to provide experiential learning opportunities so that new skills can be immediately applied at work. Successful candidates will be an expert in one or more fields of expertise: Data Analytics Machine Learning / Artificial Intelligence Project Management The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Teach custom learning courses to partner employees. The successful candidate will have expertise in teaching partner requested subject areas and/or with adult course facilitation. Candidate places a high value on experiential learning and classroom engagement. Qualifications The primary criteria for this position are credibility with a business and technical audience through relevant industry experience in our targeted areas, as well as excellent teaching and facilitation skills. Candidates will be evaluated on a case-by-case basis for fit with the needs of the program. In addition to industry and facilitation experience, it is expected that most candidates will have obtained a minimum bachelor's degree, though masters and/or doctoral degrees may be required for consideration for certain opportunities. Industry certifications (technical certifications, accredited certifications in targeted subject areas) are also a plus. Industry relevant experience in a partner related field. Experience teaching online courses to adult learners is preferred. Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter. Please direct questions to Zach Blattner at z.blattner@northeastern.edu. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The per credit rate is $2,086.44."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Vertex---Medical-Affairs-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142351": {
      "title": "Vertex – Medical Affairs Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Vertex Pharmaceuticals is offering a two-year Medical Affairs fellowship, based in Boston, MA. About Vertex Pharmaceuticals Vertex is a global biotechnology company that invests in scientific innovation to create transformative medicines for people with serious diseases and conditions. The company has approved therapies for cystic fibrosis, sickle cell disease, transfusion-dependent beta thalassemia and acute pain, and it continues to advance clinical and research programs in these areas. Vertex also has a robust clinical pipeline of investigational therapies across a range of modalities in other serious diseases where it has deep insight into causal human biology, including IgA nephropathy, neuropathic pain, APOL1-mediated kidney disease, primary membranous nephropathy, autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, type 1 diabetes and myotonic dystrophy type 1. Vertex was founded in 1989 and has its global headquarters in Boston, with international headquarters in London. Additionally, the company has research and development sites and commercial offices in North America, Europe, Australia, Latin America and the Middle East. Vertex is consistently recognized as one of the industry's top places to work, including 16 consecutive years on Science magazine's Top Employers list and one of Fortune’s 100 Best Companies to Work For. For company updates and to learn more about Vertex's history of innovation, visit  www.vrtx.com  or follow us on  LinkedIn ,  Facebook ,  Instagram ,  YouTube  and  X . Responsibilities Medical Affairs Fellowship Description :  In Medical Affairs, our team works to ensure that clinical decision-makers have a deep understanding of the benefits and risks of our medicines through scientific communication and disease state education. We gather insights from stakeholders, identify data and knowledge gaps, and respond to medical inquiries in a clear and scientifically accurate manner to guide healthcare provider and payer decision making. During the program, fellows will work collaboratively and cross-functionally within Vertex's Medical Affairs group across Global Medical Affairs, Global Medical Information, and Medical Operations. The fellows will also have the opportunity to rotate to an elective of their choice, with options including Field Medical, US Medical, and others.    Medical Affairs Fellowship Objectives :  ·       Deepen scientific expertise in disease pathophysiology, epidemiology, and treatment landscape to support scientific exchange within the medical community      ·       Develop scientific communication materials for internal education and field medical       ·       Support the development and execution of key Medical Affairs deliverables, including the medical plan, strategic publication plan, and disease state communication framework ·       Collaborate with US and Global cross-functional stakeholders to prepare materials for key scientific conferences and support on-site medical activities       ·       Utilize clinical expertise to develop timely, accurate, and scientifically balanced responses to unsolicited medical information inquiries       ·       Network across the Medical Affairs organization to gain a thorough understanding of industry opportunities and expand upon experiences through additional projects of interest  Qualifications • Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution • Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong time management and leadership abilities • Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026. 1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Abbott-Diabetes-Care---Medical-Affairs-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142330": {
      "title": "Abbott Diabetes Care – Medical Affairs Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of PharmD graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Abbott Diabetes Care is offering a two-year Global Medical Affairs PharmD fellowship, based remotely. About Abbott Diabetes Care As a global leader in diabetes care, Abbott is constantly working to deliver the highest quality products and unwavering support to our customers. Consistent and accurate glucose monitoring is the foundation of any diabetes management plan, so we’re committed to continuous improvement in the way patients and professionals measure, track and analyze glucose levels. Our science-driven products empower people living with diabetes. Through our innovative product development program, Abbott has developed many cutting-edge glucose monitoring technologies. Responsibilities The primary function of the fellow is to create and sustain the relevant connections with the diabetes scientific community to support FreeStyle Libre products. The fellow will develop relationships and foster advocacy among targeted Key Opinion Leaders and will establish excellent clinical use practices and ongoing support for healthcare professionals. In order to develop commercially and clinically competitive products and services, the fellow will participate in product development teams, contributing clinical and user experience insights. In addition, the fellow will assist with Abbott Diabetes Care clinical studies related to product approval and customer use, particularly related to the training and educational aspects of the product.   Job Duties ·       Provide unbiased scientifically balanced information on Abbott’s products to healthcare providers. ·       Support medical affairs by providing product information expertise on a wide range of initiatives including medical review of promotional materials, staffing medical booths at meetings and conferences, and supporting cross-functional activities. ·       Develop strong literature searching skills and critically review published and unpublished clinical data regarding marketed and investigational products and/or therapeutic areas of interest to Abbott. ·       Support the overall operations of a medical information function by participating in process improvement initiatives, tracking and reporting key operational metrics, and ensuring audit readiness. ·       Collaborate with cross-functional groups across Abbott. ·       Assist with medical content review for professional materials and key advocacy initiatives. ·       With oversight from the MSLs, participate in Core Teams, providing clinical insights for product development and creating content such as slide decks for the MSL team to utilize with their KOLs. ·       With oversight from the MSLs, present scientific educational materials to KOLs, academic centers, and professional organizations to gain awareness, acceptance, and advocacy for products. ·       Work with oversight from the Publications lead to support a publication strategy for products in the field. ·       Support and coordinate activities, information, and resources with other personnel including taking minutes at each meeting to allow dissemination. ·       Coordinate and execute regional educational programs. ·      Attend key regional and national medical meetings.   Qualifications • Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution • Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong time management and leadership abilities • Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern   Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026:   1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Ironwood--Medical-Scientific-Affairs-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142368": {
      "title": "Ironwood- Medical Scientific Affairs Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Ironwood Pharmaceuticals is offering a two-year Medical Scientific Affairs fellowship, based in Boston, Massachusetts. About Ironwood Ironwood Pharmaceuticals (Nasdaq: IRWD), is a biotechnology company focused on developing and commercializing therapies for people living with gastrointestinal (GI) and rare diseases. Ironwood is advancing apraglutide, a next-generation, long-acting synthetic GLP-2 analog being developed for patients with short bowel syndrome who are dependent on parenteral support. Ironwood has also been a pioneer in the development of LINZESS® (linaclotide), the U.S. branded prescription market leader for adults with irritable bowel syndrome with constipation (IBS-C) or chronic idiopathic constipation (CIC) and pediatric patients with IBS-C or functional constipation (FC). Founded in 1998, Ironwood is headquartered in Boston, Massachusetts, with additional operations in Basel, Switzerland. Responsibilities The Medical Scientific Affairs (MSA) fellowship is designed to provide the fellow with experience as an integrated member of the MSA team, contributing to strategic medical initiatives while developing competencies in core areas of MSA functions, such as Publications and Scientific Communications , Field Medical Affairs (Medical Science Liaison), Medical Information , Medical Education , and Evidence Generation. The fellow will work closely with colleagues across MSA and other functional areas, including clinical development, regulatory, patient safety, commercial, data sciences, and legal. The fellow will also gain exposure to external partner interactions, vendor oversight, and opportunities to precept PharmD Co-Op students. Job Duties: ·       Contribute to the development and maintenance of Ironwood Medical Scientific Affairs strategic plans.  ·       Work cross-functionally internally (commercial, medical writing, regulatory, legal, etc.) to provide subject matter expertise for medical strategy planning and content development.  ·       Collaborate cross-functionally to provide key MSA deliverables, such as the scientific communication platforms, publications strategy and medical communication strategy.  ·       Develop various types of internally or externally facing evidence-based medical content for symposia, training material and other MSA materials.  ·       Cross-functional collaboration to ensure strategic alignment, medical accuracy, evidence-based representation of Ironwood GI products in US and partnered regions.  ·       Generate medical insights from the perspectives of the patients and providers to support development of strategic plans.  ·       Participate in insight gathering activities ·       Contribute to the maintenance of key medical information deliverables, including creation and updates FAQ documents and any required custom responses.  ·       Provide comprehensive training with respect to disease state(s) and product(s) and/or investigational agents to which fellow will be assigned.  ·       Participate in scientific congress, advisory committee, and KOL engagement activities.  ·       Precept PharmD students during their co-op and/or IPPE/APPE rotations.  ·       Participate in a rotational opportunity based on business needs, availability, and fellow interest. ·       Additional responsibilities to be assigned based on individual interests.  The Successful Candidate Will Demonstrate:  ·       PharmD from an accredited college or university before the start of the fellowship term;  ·       Ability to interpret and discuss complex medical scientific data;  ·       Excellent project management, time management and organizational skills;  ·       Ability to learn new skills and apply them broadly across MSA initiatives;  ·       Effective communication skills, both written and verbal; Record of medical and/or scientific publications preferred;  ·       Proactive individual capable of assessing needs, then creating solutions that effectively meet the need;  ·       Flexibility and ability to adapt quickly as company priorities change;  ·       Excellent interpersonal skills in order to effectively manage expectations and facilitate collaborative, working relationships;  ·       A positive attitude while adjusting to different personal approaches;  ·       Advanced proficiency and extensive experience with Microsoft Office Suite, such as Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint, and Word;  Ironwood Pharmaceuticals is an equal opportunity employer welcoming diversity in our workforce.   Qualifications • Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution • Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong time management and leadership abilities • Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern • Willingness and ability to relocate to Boston, Massachusetts before the Fellowship start date of July 1, 2027. Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026: 1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115   Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Sarepta-Therapeutics---Scientific-Communications-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142334": {
      "title": "Sarepta Therapeutics – Scientific Communications Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Doctor of Pharmacy (PharmD) graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Sarepta Therapeutics is offering a two-year Scientific Communications Fellowship based in Cambridge, Massachusetts. About Sarepta Therapeutics Sarepta is a global biotechnology company on an urgent mission to engineer precision genetic medicine for rare diseases that devastate lives and cut futures short. The company is advancing drug development by driving efficiencies, shortening the time from lab to patient, and building gene therapy manufacturing capacity. Sarepta collaborates with health networks and payers while rethinking pricing models for innovative treatments in development. The organization is dedicated to transforming genetic understanding into genetic medicine for patients with rare diseases. Job Summary The Pharmacy Postdoctoral Fellow will join the Sarepta Global Scientific Communications (Sci Comms) team for a two-year commitment. The objective of the Global Sci Comms team is to develop and execute a holistic strategic communication plan that supports Sarepta’s platforms and programs by building timely, balanced, evidence-based scientific narratives that provide clear and consistent content for a wide range of cross-functional stakeholders. This fellowship is designed to provide the Fellow with in-depth experience within Scientific Communications through collaboration with internal cross-functional teams and engagement with external experts on key company initiatives. The Fellow will be immersed in publication and medical communication activities related to clinical research, health economics and outcomes research, and real-world evidence. The Fellow will support strategic dissemination of data across a wide range of channels and lead diverse projects throughout the two-year program based on business needs and professional interests. Minimum Qualifications Required Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an Accreditation Council for Pharmacy Education (ACPE)-accredited institution Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts Excellent oral and written communication skills Strong time management and leadership abilities Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern University Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Drive the development of scientific publications, including abstracts, posters, presentations, and manuscripts, in adherence to good publication practices Review draft publications for scientific quality and data accuracy Develop medical field decks, booth collateral, digital assets, and symposium content aligned with platform strategic imperatives Obtain cross-functional alignment among relevant internal stakeholders and coordinate review, approval, and related activities associated with the development of scientific communications and publications Collaborate with medical communication agencies to support the timely execution of publication, congress, and medical communication plans Identify and propose innovative solutions to resolve issues and improve efficiencies in current processes Required Documents Curriculum vitae (CV) Unofficial PharmD transcripts Cover letter Additional Application Information Applicants should submit all required application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026. Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by letter writers no later than November 23, 2026. Each letter writer must include: The candidate’s full name The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letters and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. STEM Optional Practical Training (STEM OPT) is not compatible with this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Alnylam---Clinical-Science-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142358": {
      "title": "Alnylam – Clinical Science Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Alnylam Pharmaceuticals is offering a two-year Clinical Science fellowship, based in Cambridge, MA. About Alnylam Alnylam Pharmaceuticals (Nasdaq: ALNY) has led the translation of RNA interference (RNAi) into a whole new class of innovative medicines with the potential to transform the lives of people afflicted with rare and more prevalent diseases. Based on Nobel Prize-winning science, RNAi therapeutics represent a powerful, clinically validated approach to treating diseases at their genetic source by “interfering” with mRNA that cause or contribute to disease. Since our founding in 2002, Alnylam has led the RNAi Revolution and continues to turn scientific possibility into reality. Our culture:  Our people-first culture is guided by our core values: fiercely innovative, open culture, purposeful urgency, passion for excellence, and commitment to people, and these values influence how we work and the business decisions we make. Thanks to feedback from our employees over the years, we’ve been fortunate to be named a top employer around the world. Alnylam is extremely proud to have been recognized as the #1 Large Employer by Boston Globe Top Places to Work in 2023 for the third consecutive year, one of Science Magazine’s Top Biopharma Employers, one of America's Most Responsible Companies for 2024 by Newsweek, a Fast Company Best Workplace for Innovators, and a Great Place to Work in Canada, France, Italy, Spain, Switzerland, and UK – among others. At Alnylam, we commit to an inclusive recruitment process and equal employment opportunity. We are dedicated to building an environment where employees can feel that they belong, can bring their authentic selves to work, and achieve to their full potential. By empowering employees to embrace their unique differences at work, our business grows stronger with advanced and original thinking, allowing us to bring groundbreaking medicines to patients. Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, national origin, ethnicity, marital status, protected veteran status, disability, or any other characteristics prohibited by law. Responsibilities Clinical Science is one of the core functions of Clinical Development at Alnylam that pairs scientific acumen with practical clinical knowledge to enable efficient and effective drug development. Clinical Scientists contribute throughout the development cycle from Phase 1 to IND submission. They are data-driven, scientific leaders of programs in the clinical stage, with key responsibilities in clinical research and study execution. The fellow will develop a deep understanding of the Clinical Science function, in particular through hands-on experience and interfacing with key collaborators in the Clinical Development organization. At the conclusion of the program, the fellow should be equipped with the skills to contribute as an autonomous Clinical Scientist or other closely related role within Clinical Development.  Key Responsibilities: Development of key documents including clinical development plans, protocol concepts and full protocols for product(s) in early development along with contributing to activities for product(s) in later stages of development, including NDA filings. Contribute to the development of clinical sections of regulatory documents such as Investigators’ Brochures, briefing books, safety updates, IND/NDA submission documents, and responses to Health Authorities questions Engagement in medical data review and analysis during study execution to ensure integrity of accruing study data. Develop effective working relationship with key investigators in assigned programs to optimize scientific quality/innovation of clinical study design, execution, reporting and publication. Contribute to trial-related advisory boards. Participate in investigators meetings and protocol training meetings. Coordinate the real time availability of quality clinical trial data, including safety, efficacy, pharmacokinetic and biomarker data, to provide consolidated information for data review meetings. Create content to support internal and external clinical and scientific discussions on assigned programs and therapeutic areas. Qualifications • Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution • Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong time management and leadership abilities • Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern • Proven project management experience • Ability to manage multiple priorities and work in a fast-paced, matrix environment Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026: 1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115   Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Alnylam---Global-Scientific-Communications---Medical-Communications-and-Publications-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142359": {
      "title": "Alnylam – Global Scientific Communications - Medical Communications and Publications Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Alnylam Pharmaceuticals is offering a two-year Global Scientific Communications - Medical Communications and Publications fellowship, based in Cambridge, MA. About Alnylam We are the leader in RNAi therapeutics – a revolutionary approach with the potential to transform the lives of people with rare and common diseases. Built on Nobel Prize-winning science, Alnylam has delivered the breakthroughs that made RNAi therapeutics possible and are just at the beginning of what’s possible. Our deep pipeline, late-stage programs, and bold vision reflect our core values: fierce innovation, passion for excellence, purposeful urgency, open culture and commitment to people. We're proud to be a globally recognized top employer, where an authentic, inclusive culture and breakthrough thinking fuel one another. Our culture:  Our people-first culture is guided by our core values: fiercely innovative, open culture, purposeful urgency, passion for excellence, and commitment to people, and these values influence how we work and the business decisions we make. Thanks to feedback from our employees over the years, we’ve been fortunate to be named a top employer around the world. Alnylam is extremely proud to have been recognized as the #1 Large Employer by Boston Globe Top Places to Work in 2023 for the third consecutive year, one of Science Magazine’s Top Biopharma Employers, one of America's Most Responsible Companies for 2024 by Newsweek, a Fast Company Best Workplace for Innovators, and a Great Place to Work in Canada, France, Italy, Spain, Switzerland, and UK – among others. At Alnylam, we commit to an inclusive recruitment process and equal employment opportunity. We are dedicated to building an environment where employees can feel that they belong, can bring their authentic selves to work, and achieve to their full potential. By empowering employees to embrace their unique differences at work, our business grows stronger with advanced and original thinking, allowing us to bring groundbreaking medicines to patients. Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, national origin, ethnicity, marital status, protected veteran status, disability, or any other characteristics prohibited by law. Responsibilities The Medical Affairs organization at Alnylam plays a leading role in supporting pipeline assets, in preparing for launch, and supporting commercially available products, as we advance medicines for patients who have limited or inadequate treatment options. The Global Scientific Communications team leads the strategic dissemination of data through strong stakeholder engagement, enabling scientific exchange, with the ultimate goal of improving the care of patients. Fellows can expect to drive Global Scientific Communication strategy through engagement with cross-functional collaborators. This position is ideal for determined, highly motivated, and adaptable individuals seeking unique experiences and professional growth within Global Medical Affairs. Key Responsibilities: ·       Execute scientific communication strategy for early- and late-stage programs ·       Contribute to the tactical planning and execution of global publications plans, including peer-reviewed manuscripts ·       Lead the development of medical congress materials, including publications (abstracts, posters/presentations), booth content, or symposia ·       Engage with key opinion leaders to develop various deliverables in alignment with medical communication objectives ·       Participate in the development and execution of medical training plans ·       Help support the execution of omnichannel and digital strategy within Medical Affairs to ensure optimal orchestration of scientific information to key stakeholders Qualifications • Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution • Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong time management and leadership abilities • Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026: 1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
