    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Vertex---US-Marketing-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142348-1": {
      "title": "Vertex – US Marketing Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Vertex Pharmaceuticals is offering a two-year North America Commercial fellowship based in Boston, MA. About Vertex Vertex is a global biotechnology company that invests in scientific innovation to create transformative medicines for people with serious diseases and conditions. The company has approved therapies for cystic fibrosis, sickle cell disease, transfusion-dependent beta thalassemia and acute pain, and it continues to advance clinical and research programs in these areas. Vertex also has a robust clinical pipeline of investigational therapies across a range of modalities in other serious diseases where it has deep insight into causal human biology, including IgA nephropathy, neuropathic pain, APOL1-mediated kidney disease, primary membranous nephropathy, autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, type 1 diabetes, generalized myasthenia gravis, and myotonic dystrophy type 1. Vertex was founded in 1989 and has its global headquarters in Boston, with international headquarters in London. Additionally, the company has research and development sites and commercial offices in North America, Europe, Australia, Latin America and the Middle East. Vertex is consistently recognized as one of the industry's top places to work, including 16 consecutive years on Science magazine's Top Employers list and one of Fortune’s 100 Best Companies to Work For. For company updates and to learn more about Vertex's history of innovation, visit www.vrtx.com or follow us on LinkedIn, Facebook, Instagram, YouTube and X. Responsibilities The US Commercial department is responsible for the execution of the commercial strategy across the United States. This includes leading patient access and reimbursement for current and future therapies, establishing the product strategy for new products and marketed brands, and ongoing support for both patients and caregivers. The fellow will engage in a 2-year role on the commercial team to better understand the role of commercial in a pharmaceutical organization.  Objectives US Marketing The US Marketing/commercial role will support one or more of several workstreams, including: US commercial strategy; brand strategy; tactical execution. This fellowship offers an opportunity to learn how drugs are commercialized and what the requirements are to succeed in a commercial corporate business. This individual will develop an understanding of how medicines are commercialized and made available to those who need them, including setting commercial goals/objectives, learning core marketing principles, commercial operations, and/or business processes. They will understand our healthcare providers and patients' needs and work cross-functionally to achieve business objectives. They will learn and support ongoing marketing projects and lead new initiatives. Gain understanding of Marketing and cross-functional teams within the broader organization Support the team in executing the strategy for the US Brands including collaboration with the team on creating promotional materials for the Sales team Lead strategic and/or tactical projects that are ‘on strategy’ and help realize the goal of patients benefiting from Vertex medicines and to meet business objectives Collaborate cross-functionally to build relationships with stakeholders and develop a deep understanding of the patient treatment journey and key customer insights The ideal candidate will possess an inherent sense of urgency, curiosity, and collaboration, and will be a fast learner Qualifications • Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution • Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong time management and leadership abilities • Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026: 1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Domp-US---Global-Medical-Affairs---Medical-Information---Operations-and-Medical-Communications-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142502": {
      "title": "Dompé US - Global Medical Affairs - Medical Information & Operations and Medical Communications Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Dompé is offering a two-year fellowship in Global Medical Affairs - Medical Information & Operations and Medical Communications, based remotely. About Dompé Dompé is an Italian bio-pharmaceutical company that focuses on innovation, where a long tradition in the field of personal wellness goes hand-in-hand with a commitment to research and development to meet unsatisfied therapeutic needs. Established in 1940 in Milan, Dompé has a manufacturing hub in L’Aquila, and offices in Naples, Italy, and Tirana, Albania. The company has approximately 1,000 employees. The US headquarters of Dompé is based in San Mateo, CA, in the San Francisco Bay Area. The Pharmacy Postdoctoral Fellow will join the Global Medical Information & Operations and Medical Communications teams for a two-year commitment, where they will be immersed in a variety of Global Medical Affairs activities. Year 1 is a dedicated immersion in Medical Information & Operations (6 months) and Medical Communications (6 months). Year 2 consists of two 6-month rotations in Global Medical Affairs functions, selected from: Medical Engagement Strategy, Evidence Generation (Integrated Evidence Generation, Real-World Evidence Generation, Health Economics and Outcomes Research, Payer Engagement), Patient Advocacy, and Field Medical. Throughout Year 2, the fellow will continue to dedicate approximately 25% of their time to the Medical Information & Operations and/or Medical Communication teams, with the remaining time devoted to the active rotation. The fellow will also have the opportunity to learn about various industry functions such as Research & Development (Clinical Development, Clinical Operations, Biostatistics), Regulatory Affairs, Pharmacovigilance, or the Commercial Organization through longitudinal projects.  The fellow will also have the opportunity to attend Medical Congresses.  Rotations may be extended at the discretion of the rotation supervisor. Other rotations can be arranged based on availability and interest of the fellow. Responsibilities Global Medical Information & Operations and Medical Communications: Example job responsibilities include but are not limited to: Develop medical response letters, slide decks, infographics, videos and literature summaries for both internal and external stakeholders Partner cross-functionally to support execution of the Global Medical Strategy for the assigned product/disease area Provide medical review support of medical and promotional materials. Support medical information contact center operations, including escalation handling and analysis of inquiry volume and trend metrics Provide training on the assigned product/disease area to contact center staff, Medical Affairs vendor partners, and Field Medical teams Support the development and maintenance of the Key Scientific Platform for the assigned product/disease area Develop enduring materials for Medical Affairs booth and support the execution of the booth Attend relevant medical congresses to support the Medical Affairs booth, attend sessions and medical education symposia and provide summary reports Lead and execute a longitudinal research project Precept APPE students on rotation, as opportunities arise Qualifications Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts Excellent oral and written communication skills Strong time management and leadership abilities Strong clinical acumen or the ability to develop disease area-specific acumen Ability to work independently, collaborate in a cross-functional environment across multiple time zone, and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern Demonstrated interest in Medical Information and Medical Communications through rotation experience, industry internships, or relevant coursework   Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026:   1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: The candidate's full name The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Domp-US---Global-Medical-Affairs---Real-World-Evidence---Health-Economics-and-Outcomes-Research-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142503": {
      "title": "Dompé US - Global Medical Affairs - Real-World Evidence & Health Economics and Outcomes Research Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Dompé is offering a two-year fellowship in Global Medical Affairs - Real-World Evidence & Health Economics and Outcomes Research (RWE/HEOR), based remotely. About Dompé  Dompé is an Italian bio-pharmaceutical company that focuses on innovation, where a long tradition in the field of personal wellness goes hand-in-hand with a commitment to research and development to meet unsatisfied therapeutic needs. Established in 1940 in Milan, Dompé has a manufacturing hub in L’Aquila, and offices in Naples, Italy, and Tirana, Albania. The company has approximately 1,000 employees. The US headquarters of Dompé is based in San Mateo, CA, in the San Francisco Bay Area. The Pharmacy Postdoctoral Fellow will join the Real-World Evidence / Health Economics and Outcomes Research (RWE/HEOR) team for a two-year commitment, where they will be immersed in a variety of Global Medical Affairs activities. Year 1 is a dedicated immersion in RWE/HEOR, with experience in integrated evidence generation plan (IEGP) development, RWE generation, HEOR strategy and analytics, health technology assessment, value dossier development, and payer engagement. Year 2 consists of two 6-month rotations in Global Medical Affairs functions, selected from: Medical Engagement Strategy, Medical Communications, Medical Information & Operations, Patient Advocacy, and Field Medical. Throughout Year 2, the fellow will continue to dedicate approximately 25% of their time to the RWE/HEOR team, with the remaining time devoted to the active rotation. The fellow will also have the opportunity to learn about various industry functions such as Research & Development (Clinical Development, Clinical Operations, Biostatistics), Regulatory Affairs, Pharmacovigilance, or the Commercial Organization through longitudinal projects.  The fellow will also have the opportunity to attend Medical Congresses.  Rotations may be extended at the discretion of the rotation supervisor. Other rotations can be arranged based on availability and interest of the fellow.  Responsibilities Responsibilities include but are not limited to: Manage the generation of foundational epidemiologic data for cross-functional partners across all therapeutic areas and functions Learn the tasks, processes, and industry standards involved in conducting RWE, HEOR, and epidemiologic research activities Assist in managing integrated evidence plans across therapeutic areas Contribute to the design and execution of RWE and HEOR studies, including concept documents, protocols, statistical analysis plans, and study reports Contribute actively to the development and launch of RWE/HEOR strategies within the team Support development of global value dossiers, health technology assessment (HTA) submissions, and payer-facing value communication materials Support health economic modeling (e.g., cost-effectiveness and budget-impact models) and systematic literature reviews Collaborate with cross-functional teams (regulatory, marketing, sales, legal, and clinical development) to communicate up-to-date data generation plans and needs as a subject matter expert Support the RWE/HEOR team in overseeing operational activities for the team's projects Participate in Northeastern University academic activities, including teaching, precepting, service, and a longitudinal scholarly project Build the experience and skills needed to succeed on an epidemiology, RWE Sciences, and/or HEOR team in the biotech industry   Qualifications Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution or PhD in relevant life science Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts, if candidate has a Pharm.D. Excellent oral and written communication skills Strong time management and leadership abilities Ability to work independently, collaborate in a cross-functional environment across multiple time zone, and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern Demonstrated interest in real-world evidence, epidemiology, health economics, or outcomes research through prior research, coursework, or rotation experience Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026: 1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Alnylam---US-Medical-Affairs-TTR-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142500": {
      "title": "Alnylam – US Medical Affairs TTR Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, Alnylam Pharmaceuticals is offering a two-year US Medical Affairs fellowship, based in Cambridge, MA. About Alnylam Alnylam Pharmaceuticals (Nasdaq: ALNY) has led the translation of RNA interference (RNAi) into a whole new class of innovative medicines with the potential to transform the lives of people afflicted with rare and more prevalent diseases. Based on Nobel Prize-winning science, RNAi therapeutics represent a powerful, clinically validated approach to treating diseases at their genetic source by “interfering” with mRNA that cause or contribute to disease. Since our founding in 2002, Alnylam has led the RNAi Revolution and continues to turn scientific possibility into reality. Our culture:  Our people-first culture is guided by our core values: fiercely innovative, open culture, purposeful urgency, passion for excellence, and commitment to people, and these values influence how we work and the business decisions we make. Thanks to feedback from our employees over the years, we’ve been fortunate to be named a top employer around the world. Alnylam is extremely proud to have been recognized as the #1 Large Employer by Boston Globe Top Places to Work in 2023 for the third consecutive year, one of Science Magazine’s Top Biopharma Employers, one of America's Most Responsible Companies for 2024 by Newsweek, a Fast Company Best Workplace for Innovators, and a Great Place to Work in Canada, France, Italy, Spain, Switzerland, and UK – among others. At Alnylam, we commit to an inclusive recruitment process and equal employment opportunity. We are dedicated to building an environment where employees can feel that they belong, can bring their authentic selves to work, and achieve to their full potential. By empowering employees to embrace their unique differences at work, our business grows stronger with advanced and original thinking, allowing us to bring groundbreaking medicines to patients. Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, national origin, ethnicity, marital status, protected veteran status, disability, or any other characteristics prohibited by law. Responsibilities The Medical Affairs organization at Alnylam plays a leading role in supporting pipeline assets, in preparing for launch, and supporting commercially available products, as we advance medicines for patients who have limited or inadequate treatment options. US Medical Affairs translates and implements the disease and product strategy set at the global level and adapts it to the US healthcare landscape. US Medical works within a cross-functional team building tactical alignment with internal partners and delivers on the medical plan. US Medical directors play a key role in informing global product development and data generation strategies with perspective of US Region. The team also provides disease and product education to internal stakeholders as well as the external healthcare community. Field medical teams within US Medical engage in scientific discussions with both HCPs and payers to collect and disseminate evolving insights on the external treatment landscape, Alnylam’s clinical trial programs, and Alnylam data to inform Alnylam strategy. The US Medical Affairs Fellow will support the development and pull-through of scientific presentations to support upcoming launch activities. The fellow will also have a chance to shadow MSLs and understand the principles of engagement with HCPs, gathering insights and leading scientific discussions. Key Job Duties: ·       Contribute to the tactical planning and execution of US Medical Affairs plans ·       Understand patient pathways, care gaps, and HCP education needs in different US geographies and support development of regional field medical plans ·       Support the medical plan for the ongoing evolution of Alnylam and its products in the ATTR amyloidosis space ·       Support the development and/or pull-through of US-specific medical communications, publications, and training materials Qualifications • Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution • Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts • Excellent oral and written communication skills • Strong time management and leadership abilities • Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern   Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026: 1. Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded 2. Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded 3. Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: ·       The candidate's full name ·       The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115 Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/AstraZeneca--CVRM-Regulatory-Affairs-Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R142743": {
      "title": "AstraZeneca –CVRM Regulatory Affairs Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity Northeastern University Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowships Program is a two-year experiential program designed to advance lifelong learning and the education and training of Pharm.D. graduates. A key element of the program’s success is preparing Fellows to meet evolving workforce needs across various sectors of the pharmaceutical industry. Fellows have the opportunity to work closely with our innovative biopharmaceutical industry partners, while engaging with Northeastern University faculty in professional and career development, teaching, service, and scholarly activities. In partnership with Northeastern University, AstraZeneca is offering a two-year CVRM Regulatory Affairs fellowship, based in Cambridge, Massachusetts and AZ’s brand new facility in Kendall Square. About AstraZeneca At AstraZeneca, we’re dedicated to being a Great Place to Work , where you’re empowered to push the boundaries of science and unleash your entrepreneurial spirit. It’s an opportunity to make a meaningful difference to medicine, patients, and society. We foster an inclusive culture that champions diversity and collaboration, while supporting lifelong learning, growth, and development. Together, we’re pioneering the future of healthcare. We put patients at the heart of everything we do and strive to address unmet medical needs around the world. Working here means being entrepreneurial, thinking big, and collaborating to turn the seemingly impossible into reality. If you’re swift to act, confident in your leadership, willing to collaborate, and curious about what science can achieve, you could be the kind of person we’re looking for. Our innovative Cardiovascular, Renal & Metabolism (CVRM) pipeline continues to grow. To support this expansion and meet the increasing need for regulatory expertise, we’re recruiting and developing Regulatory Affairs Strategists . In this role, you’ll have the opportunity to contribute across the drug-development lifecycle and support a rich and diverse pipeline of small- and large-molecule medicines. You’ll work across disciplines in a collaborative and inclusive environment, where Regulatory Affairs is a respected voice and an equal partner in the business. Responsibilities Are you passionate about science and experienced within regulatory affairs and drug development? Do you possess strategic and operational leadership skills? Then, join us at AstraZeneca Cardiovascular, Renal or Metabolism (CVRM) regulatory affairs to get involved in the global regulatory strategies and support the research, development and commercialization of our products across the portfolio! As a Regulatory Affairs professional within AstraZeneca, you will play a key role in channeling our scientific capabilities to make a positive impact on changing patients’ lives. In Regulatory Affairs, our teams influence the development of our innovative pipeline, define the regulatory strategy for our therapeutic assets, and engage with Health Authorities to effectively inform our development programs. In this way, our Regulatory Affairs teams are transforming exciting science into valued new medicines for patients around the world. Strategic thinking is key within our function. The more experience you gain, the more accountability you will have for strategic input and leadership. The CVRM Regulatory Affairs Fellow is not only a learning and development opportunity to understand the drug development process and the Regulatory Affairs role – this role provides strategic and operational regulatory support to the development, commercialization and life cycle management of assigned products. As experience increases, the Fellow will have increasing responsibility and may serve as the regional regulatory lead for assigned programs, contributing to crafting and implementing the clinical strategy and influencing stakeholders to improve outcomes. The position is hybrid, with an expectation of at least 3 days per week in the office in Kendall Square. Qualifications Doctor of Pharmacy degree from an ACPE-accredited institution Eligible for pharmacist licensure in the State of Massachusetts Excellent oral and written communication and presentation skills Strong time management and leadership abilities Ability to work independently and take initiative on a variety of projects at both the industry site and Northeastern Ability to work as part of a team Strategic-oriented mindset with attention to detail Fluent in the use of AI tools for research and efficiency in the workplace Additional Information Applicants should submit the following application materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026: Curriculum vitae (CV), uploaded Unofficial PharmD transcripts, uploaded Cover letter, uploaded Letters of Recommendation Three formal Letters of Recommendation (LORs) must be submitted directly by the letter writers no later than November 23, 2026 . Each letter writer must include: The candidate's full name The fellowship program(s) to which the candidate is applying (e.g., Smith, John – AstraZeneca, Alnylam, GSK ) Please ask your letter writers to complete and submit the Letter of Recommendation Form along with their recommendation. Click here to access the form and share it with your letter writers. Recommendations must be submitted directly by the recommender and cannot be submitted by the applicant. Address cover letter and letters of recommendation to: J. Andrew Orr-Skirvin, PharmD, BCOP Clinical Professor, Department of Pharmacy & Health Systems Sciences Pharmaceutical Industry Fellowship Program Director 360 Huntington Ave, 140 The Fenway R218 Boston, MA 02115   Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  STEM OPT is not compatible for this position. The successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University's sponsorship for a visa.  Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Academic-Advisor_R142458": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Academic Advisor advises and supports College of Engineering undergraduate students with all aspects of course planning and registration from Orientation through completion of degree requirements at the time of degree conferral. Responsibilities include facilitating registration assistance, providing guidance on add/drop and withdrawal processes, and supporting students through major exploration. The advisor maintains accurate and up-to-date student academic records and facilitates academic support workshops to promote student success. This role also involves monitoring student academic progress and providing proactive support and outreach, as well as serving as a referral resource for students experiencing academic or personal challenges by connecting them to appropriate campus support services. The advisor additionally assists with the planning and execution of various college events and programs. The Academic Advisor serves as a departmental liaison and advisor to upper-level students, underrepresented populations, and pathway and incoming transfer students. This includes reviewing academic records to evaluate progress toward and completion of degree requirements, as well as providing interpretation of college and university policies and procedures to students. The advisor also plays a key role in supporting college and university retention initiatives through proactive advising interventions and data-informed outreach. Minimum Qualifications: Master's degree in Higher Education, or related field required. Previous experience in higher education recommended. Strong communication, organizational, and analytical skills are required. Must be able to work well as a team member with a variety of constituencies, work independently and take initiative; attention to detail is imperative. Teaching experience strongly preferred. Familiarity with Microsoft Office, and registration/advising support tools such as Salesforce, and Degree Audit programs preferred. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: Serve as academic advisor for all undergraduate students in the College of Engineering, interpreting and explaining College and University policies and procedures. Communicate degree and graduation options, program requirements, and curriculum changes, and provide support for schedule planning and registration. Follow up on progress reports and conduct academic review each term to assess student academic progress, working closely with students experiencing academic difficulty. Verify completion of degree requirements and conduct pre-graduation clearance reviews. Serve as liaison between students, department faculty, and cooperative education coordinators, and refer students to appropriate campus resources. Provide support for overall undergraduate academic advising team responsibilities, including participation in New Student and Transfer Orientation sessions, Welcome Week events, and college graduation ceremonies. Facilitate advising workshops and assist with departmental and college student events. Support the assessment of advising programs and practices, and participate in planning discussions to develop and enhance advising operations. Serve on College/University committees, support pathway student transition into the college and advising initiatives, and participate in professional development activities. Respond to inquiries from faculty, staff, students, and families Documents Required: Resume Cover Letter Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Analyst-Integrations---Mapping_R142634": {
      "title": "Senior Analyst Integrations & Mapping",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Senior Analyst - Integrations & Mapping is responsible for designing, documenting, and managing data integrations, mappings, and business rules that support the movement of information across Advancement systems. Reporting to the Director of Data Integrity & Governance, this role serves as a bridge between business and technical teams, ensuring data flows accurately, consistently, and securely between platforms. The Senior Analyst partners with Advancement Technology, Data Governance, ITS, and business stakeholders to document integration requirements, define mapping standards, troubleshoot data issues, and support the successful implementation of system enhancements. This role plays a critical part in maintaining data integrity and supporting a connected advancement technology ecosystem. Minimum & Preferred Qualifications Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree required. 5+ years of experience in systems analysis, data integration, business analysis, CRM administration, or related field. Experience documenting data mappings, business rules, and integration requirements. Strong understanding of data architecture, system integrations, and data flow concepts. Experience working with technical and non-technical stakeholders. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education advancement or nonprofit fundraising. Experience with Salesforce, integration platforms, middleware tools, or enterprise applications. Familiarity with APIs, ETL processes, data transformation, and system testing. Experience supporting enterprise technology implementations. Knowledge of data governance and information management practices. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Integration Design & Data Mapping Develop and maintain data mappings, transformation rules, and integration documentation that support the exchange of information across Advancement systems. Ensure mappings accurately reflect business requirements and data governance standards. Serve as the authoritative resource for data mappings and integration documentation. Business Analysis & Requirements Documentation Partner with stakeholders to understand business processes, identify integration requirements, and document business rules that support system functionality and data consistency. Integration Support & Troubleshooting Investigate data issues, integration failures, and system discrepancies. Coordinate with Advancement Technology and ITS to identify root causes and implement corrective actions that improve data reliability. Support migration projects and large-scale system enhancements. Data Flow Governance & Documentation Maintain documentation related to integrations, business rules, data definitions, and system dependencies. Support governance efforts by ensuring transparency and consistency across data movement processes. Project Support & Continuous Improvement Participate in technology initiatives, system enhancements, and process improvement efforts. Recommend opportunities to simplify integrations, improve efficiency, and strengthen data quality. Develop user stories, use cases and functional specifications for ITS developer to enhance integrations. Key Competencies Experience with system integrations and data mapping Strong understanding of business process design and data flows Ability to bridge technical and business requirements Strong analytical and troubleshooting skills Documentation and process management expertise Collaborative approach to problem solving and stakeholder engagement Position Type Information Technology Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 112S Expected Hiring Range: $99,010.00 - $143,567.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Charlotte-NC/Part-Time-Lecturer---Clinical-Instructor--School-of-Nursing--Charlotte--NC_R142752-1": {
      "title": "Part Time Lecturer - Clinical Instructor, School of Nursing, Charlotte, NC",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description The School of Nursing is seeking part-time faculty to teach clinical courses in the following areas: Adult Medical/Surgical, Public Community Health, Maternal Child/Women's Heath, Pediatrics, and Psychiatric/Mental Health, and Senior Practicum. Part-time instructor positions are available as determined by the department’s curricular and institutional needs. A nursing clinical instructor plays a crucial role in the education and training of nursing students. Their primary responsibility is to facilitate the clinical learning experiences of nursing students and ensure that they develop the knowledge, skills, and competencies required to become competent and safe nurses. Responsibilities include educating, evaluating, supervising, mentoring, and professional role modeling.    Requirement Requirement to teach a clinical course include a minimum of a Bachelor's Degree in Nursing, at least two years of full time equivalent experience as a registered nurse, and an active, unencumbered North Carolina RN license or multistate licensure privilege to practice in North Carolina. Previous teaching experience is highly desirable. Start dates: May, September, and January. Seeking applicants with significant career accomplishments within subject area and college teaching experience in a large, global, higher education institution. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $1395"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Director--Global-Entrepreneurship_R142786": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Global Entrepreneurship",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY Northeastern University is creating one of the world's most ambitious university entrepreneurship ecosystems. Anchored by the new 300 Mass Ave innovation hub in Boston and connected across Northeastern University's global campus network, Global Entrepreneurship brings together students, founders, researchers, investors, alumni and corporate partners to create new opportunities for innovation, venture creation and experiential learning. Reporting directly to the Vice President, Global Entrepreneurship, the Associate Director will serve as a strategic partner responsible for helping shape and deliver Northeastern's global entrepreneurship agenda. Working across strategy, partnerships, ecosystem development, innovation programs and 300 Mass Ave, the role combines strategic leadership with hands-on execution, ensuring ambitious ideas become measurable outcomes. This is a highly collaborative leadership role requiring an entrepreneurial individual who is equally comfortable developing strategy, building relationships with senior stakeholders and delivering complex projects. The successful candidate will help establish 300 Mass Ave as a globally recognized center for innovation while strengthening entrepreneurship across Northeastern's international network. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Essential Significant experience within entrepreneurship, innovation, venture creation, higher education or a related ecosystem. Experience leading complex strategic projects involving multiple stakeholders. Excellent relationship-building, communication and influencing skills. Ability to balance strategic thinking with operational delivery. Commercial awareness and an entrepreneurial mindset. Confidence engaging with senior leaders, founders, investors and partners. Willingness to travel internationally. Desirable Founder, start-up or scale-up experience. Experience working with venture capital, accelerators or innovation organizations. Experience developing international partnerships. Knowledge of experiential entrepreneurship and innovation ecosystems. Personal Attributes We are seeking an individual who is entrepreneurial, collaborative and driven by the opportunity to build something exceptional. The successful candidate will be comfortable working in a fast-paced environment, leading through influence rather than hierarchy, and turning ambitious ideas into tangible outcomes. They will possess excellent judgement, a bias for action and a genuine passion for creating opportunities that help entrepreneurs, students and partners succeed. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Strategy & Global Entrepreneurship Working closely with the Vice President, the Associate Director will lead the planning, coordination and execution of strategic initiatives that support the continued growth of entrepreneurship across Northeastern University. They will translate strategic priorities into deliverable programs, coordinate activity across multiple campuses and ensure projects are implemented effectively. Support delivery of the Global Entrepreneurship Strategy. Lead strategic projects from concept through implementation. Develop governance papers, reports, presentations and performance metrics. Coordinate cross-campus initiatives and strengthen collaboration. Identify opportunities to enhance entrepreneurship across Northeastern's global network. 300 Mass Ave Leadership The Associate Director will play a leading role in developing and growing 300 Mass Ave as Northeastern's flagship entrepreneurship hub. Working alongside the Hub Operations Administrator, they will help create an outstanding environment for founders, students, researchers, investors, alumni and corporate partners while ensuring programs and activities operate to the highest standard. Support the strategic development and operational leadership of 300 Mass Ave. Develop new entrepreneurship programs and initiatives. Coordinate founder engagement and community activity. Support international visitors, corporate partners and senior delegations. Continually enhance the experience and reputation of the Hub. Partnerships, Programs & Ecosystem Development The Associate Director will help develop relationships that expand opportunities for students, founders and the wider Northeastern community. They will work with corporations, investors, accelerators, government organizations and innovation partners to create new collaborations while leading the delivery of flagship entrepreneurship programs and events. Develop strategic partnerships across industry, government and the entrepreneurial ecosystem. Support partnership negotiations and relationship management. Deliver entrepreneurship programs, competitions and innovation initiatives. Coordinate international events and ecosystem engagement. Represent Northeastern at meetings, conferences and external events. Leadership, Operations & Communications The Associate Director will provide leadership across the Global Entrepreneurship team, ensuring projects are delivered effectively and that operational excellence underpins every activity. They will prepare strategic communications, monitor performance and help position Northeastern as one of the world's leading entrepreneurial universities. Work collaboratively across colleges and campuses to deliver shared objectives. Prepare executive reports, presentations and strategic briefings. Support marketing, communications and impact reporting. Monitor KPIs and drive continuous improvement. Act as a trusted strategic partner to the Vice President, Global Entrepreneurship. Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $76,335.00 - $107,823.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Director--Major-Gifts_R140216-1": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Major Gifts",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Associate Director, Major Gifts (ADMG) will qualify, cultivate, and solicit major gift prospects ($250K–$1M+) in a designated geographic region. Operating within the One Northeastern model, the ADMG maintains a university-wide perspective, raising support for a broad range of institutional priorities spanning individual colleges, multidisciplinary programs, and strategic initiatives. While possessing a solid understanding of Northeastern University's overall academic enterprise, the ADMG may also build deeper expertise in a college, program, or campus in order to serve as a content resource to other development colleagues and to strengthen collaborative fundraising efforts. In keeping with the One Northeastern Advancement structure, this position serves as both a front-line fundraiser and content strategist for Northeastern University in related subject areas, including engineering, computer science, science, and other disciplines, working in close partnership with the One Northeastern advancement team, faculty, and programmatic leaders. The role focuses on securing major gifts within at least two regions and developing interdisciplinary strategic opportunities for funding that elevate Northeastern's philanthropic profile and align with its priorities. This position is Boston-based and reports to an Associate Vice President of Advancement. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: Portfolio Management and Prospect Strategy Build, manage, and engage a portfolio of approximately 120 or more high-capacity major gift prospects, with an emphasis on raising gifts in the $250,000–$1M+ range, in concert with One Northeastern fundraising colleagues. Develop prospects using a geographic and subject matter overlay, working in partnership with other One Northeastern colleagues as needed. Develop and implement tailored cultivation and solicitation strategies for each prospect, leveraging programmatic leadership where appropriate. Discover new prospects and build pipeline to feed gifts at the major gift level, leveraging a data-driven mindset and sophisticated prospect management systems. Cultivation, Solicitation, and Relationship Management Cultivate, solicit, and build relationships with alumni, parents, and friends of the University. Strategize with faculty and other colleagues on next steps with prospects. Travel as needed to designated regions to build relationships with existing volunteers, prospects, and donors, and to solicit gifts in the $250K–$1M+ range. Partner with One Northeastern colleagues in athletics, gift planning, family philanthropy, leadership giving, annual giving, and alumni relations to develop and support interdisciplinary gift opportunities within the regional structure, as needed. Regional Planning and Reporting Work with regional leadership, including the Assistant Vice President for Advancement, to create a strategic development plan to advance the portfolio. Establish region-based plans for cultivating and soliciting alumni and current parents in targeted regions. Engage faculty strategically with prospects and maintain ongoing correspondence. Keep the Assistant Vice President, faculty, and appropriate University administrators apprised of funding opportunities and trends. Prepare monthly progress prospect reports for the Assistant Vice President. Data Management and Stewardship Maintain a high-quality development process for active alumni, parent, and friend prospects and donors, including creating call reports. Maintain the data integrity of the prospect portfolio using the Development Office's data management system (Ascend). Work with Advancement's Stewardship team to ensure that past, present, and future donors are appropriately recognized, including their administration and accounting. Goal Achievement Achieve annual fundraising goals and activity metrics (visits, video meetings, calls, proposals, etc.) as set by the Associate Vice President. Minimum Qualifications: Required Minimum of 5–7 years of progressive fundraising experience, with demonstrated success in securing six-figure gifts Experience managing complex projects, strategic volunteer engagement, and donor relationships requiring interdisciplinary collaboration Comfort working in an entrepreneurial environment with high levels of autonomy and responsibility Preferred Background in higher education, cultural institutions, healthcare, or other complex nonprofits Position Type Advancement Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 111S Expected Hiring Range: $87,785.00 - $123,998.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Temporary-Assistant-Research-Coordinator---Brain-Game-Center-for-Mental-Fitness-and-Well-Being_R142199-1": {
      "title": "Temporary Assistant Research Coordinator - Brain Game Center for Mental Fitness and Well-Being",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Temporary Assistant Research Coordinator will assist in studies focused on measuring and training cognitive processes in adolescents with and without ADD/ADHD (project 1) and older adults 60+ (project 2). The individual will be trained and become responsible for coordinating and administering research study activities, assisting in project planning, and ensuring that established work scope, study protocols, and regulatory requirements are followed. This position will facilitate and lead coaching sessions with participants, recruit and coordinate research assistants and study participants, serve as a principal point of contact for participant-facing operations and a principal administrative liaison for the lab, and support research procedures and data analyses related to the Brain Game Center’s mission of understanding and improving cognitive functions in individuals across the lifespan. This position requires evening and/or weekend availability to accommodate study scheduling needs given that participants are recruited nationwide. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Facilitate and lead coaching sessions with participants Recruit research participants for ongoing studies Manage and coordinate research studies and associated activities Maintain and support research data management processes Recruit, manage, and train Research Assistants (RAs) Assist with project planning and ensure compliance with study protocols and regulatory requirements Serve as a principal administrative liaison for the laboratory and as a point of contact for participant-facing operations Support research procedures and data analysis activities Perform other laboratory-related responsibilities as assigned Minimum Qualifications Excellent communication and customer service skills Coursework completed in research methods At least six months of experience administering experimental procedures involving human subjects Experience or training in clinical psychology, sports psychology, counseling, coaching, or related fields is preferred Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $19.00 per hour"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Dedham-MA/Assistant-Director--Student-Enrollment_R141356": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Student Enrollment",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Assistant Director, Student Enrollment is responsible for managing a caseload of undergraduate and graduate prospective students, applicants, and newly admitted students. This role has a primary focus on supporting the Northeastern Online portfolio of programs, including master’s degree programs and graduate certificate programs. The Assistant Director conducts follow-up communication by phone, email, or other digital engagement platforms with prospective students to ensure the highest level of service and enrollment counseling. They serve as the central point of contact for students within their assigned caseload. In addition, they provide support with inbound calls on an as-needed basis and participate in in-person and digital open houses and information sessions. The Assistant Director assists with various stages of the enrollment funnel and works with prospective students by helping them select the program that best fits their educational needs and professional goals; assisting applicants through the admissions process by counseling applicants to successfully complete their application file; and working with newly admitted students and appropriate college liaisons to guide students with their initial course registration. The individual in this position is expected to provide students with relevant and timely information about the university’s programs, admissions requirements, deadlines, financial aid and visa support, and other student-related items. The Assistant Director’s enrollment strategy includes significant call and email engagements, new student follow-ups, and virtual meetings designed to attract students in line with the priorities of Colleges and Campus Deans. This is a fully remote position. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Management of the Enrollment Cycle Provide applicants and prospective students with information and guidance on programs, admissions requirements, application directions, course format options, financial aid, registration, college services, and other enrollment activities Work with supervisor to facilitate ongoing improvements within the enrollment cycle Enrollment Counseling to Prospective Students Provide prospective students with the counseling and information necessary to help them select the appropriate program by identifying their skills, interests, and career goals Assist applicants with the admissions process, including program requirements, application timelines, and submission of all required credentials, with the goal of helping them fully complete an admissions application Assist students in the course registration process by guiding students in initial course selection and registration Execution and Follow-Up Complete designated outreach to leads generated by marketing and/or recruitment in order to meet enrollment goals Complete designated outreach to applicants and newly accepted students in order to meet enrollment goals Complete pre-application screenings for designated programs and follow up on potential issues Conduct timely application reviews in conjunction with Academic Deans and Program Directors, when applicable Confirm enrollment for students within the assigned caseload, including managing applicant options, withdrawals, deferrals, and program changes Coordinate and perform quarterly outbound call campaigns to current and prospective students Provide insight and feedback to supervisor on ways to improve enrollment management for the assigned caseload Northeastern Online-Specific Responsibilities Develop and maintain working knowledge of the full Northeastern Online program portfolio, including degree programs, graduate certificates, focus areas, start dates, and admissions pathways Advise prospective students on the program and admissions pathway that best fits their background, goals, and timeline, whether through a degree or certificate pathway and through the Fast App or Standard Application process Provide backend support as needed to assist prospective students through key steps in the Northeastern Online enrollment process Customer Service and Recruitment Support Support the overall goal and mission of the Enrollment Management Contact Center by demonstrating a positive, collaborative, and supportive attitude toward team members, students, and university departments Provide backup support for the Contact Center inbound line Support Enrollment Management Contact Center inbound calls by serving as a first-level contact for callers when additional support is necessary Answer telephone inquiries from applicants, current students, and prospective students regarding admissions, advising, course formats, registration, financial aid, and other student-related questions Resolve student questions by providing program information, accessing student record information, directing callers to appropriate website resources, or emailing appropriate forms and documents General Admissions Support Provide accurate and timely responses to emails received through shared admissions inboxes Provide visitor support coverage for admissions when needed Provide recruitment support for events within the scope of the assigned caseload and for on-campus admission events Assist with training and development of new employees Northeastern Online-Specific Responsibilities Monitor and manage Northeastern Online prospective student inquiry channels, including shared email and Short Message Service (SMS) inboxes Work in close coordination with the Enrollment Management Online team to stay current on program details, admissions messaging, and portfolio updates Support occasional virtual enrollment events hosted by the Enrollment Management Online team, including Ask Me Anything (AMA) sessions and online information sessions, serving as a knowledgeable enrollment resource for prospective students Record Management Maintain accurate and timely records of all student interactions to support accurate reporting Maintain accurate records of applicant and prospective student interactions in Customer Relationship Management (CRM) and application systems in order to track activity and produce reports Prepare reports as requested on applicant and prospective student contact activity Provide feedback on issues and interests that arise during applicant and prospective student interactions to support continuous improvement of the student experience Maintain current knowledge of all assigned programs to properly assist applicants and prospective students throughout the enrollment cycle Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor’s degree or equivalent experience Two to four years of experience in student enrollment, nontraditional college admissions, and/or sales Proven ability to work independently Excellent interpersonal, communication, and presentation skills Ability to be sensitive and responsive to the needs of diverse constituencies while managing goals Positive attitude and self-starter mentality Ability to work in a fast-paced, metric-driven environment Ability to work a flexible schedule, including some nights and weekends Ability to travel periodically, as required Preferred Experience in counseling Experience conducting call campaigns Experience delivering small and large group presentations Required Documents Resume Position Type Enrollment Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106S Expected Hiring Range: $48,610.00 - $66,838.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Community-Service---Civic-Engagement-Program---Administrative-Operations-Manager_R142767-1": {
      "title": "Community Service & Civic Engagement Program & Administrative Operations Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Community Service & Civic Engagement (CSCE) is a part of Education Innovation in the Office of the Chancellor and plays an integral role in meaningfully engaging students and staff with communities in Boston and beyond. For students, community engagement offers critical experiential learning opportunities that allow them to understand and have impact in the world. This team oversees a robust suite of community engagement programs and offers trainings, reflections, and consultations. With an asset-based lens to community engagement and a commitment to strong reciprocal community partnerships, CSCE facilitates meaningful experiences for students, staff, and community partners.   The Operations Manager leads the operational infrastructure that enables CSCE to advance its mission at scale. This position develops and implements the operational, logistical, and administrative systems that drive program delivery across the full team. The Operations Manager directs logistics for CSCE programs and events, establishes and optimizes office systems, stewards program budgets, and serves as the strategic operational resource for the team. This role reports to the Director of CSCE and partners collaboratively with all CSCE staff to build a well-functioning, efficient, and mission-aligned office. Key Responsibilities and Accountabilities Events and Program Logistics: Direct operational logistics for CSCE-wide events and programs, including travel arrangements, room reservations, catering, supply procurement, and vendor coordination. Serve as the lead operational architect for signature programs such as Alternative Breaks, Global Service Week, and Volunteer Fairs. Develop and implement replicable logistics frameworks that improve efficiency and scalability across the program portfolio Budget Stewardship and Financial Management: Lead accurate and proactive tracking of CSCE program budgets in coordination with the Associate Director and Director. Direct purchases, reimbursements, and vendor payments in alignment with institutional policies. Develop and deliver regular budget reports, identify and escalate fiscal concerns, and drive grant-related financial compliance for AmeriCorps and other funded programs. Office Management: Establish and maintain the operational systems that support the day-to-day functioning of the CSCE office, including supply management, scheduling coordination, and staff operational support. Develop onboarding frameworks for new staff and student workers to ensure consistent and efficient integration into team operations. Student Worker Supervision: Recruit, hire, and direct 2-3 part-time student workers who support operational and administrative functions. Establish clear performance expectations, provide ongoing mentorship and feedback, and invest in the professional development of student staff. Other CSCE Responsibilities: Contribute to office-wide and division-wide committees, staff meetings, trainings, retreats, and special events. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree in business administration, higher education, or related field required 3-5 years of progressive experience in office management, program operations, or administrative coordination, preferably within a higher education or nonprofit setting Demonstrated ability to develop and implement operational systems and manage competing priorities across a dynamic team environment Experience with budget stewardship and financial record-keeping. Experience with grant-related financial tracking a plus Strong interpersonal and communication skills; ability to build collaborative relationships across a team and with external partners Proficiency in Microsoft Suite; experience with Salesforce, WorkDay, and Concur preferred Demonstrated experience planning and executing large-scale events and programs Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Seattle-WA/Open-Rank-Clinical-Professor--Physician-Assistant-Program--Department-of-Medical-Sciences_R142760": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical Professor, Physician Assistant Program, Department of Medical Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Position Overview   The Department of Medical Sciences is recruiting an Open Rank Clinical Professor to serve as a Principal Faculty Member in the Physician Assistant Program on our Seattle campus. The Open Rank Clinical Professor and Principal Faculty Member is responsible for teaching in the didactic component of the Physician Assistant Program curriculum, ensuring the high educational and medical quality of the curriculum, program organization and administration, and student education and mentoring. As a member of the program’s founding faculty, this individual will have a unique opportunity to help shape the culture, curriculum, educational practices, and student experience of a developing PA program in the Pacific Northwest.   The ideal candidate will be a collaborative and creative educator who values excellent teaching, student mentorship, continuous improvement, and meaningful engagement with the regional healthcare community. The position also carries expectations for scholarship, including contributions to the advancement of PA education and clinical practice, as well as service to the program, university, and broader professional community. The Open Rank Clinical Professor will assist in maintaining accreditation standards and participating in any activities necessary to maintain Northeastern at the forefront of PA education.    The position is a 52-week, 1.0 FTE, non-tenure track faculty position.     Minimum Qualifications Minimum of a Master's degree in a field related to physician assistant practice or the education of physician assistant students Currently certified by the National Commission on Certification of Physician Assistants (NCCPA) Licensed or eligible for licensure in the state of Washington A minimum of three (3) years of clinical experience as a practicing clinician Experience in PA education (e.g., lecturer, preceptor, or core PA faculty member) Strong interpersonal, organizational, problem-solving, and critical thinking skills Strong written and oral communication skills Proficiency with standard computer applications (e.g., word processing, email, spreadsheets, databases)   Principal Faculty Responsibilities As a principal faculty member, the Open Rank Clinical Professor shares responsibility, alongside the Program Director and other principal faculty members, for the development, implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the PA program, including the following core functions: Develop, review, and revise the program's mission statement, goals, and competencies Serve on the admissions committee, including selecting applicants for admission Coordinate, teach, and instruct courses based on areas of content and clinical expertise Provide continuous development, maintenance, and evaluation of both didactic courses and clinical rotation sites Facilitate small group discussion sessions; participate in simulation, and physical diagnosis and skills labs Monitor, evaluate, and document student performance to ensure that they successfully achieve the criteria for successful completion of courses Assist in the development and implementation of remediation plans for students in the didactic and clinical years, as needed Advise and mentor students; participate in all aspects of retention and academic progression Design, implement, coordinate, and evaluate the curriculum Participate in clinical site development Participate in clinical site monitoring, including performing site visits during clinical rotations Participate in all aspects of continuous quality improvement activities and using programmatic data to support ongoing program enhancement Participate in all aspects of Program administration Perform additional assignments at the direction of the Program Director of the Physician Assistant Program   Additional Information:   This position may be structured as a four-day academic schedule to accommodate one day per week of clinical practice. Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, a statement addressing the prompt below, and at least three references. Successful faculty at Northeastern are dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering a sense of belonging among all members of the Northeastern community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the experiences, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your experience, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with a written statement addressing the following prompt: Teaching statement: Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the Bouvé College of Health Sciences, and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a cohort of graduate PA students. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by the law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: 52-week Salary range(s): Assistant Clinical Professor: $117,000.00 - $143,000.00 | Associate Clinical Professor: $134,000.00 - $154,000.00 | Clinical Professor: $154,000.00 - $198,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Research-Technician_R142650": {
      "title": "Senior Research Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Senior Research Technician will assist with research related to the work being conducted in the lab. This staff member will manage the schedules and projects of other technicians and undergraduates within the lab. They will head recruiting for all studies within the lab and create databases to organize recruiting efforts within the community. The Senior Research Technician will ensure all eligible participants are screened according to eligibility criteria and obtain medical and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) safety clearance for each participant by communicating with doctors’ offices. This technician will have knowledge of how to run all sessions and be capable of performing them independently. They will oversee data accountability and ensure data is uploaded to the appropriate databases. The Senior Research Technician will be the primary contact for all undergraduates within the lab. They will organize and collect documentation, create schedules for new members, and train all undergraduates to assist with multiple studies. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Manage and organize undergraduate training, scheduling, and duties Head all organization for recruitment efforts for multiple research studies Perform data collection and analysis Work closely with participants, graduate students, research technicians, undergraduate research assistants, and other staff members to ensure research protocols are followed Assist with ordering and organizing supplies and draft materials for presentations and publications Screen and schedule participants, including obtaining MRI safety clearance Manage Research Electronic Data Capture (REDCap) data and shared databases with other universities Oversee all safety documentation for staff, including MRI safety, cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), Collaborative Institutional Training Initiative (CITI) training, Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire (PAR-Q) documentation, and waivers Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor’s degree in a related field Strong leadership skills and the ability to train and manage fellow lab members Organizational skills to monitor recruitment numbers for multiple research studies Enthusiasm for research and a passion for learning laboratory methods Ability to work independently and cooperatively with others as part of a team Ability to accurately organize and analyze data generated from experiments Excellent oral and written communication skills Exceptional attention to detail Highly organized and dependable Skilled in the use of technology, including Microsoft Office, statistical processing software, and data collection software Excellent interpersonal, time management, and solution-oriented skills Preferred Master’s degree in a related field Required Documents Resume Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108H Expected Hiring Range: $29.00 - $40.96 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Director--Global-Engagement_R142720": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Global Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary  The Leadership Relations Team (LRT) operates as both an innovation team and a success and operations team — building new engagement models while also running the day-to-day infrastructure that keeps them working. As a result, this role requires comfort with a fast-paced, priority-shifting environment and the flexibility to move fluidly between big-picture thinking and hands-on execution as the team’s needs evolve. This role exists to grow, diversify, and activate Northeastern’s Global Volunteer Network — the volunteers, ambassadors, and leadership constituents who power Northeastern’s international engagement and giving. Reporting to the Director, Leadership Relations, this person supports the connective tissue between the University and its global volunteer base, driving: Volunteer recruitment, matching, and retention across all global networks — including Young Global Leaders (President Aoun’s appointed network of emerging global leaders), Campaign volunteer leadership, community leaders, and affinity networks Full-circle integration connecting emerging leaders, students, alumni, and Campaign volunteer leadership with one another and with key campus partners Strategic communications and marketing on behalf of volunteer programs Event planning, content generation, and signature program execution International donor participation, tied directly to volunteer engagement Integration of Campaign priorities across the Leadership Relations portfolio This position is required to perform its duties in person, on the Boston campus. Other duties as assigned.     Qualifications Bachelor’s degree with 5–7 years of experience in alumni relations, advancement, volunteer management, or a related field; experience in higher education is a plus. Experience recruiting, onboarding, or managing volunteer or leadership networks, ideally including young professional, affinity, or committee/co-chair models. Event planning experience and the ability to work independently and productively with minimal supervision, while managing several projects at once in a team environment. Detail-oriented with strong customer service and interpersonal skills, and the ability to engage professionally and diplomatically with alumni, volunteers, students, parents, faculty, staff, and external partners. Experience partnering across departments — such as career services, cooperative or experiential education, or corporate relations — to build referral pipelines is a plus. Effective coordination, organization, and written and oral communication skills, including a strong phone presence and proficiency with virtual communication platforms and online learning tools. A self-starter who thrives in a fast-paced, priority-shifting environment, is flexible to changing priorities, and is adaptable to new systems, with knowledge of and interest in international affairs and sensitivity to diverse cultures highly desirable. Comfort working with and coaching student, young alumni, and volunteer leaders across a range of career stages, and supervising junior staff and student employees.   Key Responsibilities & Accountabilitie s Build & Grow the Global Volunteer Network Recruit and diversify volunteers through direct outreach and partnership with Advancement colleagues, matching each volunteer to the network best suited to their interests and goals -- including Young Global Leaders (President Aoun’s appointed network of emerging global leaders), Campaign volunteer leadership, community leaders, and affinity networks. Build full-circle connections across the volunteer network, integrating emerging leaders, students, alumni, and Campaign volunteer leadership so that each constituency helps grow and strengthen the others. Extend the network’s reach by partnering with campus stakeholders across the global university system. Cultivate and retain volunteers through personalized, experiential offerings tied to their interests and philanthropic goals; serve as the primary liaison between Northeastern Advancement and the volunteer network. Turn Network Engagement Into Giving Manage donor appeal projects and promote international donor stories across channels. Steward international parent leads in partnership with Family Philanthropy. Lead the launch of Global Giving campaigns across all volunteer networks. Own the YGL annual giving participation ask and goal in partnership with Leadership Annual Giving, and serve as team liaison with Annual Giving and Giving Day on donor challenge gift strategy. Communicate on Behalf of the Network Develop and implement the strategic communications and marketing plan for all volunteer networks and programs. Drive communications strategy for flagship volunteer-facing programs including the Global Leadership Summit and the Intergenerational Leaders Exchange (ILE). Weave Campaign priorities and messaging into all volunteer-facing communications, in partnership with the AVP. Plan & Execute Signature Programs and Events Oversee planning and execution of signature content driven sessions integrated into Leadership Relations programming Manage a portfolio of global communities and their associated volunteer community leaders. undefined Team Management Manage a team of one full-time administrative/professional staff member and student employees including coop students; participate in hiring, orientation, and training of Leadership Relations Team staff. Provide guidance and mentorship, lead by professional example, and drive annual goal-setting and benchmarking for the team. Position Type Advancement Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Dean-of-Administration---Finance---College-of-Social-Sciences-and-Humanities_R142656": {
      "title": "Associate Dean of Administration & Finance - College of Social Sciences and Humanities",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the College of Social Sciences and Humanities The College of Social Sciences and Humanities  (CSSH) combines Northeastern University’s signature approach to experiential learning with liberal arts education, interdisciplinary research, and community engagement, both locally and globally.   The College comprises 20 academic units, including two schools, eight departments, and ten interdisciplinary programs, as well as 15 research centers and institutes . Our faculty connects liberal arts education with the challenges of our time, and fosters ethical reasoning and critical thought, with attention to the enduring significance of history, literature, and culture.  CSSH promotes inquiry with global perspectives across interdisciplinary clusters of expertise including: Resilience, Sustainability, Health, and Equity; Cultural studies, Governance, and Globalization; and Network Science, Computational Social Science, Digital Humanities, and Data Ethics. Job Summary The Associate Dean of Administration and Finance is the principal administrative and budget officer for the college and reports to the Dean. As a member of the college’s senior leadership team, the Associate Dean helps formulate the college’s strategic plan in a dynamic and fast-moving university environment. As the college’s lead administrator, the Associate Dean oversees the implementation and planning of processes, policies, and projects related to budgeting, human resources, sponsored research administration, space, and college-specific information technology. The Associate Dean advises the Dean on the development of the college’s annual revenue and expense budgets and models multi-year financial scenarios. The position works closely with other associate deans, department chairs, and staff to determine budgetary needs and plan initiatives aligned with the college’s and university’s strategic goals. The Associate Dean manages the budget and finance administration and research administration teams and leads staffing allocation decisions across the college. The position proactively identifies options to meet evolving academic and operational needs and applies sound change management practices to guide units through organizational and staffing transitions. The Associate Dean supports strategic planning by providing analytical reports on enrollment, staffing, financial, and space trends and requirements to inform resource allocation decisions. The position works closely with Human Resources on faculty and staff recruitment, retention, performance reviews, and compensation changes. In collaboration with the Associate Dean of Research, the Associate Dean oversees sponsored research administration, including all pre-award and post-award grant management, and ensures compliance with federal, state, and other sponsor regulations. The Associate Dean manages the college’s space portfolio of nearly 90,000 square feet across the university and coordinates space planning and capital projects in collaboration with university offices. The position regularly leads programmatic projects as directed by the Dean, works closely with counterparts in other colleges, and represents the Dean and the college to internal and external audiences and constituents. The Associate Dean is also responsible for providing college-level financial and space reporting to the Office of the Provost and other university-wide units. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree required, MBA preferred At least 10 years of increasingly responsible leadership experience successfully managing all administrative facets of a large, multimillion-dollar operation At least five years of managerial or supervisory experience Experience with project management, change management, and resource and staff allocation Experience using financial databases and developing reports High level of analytical skill Ability to manage multiple concurrent projects and activities Excellent communication, negotiation, and facilitation skills Commitment to a culture of professionalism with a service focus Experience in higher education preferred Key Responsibilities and Accountabilities Financial Management and Budget Administration Develop and manage the college’s annual operating budget and multi-year financial models under the university’s hybrid budget model Report quarterly budget-to-actual performance and advise the Dean on revenue and expense planning Oversee contracts and sponsored research administration, including pre-award and post-award grant management, and ensure compliance with federal, state, and sponsor regulations Collaborate with the central budget office, the Office of the Provost, and counterparts across colleges on finance and administration matters Strategic Planning and Analytics Serve as a member of the senior leadership team and help formulate and implement the college’s strategic plan Provide analytical reports on enrollment, staffing, financial, and space trends to guide resource allocation Identify opportunities to enhance the college and proactively surface challenges and alternatives to keep college and university goals on track People Operations and Management Partner with Human Resources and Faculty Affairs throughout the employee lifecycle, including recruitment, retention, morale, training, performance reviews, promotions, job transfers and changes, and compensation for faculty and staff Lead staff allocation across the college by assessing unit-level needs, evaluating options such as reallocation, restructuring, and shared services, and addressing gaps or imbalances in coverage Lead staffing and organizational changes using change management practices, including stakeholder communication, phased implementation, and impact assessment, to minimize disruption and build support Establish effective communication methods to ensure timely and accurate information is shared with faculty and staff and foster strong relationships throughout the college Directly lead, coach, and develop the budget and finance administration and research administration teams by setting objectives, providing feedback, and fostering a high-performance work environment Supervise the Director of Finance and Administration; Director of College Research Administration and Research Finance; Director of Belonging; and Director of Reporting and Financial Planning Operations, Space, and Representation Manage the college’s space portfolio of nearly 90,000 square feet across the university Coordinate space planning and capital projects with university offices Lead college-wide programmatic initiatives as directed by the Dean Represent the Dean and the college to internal and external audiences and constituents Represent the college on standing and ad hoc committees related to finance, administration, business processes, and data reporting Provide college-level financial and space reporting to the Office of the Provost and other university-wide units Applicants should include the following documents with their application: Resume/CV Cover letter Name and contact information of three references Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 115S Expected Hiring Range: $157,130.00 - $227,840.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Animal-Care-Technician_R142726": {
      "title": "Animal Care Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Animal Care Technician provides husbandry and care to laboratory animals, maintains animal inventory records and Animal Husbandry and Room Maintenance Records and performs facility sanitation in DLAM’s Animal Facilities.  This person is required to complete assigned work schedules with little to no supervision.  The technician should be able to work independently and make correct decisions in their daily routine. The position is under an alternate schedule, working Tuesday through Saturday, as well as requiring work on weekends and holidays. The position involves daily exposure to laboratory animals and their waste products. Minimum Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalency degree.  A minimum of two years of animal care experience.  ALAT certification preferred. Job Duties: The staff member must be able to demonstrate the knowledge and skills necessary to provide appropriate animal care for his/her assigned responsibilities. With little to no supervision, follow assigned schedules for husbandry, care and housing of laboratory animals.  Technician responsible for feeding, watering, and cleaning the cage of their assigned animals. Reports all animal heath or behavioral problems to the appropriate staff. Maintains animal inventory records and Animal Husbandry and Room Maintenance Records for each assigned room. Able to cover and work in all/most areas in animal facility. Reports all facility and environmental issues to the Director. Provide guidance and on the job training to new animal technicians. Provide assistance with receiving and processing incoming animals and supplies Sanitizes and maintains the animal research facility equipment. Operates cage-wash, autoclave, and other animal facility related equipment. Assembles and prepares caging supplies for use in the animal facility. Performs euthanasia on small laboratory animals. Participates in technician meetings as scheduled by supervisory staff. Participates in continuing education training programs throughout employment as requested May be trained to provide specialized technical assistance to the DLAM Veterinary Staff and investigator staff. Required to work weekend and holidays.  Occasional overtime work is also required to cover work due to absences of other animal care technicians. Required to comply and follow DLAM and NU-IACUC policies and procedures. Performs all other duties necessary for the proper functioning of the animal facility as assigned by the supervisory staff. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 105H Expected Hiring Range: $20.77 - $28.56 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142715": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description The Postdoctoral Research Associate provides scientific and operational leadership for clinical trials in the Movement Neuroscience Lab investigating neuromodulation for pain and muscle function in orthopedic conditions. The lab studies arthrogenic muscle inhibition, treating pain and muscle weakness as co-occurring features that share nervous system pathways rather than as separate problems, in populations including knee osteoarthritis, ACL injury and reconstruction, patellofemoral pain, and knee replacement. The incumbent leads day-to-day conduct of two ongoing trials on pain pathways, collects and analyzes neurophysiologic and biomechanical data, maintains regulatory compliance, and contributes to a research direction on personalization and optimization of neuromuscular electrical stimulation. The position includes support to develop additional research and pursue independent funding within the lab's scope. Responsibilities Lead day-to-day scientific and operational conduct of two clinical trials examining pain pathways and neuromodulation. Manage recruitment, screening, enrollment, retention, and protocol fidelity. Coordinate scheduling and study visits with clinical collaborators and lab personnel. Collect, process, and analyze neurophysiologic and biomechanical data, including transcranial magnetic stimulation, EMG, quantitative sensory testing, and isokinetic dynamometry. Develop and maintain analysis pipelines and ensure data quality across sessions. Prepare manuscripts, conference abstracts, and grant applications as first author and contributing author. Develop an independent line of research within the lab's scope and pursue independent funding. Maintain regulatory compliance and data integrity, including IRB submissions and amendments, reportable event documentation, consent processes, and REDCap database management. Contribute to development and testing of the lab's neuromuscular electrical stimulation platform, focused on individualization, usability, and adherence. Train and mentor doctoral and undergraduate students in data collection and analysis. Qualifications Knowledge of human neurophysiology and motor control, clinical trial methodology, and human subjects research regulation; Ability to independently plan and execute data collection with human participants, manage study data and regulatory documentation, analyze quantitative physiologic data, and communicate findings in peer-reviewed publications; Ability to work collaboratively across clinical and engineering teams. Normally acquired through a PhD in rehabilitation science, neuroscience, biomedical engineering, kinesiology, human movement science, or a closely related field, with prior research experience involving human participants and a record of first-author peer-reviewed publication. Preferred Clinical licensure or clinical training (PT/DPT, MD, DO, OT, or equivalent); Experience with non-invasive brain stimulation or neuromuscular electrical stimulation; Experience with clinical trial coordination; Background in pain neuroscience or sensitization; Proficiency with REDCap and with R, MATLAB, or Python. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Subaward-Finance-Administrator_R142678": {
      "title": "Subaward Finance Administrator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Northeastern University’s Research Enterprise Services (NU-RES) provides leadership and expertise to faculty, investigators and college administrators in support of research and sponsored programs at Northeastern University. NU -- a dynamic, innovative, and entrepreneurial university with a diverse and growing research portfolio -- seeks to hire talented, experienced administrators who are dedicated to supporting investigators secure and manage their extramurally sponsored activities. The University is committed to hiring and developing personnel who enjoy working in a high-performing culture that rewards excellence. To learn more about Northeastern’s unique academic environment and generous benefits please see: http://www.northeastern.edu/hrm/. Northeastern is a global university and NU-RES supports its team working remotely. Remote locations and work schedules are determined based on operational needs and in consultation with Team members, consistent with University policies and guidance. Reporting to the Assistant Director, Subawards, Research Administration, is the point of contact and primary resource for the faculty, department staff and subrecipients related to the management of invoices received by Northeastern University under externally sponsored programs. The Subaward Finance Administrator works in partnership with faculty, University staff and subrecipients by facilitating the coordination and flow of information in support of effective payment of invoices related to sponsored programs. Responsibilities include : Review and comprehend the payment terms under a wide range of funding sources including Federal, state, local government agencies and industry sponsors to ensure invoices are compliant; serves as primary administrative contact for faculty and researchers related to financial aspects of outgoing subawards; facilities communications with subrecipients; conducts subrecipient monitoring in accordance with Federal regulations; liaise with NU stakeholders such as Procurement, Accounts Payable and Finance. This position communicates directly with faculty, researchers and administrators to assist them with the required financial aspects and regulatory monitoring of their subawards. Provides operational support, including data management and reporting, processing and tracking incoming subcontract invoices through the Research Invoice Inbox. Work with Northeastern Faculty, Administrators and subrecipients to obtain approval for payment; updates and maintains relevant information in respective databases. Contact subcontract sites when issues arise and to respond to inquiries. P repares and submits Federal Funding Accountability and Transparency Act (FFATA) subaward reports . Collaborates with Research Finance annually to ensure compliance with Uniform Guidance requirements. As a collaborative team member, the candidate will support the other members of NU-RES, interface with colleagues across organizational units such as KRI at Northeastern University, LLC, NU-RES Research Finance, Accounts Payable, Office of General Counsel, the Procurement Office, and Research Compliance, and perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications Ability to work in a fast-paced, deadline driven environment; e.g. multiple projects, with routine interruptions and changing priorities Exceptional written and oral communication skills and the ability to facilitate group activities Ability to work with a wide range of individuals with varying sophistication and information needs Demonstrated experience utilizing critical thinking skills to proactively solve or mitigate problems, including anticipating issues, identifying solutions and providing appropriate communication to impacted parties Ability to work with minimal supervision Solid judgment and capacity to exercise discretion to resolve ambiguity and promote effective solutions. Strong commitment to constructively contribute to a customer service environment, providing professional, timely, efficient, and courteous service to the university research community. Ability to navigate through multiple systems and platforms to execute roles and responsibilities efficiently Bachelor’s degree and a minimum of 1year relevant experience in the field of research administration in an academic, industry, Federal contracting non-profit, or similar setting is required. Proficiency in Microsoft Excel and SharePoint is preferred. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities (80%) Review and comprehend the payment terms under a wide range of funding sources including Federal, state, local government agencies and industry sponsors to ensure invoices are compliant; serves as primary administrative contact for faculty and researchers related to financial aspects of outgoing subawards; facilities communications with subrecipients; conducts subrecipient monitoring in accordance with Federal regulations; liaise with NU stakeholders such as Procurement, Accounts Payable and Finance. This position communicates directly with faculty, researchers and administrators to assist them with the required financial aspects and regulatory monitoring of their subawards. Provides operational support, including data management and reporting, processing and tracking incoming subcontract invoices through the Research Invoice Inbox. Work with Northeastern Faculty, Administrators and subrecipients to obtain approval for payment; updates and maintains relevant information in respective databases. Contact subcontract sites when issues arise and to respond to inquiries. Prepares and submits Federal Funding Accountability and Transparency Act (FFATA) subaward reports. Collaborates with Research Finance annually to ensure compliance with Uniform Guidance requirements. (15%) Works with the Assistant Director, Subawards to create robust processes, procedures and best practices for management, intake of requests, and administration of subaward invoices; Identify and implement process changes that reduce risk and increase automation of various business processes, develop tools and relevant in support of NU Research Enterprise Services. (5%) Support management with ad hoc assignments as needed. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Administrative-Operations-Manager_R142668": {
      "title": "Administrative Operations Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Center for Theoretical Biological Physics The Center for Theoretical Biological Physics (CTBP) in the College of Science is one of the ten Physics Frontiers Centers established by the Physics Division (PHYS) of the National Science Foundation. CTBP is at the forefront of research and training at the biology-physics interface, focusing on fundamentals of physical genetics, interacting active elements and biological functionality, integrated living systems, and development of core methodology. About the Center for Interdisciplinary Research on Complex Systems The Center for Interdisciplinary Research on Complex Systems (CIRCS) in the College of Science fosters collaborations between researchers from different scientific and engineering disciplines who share a common interest in elucidating fundamental aspects of the structure and function of complex physical and biological systems across multiple levels of organization using a combination of quantitative state-of-the-art experimental and theoretical research tools, and enhances interdisciplinary educational training of both undergraduate and graduate students through its various activities. Job Summary Reporting to the Director of CTBP, the Administrative Operations Manager provides comprehensive operational, administrative, research administration, and financial management support to the Centers. This position plays a critical role in supporting the day-to-day operations and long-term strategic objectives of both Centers. The Administrative Operations Manager oversees research administration and finance, space allocation, visitor logistics, trainee support for undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral researchers, and external liaison activities. Responsibilities also include coordination of the National Science Foundation (NSF)-funded Physics of Living Systems Graduate Student Network, management of Center websites, and logistical oversight of CTBP’s annual undergraduate summer research program. The Administrative Operations Manager works closely with the Physics Department, the Colleges of Science and Engineering, and Northeastern University’s Offices of Research Administration and Research Finance. Key Responsibilities and Accountabilities CTBP and CIRCS Operations Management Oversee daily operations of CTBP and CIRCS, including project coordination, staff direction, office and space management, and issuance of ID badges Manage operational needs, including office supplies, shared spaces, and logistical support for meetings and special events Administer the NSF-funded CTBP Summer Undergraduate Research Program Administer the NSF-funded Physics of Living Systems Graduate Student Network, including coordination of annual meetings and workshops at domestic and international locations. Direct staff in the development and maintenance of administrative systems and workflows to improve operational efficiency Coordinate Center seminar series, including speaker invitations, scheduling, publicity, travel arrangements, and reimbursements Maintain and update Center websites, including event and announcement postings Supervise review and reconciliation of monthly, quarterly, and annual financial transactions in compliance with University policies Research Administration Lead grant proposal preparation, budgeting, and administration for National Science Foundation (NSF), National Institutes of Health (NIH), Department of Energy (DOE), National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), and private foundation proposals supporting the Directors of CTBP and CIRCS Prepare and coordinate proposal components, including curricula vitae (CVs), current and pending support documentation, and conflict of interest disclosures Partner with the Office of Research Administration on proposal submissions and award setup activities Manage incoming and outgoing subawards, including invoicing, data transfer agreements, and coordination with collaborating institutions Research Finance Coordinate with the Office of Research Finance on financial reporting and award closeout activities for the Directors of CTBP and CIRCS Serve as a resource to faculty regarding sponsor requirements and University policies related to research finance Communicate policy updates and changes as needed Oversee grant financial management activities, including budgeting, forecasting, and expenditure monitoring Manage purchasing and allocation of computing equipment Prepare purchase orders and review, reconcile, and approve expense and travel reports Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree required, Master's degree preferred Five to seven years of progressively responsible experience, preferably within an academic or research environment Demonstrated ability to prioritize and manage multiple projects simultaneously Excellent written and verbal communication skills with strong attention to detail Solid knowledge of budgeting, accounting, and fiscal policies and procedures Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, familiarity with Banner Experience supporting interdisciplinary research initiatives, including financial management, budget preparation, and resource allocation Applicants should include the following documents with their application: Resume/CV Cover letter The University is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future. Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Upward-Bound-Academic-Advisor_R142696-1": {
      "title": "Upward Bound Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity This job description is intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to this classification. It is not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties and skills required of personnel so classified. Job Summary Summary of Position:    Under the supervision of the Associate Director and leadership of the Program Director, the Upward Bound Academic Advisor is responsible for recruiting Upward Bound participants; providing academic and personal counseling; planning, implementing, and chaperoning students at workshops and trips; advising on college admission and financial aid; recruiting and training summer residential staff; completing written student evaluations. This position also provides administrative, professional, technical, and operational support through independent judgment, analytical skills, and professional or technical expertise.   Essential Job Functions:   Recruitment efforts: conducting regular oral presentations at target schools, community events, and other relevant outreach programs. Assist with interviewing potential participants and ensure all participants meet the necessary eligibility requirements as established by the U.S. Department of Education. Provide academic, career, and college admission advising to Upward Bound participants. Provide appropriate disciplinary actions when necessary. Develop an individual academic plan (IAP) for participants in a designated grade level including transcript evaluation, academic and extracurricular activity advising, and social/personal/academic intervention. Monitor and record attendance and academic performance of students and assist the Program Coordinator in the management of information in the database to ensure compliance with the Department of Education's regulations. Maintain weekly office hours at one or more of the target schools (which include Oakland High Schools and/or Richmond High Schools). Act as the liaison between the program and parents target school personnel, community representatives, and post-secondary institutions. Coordinate and facilitate academic support programs including advising, workshops, assessment, and follow-up as well as resource referral. Assist with all aspects of the planning of the Summer Residential Enrichment Program; this includes an onsite residence with students for a designated period of time. Develop, plan, and chaperone activities, events, field trips, and college tours. Dedicate ongoing attention to personal professional development and remain current with the U.S. Department of Education's regulations regarding TRiO Programs to ensure compliance with grant requirements. Perform other related duties as needed.                 Minimum Qualifications Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Experience and ability to effectively work with students with diverse academic, socioeconomic, cultural, and ethnic backgrounds. Mission-driven and passionate about programs designed to identify and provide services for individuals from disadvantaged backgrounds. Knowledge of TRiO student services database preferably Student Access Database and another office database such as Microsoft Access, Excel, Word, and PowerPoint. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to demonstrate effective teaching and academic counseling skills. Must have a valid California driver's license, safe driving record, current automobile insurance, and use of reliable transportation. Must be available to work evenings and weekends as needed, including onsite residence with students in dorms while on college tours and field trips. Must be available to maintain onsite residence with students for a designated period of time during the Summer Residential Enrichment Program. Requires passing fingerprinting and a background check. Ability to complete multiple tasks in a timely manner with minimal supervision and independent judgment. Flexibility and initiative.  Ability to take direction, prioritize, and work independently. Demonstrated experience-solving problems including conflict resolution. Demonstrated ability to work well under pressure to meet deadlines, maintaining a professional composure when dealing with common problems that occur in events and classroom settings. Education and Training: Minimum education is a Bachelor's degree, preferably in education, social work, psychology, or a related field or the equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferably experience as a teacher, counselor or program coordinator. Minimum of two years of experience working for a U.S. Department of Education TRiO Program or similar program.   Physical, Mental, and Environmental Demands :   Work involves sitting for hours at the computer, which requires attention to detail and high levels of accuracy. Long hours during overnight travel and on-call chaperoning during trips. Work includes multiple activities and services with varying deadlines and priorities.  Some lifting up to 40 lbs. Driving is required.   Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Academic Advisement of UB Students Provide academic, career, and college admission advising to Upward Bound participants. Provide appropriate disciplinary actions when necessary. Develop an individual academic plan (IAP) for participants in a designated grade level including transcript evaluation, academic and extracurricular activity advising, and social/personal/academic intervention. Perform transcript evaluations to determine student college readiness in regard to CSU and UC admission criteria. Maintain weekly office hours at one or more of the target schools (which include Oakland High Schools and/or Richmond High Schools). Administer College Seminar Sessions during UB Saturday College Act as a substitute as needed during UB Saturday College Provide financial aid counseling to UB students and families. Administrative Program Support Act as the liaison between the program and parents, target school personnel, community representatives, and post-secondary institutions. Coordinate and facilitate academic support programs including advising, workshops, assessment, and follow-up as well as resource referral. Monitor and record attendance and academic performance of students and assist the Office Manager in the management of information in the database to ensure compliance with the Department of Education's regulations. Assist with all aspects of the planning of the Summer Residential Enrichment Program; this includes an onsite residence with students for a designated period of time. Develop, plan, and chaperone activities, events, field trips, and college tours. Attend weekly staff meetings. Dedicate ongoing attention to personal professional development and remain current with the U.S. Department of Education's regulations regarding TRiO Programs to ensure compliance with grant requirements.   Program Recruitment Recruitment efforts: conducting regular oral presentations at target schools, community events, and other relevant outreach programs. Assist with interviewing potential participants and ensure all participants meet the necessary eligibility requirements as established by the U.S. Department of Education.   Other duties as assigned by Associate Director, Director, or Office Manager Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Base Pay Rate: $72,320.11"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor--Khoury-College-of-Computer-Sciences_R142434": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor- Khoury College of Computer Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Khoury College of Computer Sciences at Northeastern University has multiple tenure-track/tenured faculty positions at all ranks (Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, Full Professor), beginning academic year 2027-28 or 2028-29.  The tenure home for this position will reside in the Khoury College of Computer Sciences, with a potential joint appointment in another Northeastern College (Bouvé College of Health Sciences; College of Art, Media, and Design; College of Engineering; College of Science; College of Social Sciences and Humanities; D'More-McKim School of Business; Mills College; School of Law), to be determined in consultation with the successful candidate.  Academic rank at the Associate Professor and Full Professor levels will be commensurate with experience and qualifications reflecting a record of demonstrated teaching and scholarly excellence. We will consider candidates across all areas of computer science, whose research connects to the following sub-areas of AI: Physical AI: Designing AI systems that sense, reason and act within real-world environments. Areas of interest include machine perception and multimodal sensing, embodied and egocentric AI, world models and planning, wearable and interactive systems, robot manipulation and mobility, multi-agent systems and safe human-robot collaboration, and assistive and rehabilitation technologies for clinical, home, and community settings. Human Centered AI: Designing, building, and evaluating interactive AI systems that augment human agency, creativity, collaboration, while accounting for their societal, cognitive, and economic impacts. Areas of interest include human-AI interaction, co-creation and AI-supported work; human control over generative and agentic AI systems; human-centered explainability and decision-making; community-centered technologies as well as critical AI for the public good. We welcome a broad range of research approaches. AI and Software Engineering: Studying software engineering, programming languages, and formal methods for the robustness, performance, correctness, and security of code generated by AI or interacting with AI. Areas of interest include the coordination and validation of concurrent AI agents acting on shared code at ecosystem scale, and the engineering of AI-based systems. AI Safety, Security and Alignment: Ensuring that increasingly capable AI systems remain safe, reliable, interpretable, and aligned with human intent. Areas of interest include formal foundations of alignment, scalable AI oversight and control, interpretability, adversarial robustness, auditing and red teaming AI models, formal verification of AI systems, provenance of AI-generated content, privacy-preserving learning, agentic AI security and privacy, and cryptographic techniques for AI security.   We will begin reviewing applications as soon as they are received, starting November 15, 2026, and continuing through the academic year until the search is completed. Responsibilities: Responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in AI, Computer Science, Cybersecurity, Data Science and related areas, mentoring students and postdoctoral researchers, conducting an independent research program that attracts external funding, and service to the college, university and the profession. Qualifications: Candidates must hold a Ph.D., or foreign equivalent, in Computer Science or a related field from an accredited institution by their employment start date.  Candidates are expected to develop an independently funded research program of international caliber.  Candidates should also have demonstrated commitment to fostering diverse and inclusive environments as well as to promoting experiential learning, which are central to a Northeastern University education.  Senior candidates must have an established history of grant support or industry research, with the potential for strong leadership in their research field.  For Associate Professor appointments, at least five years of related experience is required by employment start date.  For Full Professor level appointments, at least eight years of related experience is required by employment start date. Northeastern University is a global university system.  In addition to Boston, Khoury College currently offers programs in Arlington (VA), Miami, New York, Oakland, Portland (ME), Seattle, Silicon Valley, Vancouver and London.  Positions will primarily be on the Boston, Oakland, and Arlington campuses but the candidate may indicate their interest in other campuses. Khoury College has a diverse tenured/tenure-track faculty of 113 and it offers a broad array of research and educational opportunities to students. Since 2012, the college has hired 174 outstanding tenured/tenure-track and full-time-non-tenure-track faculty members and plans to continue this strategic growth in the coming years. Faculty research spans all areas of computing and is interdisciplinary across seven of Northeastern’s colleges; 44 of the 113 tenured/tenure-track faculty have joint appointments with other academic departments, including Electrical and Computer Engineering, Art and Design, Health Sciences, Communication Sciences and Disorders, Physics, Political Science, Psychology, Philosophy and Religion, Business, Mathematics, and Law. Khoury faculty members are integral to Northeastern University’s multidisciplinary institutes including the Network Science Institute, the Cybersecurity and Privacy Institute, the Institute for Experiential Robotics, and the Institute of Experiential Artificial Intelligence. The college offers three core undergraduate degrees (Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, and Cybersecurity); eleven MS degrees (Computer Science, Health Informatics, Data Science, Cybersecurity, Game Science and Design, Artificial Intelligence, Robotics, Complex Network Analysis, Statistics, Extended and Virtual Reality, and Internet of Things), and four PhD degrees (Computer Science, Network Science, Personal Health Informatics, and Cybersecurity). Many of these are interdisciplinary degrees with other Colleges at Northeastern. Khoury College is committed to broadening participation in CS to increase diversity of thought and demographics.  For undergraduates, Khoury offers over 60 combined majors (CS+X) and works closely with the Center for Inclusive Computing, which has the mission to broaden participation in undergraduate computing programs nationally. At the graduate level Khoury is now scaling its successful Align MS in CS program for people who did not study CS as an undergraduate. This includes building a consortium of universities in the U.S. to offer similar programs. To Submit: CV, Cover Letter, Teaching and Research Statements, links to up to three most significant papers, and contact information for three References (upon request).  A link will be sent to you asking for the names of your letter writers shortly after you submit your application. Research statement: Please describe the focus of your research, including the problems you have identified, the funding you have received to support the work (if applicable), the results you have obtained, and the products of these efforts.  Also, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing at Northeastern.  Teaching statement: Please summarize your past instructional and/or mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching at Northeastern (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a diverse cohort of undergraduate and graduate students.  Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $131,000.00 - $163,000.00 | Associate Professor: $161,000.00 - $193,000.00 | Professor: $176,000.00 - $255,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Patient-Service-Specialist_R142034": {
      "title": "Patient Service Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY Reporting to the Patient Care and Operations Manager, the Patient Service Specialist performs an array of administrative duties using specific knowledge of medical terminology and hospital, clinic, or laboratory procedures. Duties will include reception, registration, and scheduling of patients; answering a high volume of phone calls; working with clinical providers regarding patient requests, concerns, and services in person and/or by means of an electronic messaging system; data entry; and other health center-related correspondence. This is a 40-hour-a-week position that includes one late night a week. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Professional, diplomatic, reliable, personable, and consistent customer service skills.  Proficiency in using Windows-based personal computer workstations, sufficient to demonstrate proficiency in several computer-based applications after training. This includes EClinical Works (ECW), Five9, and other software. Ability to process phone and in-person inquiries in a fast-paced environment. Ability to provide clear, consistent, and accurate detailed information regarding University Health and Counseling Services.  Must work exceptionally well with students identifying as BIPOC (Black, Indigenous, and People of Color), LGBTQ students, international students, and other under-represented students and communities on campus. Knowledge and skills required for this position are often obtained with at least two years of related office experience, preferably in a university/college health center or an applicable customer service setting and a high school diploma or GED .   KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Reception, registration, and scheduling of patients both in-person and over the telephone. Data entry: Review student immunization records, verify compliance, and enter information into the electronic medical record. Review and respond to correspondence. Interview patients to complete documents, case histories, and forms such as intake and insurance forms. Compiles and records medical charts, reports, and correspondence using a computer and various software programs. Transmits correspondence and medical records by mail, email, or fax. Communicate with clinical providers regarding patient requests, concerns, and services in person and/or by means of an electronic messaging system. Correspondence: Manage and respond to questions/requests received via email, process requests for release of medical records, etc. Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106H Expected Hiring Range: $23.37 - $32.14 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-Associate-Professor-Professor--Open-Rank---Physical-AI--Robotics---Autonomous-Systems--Communication-Sciences-and-Disorders_R142674": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor (Open Rank), Physical AI, Robotics & Autonomous Systems, Communication Sciences and Disorders",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Northeastern: Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Boston; Arlington; Charlotte; New York City; Miami; London; Portland, Maine; Oakland; San Francisco; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. The Bouvé College of Health Sciences has over 250 faculty members, with approximately 2,000 undergraduate and 2,400 graduate students. It is the leading national model for education and research in the health, psychosocial and biomedical sciences and supports the University’s mission of educating students for a life of fulfillment and accomplishment and creating and translating knowledge to meet global and societal needs. The Department of Communication Sciences & Disorders at Northeastern University offers minors, a Bachelor of Science, and a Master of Science in Speech-Language Pathology (MS-SLP). The MS-SLP program is offered on the Boston and Charlotte campuses and is a 60-credit ASHA accredited degree program. About the Opportunity Bouvé College of Health Sciences invites applications for open rank tenure-track or tenure-on-entry positions with a possibility of a joint appointment in the College of Engineering or the College of Arts, Media, and Design (CAMD) or the College of Engineering (COE) beginning in academic year 2027-2028 or 2028-2029. This interdisciplinary hire is part of our hiring theme in Physical AI, Robotics & Autonomous Systems. The successful candidate will develop and apply semi-autonomous and autonomous physical AI systems for modeling, monitoring, and collaborating with humans in health and healthcare contexts, spanning digital twins to embodied health technologies within translational and implementation science. Within Communication Sciences and Disorders (CSD), this translates into building the physical AI systems that sense, model, and act on the human body and communication behavior — for example, voice-controlled assistive technology, mobile health, virtual reality, socially assistive robotics, wearable biosensors, or digital twins — developed through user needs research, concept development, and usability/use-related risk evaluation for regulated health products. Candidates whose work sits at the intersection of CSD and engineering, robotics, industrial design, or computer science are strongly encouraged to apply regardless of their disciplinary training. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Belonging, the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to further develop and support a thriving faculty. Responsibilities Responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, conducting an independent and externally funded research program, and participating in departmental, college, and university service. Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Qualifications PhD, EdD, ScD in Communication Sciences and Disorders, Rehabilitation Sciences, Biomedical Engineering, Robotics, Computer Science, Electrical Engineering, Neuroscience, Computational Psycholinguistics, Human-Centered Design, Industrial Design, Human Factors Engineering, or broadly related fields by the employment start date. Expertise in physical AI, robotics, or embodied/wearable sensing systems applied to communication or rehabilitation sciences, evidenced by publications, funded research, developed technologies, design portfolios, usability/human factors validation studies, or regulatory submissions. Appointments at the Associate Professor rank require a minimum of 5 years of related experience. Appointments at the Full Professor rank require a minimum of 10 years of related experience. Additional Information: Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, patents, designs or other developed work, statements addressing the prompts below, and names and contact information for at least three professional references from whom letters of reference can be solicited as needed. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community.  Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts:   Research statement (no longer than 3 pages): Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, the funding you have received to support the work (if applicable), the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Highlight any cross-disciplinary research efforts you have undertaken. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students and/or with the external community.   Finally, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing in the Bouvé College of Health Sciences, any cross-disciplinary and collaborative aspects, and the strategies you will take to address each direction. Teaching statement (1 page): Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, including studio-based or project-based methods where relevant, your plans/goals for teaching in the Bouvé College of Health Sciences (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring global student populations. Leadership statement (1 page): Please describe your past experiences and activities and/or your future plans detailing  your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by the law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $99,000.00 - $120,000.00 | Associate Professor: $117,000.00 - $139,000.00 | Professor: $182,000.00 - $214,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Vice-President-of-Strategic-Enrollment-Initiatives_R142675": {
      "title": "Assistant Vice President of Strategic Enrollment Initiatives",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: Northeastern University's Division of Enrollment Management seeks a dynamic, strategic, and driven leader to serve as Assistant Vice President of Strategic Enrollment Initiatives. Reporting directly to the Chief Enrollment Officer and Head of Global Expansion, this senior role serves as a key architect of the Division's strategic execution, responsible for ensuring that the Division's most important initiatives move from vision to reality.   This is a high-impact leadership position for someone who combines strategic acumen with the interpersonal credibility to hold people accountable, build authentic relationships, and drive results across a complex, matrixed organization. The Assistant Vice President is a trusted ally, a driver of accountability, and a steward of commitments, unwavering in their dedication to moving the Division's most important work forward.   The ideal candidate is an experienced enrollment professional and emerging executive leader who pushes through obstacles, inspires confidence, and brings out the best in the teams and individuals around them.  Alongside this strategic leadership, the AVP oversees a high-performing team of Senior Directors and Directors who manage the Division's best-in-class enrollment technology systems, while also maintaining responsibility for the unit's budget and the contracts that support the Division's operations. Required Experience  8–10 years of progressive leadership experience in enrollment management, higher education administration, or a closely related field Demonstrated success leading complex, cross-functional initiatives in a large, dynamic institution Proven ability to hold colleagues and teams accountable while maintaining strong, trust-based relationships Exceptional interpersonal and communication skills — written, verbal, and executive presence A track record of pushing through obstacles and delivering results, even under pressure or in ambiguity Experience managing and developing direct reports Preferred Experience Master's degree in Higher Education or Higher Education Administration Experience working closely with or within a senior enrollment or university leadership team Familiarity with enrollment strategy, including undergraduate and/or graduate recruitment, retention, financial aid, and student success Experience at a large research university or similarly complex institution Familiarity with Northeastern University's mission, culture, and enrollment landscape Position Type Enrollment Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 115S Expected Hiring Range: $157,130.00 - $227,840.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Miami-FL/Director-of-Partnerships-and-Strategic-Growth_R140611": {
      "title": "Director of Partnerships and Strategic Growth",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and a world leader in experiential learning. The same commitment to connecting with the world drives our use-inspired research enterprise. The university offers a comprehensive range of undergraduate and graduate programs leading to degrees through nine colleges and schools, up to the doctorate. Our campuses in Arlington, VA, Charlotte, N.C., San Francisco, Seattle, London, Toronto, and Vancouver are platforms for undergraduate and graduate learning and collaborative research. Summary Reporting to the Managing Director of the Southeast Network Corridor, the Director of Partnerships and Strategic Growth will serve as a senior member of the Northeastern Miami Campus team responsible for the strategy and implementation of innovative initiatives and partnerships that advance the university’s mission in the Miami region and across the Southeast Corridor. This role carries an initial emphasis on health and the healthcare sector, reflecting Miami’s position as a dynamic hub for health innovation, life sciences, and a robust bilingual, internationally connected healthcare workforce. As the campus’s program portfolio diversifies, this role will expand to cultivate partnerships in other sectors aligned with institutional priorities, including entrepreneurship and innovation ecosystems. The Director of Partnerships and Strategic Growth plays a lead role in driving Southeast Corridor activities and programs in alignment with both regional market needs and Northeastern’s strategic goals. This position works closely with Miami Campus leadership, the Bouvé College of Health Sciences (including the Assistant Dean for Clinical Affairs and Partnerships), and other colleges and university stakeholders as needed. The Director leverages institutional expertise to build, expand, and nurture strategic partnerships with industry, philanthropic, governmental, and community stakeholders, while also contributing to enrollment pipeline development and thought leadership that elevates the Miami Campus’s regional profile. Qualifications • Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Education Administration, Healthcare Administration, or a relevant field required; advanced degree strongly preferred. • Minimum 8–10 years of relevant experience in business development, partnership management, or healthcare administration. • Direct healthcare or health sciences experience preferred; familiarity with the South Florida healthcare landscape is a significant asset. • Bilingual proficiency in English and Spanish preferred, reflecting Miami’s market and community demographics. • Superior skills in corporate outreach, business development, and relationship management, with a demonstrated track record of converting prospects into active, sustained partnerships. • Mission-driven, with a passion for broadening participation in health sciences, workforce pipelines, and healthcare innovation. • Ability to develop and lead strategic initiatives from concept through completion; excellent management, organizational, and communication skills. • High degree of self-motivation and self-direction, with a proven track record of collaborative success in matrixed environments. • Organizational tact and diplomacy; ability to build trusted relationships with external partners, health system leaders, community stakeholders, and internal academic administrators including Deans, Associate Deans, and Program Directors. • Experience tracking partnership activity and reporting outcomes against defined goals and metrics. • Demonstrated ability to serve as an organizational ambassador, representing campus and Corridor strategic interests with credibility and professionalism. • Commitment to belonging, equity, and broadening access to health sciences education. • Flexibility for regional travel and availability for occasional extended-hour events Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Responsibility % of Time: 30% Community Engagement Engage with healthcare leaders, providers, and Northeastern alumni to raise awareness of Northeastern’s and Bouvé’s mission, centers of expertise in education and workforce development, and capabilities in scientific and clinical research. Cultivate relationships with community-focused organizations, non-profits, and civic institutions to create mutually beneficial partnerships that advance local health equity and workforce initiatives. Represent the Miami Campus in regional health and innovation forums, conferences, and professional networks. Work with Northeastern college and network representatives to advance community-based collaborations across the global university network. Industry Relationship Growth Responsibility % of Time: 35% Develop and manage an active portfolio of health sector partnerships in consultation with Bouvé College, Miami Campus leadership, and university stakeholders. Identify, cultivate, and convert new companies and organizations to active partnerships with the Miami Campus annually, with measurable goals tied to enrollment pipeline, experiential learning placements, and program visibility. Attend and present at healthcare industry conferences, health and life sciences events, health innovation summits, and relevant community forums. Operationalize partner engagement through high-value events, meetings, and programming aligned with partner business objectives. Report regularly to Campus leadership on pipeline status, conversion strategies, and active partnership performance. Relationship, Research & Operational Management Responsibility % of Time: 35% Maintain a current and comprehensive inventory of all existing partnerships and relationships. Fully utilize university-wide platforms (CRM and reporting tools) to track all partnership engagement and inform future relationship development strategy. Provide periodic internal and external reports and updates to key stakeholders. Collaborate with marketing to increase regional and Bouvé College visibility, promote academic programs, and enhance institutional reputation in the South Florida market. Remain current on trends and emerging needs in healthcare and health sciences to position the Miami Campus as a leader in addressing industry needs. Work internally with academic programs, clinical placement offices, co-op operations, and enrollment teams to identify and expand student experiential learning opportunities and support enrollment pipeline goals. Collaborate with the philanthropy development team to steward corporate and foundation relationships. Partner with all campus, college, and recruitment stakeholders to organize and execute thought-leadership events and partnership programming that drive brand recognition and enrollment. Comments This position is based on the Northeastern Miami Campus and reports to the Managing Director of the Southeast Network Corridor. The ideal candidate brings deep familiarity with the South Florida health ecosystem, strong bilingual capabilities, and the entrepreneurial orientation to thrive in a growing, dynamic campus environment. Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 113S Expected Hiring Range: $102,480.00 - $148,593.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor---College-of-Engineering---Electrical-and-Computer-Engineering---Embodied--wearable---assistive-AI-Physical---Boston_R142638": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor – College of Engineering - Electrical and Computer Engineering – Embodied, wearable & assistive AI Physical - Boston",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Arlington; Boston; Charlotte, North Carolina; London; Miami; New York City; Oakland; Portland, Maine; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. Northeastern University’s College of Engineering is a globally recognized leader in engineering education and research, distinguished by its interdisciplinary culture, strong industry partnerships, and commitment to translating fundamental discoveries into real-world impact. Located in Boston and supported by a global network of campuses and partners, the College brings together researchers across computer engineering, electrical engineering, mechanical engineering, civil engineering, robotics, artificial intelligence, materials, biotechnology, and other emerging fields. The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at Northeastern University is a dynamic and rapidly growing academic community at the forefront of research and education in electrical engineering, computer engineering, and emerging computing technologies. The department brings together faculty with expertise spanning computer architecture and systems, artificial intelligence and machine learning, robotics, embedded and cyber-physical systems, communications and networking, signal processing, semiconductor and VLSI design, quantum computing, and emerging hardware technologies. Located in Boston, within one of the world’s leading technology and innovation ecosystems, Northeastern ECE offers exceptional opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across engineering, computer science, robotics, materials science, and the physical sciences. The department’s research is strongly connected to real-world applications through partnerships with industry, government, and leading research institutions, while Northeastern’s distinctive experiential-learning and cooperative education programs provide students with opportunities to connect classroom learning with cutting-edge engineering practice.   About the Opportunity: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Northeastern University is seeking outstanding candidates for Tenure-Track Faculty at the Assistant/Associate/Full Professor levels to strengthen the interdisciplinary thematic area of physical AI, embodied, wearable and assistive AI, described more broadly below.  Candidates with expertise in AI, machine learning, multi-modal sensing and learning, computer vision, embodied AI, human-centric AI, explainable and interpretable AI, trustworthy AI, AI on edge devices, physical intelligence, neuro-symbolic AI, AI reasoning are examples of areas that fit this theme.     The university-wide Physical AI, Robotics & Autonomous Systems main theme brings together researchers working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, robotics, and the physical world to build systems that sense, reason, and act within real-world environments. This theme spans intelligent infrastructure and adaptive construction and manufacturing systems using digital twins and multi-agent controls; embodied and wearable AI systems that bridge HCI/design with rehabilitation and safe human-robot collaboration in clinical, home, community, and informal settings designed to support and empower the people they work alongside; automated self-driving laboratories that pair hardware instrumentation with active-learning machine learning to accelerate chemistry, materials, and biomanufacturing experimentation; AI-mediated telepresence enabling immersive, distance-spanning connection for shared environments to support creative collaboration; and telemedicine and physical AI systems for health that use autonomous modeling, monitoring, and digital-twin technologies to support translational and implementation health science. Spanning Boston and Charlotte, the theme draws on cross-college collaboration among COE (College of Engineering), CAMD (College of Arts, Media and Design), COS (College of Science), Khoury College of Computer Sciences, and Bouvé College of Health Sciences, together with Northeastern’s interdisciplinary ecosystem.  The theme is further strengthened by Northeastern's recently launched Physical AI Research (PAIR) Initiative, which advances the foundational science of Physical AI and fosters collaborations across strategic application domains, and by the Institute for Experiential Robotics (IER). Appointment will be primarily or fully in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) within Northeastern University’s College of Engineering, with opportunities for joint cross-college appointments with the Khoury College of Computer Sciences, the College of Arts, Media and Design, or the Bouvé College of Health Sciences. The position will be based on Northeastern University’s Boston campus. Candidates with interest in joint appointments with other departments or colleges should indicate their intent in their application materials in the cover letter. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Belonging, the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to further develop and support a thriving faculty. Responsibilities: Responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, lead an independent and externally funded research program, and participate in departmental, college, and university service. Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Qualifications:   A Ph.D., or foreign equivalent, in Electrical & Computer Engineering, Computer Science, Math, Physics, or closely related fields  by the employment start date. Senior-level candidates at the Associate and Full Professor levels should have a demonstrated record of developing transformative solutions to global challenges, sustaining a research program with a strong emphasis on interdisciplinary and translational research, teaching both undergraduate and graduate classes, and being an active, recognized leader nationally and internationally in their discipline. Applicants at the Associate level should have at least 4 years of academic or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research) and at the Full Professor level at least 8 years or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research). How to apply: Interested candidates should apply using the Northeastern University website. Applications must include the following, for further information on the statements, please see below. Cover letter – may identify a department of interest for a joint appointment within the College of Engineering or other colleges Full curriculum vita that includes a list of publications Statement of Research Interests, Objectives & Vision Statement of Teaching Interests & Philosophy Leadership statement Names and contact information for at least three professional references from whom letters of reference can be solicited as needed Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. The review is expected to start immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community.  Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts:   Research statement (no longer than 5 pages): Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, the funding you have received to support the work (if applicable), the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Highlight any cross-disciplinary research efforts you have undertaken. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students and/or with the external community.   Finally, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing in the College of Engineering, any cross-disciplinary and collaborative aspects, and the strategies you will take to address each direction. Teaching statement (1 page): Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the College of Engineering (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring global student populations.   Leadership statement (1 page): Please describe your past experiences and activities and/or your future plans detailing your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice. Northeastern University is an equal opportunity employer, seeking to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff.  Northeastern values and celebrates diversity in all its forms and strives to foster an inclusive culture built on respect that affirms inter-group relations and builds cohesion. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $118,000.00 - $137,000.00 | Associate Professor: $126,000.00 - $168,000.00 | Professor: $150,000.00 - $251,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Staff-Nurse_R142649": {
      "title": "Staff Nurse",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Registered Nurse (RN) works under the supervision of the Nursing Supervisor and/or Medical Director and functions as an active, collaborative member of Northeastern University Health and Counseling Services (UHCS). The RN provides nursing services for preventative health, urgent care, triage, immunization compliance, follow-up care, health education, and clinical support for undergraduate and graduate students.   This is a full-time position, 35 hours per week, typically scheduled over five days. Hours include one weekday evening.    Primary Responsibilities    Provides telephone and walk-in triage for students with urgent and routine health concerns, using nursing assessments, established protocols, standing orders, and clinical judgment to determine appropriate next steps.   Prepares students for clinical visits, completes check-in clinical support tasks, obtains and documents vital signs and other ordered parameters, and performs point-of-care and CLIA-waived testing as directed.   Administers immunizations, medications, and therapeutic injections in accordance with approved protocols and supports Massachusetts immunization compliance workflows, including documentation and database review as assigned.   Assist clinicians with procedures and treatments, including EKGs, ear lavage, suture removal, crutch fitting, specimen collection, and other clinical tasks within RN scope and UHCS protocols.   Provides follow-up outreach after clinic visits, hospital transfers, emergency care, specialist visits, or abnormal test results, and supports scheduling or care coordination needs.   Provides student-centered health education, self-care guidance, discharge instruction, procedure preparation and aftercare education, and support for navigating the health care system.   Maintains a safe and well-prepared clinical environment through exam room turnover, supply stocking, inventory monitoring, vaccine storage monitoring, equipment checks, and adherence to infection control standards.   Provides clinical support, guidance, and collaboration with Medical Assistants, LPNs, RNs, providers, and administrative colleagues to promote safe, efficient, inclusive, and coordinated student care.   Participates in quality assurance activities, staff development, peer review, team meetings, student outreach activities, and development of educational materials for students and colleagues.   Qualifications Current, unrestricted Massachusetts Registered Nurse license required.   Current Basic Life Support certification required.   Bachelor of Science in Nursing preferred; equivalent RN experience may be considered.   Minimum of three years of nursing experience preferred, ideally in ambulatory care, college health, urgent care, triage, primary care, pediatrics, adolescent/young adult health, or a fast-paced student-facing environment.   Strong knowledge of Massachusetts immunization compliance requirements preferred.   Experience with electronic health records, web-based systems, and Microsoft Office applications required.   Demonstrated ability to work inclusively and collaboratively with domestic and international students and with a diverse interdisciplinary team.   Demonstrated ability to perform safely while managing sequential and simultaneous responsibilities in a busy outpatient clinical setting.   Strong written and verbal communication skills, sound clinical judgment, professionalism, punctuality, and a proactive approach to work responsibilities required.   Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $76,335.00 - $107,823.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor---College-of-Engineering---Civil-and-Environmental-Engineering---Critical-Infrastructure-Resilience-in-the-Coastal-Environment---Boston_R142637": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - College of Engineering - Civil and Environmental Engineering - Critical Infrastructure Resilience in the Coastal Environment - Boston",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Arlington; Boston; Charlotte, London; Miami; New York City; Oakland; Portland, Maine; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—leads to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. About CEE and COE at Northeastern University:  The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at Northeastern University has a faculty of more than 40 members (several with joint appointments with other colleges) pursuing use-inspired research in structures, urban coastal systems, urban transportation and mobility, construction management, air quality, wind energy, water quality, environmental health, resilience, sustainability and climate change, life cycle assessment, green infrastructure, urban informatics, and robotics. CEE sits within Northeastern's  College of Engineering (COE) , a leader in experiential education and interdisciplinary research focused on innovating for global impact, anchored by Northeastern's signature cooperative education program and R1 research rating. The college enrolls more than 10,000 students pursuing BS, MS, and PhD degrees and includes more than 450 faculty. COE leads four university-wide research institutes while partnering in ten more and brings together researchers from a global network of campuses and partners. About the Opportunity: Northeastern University seeks multiple faculty hires in the broad theme of Critical Infrastructure Resilience and Cyber-AI Security . This theme focuses on protecting and strengthening the physical and digital systems that underpin modern society. The theme spans (1) hardware and critical infrastructure resilience (HCIR), leveraging AI, digital twins, and sensing to build secure and adaptive infrastructure; (2) cyber-AI security and safety, which addresses securing embodied AI/robotics, critical infrastructure systems, critical industrial control systems, security infrastructures and cryptographic techniques for AI systems; and (3) advanced manufacturing for future semiconductor materials and energy storage systems, aligned with CHIPS Act priorities in battery and energy-storage research. Anchored in Boston, the theme builds on cross-college collaboration among COE (College of Engineering), Khoury College of Computer Sciences, Mills (Mills College at Northeastern), CSSH (College of Social Sciences and Humanities), CAMD (College of Arts, Media and Design), COS (College of Science), and Law (School of Law), together with Northeastern's interdisciplinary ecosystem. It builds on the Kostas Research Institute (KRI), CHEST (Center for Hardware and Embedded Systems Security and Trust), SENTRY (Soft Target Engineering to Neutralize the Threat Reality), DIBI (Defense Industrial Base Institute), and the Center for Advanced Materials for Resiliency (CAMR), connecting cryptography, AI, physical infrastructure resilience, cyber-AI security, and advanced manufacturing to safeguard the systems the nation depends on against natural disasters and both conventional and emerging (including quantum-era) threats. As part of this strategic initiative, coordinated across the university under sub-theme #1 (HCIR), the Civil and Environmental Engineering Department seeks faculty candidates for tenured/tenure-track appointments at the assistant, associate, or full professor ranks. We seek candidates whose research leverages physics-informed AI, digital twins, and/or sensing to enable secure, adaptive, and resilient critical infrastructure in coastal environments. Candidates should apply and develop novel experimental and/or computational methods that advance Coastal System Engineering. Academic rank at the Associate Professor and Full Professor levels will be commensurate with experience and qualifications reflecting a record of demonstrated teaching and scholarly excellence. Primary academic appointments will be in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) within Northeastern University’s College of Engineering, with opportunities for joint cross-college appointments with the  Khoury College of Computer Sciences, the College of Social Sciences and Humanities, or the College of Arts, Media and Design. The position will be based primarily on Northeastern University’s Boston campus. Candidates with interest in joint appointments with other departments or colleges should indicate their intent in their application materials, including the cover letter. We seek applicants with expertise in coastal system engineering related to (1) AI-Powered Digital Twins for Continuous Coastal Infrastructure Risk and Resilience Assessment, (2) Autonomous and Robotic Sensing Networks for Coastal Ocean Monitoring and Early Detection of Coastal Infrastructure Failure, (3) AI-Enabled Modeling and Monitoring of Coastal Infrastructure Stability Under Extreme Loading, Scour, and Erosion ,  (4) Physics-AI Digital Twins for Compound Coastal Hazards, or (5) Self-Learning Digital Twins for Climate Adaptation and Critical Infrastructure Management. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Belonging, the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and training to further develop and support a thriving faculty. We encourage applicants from a wide range of backgrounds, including academia, government, and industry. Candidates should be committed to fostering diverse and inclusive environments as well as promoting experiential learning, which are central to Northeastern University education. Responsibilities: Tenured/tenure-track faculty in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering will be responsible for: teaching courses at the graduate and undergraduate level; developing vigorous research programs that attract external funding; advising, mentoring, and overseeing students and post-doctoral associates in their area of research; and participating in service to the department, college, university, and discipline. Candidates are encouraged to collaborate with other departments and colleges across the university on interdisciplinary research. Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Qualifications: This tenured/tenure-track faculty position requires a Ph.D., or foreign equivalent, in civil engineering, coastal and ocean engineering, or a related field by the employment start date. Applicants at the Associate level should have at least 4 years of academic or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research) and at the Full Professor level at least 8 years or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research).   How to apply: Interested candidates should apply using the Northeastern University website. Applications must include the following: 1. Cover letter 2. Full curriculum vita 3. Statement of Research Interests, Objectives, & Vision 4. Statement of Teaching Interests & Philosophy 5. Statement of Leadership 6. Names and contact information for at least three professional references   Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, statements addressing the prompts below, and names and contact information for at least three professional references from whom letters of reference can be solicited as needed. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. The review is expected to start immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community.  Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts: Statement of Research Interests, Objectives, & Vision (No longer than 3 pages): Please briefly describe the current focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, funding you have received to support the work, results you have discovered, and products of these efforts. Please include details on research you have undertaken with students, with the external community. Finally, outline the research directions you foresee pursuing on the ~5-year future time frame in the College of Engineering, any cross-disciplinary and collaborative aspects, and the strategies you will take to address each direction. Statement of Teaching Interests & Philosophy (1 page): Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a diverse cohort of undergraduate and graduate students. Statement of Leadership (1 page): Please describe your past experiences and activities and/or your future plans detailing your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice. Northeastern University is an equal opportunity employer, seeking to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. Northeastern values and celebrates diversity in all its forms and strives to foster an inclusive culture built on respect that affirms inter-group relations and builds cohesion.   All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $106,000.00 - $121,000.00 | Associate Professor: $123,000.00 - $141,000.00 | Professor: $138,000.00 - $207,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Open-Rank-Tenure-Track-faculty--School-of-Nursing-2_R142658": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenure Track faculty, School of Nursing-2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The School of Nursing within the Bouvé College of Health Sciences at Northeastern University invites applications for multiple full-time open rank tenure-track/ tenured positions at the Assistant, Associate, and Full Professor levels with an anticipated start date in 2027.   We are seeking highly motivated candidates with research programs that broadly align with our mission of advancing health for everyone, from the individual to society, across the healthspan. The research areas for these positions are open to candidates studying topics across the many diverse disciplines in nursing research within the broad umbrella of oncology. We welcome candidates with interdisciplinary expertise who is capable of leading and collaborating with researchers at Bouvé and across Northeastern University.  Junior researchers are welcome to apply, as are senior faculty with internationally recognized research programs and strong funding.  Candidates will have demonstrated teaching excellence and a commitment to supporting a global faculty, staff, and student environment. The candidates should also have a PhD in Nursing or a related field and be eligible for RN licensure in Massachusetts. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to develop further and support a thriving faculty.   Responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, conducting an independent and externally funded research program, and participating in departmental, college, and university service. Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations.    Qualifications   Terminal degree (PhD or equivalent) in Nursing or related field by employment start date. Evidence of an established area of research and scholarship, teaching excellence, and demonstrated excellence and commitment to supporting a global faculty, staff, and student environment. Candidates with Nursing degrees must be eligible for MA licensure.   All applicants should have a strong record of scholarly accomplishment that demonstrates the promise (for Assistant Professor-level candidates) and ability to build a strong research program. Academic rank at the Associate or Full Professor level will be commensurate with experience and qualifications reflecting a record of substantial research productivity and an established history of grant support, teaching, and academic service.  Appointments at the Full Professor rank require at least 10 years of teaching and research experience, and Associate-level appointments require a minimum of 5 years of teaching and research experience.  Full and Associate-level appointments require conducting independent research that attracts external funding. Research excellence is the top-most priority.   Additional Information   Interested candidates should apply here with a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and statements addressing the prompts below. Applications will be reviewed as they are received.   Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belong in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your experience, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts:   Research statement: Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, the funding you have received to support the work (if applicable), the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Highlight any cross-disciplinary research efforts you have undertaken. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students and/or with the external community.   Finally, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing in the Bouvé College of Health Sciences, any cross-disciplinary and collaborative aspects, and the strategies you will take to address each direction. Teaching statement: Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the Bouvé College of Health Sciences (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring global student populations. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $108,000.00 - $130,000.00 | Associate Professor: $122,000.00 - $137,000.00 | Professor: $148,000.00 - $244,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
