    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Department-of-Sociology-and-Anthropology_R142600-1": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Department of Sociology and Anthropology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.        Responsibilities: The Department of Sociology and Anthropology in the College of Social Sciences and Humanities seeks applicants to teach undergraduate courses in one or more of the following fields: Introduction to Sociology, Introduction to Cultural Anthropology, Drugs and Society, Sociology of Mental Health Gender; Poverty; Urban Sociology; Race and Ethnicity; Social Movements; Sociology of the Family; Disability; Work and Organizations; Anthropology of Latin America; Anthropology of Native North America, and other undergraduate courses. Seeking instructors able to teach on ground on the Boston campus or online asynchronously.  Applicants will be expected to deliver lectures, develop assignments, grade assignments, and hold appropriate office hours.   Positions are available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and departmental need. Qualifications:  M.A. required, Ph.D. or A.B.D. preferred. Preference will be given to persons experienced in teaching large and diverse groups of students. Documents to Submit: Cover letter CV Statement of teaching philosophy Sample syllabi Results of student evaluations Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit $2,085."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Adjunct-Faculty---Department-of-History---Oakland--CA_R142595-1": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Department of History - Oakland, CA",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe.   Responsibilities: Teach European, World, or American history survey courses. Teach upper level undergraduate or graduate courses. Start dates in September, January, May and July.   Positions are available contingent upon enrollment, funding and departmental needs.   Qualifications: M.A. required, Ph.D. preferred for European, World and American History Survey.    Documents to Submit: CV, and Cover Letter required. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,484.48."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/CNC-Machinist_R139161": {
      "title": "CNC Machinist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The CNC Machinist supports teaching, research, and student project activities within the Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering (MIE) by providing high-quality machining and fabrication services. This position is responsible for fabricating metal and polymer components using both manual and CNC/NC machine tools in support of Capstone design teams, student activity groups, research faculty, and instructional needs. The role requires the application of industry-standard machining practices to ensure that all parts meet required quality, tolerance, and performance specifications. In addition to fabrication, the CNC Machinist & Programmer maintains machining equipment, coordinates equipment acquisition, supervises student workers, and provides technical guidance and mentoring to students on manufacturability and part design. This position plays a critical role in supporting hands-on learning, research productivity, and shop operations within the department. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Experience & Education 3–5 years of machining experience (or equivalent combination of education and experience) strongly preferred Perform all standard machinist functions Manufacture components using manual and/or automated machining tools Read and interpret CAD plans/files Provide design advice when needed Manage multiple machining jobs under tight deadlines Maintain manual and computer-aided machining equipment CAM/CAD software programming MS Office Suite proficiency (Excel, Outlook, etc.) Ability to mentor and supervise undergraduate and graduate students in machining best practices Support student project work and research by machining parts as needed Maintain shop safety standards Additional Requirements Flexibility to work occasional Saturdays (preferred) KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Machining, Programming & Fabrication Operations — 50% Fabricate metal and polymer components using manual and CNC/NC machine tools (lathes, mills, grinders, drill presses, etc.) Set up, program, operate, and adjust CNC/NC machines for accurate, efficient production per design specifications Apply industry-standard practices to meet dimensional, functional, tolerance, and surface finish requirements Interpret CAD drawings and translate designs into manufacturable components using appropriate methods Support fabrication for Capstone design teams, student groups, research faculty, and instructional projects Equipment Maintenance, Shop Operations & Procurement Support — 30% Maintain machining and shop equipment for safe, reliable, continuous operation Perform scheduled preventive maintenance, calibration, and troubleshooting of manual and CNC equipment Order shop supplies, tooling, materials, and consumables; manage inventory tracking and stock Coordinate acquisition of new equipment by obtaining quotes, advising on technical specs, and supporting purchasing decisions Collaborate with departmental staff and other Northeastern University units on machining and fabrication needs Student Supervision, Training & Technical Consultation — 20% Supervise and support work-study students; ensure proper equipment use and adherence to shop procedures Train student operators on manual and CNC equipment, workflows, and best practices Consult with Capstone directors, faculty, and students on machining projects and feasibility Mentor students on part design, manufacturability, tolerancing, material selection, and machining best practices Provide technical guidance to enhance student project outcomes and research productivity Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108H Expected Hiring Range: $29.00 - $40.96 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Lab-Manager_R139187": {
      "title": "Lab Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: The Electronic Lab Manager supports the Mechanical and Industrial Engineering (MIE) Department by ensuring the safe, effective, and reliable operation of electronic teaching and research laboratories. This fully in-person position provides hands-on technical expertise in electronics assembly, testing, troubleshooting, and instrumentation; supports undergraduate laboratory courses and Capstone design projects; enforces laboratory safety and compliance; and collaborates closely with faculty, staff, and students to maintain high-quality instructional and research environments. Because the role involves direct interaction with laboratory equipment, real-time troubleshooting, safety oversight, and on-site student and faculty support, all duties are performed on campus. The position requires a strong commitment to laboratory safety, technical accuracy, student mentorship, and cross-lab coordination in a fast-paced, hands-on academic environment. Minimum Qualifications A minimum of 10 years of experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience, is required Vocational training or technical certification in electrical controls or a similar area is preferred Programming experience including Arduino, Raspberry Pi, and some circuit design and sensor selection Proficiency with the MS Office Suite (Word, Excel, Outlook, and PowerPoint) Ability to train, instruct, and mentor students and teaching assistants Ability to fabricate samples for mechanical and electrical testing Knowledge of mechanical and electrical testing equipment Knowledge of power supplies and their safe operation Ability to work and coordinate with relevant offices including Environmental Health and Safety (EHS), Facilities, and Risk Management Must have flexibility to work occasional evenings or Saturdays Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities 1. Electronic Laboratory Operations & Technical Support Ensure electronic equipment, instrumentation, and experimental systems are properly assembled, configured, tested, and maintained to support teaching and research activities. Work safely with, in, and around electrical and electronic equipment Assemble, install, and repair equipment or components requiring wiring, soldering, and electrical connections Perform technical tasks and tests, including configuring, operating, and adjusting electronic test equipment Record, document, and report test data in support of teaching laboratory needs Conduct circuit troubleshooting on electronic devices and experimental systems Troubleshoot, modify, and adjust electronic and experimental circuitry prototypes, resolving practical and technical issues Follow specific instructions for the assembly, construction, servicing, and repair of lab instruments and equipment 2. Student Support, Instructional Assistance & Capstone Oversight (20%) Support student learning and project execution through hands-on assistance, mentoring, and oversight in electronic-based coursework and design projects. Support and, when needed, oversee student work in MEIE Capstone Design courses, particularly in the construction and testing of electronic assemblies Provide direct assistance to students by: Ensuring safety oversight for electrical and electronic projects Advising and mentoring students during project work Managing and loaning sensors, data acquisition systems, and electronic equipment Advising on sensor selection and system integration Troubleshooting electronic and instrumentation issues encountered during projects Instructing and assisting students in designing and troubleshooting sensor-based systems (e.g., Arduino-based platforms) 3. Teaching Laboratory Support, Safety & Curriculum Development (20%) Ensure undergraduate teaching laboratories operate safely, effectively, and in alignment with curricular goals. Support undergraduate laboratory classes by: Preparing samples and lab setups Solving technical and equipment-related problems Training and supervising teaching assistants Assist faculty in the development of new laboratory experiments and instructional activities Coordinate new equipment acquisition, installation, and commissioning for teaching labs Order laboratory supplies and maintain appropriate inventory Ensure safe laboratory environments and practices, including compliance with departmental and university safety standards 4. Faculty Research & Shared Laboratory Support Provide technical electronics support to faculty research labs and shared laboratory facilities as needed. Solve electronics and equipment issues in faculty research laboratories Advise researchers on sensor selection, data acquisition systems, and instrumentation challenges Maintain and service shared laboratory equipment used for research Train graduate researchers on the proper and safe use of shared electronic equipment and systems Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director--Leadership-Giving_R140474": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Leadership Giving",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Reporting directly to the Executive Director, Development, the Assistant Director, Leadership Giving will join the newly formed Leadership Giving team with the goal of advancing the leadership giving program at Northeastern University.  The Assistant Director, Leadership Giving will work in close collaboration with University Advancement and college and unit staff to: Identify and build relationships with prospective donors who have the potential to give at the $10,000 - $100,000 level Develop strategies for cultivation of these leadership level donors and prospects Prepare for and make solicitations to these leadership level donors and prospects Determine the trajectory of these leadership level donors and prospects. By working collaboratively across the university, the Assistant Director, Leadership Giving will play an active role in the success of the leadership giving program in terms of both development activity and philanthropic giving to all colleges, units, and programs. The Assistant Director, Leadership Giving will execute the strategy established by the Executive Director, Development to ensure the success and growth of the leadership giving program. The Assistant Director, Leadership Giving will work to engage mid-level donors ($10,000 - $100,000) who exhibit considerable promise (capacity and inclination) to elevate to the major gift level to ensure greater long-term success for the major gift and principal gift programs. The Assistant Director will hold face-to-face and virtual meetings with donors/prospects as needed to move the constituent closer to a leadership level gift. The Assistant Director will quickly but thoughtfully make decisions on the trajectory of the donor/prospects based upon the leadership giving activities, conversations, and research. The Assistant Director will be a highly effective collaborator and partner who is able to work across both University Advancement and the greater university to ensure the fundraising success of the program. They will acquire and articulate an understanding of Northeastern University and its various colleges and units to be able to have informed and educated conversations with donors/prospects. The Assistant Director, Leadership Giving will manage a portfolio of a select group of major gift level alumni, parents, and friends, with a focus on raising gifts at the leadership giving level. The Assistant Director will close gifts and cultivate qualified donors/prospects for larger gifts. Minimum Qualifications The university is seeking a highly entrepreneurial, energetic individual who has 1-3 years in a development-related environment Strong interpersonal skills necessary for building both internal and external relationships with donors/prospects, deans, faculty, staff, and colleagues. Collaborative in approach Able to take direction Flexibility to adapt as the leadership giving program evolves Excellent verbal and written communication skills Ability to travel as necessary Bachelor’s degree is required Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Leadership Giving donor/prospect activities – (75%) Build and manage a portfolio of prospective donor/prospects at the leadership level who exhibit considerable promise for evolving to major gift level capacity over time Identify, qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward this portfolio of donor/prospects to strategically enhance their giving potential and likelihood Work to connect with donors/prospects at the leadership giving level who exhibit the potential to give at the major and principal giving levels Achieve a high level of engagement and financial support from these constituents Work across University Advancement and the greater university to build relationships that will enhance active solicitations of leadership giving donor/prospects to help move these prospects to higher giving levels Meet and exceed established metrics and benchmarks Utilize Advancement data management system, Salesforce, to log activity, create call and progress reports and maintain data integrity of leadership giving donors and prospects Execute an effective stewardship plan for this portfolio of donors/prospects Collaborate with University Advancement colleagues and staff along with college colleagues and staff to enhance cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship as necessary Acquire and articulate an understanding of Northeastern University’s various colleges and units to be able to have informed and educated conversations with donors and prospects – (15%) This understanding and knowledge will come from conversations with stakeholders in University Advancement and across the Northeastern University ecosystem Frontline fundraising learning and experience – (10%) Learn, experience, and enhance appropriate strategies to grow and improve as a frontline fundraising, including but not limited to: Telephone call strategies and best practices Email strategies and best practices Face-to-face meeting and execution Follow-up timing and cadence Portfolio management Salesforce usage to enhance fundraising Collaboration within and across Advancement Position Type Advancement Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Dublin-Ireland/Assistant-Associate-Full-Professor--Dublin-Innovation-Institute_R142041": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Dublin Innovation Institute",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary:   Northeastern University invites applications for two open-rank   faculty   positions   which include a period of appointment at the university’s   Dublin Innovation Institute   (DII ) .      This is a full-time position with a two-part appointment structure   in which t he successful applicant will be required to s pend up   to 4 years performing research at   DII   before transitioning to a tenured or tenure-track role   (commensurate with experience and qualifications)   at   Northeastern’s   campus in Boston, Massachusetts, USA .     While at DII, successful applicants will support   the work of   Northeastern   University ’s Institute for Information, the Internet & Democracy   to build   a transatlantic research network and   research   agenda that contribute to the understanding of systemic risks posed by   digital and information technologies to democratic societies.   We   therefore   seek scholars whose research advances our understanding of how people   acquire , process, and share information in democratic societies and/or in the service of democratic norms .     We welcome applicants from a wide variety of social, scientific, and computational disciplines.       Dublin Innovation Institute:   DII   serves as a bridge between Northeastern University’s global network and the European research ecosystem, creating opportunities for innovative collaboration throughout the European Union and North America .     One of   DII’s areas of focus is   build ing   a trans-Atlantic research community around the themes of information, the internet, and contemporary democracy .        About Northeastern University:   Established in 1898, Northeastern University stands as a leader in global experiential learning and interdisciplinary research, addressing international issues in health, security, and sustainability. Externally funded research programs and graduate Ph.D. education programs at Northeastern University have been executed for almost 80 years. Transforming into a global research university over the last two decades, Northeastern has experienced remarkable expansion through strategic collaborations with businesses, government entities, and non-profit organizations.   This surge in partnerships has propelled the university's research initiatives and enriched experiential learning avenues for students   across all academic levels. Through a focus on, and substantial investment in, impactful, interdisciplinary research, Northeastern University is fostering innovation in emerging fields. This approach recognizes the need for inventive configurations to   comprehend   intricate systems and effectively translate foundational discoveries into practical, real-world applications. Currently, Northeastern is the fifth-highest funded private research university without a medical school, a list that includes MIT,   CalTech , Princeton, and Carnegie Mellon. The University has also been a leading generator of spin-out companies, a testament to its orientation to use-inspired research and faculty and professional researchers strongly engaged with the world outside academia.     Position Overview:   We welcome applications from exceptional scholars from all sectors of the community who employ computational social science approaches to study information ecosystems and their implications for democratic governance. The successful candidate will contribute to our growing interdisciplinary community of scholars examining the social, political, and technological dimensions of information flows in contemporary democracies.    The successful candidate will initially be based   at   DII ,   where   the focus   will   be   exclusively   on research , helping DII and Northeastern University’s Institute for Information, the Internet & Democracy build a world-class transatlantic research network . After the   initial   Dublin-based phase, expected to last   up to four years, the appointment will transition to a tenured or tenure-track faculty role at   Northeastern’s   Boston , Massachusetts   campus , with responsibilities that include teachin g, research, and service .     A ppointment at a   differen t US campus may   be pos sible   depending   on   the   candi date ’s   college and   department   of appo intment .     The   candidate’s   academic home and   faculty   appointment will be   determined   in accordance with   the candidate’s discipline, experience, qualification, and applicable university policies and processes.    It is a precondition of employment that the applicant will   have or   successfully obtain the right to live and work in the Republic of Ireland   for the duration of their employment at DII   and have   the   appropriate work authorization for the US portion of th e role .     DII and the university will sponsor eligible candidates for the necessary work permit(s) subject to local immigration requirements.     Research Focus:   The ideal candidate's research will address questions at the intersection of information acquisition and democratic processes. Of particular interest with respect to the position is a research portfolio focused on potential harms and/or approaches to mitigating harms related to digital information technologies. Demonstrated interest and engagement in translational research that informs public policy would be a plus. Topics of interest include, but are not limited to:     • How AI is affecting the ways in which people get civic and political information   • Information exposure and political behavior in digital environments   • Social media dynamics and democratic participation   • Misinformation, disinformation, and their effects on public opinion   • News consumption patterns and media fragmentation   • Computational approaches to studying political communication   • Algorithmic curation and its democratic implications   • Digital platforms and civic engagement   • Information networks and political polarization   • Media effects on electoral processes and accountability   • Public opinion formation in information-rich environments     Qualifications:   • Ph.D. in Political Science, Communication, Computer Science, Information Science, Computational Social   Science   or related fields   in   hand by the employment start date is   required .     Documents to Submit:   Applicants should submit a cover letter that addresses their interest in and qualifications for the position, a curriculum vitae, a research statement, a teaching statement, sample syllabi, evidence of teaching effectiveness with complete sets of recent student evaluations and the names and contact information for at least three individuals who can each provide a letter of recommendation.     Closing date:   November 1, 2026     Please address applications by end of the day from anywhere in the world no later than   November 1, 2026 , and any questions to David Lazer, Search Committee Chair, at d.lazer@northeastern.edu.     Benefits:   Employee benefits associated with the   DII appointment will be   communicated to candidates   during the   interview   process .    Benefits associated with the Boston -based   phase of the   appointment are summarized below.   This search   does not require   assistance   from recruitment agencies. Any applications made by recruitment agencies will be returned.   Pay Ranges:   Assistant Professor:    $110,000 - $139,000   Associate Professor:    $127,000 - $162,000   Full Professor:    $156,000 - $208,000 Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $110,000 - $139,000 USD | Associate Professor: $127,000 - $162,000 USD | Full Professor: $156,000 - $208,000 USD With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Associate-Professor--Full-Professor_R142556": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/ Associate Professor/ Full Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its ten tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe. Successful faculty in the College of Social Sciences and Humanities will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence. Strong candidates for this position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and the School of Law at Northeastern University seek to fill an open-rank faculty position at the tenured/tenure-track Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Full Professor level in the area of Artificial Intelligence Policy, Regulation, and Governance, broadly construed to include how human-centered AI systems can be used, governed, and designed responsibly as well as how they impact politics and society.  A comparative or international perspective is desirable.  The tenure home will be in the Department of Political Science or School of Public Policy and Urban Affairs, with a joint appointment possible in the School of Law.  Policy-relevant Economists may also be considered.  This position will be at the Boston campus. This is a full time, benefits-eligible position with an expected start date of August 16, 2027. Responsibilities: The person filling this position will teach foundational courses in AI governance, comparative politics or international relations, and/or AI policy and law, advanced courses in their area of expertise, and contribute to joint curriculum and research initiatives between the two colleges. Their research will be on topics that have relevance to the design and use of AI across different regional and global contexts. Qualifications: JD and/or Ph.D. in Political Science, Public Policy, Economics, or a related field in hand by the employment start date is required. The successful candidate will have a strong scholarly record or research program on issues related to AI policy and impact, a demonstrated commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching, and be prepared to work with global student populations.  Candidates seeking appointment at the Associate level should already hold a position at the rank of Associate, or have a comparable level of at least 5 years of related experience by employment start date. Candidates seeking appointment at the Full Professor level should already hold a position at the rank of Full Professor or have a comparable level of at least eight years of related experience by employment start date. Documents to Submit: Applicants should submit a cover letter that addresses their interest in and qualifications for the position, a curriculum vitae, a teaching statement, a research statement, evidence of teaching effectiveness (including sample syllabi and teaching evaluations), and the names and contact information for at least three individuals who can provide a letter of recommendation.  The research statement should address the research topics/questions you have worked on in the past, key findings you have discovered, key products (or outputs) of these efforts, funding you have received, as well as research plans for the future. Please also highlight cross-disciplinary research efforts you have undertaken, research done with students or community partners, and/or public facing scholarship. The teaching statement should summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, and what you would like to teach at Northeastern. Please highlight any pedagogical innovations, experiential learning activities, or strategies for teaching a global student population. Review of applications will begin on October 31, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Please address questions to Mai’a K. Davis Cross, Search Committee Chair, at m.cross@northeastern.edu Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $94,000.00 - $122,000.00 | Associate Professor: $111,000.00 - $131,000.00 | Professor: $140,000.00 - $230,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Student-Services---Data-Manager_R142584": {
      "title": "Student Services & Data Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Student Services and Data Manager will provide support to the College Assistant Dean, Co-op leadership, and faculty Co-op Coordinators in the day-to-day workflow and effective operation of the co-op program, assist Co-op Coordinators with projects to prepare students , and guide co-op employer partners through the hiring process .  R esponsible for manag ing data reporting and administrative support for a growing fast-paced team. The individual in this position will have responsibilities in the areas of complex data projects, technology initiatives, program processes, and training and development team members.  Additionally, they will be the interface between colleagues, students, employer partners, and leadership to ensure the success of the co-op program. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities 1) Data and Operations Support  Compile and analyze data/information and develop advanced data reports to evaluate student hiring, events, etc.   Manage work-study programs; effectively delegate projects and ensure completion to meet deadlines.   Coordinate efforts to uphold the completeness and integrity of data in various co-op systems .   S erve as liaison with central co-op technology team.   Maintain relevant student and co-op databases including Banner, SalesForce , and Symplicity .   Develop and administer surveys, update/manage content on co-op websites and LinkedIn group(s).   Develop communication materials for students and employers .   2) Student & Employer Support  Assist with preparation of co-op class materials; provide support for Blackboard course administration and course enrollment activities.   Review and comment on student guided inquiries, updating Co-op Coordinators; review status of responses and send reminders as needed.   Review employer evaluations and student self-assessments of the student co-op work experience.   Lead and/or assist with organization of co-op related events and on-campus employer interviews.   Conduct industry research on companies and opportunities that align with the college's job development efforts.   3) Complex Project Support & Senior Level Responsibilities  Complete complex data-related projects for both the individual’s direct team as well as the Departmental Co-op group in support of College and University goals and initiatives .   Lead new computer/data/software initiatives and/or transitions ( Symplicity , Navigate, etc ).   Develop and organize training manuals and educational materials for both Student Services and Data Manager and Co-op Faculty.   Document processes and procedures to ensure seamless continuity of program operations during periods of transition .   Collaborate with S tudent Services and Data Manager and Co-op Faculty direct supervisors to provide coaching and development, as needed .   Liaise regularly with Central IS/Co-op IS teams and the Registrar’s office on behalf of the college and disseminate information .   Oversee the development of business processes to ensure efficient and effective workflows of Student Services and Data Manager tasks .   Minimum Qualifications Must demonstrate strong organizational and time management skills, including attention to detail and the ability to manage multiple projects and complete tasks for several different constituents . Problem solving and troubleshooting, with a high level of proficiency with technology including Excel (complex formulas and logic). Basic knowledge of SQL is a plus. Excellent verbal and written communication skills are expected .  P roven success working both independently and as a member of a team in a fast-paced and diverse environment is preferred.   Knowledge and skills are normally acquired through the completion of a Bachelor's degree, with interest in pursuing an advanced degree program related to data science or data analytics preferred. A minimum of three years of relevant professional experience in an academic or business environment. Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Assistant-Director--Business---Auxiliary-Operations_R142583": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Business & Auxiliary Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Assistant Director, Business & Auxiliary Operations serves as a key operational leader and proxy for the Director across the auxiliary enterprise portfolio, with direct supervisory ownership of the Mail/Copy Center and its staff. Innovation and outstanding service are central to this leader’s focus, with an emphasis on building programs that create an inclusive campus environment for students, staff, administrators, faculty, and guests while promoting excellence in every experience. The Assistant Director supports the Director in delivering high-quality, customer-centric outcomes across dining, bookstore, mail and print production, laundry, vending, and additional auxiliary enterprises. This role stewards operational budgets with integrity, advances the Division’s values and principles for both contracted and non-contracted staff, and serves as a thought-partner to the Director and the broader Oakland campus community. The Assistant Director must be agile in supporting multiple revenue streams while successfully balancing student, faculty, and staff experiences with contract obligations and service delivery. Minimum Qualifications BA/BS required with a minimum of four years’ experience managing business or auxiliary operations, including direct supervision of a team, in hospitality, higher education, or a comparable industry; or a combination of education and experience that is commensurate. Financial acumen with an ability to understand and track key performance indicators, manage budgets, and support variance analysis. Technologically savvy and able to leverage data to modernize and gain efficiencies across business units. Strong communication skills with demonstrated ability to build collaboration with students, staff, faculty, and administrators. Adept at leading diverse work teams with emphasis on diversity, equity, and inclusion. Proven skills in operations, project management, team development, and contract management. Strong organizational, interpersonal, and conflict resolution skills with strict attention to detail and a customer/student-centric orientation. Preferred Qualifications Experience working with diverse groups in an educational or other non-profit organization. Demonstrated ability to work with faculty, students, and staff. Self-motivated, goal-oriented, strategic thinker, and able to work both independently and as part of a team. Experience in project management is highly desirable. Strong computer skills in Windows and Mac platforms. Experience using and supporting Workday or similar ERP software. Key Responsibilities Management & Operations Directly supervises the Mail & Copy Center team, including three full-time employees and seasonal/temporary workers. Provides tactical, day-to-day operational leadership for parcel and mail receiving and processing, ensuring regulatory compliance (city/state/federal), service excellence, and customer-centric outcomes. Develops and maintains standard operating procedures, leads safety training, and serves as the Auxiliary point of contact for emergency response preparedness. Serves as the Director's operational proxy for Dining, supporting vendor relationships, service delivery, and customer experience in alignment with the Director's oversight and strategic direction. Facilitates smooth operations across the full auxiliary portfolio — including contracted laundry, vending, and parking permits — aligning enrollment and admissions patterns with service planning. Administrative Manages administrative operations across the auxiliary portfolio in support of the Director, including purchasing and procurement of goods and services, data analysis, revenue and expense tracking, and maintenance of standard operating procedures. Leverages technology and data to identify efficiencies and modernize business unit operations. Communicates operational status accurately to the Director and partners with Finance, HR, and divisional stakeholders to ensure adherence to University policy and industry best practices. Applies problem-solving skills and sound judgment in decision-making; manages project calendars, deadlines, and deliverables. Policy & Compliance Supports the Director in implementing and upholding university-wide policies across Division Services, reviewing operational practices for relevance, consistency, and alignment with institutional standards. Coordinates policy updates and contributes to compliance through internal audits, reporting functions, and engagement with University partners including Finance and HR. Promotes sustainability efforts and participates in University-wide initiatives to advance broader institutional goals. Budget Supports the Director in annual revenue forecasting, budget development, and ongoing financial management across auxiliary operations. Organizes and analyzes productivity and budgets against OKRs, tracking variance and surfacing budget variances as required. Contributes to fiscal stewardship by developing realistic sales estimates, supporting contract management, and identifying creative approaches to improving operational efficiencies. Other Duties as Assigned Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $74,640.00 - $105,427.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Benefits-Administrator-Analyst_R140928": {
      "title": "Senior Benefits Specialist - Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Senior Benefits Specialist/Analyst is a subject-matter expert responsible for the day-to-day administration, analysis, and continuous improvement of the university's health and welfare and retirement benefit programs. This role serves as an escalation point for complex benefits and retirement inquiries, partners closely with vendors and carriers to ensure accurate plan administration, and delivers analytics that inform program design, compliance, and cost management decisions. The role requires a highly organized, detail-oriented professional with exceptional customer service instincts who thrives in a fast-paced, high-volume environment and is comfortable balancing competing priorities across multiple stakeholders. This position reports to the Associate Director, Benefits, with a dotted-line relationship to the Manager, Benefits on matters related to day-to-day benefits operations, retirement administration coordination, and team-level service delivery. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Finance, or a related field, or equivalent combination of education and experience. 10+ years of progressive experience in benefits administration, retirement plan administration, or total rewards analytics, ideally within a large, complex organization. Demonstrated expertise administering health and welfare plans (medical, dental, vision, life, disability) and retirement/403(b)/401(k) programs, including eligibility, enrollment, and vendor file feeds. Strong analytical skills with proven ability to build reports, dashboards, and data analyses (advanced Excel required; experience with Workday or similar HRIS strongly preferred). Exceptional customer service orientation with a track record of building trust and rapport with employees, retirees, and leadership; skilled at translating complex benefits concepts into clear, accessible guidance. Working knowledge of applicable regulations, including ERISA, HIPAA, ACA, COBRA, and IRS retirement plan rules. Proven ability to manage multiple concurrent priorities, deadlines, and stakeholders in a fast-paced environment, with sound judgment and discretion when handling sensitive employee data. Excellent verbal and written communication skills, including experience developing employee-facing communications and job aids. Experience in higher education or another academic/nonprofit setting is a plus. CEBS, CBP, PHR, or SPHR certification preferred. Key Responsibilities & Areas (KRAs) 1. Benefits Administration & Employee/Retiree Support Serve as a senior point of contact for escalated benefits inquiries from employees and retirees, resolving complex eligibility, enrollment, billing, and claims issues. Administer day-to-day health and welfare plan operations, including new hire enrollment, qualifying life events, open enrollment support, and COBRA coordination. Partner with carriers and vendors to troubleshoot data discrepancies, file feed errors, and billing reconciliation. Deliver consistently high-quality customer service, modeling a proactive, empathetic approach to employee engagement. 2. Retirement Program Administration Administer the university's retirement program(s), including enrollment, contribution changes, loans/withdrawals coordination, and vendor data feed oversight. Support annual retirement plan compliance activities (e.g., nondiscrimination testing coordination, Form 5500 support, plan audits). Serve as the primary liaison with retirement plan recordkeepers and investment providers on day-to-day service issues. Assist employees with retirement planning questions and coordinate financial wellness education sessions. 3. Analytics & Reporting Build and maintain recurring and ad hoc reports and dashboards on benefits utilization, retirement participation, and plan costs to support decision-making. Analyze plan performance data to identify trends, anomalies, and opportunities for cost savings or process improvement. Support the Associate Director and Manager, Benefits with data and analysis for RFPs, renewals, and executive-level presentations. 4. Process Improvement & Compliance Identify and implement process improvements to streamline benefits and retirement administration workflows. Maintain accurate documentation of standard operating procedures and job aids. Ensure administration practices remain compliant with applicable federal and state regulations. 5. Team Collaboration & Special Projects Collaborate cross-functionally with HRBPs, Payroll, and HR Operations on benefits-related matters. Support special projects, including open enrollment, vendor transitions, and system implementations, as assigned. Financial Measures This position does not have budget or financial approval authority. The role supports cost analysis and vendor invoice reconciliation activities that inform benefits and retirement program budget decisions. Supervision This position does not have direct reports. The Senior Benefits Administrator/Analyst may provide functional guidance and mentorship to Benefits Coordinators or junior team members on complex cases. Working Conditions Standard office environment with occasional flexibility for hybrid/remote work per university policy. Requires the ability to manage high transaction volumes and shifting priorities, particularly during open enrollment and other peak periods.   Position Type Human Resources Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Infrastructure-Project-Manager_R142560-1": {
      "title": "Senior Infrastructure Project Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Summary The Division of Information Technology Services (ITS) is seeking a highly experienced and motivated individual to join our team as an Senior Infrastructure Project Manager . This role is responsible for driving the successful delivery of large-scale, complex IT projects and programs that support the university’s strategic priorities. The Senior Project Manager will lead cross-functional teams, manage diverse stakeholder expectations, and ensure that all initiatives are delivered within scope, schedule, and budget—while aligning to business value and user needs. The ideal candidate will be a self-starter with a proven track record of managing complex strategic IT initiatives in dynamic environments. This role requires a strategic thinker who is also hands-on, with strong leadership and influencing skills. This is a hybrid position that requires on-site presence in the Boston office 3 days per week . *A pplicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The University is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future Minimum Qualifications Education Bachelor’s or Master’s degree in Computer Science, Information Systems, Business Administration, or a related field. Project management training/certification preferred (e.g., PMP, Agile, PRINCE2) Experience 10–15 years of overall IT experience, including 8-10 years of progressive IT project management experience, including managing large, complex, cross-functional IT initiatives in matrixed organizations. Demonstrated experience in delivering enterprise-level technology projects, such as CRM, ERP, infrastructure, data integration, or cloud-based systems. Minimum of  7-9 years of experience engaging with business units, technical teams, and executive stakeholders to translate business needs into actionable project plans. Proven ability to lead diverse project teams, set direction, communicate vision, and influence outcomes without direct authority. Experienced in navigating organizational change, managing competing priorities, and driving projects forward in ambiguous or evolving environments. A strong history of using data-driven insights to guide project planning and risk management.   PMO Knowledge and Skills Expert in project management methodologies, including Waterfall, Agile, and hybrid approaches. Proficient with project management tools such as MS Project, Smartsheet, ServiceNow, Confluence and, Jira. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to make sound decisions under pressure. Excellent verbal and written communication skills with experience presenting to senior leadership. Demonstrated ability to build relationships, foster collaboration, and resolve conflicts across diverse stakeholder groups. Deep understanding of business processes and IT alignment, with the ability to deliver measurable value. Skilled in resource planning and budgeting for large-scale projects. Self-motivated, organized, and capable of working independently in fast-paced environments. Good understanding of current and emerging technologies and how other enterprises are employing them to drive digital business. Strong knowledge and understanding of business needs, with the ability to establish and maintain a high level of customer trust and confidence. Infrastructure Knowledge and Skills Progressive work experience in Audio Visual (AV) & Network infrastructure; hands-on experience. Excellent problem solving and decision-making skills. Familiarity with blueprints, construction documentation, and AutoCad Knowledge of cabling structures including fiber optic, copper, and coaxial. Working knowledge of audio-visual (AV) systems, including AV upgrades and installations for classrooms, conference rooms, and event spaces, and their integration with campus IT infrastructure. Familiarity with low voltage cabling standards and practices, including structured cabling, cable pathways/tray systems, terminations, and testing and certification. Understanding of enterprise networking concepts, including LAN/WAN architecture, switches, routers, wireless access points, and network closet (MDF/IDF) design and buildout. Broad IT infrastructure knowledge spanning data centers, telecom/network rooms, and campus facilities, with the ability to coordinate cross-functional infrastructure projects that meet industry and university standards. Higher Education experience.is a plus, though not required: Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Project Leadership & Execution  Lead the planning, execution, and delivery of multiple large and complex IT projects across departments, ensuring alignment with university priorities and strategies. Develop and manage detailed project plans, milestones, scope statements, budgets, timelines, and resource allocations. Ensure project deliverables are completed on time, within scope, and on budget while maintaining high quality standards. Conduct risk assessments and implement proactive mitigation strategies. Monitor project activities, ensuring the currency, quality, and integrity of information, while providing consistency in content. Stakeholder Engagement & Communication   Serve as the liaison between technical teams, business units, and executive sponsors. Build and maintain strong relationships with stakeholders at all levels. Facilitate effective communication across cross-functional teams to ensure shared understanding of objectives, progress, and changes. Present project updates, risks, and performance metrics to governance groups and leadership. Team Leadership & Collaboration   Provide direction and support to cross-functional project teams, including internal staff and external vendors. Establish clear goals and responsibilities and promote accountability across team members. Foster a collaborative environment that encourages innovation, ownership, and problem-solving. Monitoring, Reporting & Financial Oversight   Track and report on project progress, milestones, risks, issues, and financials. Develop and publish status reports, prepare and modify schedules or plans as required.  Ensure post-project evaluation, lessons learned, and knowledge documentation. Manage project budgets responsibly, identify cost-saving opportunities, and ensure financial accountability. Management and coordination of Infrastructure Initiatives and Vendors Appoint and manage vendor/subcontractor projects such as installation and servicing cable (copper, coaxial, fiber-optic), optical, telecommunications and T1 networks as well as enterprise-wide, student residential and cabling networks such as NUnet, ResNet and HuskyCable. Review job reports constantly to track labor, material, and over-all job progress and status. Ensure quality of inventory and timely procurement and delivery of materials to jobsite. Coordinate installations of new networks and extensions of current network.  Lead planning and execution of AV upgrade projects, including classroom, conference room, and event space audio-visual system installations and refreshes. Manage low voltage cabling projects, including structured cabling, cable pathway/tray installations, and coordination of testing and certification. Oversee networking infrastructure initiatives, including switch/router upgrades, wireless access point deployments, and network closet (MDF/IDF) buildouts. Coordinate broader IT infrastructure initiatives across campus, partnering with ITS teams and departments to plan and execute technology upgrades supporting Northeastern's facilities and academic spaces. Facilitate construction related technology relocation activities with external vendors, IS Customer Services and Northeastern departmental customer contact. Keep appropriate individuals informed of all installation and/or troubleshooting activities. Position Type Information Technology Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 113S Expected Hiring Range: $113,865.00 - $165,105.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Financial-Analyst_R142483": {
      "title": "Financial Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Develops and maintains financial models that support the university's budgeting, forecasting, and long-range planning efforts. Pulls and analyzes data across multiple university systems, applying a sharp eye for accuracy and a problem-solving mindset to dig into discrepancies wherever they turn up. Builds and maintains Tableau dashboards that keep leadership informed on budget, headcount, and operating KPIs. Connects the data back to what's actually happening across the university's campuses — keeping dashboards and models grounded in real operations. Works across departments and functions to support university-wide financial priorities, and stays open to new tools and approaches — including AI-assisted tools like Claude — as reporting needs evolve.   Minimum Qualifications   Knowledge of financial analysis, budgeting, and forecasting principles, as normally acquired through a Bachelor's degree in Finance, Accounting, Economics, or a related field and 2-4 years of relevant experience Ability to use statistical analysis, simulations, predictive modeling, or other methods to analyze information and develop solutions that support the university's mission Advanced proficiency in Excel, including complex formulas and financial modeling Ability to write and execute SQL queries to extract and analyze data from relational databases Ability to build and maintain dashboards in Tableau or similar BI tools (e.g., Power BI) Ability to apply data visualization best practices to turn complex data into clear, compelling visual narratives for audiences with varying levels of technical expertise Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with high attention to detail and a demonstrated focus on data accuracy Ability to communicate complex data and analysis clearly, both in writing and verbally, to audiences with varying levels of technical expertise Ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines, working both independently and collaboratively in a fast-paced environment Preferred Knowledge of ERP/financial systems (e.g., Workday, Adaptive Planning, Oracle, or similar) Working knowledge of Python or R for data automation and analysis Experience with cloud data platforms (e.g., Snowflake) Familiarity with AI-assisted tools (e.g., Claude) for data analysis, forecasting, or process automation Genuine interest in and commitment to Northeastern's mission   Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Data Aggregation & Analysis Pull, clean, and consolidate financial, enrollment, headcount, and operational data from multiple university systems (Workday, Snowflake, and others) into unified, reliable data sets Identify and resolve data inconsistencies, gaps, and anomalies at the source before they reach downstream reporting Build and maintain automated data pipelines and queries (SQL, Python) to reduce manual data pulls and improve turnaround time on recurring reporting needs Partner with system administrators and data owners to understand source system logic and ensure data is interpreted and mapped correctly Reporting & Dashboard Development Design and maintain Tableau dashboards that translate complex financial and operational data into clear, actionable views for leadership Distill analysis into concise, decision-ready summaries — highlighting key trends, risks, and opportunities rather than raw data Partner with stakeholders to understand reporting needs and iterate on dashboard design as priorities shift Financial Modeling & Forecasting Build and maintain financial models supporting budgeting, forecasting, and long-range planning Conduct scenario and sensitivity analysis to inform planning assumptions and decision-making Support annual budget development and periodic forecast updates in partnership with departmental leadership Cross-Functional Support & Process Improvement Collaborate with departments across the university to support financial planning needs tied to the broader institutional mission Evaluate and adopt AI-assisted tools (e.g., Claude) and other automation technologies to improve efficiency and accuracy of financial processes Document processes and methodologies to support consistency and knowledge transfer across the team Position Type Finance Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Payroll-Business-Systems-Analyst_R142484": {
      "title": "Payroll Business Systems Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY To meet the challenges of the university’s continued growth and expansion, Payroll is looking for a qualified candidate who will contribute to the support and continued enhancements of Workday Time Tracking and Payroll while working collaboratively with colleagues also supporting Workday HCM and Workday Financial Management.    The ideal candidate will serve as a functional liaison between Human Resources, Finance, Payroll, Information Technology, and university stakeholders to support Time Tracking and Payroll systems and its business processes, operational improvements, and user experience initiatives. This role is responsible for partnering with functional teams and customers across colleges and divisions to identify business needs, support system functionality, document processes, and improve operational effectiveness through Workday configuration while ensuring compliance with university policies and regulatory requirements.   The qualified candidate will be responsible for leading functional solutions, providing project management oversight and executing on all phases of the project life cycle, including project planning and communication. In addition, the candidate will assist with planning and execution of the testing efforts including development of test cases, coordination between technical and business resources, and documentation of the testing results. In addition, the candidate will participate in change management activities including developing training materials and providing system support.   The successful candidate must demonstrate strong analytical, problem-solving, organizational, and communication skills, with the ability to build collaborative relationships and provide exceptional customer service in a fast-paced environment.   To ensure that essential services are provided to the university community, the employee will be required to work outside their regular working hours as needed. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in Business, Information Systems, Human Resources, Technology, or a related field; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum of 5 years of experience in business analysis, systems analysis, and/or technology support. Experience supporting and administering Workday HCM specifically Time Tracking and/or Payroll, including business process analysis, testing, troubleshooting, and end-user support. Strong understanding of Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) methodologies and system implementation practices. Demonstrated ability to analyze business processes, identify operational challenges, and recommend effective solutions. Experience developing and executing test plans, test cases, and user acceptance testing activities. Strong project management, organizational, and prioritization skills with the ability to manage multiple initiatives and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and critical-thinking skills with strong attention to detail and accuracy. Strong interpersonal, customer service, and relationship management skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively across technical and functional teams. Excellent verbal and written communication skills, including the ability to communicate complex concepts to both technical and non-technical audiences. Ability to handle sensitive and confidential information with discretion and professionalism. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, including Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Visio. Preferred Qualifications Workday HCM configuration experience, specifically Time Tracking and Payroll. Experience supporting HR and Payroll systems in a higher education environment. Experience leading or participating in cross-functional projects involving Payroll, Time Management, HR, Benefits, or Talent functions Key Competencies Customer-focused service orientation. Strong collaboration and teamwork skills. Sound judgment and decision-making abilities. Ability to influence and build effective working relationships. Adaptability and effectiveness in a dynamic, execution-oriented environment.   KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Work with customers to analyze Workday HCM Time Tracking and Payroll system requirements and identify problems, gather requirements, research options, and recommend solutions. Works with users to determine needs, identify changes and enhancements Identify and assess solution options Documents required system modifications Analyze data to inform problems Create user stories Document as-is and to-be process flows Identify gaps in operational business processes, as well as document business process improvements and flow diagrams Prepare detailed business requirements Work closely with business teams to strategize solutions that support short, mid, and long-term business objectives Present and evaluate design solutions Communicate design to all stakeholders Coordinate and implement solutions Act as the liaison between Business, Developers, Project Managers and 3rd party vendors Configure solutions in Workday HCM Time Tracking and Payroll Document mapping requirements for interfaces with internal and 3rd party solutions Work closely with developers to ensure requirements and design are completely understood Develop and coordinate test plans, test scripts, facilitate and participate in User Acceptance Testing Ensure deliverables align with business requirements with measurable results and drive positive results Participate in the creation of training materials and the delivery of training Effectively resolves problems and roadblocks before they occur Facilitate project meetings Perform ad-hoc project management activities  Provide support on application solutions Work with users to trouble shoot issues Coordinate with appropriate resources to resolve issues Update applicable documentation Communicate resolutions to appropriate parties Maintain operational processes streamlined and/or scheduled within Workday Collaborate as well as be responsible for Calendar and Fiscal Year End activities Position Type Information Technology Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $76,335.00 - $107,823.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Chemistry-and-Chemical-Biology_R142430": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Chemistry and Chemical Biology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Northeastern: Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Arlington; Boston; Charlotte, North Carolina; London; Miami; New York City; Oakland; Portland, Maine; San Francisco; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. The College of Science comprises six departments: Biology, Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Marine and Environmental Sciences, Mathematics, Physics, and Psychology. The Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology (“CCB”) ( https://cos.northeastern.edu/chemistry-chemical-biology/ ) includes 38 tenured/tenure-track faculty, including joint appointments, 30% of whom are women; 25 full-time teaching faculty; two research faculty; and nine faculty with affiliated appointments. CCB enrolls approximately 150 undergraduate majors; 30 M.S. and PlusOne (BS/MS) students; and 100 Ph.D. students. In addition to degrees in Chemistry, a professional science Master’s degree in Biotechnology is also offered. The Department of Biology ( https://cos.northeastern.edu/biology/ ) includes 25 tenured/tenure-track faculty, including joint appointments; and 26 full-time teaching faculty. With its core undergraduate degree programs in Biology and in Cell & Molecular Biology, the department enrolls around one-quarter of all undergraduate majors in the College of Science; it also co-manages the Behavioral Neuroscience major with the Department of Psychology. The graduate program in Biology currently has over 50 Ph.D. students in two primary concentrations (Cell & Molecular Biology, and Molecular Microbiology), including 15 Industry Ph.D. students who offer exciting partnership opportunities with pharma. In addition to degrees in Biology, professional science Master’s degrees in Bioinformatics and in Cell and Gene Therapies are also offered. The two departments co-manage the Biochemistry major. Experiential education is a hallmark of our programs, where students gain experience at leading biotechnology companies based in Boston's globally-dominant entrepreneurial space (Moderna, Editas, Gingko Bioworks, for instance). Northeastern's integrative education model is ranked #1 in Cooperative education programs (US News and World Report: 2022) which leads directly to the remarkable career placement rate of 96% for our students. The Massachusetts campuses are physically located at the nexus of the largest concentration of pharmaceutical and biotechnology companies in the world. Northeastern is home to the Coastal Sustainability Institute , the Global Resilience Institute , the Barnett Institute for Chemical and Biological Analysis , the Center for Drug Discovery , the Network Science Institute , the Northeastern University Center for Renewable Energy Technology , an NSF Frontier Center in Theoretical Biological Physics , and the Center for Interdisciplinary Research on Complex Systems , providing a rich multidisciplinary intellectual environment. About the Opportunity: The College of Science at Northeastern University invites applications for positions at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor in Chemistry and Chemical Biology and/or Biology beginning in academic year 2027-2028 or 2028-2029. Candidates who are completing their PhD or at an early stage of their postdoctoral training are encouraged to apply to these tenure-track faculty appointments. Areas of interest in the current search include Physical AI, Robotics & Autonomous Systems for Chemistry and Biology, broadly interpreted. This spans intelligent infrastructure and adaptive construction/manufacturing robotics using digital twins and multi-agent controls; embodied and wearable AI systems that bridge human-computer interaction and design; automated self-driving laboratories that pair hardware instrumentation with active-learning machine learning for experiment design to accelerate chemistry, materials, and biomanufacturing experimentation. Primary appointments will be in Boston at the College of Science (Chemistry and Chemical Biology and/or Biology) with possible other joint appointments across the College of Engineering and Khoury College of Computer Sciences. Appointments will have the opportunity to collaborate in cross-disciplinary teams across the University and will complement existing strengths. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Belonging, the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to further develop and support a thriving faculty. Responsibilities: The potential hires are expected to develop vigorous research programs cross cutting the fields of automated/self-driving labs for chemistry and biology. Faculty members at Northeastern are expected to develop independent research programs that attract external funding; teach courses at the graduate and undergraduate levels; mentor students and postdocs in their area of research; and participate in service to the department, university, and discipline. Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Qualifications: Applicants must have a PhD in chemistry, biology, engineering or related fields by the employment start date. We encourage applicants from a wide range of backgrounds, including academia and industry. Applicants should have a strong record of scholarly accomplishment that demonstrates the ability to build a strong research program. Evidence of potential as a research scientist is expected, but postdoctoral experience is not required for appointments at the Assistant Professor level. Candidates must be committed to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching. They should embrace the Northeastern emphasis on experiential education and the college philosophy that access to science education and careers must be inclusive. Depending on the research profile and expertise, a joint or affiliate appointment in another department(s) within the University is possible. Additional Information: Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, statements addressing the prompts below, and names and contact information for at least three professional references from whom letters of reference can be solicited as needed. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to contributing to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts: Research statement (no longer than 5 pages) : Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, the funding you have received to support the work (if applicable), the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Highlight any cross-disciplinary research efforts you have undertaken. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students and/or with the external community. Finally, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing at Northeastern, any cross-disciplinary and collaborative aspects, and the strategies you will take to address each direction. Teaching statement (1 page) : Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the College of Science (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a diverse cohort of undergraduate and graduate students. Leadership statement (1 page) : Leadership Statement: Please summarize your experience in leadership positions. Describe your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice. Applications will be reviewed beginning on October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to the search committee chairs, Professor Sijia Dong, s.dong@northeastern.edu and Professor Win Chai y.chai@northeastern.edu . Northeastern University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $105,000.00 - $125,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Aquatics-Operations-Coordinator-SEIU_R141637": {
      "title": "Aquatics Operations Coordinator SEIU",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY Reporting to the Aquatics Director, the Aquatics Operations Coordinator assists with planning and coordinating pool operations, including training and supervising lifeguards , developing and executing staffing schedules, overseeing the facility and equipment operations, implementing policies and procedures, and ensuring the pool is in compliance and operating under best demonstrated practices. This position will maintain a safe environment that upholds the Trefethen Aquatic Center’s written standards, while ensuring that the facility remains a major part of a vibrant campus, including its facilities, programming, and the safety of all users. Weekend, early morning, evening, and holiday work hours will be required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's Degree required with 3-5 years of related experience and/or training or equivalent combination of education and experience. Knowledge of best practices in Aquatic Facility management and programming. Current Red Cross Lifeguard certification. Current Red Cross Lifeguard Instructor certification (or ability to obtain within 60 days). Current First Aid for Public Safety Personnel certification (Title 22). Certified Pool Operator/Aquatic Facility Operator (preferred). Ability to navigate the American Red Cross SABA platform. Demonstrated strength in ability to communicate, negotiate, and problem-solving skills necessary to successfully manage issues when resolving conflicts related to direct reports/members/others. Must be able to perform duties of a physical nature, including standing, lifting, sitting, walking, and swimming. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Train and supervise student workers and part-time temporary staff. Assist with scheduling the staff using When to Work software, and substitute when needed. Weekly timekeeping review, experience with Workday preferred. Assess Lifeguard performance through in-service training and ongoing drills. Ability to identify  problems, assess alternatives, render consistent and logical decisions. Provide general customer service and cashiering; assist with signage, schedules and updating the website. Assist with set-up, management, and coordination of special events (swim team practices and meets, water polo practices and games/tournaments, campus events, etc.). Help plan, organize, schedule, and coordinate summer swim lesson programs. Teach American Red Cross courses and provide certificates. Work with staff to ensure student program support. Manage conflicts and effectively negotiate resolutions. Maintain safe work conditions and comply with established safety policies, practices, procedures, and requirements. Establish and maintain an effective customer service orientation toward all campus users. Conduct surveys as needed. Evaluate and recommend marketing and advertising venues that align with Northeastern University’s goals. Support the implementation, review, and recommendations related to facility policies and procedures, including facility access, emergency action plans, and standard operating procedures. Assist with social media, pool website, and online marketing. Establish and maintain effective and productive working relationships while working in a diverse and multicultural environment. Remain abreast of developments in all aspects of aquatics by reading, attending conferences,workshops, trainings, and clinics. Ensure the pool is in compliance with all regulatory requirements. Assist in the University’s health and safety programs. Participate in campus staff meetings and professional training, as appropriate. Assume and perform other related duties as assigned Position Type Athletics Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Base Pay Rate: $35.70"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Director---Board-Operations_R142498": {
      "title": "Associate Director - Board Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY Reporting directly to the Board Secretary, the Associate Director of Board Operations and Assistant Secretary to the Board of Trustees is responsible for 1) providing organization and leadership for activities and responsibilities of the Board of Trustees and its committees and 2) assisting with the strategic direction for Advancement’s Community Engagement Initiative. The Associate Director must be able to: Develop and maintain a board relations manual, board calendars, and a repository of all board documents. Develop and create innovate ways to engage university stakeholders with an emphasis on Trustees’ relationships, Implement relationship and engagement strategies. Develop working knowledge of Salesforce and ensure activity is accurate and up to date in order to inform relationship strategies. Provide support for all Northeastern University board activities as assigned and develop understanding of governance rules and regulations for all national and international entities. Initiate and provide project management for the creation and/or improvement of relationship management and business processes; monitor and evaluate operational effectiveness and effect changes required for improvement. Support all activities of the Board Secretary, including autonomously implementing short- and long-range organizational goals, objectives, and operating procedures. Coordinate logistics for board meetings including technology, remote communications, and materials. Communicate and coordinate with board members for ad hoc/special meetings and assist in development of communications to the board, including dashboard updates. Manage OnBoard secure portal, including providing training to users and assisting with accessibility and functionality problems experienced by users. Manage regulatory and policy requirements (annual trustee Conflict of Interest disclosure, ITAR filings, FCC filings, Signature Authority Delegations) Draft, circulate, and maintain minutes of board and committee meetings and other university governance documents. Coordinate with staff across the university on developing board materials. Develop and deploy strategic benchmarks and KPIs within a multi-year plan to measure success in ways that demonstrate positive impact on staff, alumni, and the global university system and its surrounding communities. Attend Board and committee meetings to provide procedural, governance, and operational support. Maintain strict confidentiality. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree required and a minimum of 5 years of relevant experience. Significant demonstrated experience with project management, organization, documentation, process development, communications, and presentations. Strong technology skills are critical. Must have clear and concise writing ability, a process orientation, exemplary customer service skills and orientation, and impeccable attention to detail. Must be able to exercise absolute discretion and good judgement when dealing with confidential and sensitive material. Demonstrated ability to manage an intense workload and produce quality results in a fast-paced environment. Possess the skills necessary for building relationships with external (trustees, donors, prospects) and internal constituents (senior administration, deans, faculty, staff, students) and be highly professional and collaborative in approach. Ability to travel and a flexible schedule on occasion are required. The successful candidate will be an innovative, strategic and results-oriented leader with a broad vision for the importance of relationship management in achieving division and institutional success and excellence. They must be adept at facilitating difficult discussions, shepherding change, creating and strengthening partnerships, and aligning an array of efforts across the division and within the context of the greater university. Demonstrated interest and ability as a collaborator and relationship builder, with strong interpersonal skills and ability to build coalitions and collaborative working relationships with a broad range of individuals and groups, including senior administration, to achieve results. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Board Relations; Project Management; Process Documentation and Maintenance; Communication Develop and create innovate ways to engage university stakeholders with an emphasis on Trustees’ relationships, and engagement strategies. Develop working knowledge of Salesforce and ensure activity is accurate and up to date in order to inform relationship strategies. Provide support for all Northeastern University board activities as assigned and develop understanding of governance rules and regulations for all national and international entities. Initiate and provide project management for the creation and/or improvement of business processes; monitor and evaluate operational effectiveness and effect changes required for improvement. Support all activities of the Board Secretary including autonomously implementing short- and long-term organizational goals, objectives, and operating procedures Develop and maintain a board relations manual, board calendars, and a repository of all board documents Coordinate all logistics for board meetings including technology, remote communications, and materials Communicate and coordinate with board members for ad hoc/special meetings; assist in development of communications to board, including dashboard updates. Manage OnBoard secure portal including providing training to users and assisting with accessibility and functionality problems experienced by users Draft, circulate, and maintain minutes of board and committee meetings and other university governance documents Coordinate with staff across the university on developing board materials Support implementation of Board priorities, strategic initiatives, governance goals, and special projects. Maintain systems to track Board actions, directives, assignments, and implementation progress. Prepare reports, dashboards, and status updates regarding Board actions and strategic initiatives. Coordinate with stakeholders to ensure timely follow-through on Board-approved actions and projects. Assist in evaluating governance processes and recommending improvements that increase effectiveness, transparency, and accountability. Manage regulatory and policy requirements (annual trustee Conflict of Interest disclosure, ITAR filings, FCC filings, Signature Authority Delegations) Trustees’ Emeriti Program Management Manage and support at all activities and events involving Trustees' Emeriti program. Develop and create innovate ways to engage Trustees’ Emeriti with an emphasis on relationships,  and engagement strategies. Develop working knowledge of Salesforce and ensure activity is accurate and up to date in order to inform relationship strategies. Office Management Hire, orient, and train one administrative professional staff member, temporary employee, or student intern, as needed. Provide guidance, feedback, coaching, and mentoring to direct report and lead by professional example to promote their professional and personal development. Daily management of direct report including goal setting and performance evaluation. Manage department budget and reconcile expenses. Compliance Program Support Serve as backup to guide and advise Board Secretary on compliance program matters within the Advancement Division. Be willing to collaborate with Advancement team members and create a working knowledge of compliance-related matters and their impact on philanthropic initiatives.   Support the implementation of strategic initiatives. Partner with stakeholders and internal and external constituencies to ensure programming supports the divisions and university’s values and strategic goals. Other Duties as Assigned Recognize that the nature of the work is dynamic and be flexible in accepting other responsibilities that further the mission and vision of the university when needed. Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $76,335.00 - $107,823.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Counselor-Psychotherapist_R140622": {
      "title": "Counselor/Psychotherapist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Northeastern University Health and Counseling Services (UHCS) Counselor and Psychotherapist provides psychological counseling services to Northeastern University students, including psychological evaluation, crisis intervention, and brief psychotherapy. The Counselor and Psychotherapist may also engage in outreach activities to the Northeastern University community and collaborate with various support services within the University. The Counselor and Psychotherapist makes appropriate referrals for psychopharmacological evaluation and ongoing counseling in the local community, and for referral to emergency mental health services when appropriate. Time spent across the various clinical service activities may shift in proportion to changes in service demands at any given time of the year. The Counselor and Psychotherapist works closely with University academic departments and Student Life, and collaborates with other members of the UHCS Behavioral Health and Medical staff, all in the interest of providing the best possible team-oriented health care to Northeastern students. The Counselor and Psychotherapist may also occasionally participate in health promotion outreach to the University community. Availability by pager or phone on occasional evenings and overnight is required. Minimum Qualifications: Required Master's degree in Social Work, Psychology, or Counseling Full state licensure as an LICSW, LMHC, LMFT, or Psychologist with relevant work experience Comprehensive knowledge of principles of Social Work, Psychology, or Counseling Demonstrated experience and competency in urgent and crisis assessment and treatment, short-term psychotherapy, and assessment and treatment of young adults with alcohol and drug abuse, eating disorders, and ADHD Demonstrated effectiveness collaborating with health center and academic support staff, as well as community-based hospitals and healthcare providers Demonstrated excellence in written and oral communication skills Demonstrated success working within the clinical and administrative policies and procedures of a university health center setting Demonstrated experience working effectively in a highly diverse student and staff setting Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: Direct Clinical Services Provide direct clinical services including triage and assessment (in person and via telehealth), individual and group counseling, crisis intervention, disposition assignment, case management and documentation, and campus and community referrals. Consultation and Outreach Provide psychoeducational programming and outreach to the University community. Serve on University, divisional, and UHCS committees and participate in health promotions outreach. In-Service and Interdisciplinary Supervision Participate in training and supervision with other Behavioral Health and Medical staff, as appropriate. Required Documents Resume Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Administrative-Officer--Operations-and-Events_R142476": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer, Operations and Events",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity About the College of Arts, Media and Design We are a vibrant community of creators, designers, technologists, and communicators applying creativity across disciplines and harnessing emerging technologies to advance human potential and solve the challenges of the future. Powered by experience-driven teaching, learning and research, CAMD demonstrates that the fields of design, communication, media, and the arts are crucial to the human experience, society, and the development of technology. We empower the next generations of students, researchers, and creative practitioners to create the future by sparking their innovative, creative, curious, and entrepreneurial spirit. Job Summary Under the supervision of the Senior Academic Program Manager of Art + Design, the Administrative Officer, Office Operations & Events serves as the department’s primary resource for office operations, facilities and studio management, event coordination, onboarding, and administrative support, ensuring efficient day-to-day operations and a high level of service for faculty, staff, students, and visitors. Serving as the primary liaison between the department and university service partners, this position coordinates office and studio operations, facilities requests, onboarding logistics, and department events, including workshops, speaker series, award ceremonies, orientations, and guest travel arrangements. The role oversees front-office operations, supports department leadership through administrative coordination, and collaborates with campus partners to maintain safe, functional, and well-managed departmental spaces. The position exercises a high degree of independent judgment and problem-solving in managing complex operational activities and serves as a trusted resource to Art + Design faculty, staff, students, and university partners. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Office Operations & Facilities Management (35%) Oversee the department’s day-to-day operations, ensuring efficient office administration, departmental communications, meeting logistics, and continuity of operations Manage office and studio spaces, including facilities requests, capital improvement projects, keys and access, furniture, equipment, supply inventory, and space utilization Coordinate with Facilities, Information Technology, Compliance, and Safety to maintain safe, functional, and technology-enabled departmental spaces Oversee studio operations and maintenance, including scheduling, booking systems, and open studio hours Administrative Support & Onboarding (25%) Serve as the primary front-office contact for faculty, staff, students, visitors, and prospective students, providing exceptional customer service and resolving operational inquiries Provide administrative support to Department Chairs, including calendar coordination, meeting logistics, committee administration, departmental communications, and operational reporting Coordinate the onboarding of new faculty and staff, including workspace setup, technology, university systems access, and departmental orientation Hire, train, schedule, and supervise student office assistants Supervise four temporary non-student (TNS) Figure Models and three Work-Study Office Assistants Event Management (20%) Coordinate department and college events, including workshops, speaker series, award ceremonies, orientation, and department productions Provide event support for year-end events, including commencement and student awards Organize hotel, lunch, and dinner reservations for lectures and university guests Archive and maintain records of event promotions and awards received Distributed Shared Service Support (20%) Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to develop standardized procedures and protocols for shared job functions to ensure consistency Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to implement robust data management practices that ensure data accessibility and integrity, with a focus on office and space records Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to create opportunities for cross-training on departmental practices, support Department Chairs, and promote broad efficiencies across the College Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to provide office coverage during absences or scheduled paid time off (PTO) Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor’s degree Minimum of two to three years of administrative experience Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite, particularly Excel Strong organizational, analytical, and interpersonal skills, with exceptional attention to detail Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a fast-paced, service-oriented environment, manage multiple priorities, and adapt to changing business needs, shifting priorities, and evolving processes Preferred Administrative experience in higher education Experience with Workday and Concur Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Director-of-Intelligence--Analytics---Data_R142494": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Intelligence, Analytics & Data",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Senior Director of Intelligence, Analytics & Data provides strategic leadership for Advancement’s reporting, analytics, data governance, innovation, and organizational learning functions. Reporting to the Vice President of Advancement Resources, this role establishes priorities, governance structures, and analytical strategies that enable data-informed decision-making across the Advancement enterprise. The Senior Director oversees a portfolio that includes reporting and business intelligence, data governance, integrations and data architecture, innovation and emerging technologies, artificial intelligence enablement, and organizational learning. This role serves as a strategic advisor to Advancement leadership, ensuring data, technology, and analytics capabilities are aligned with fundraising priorities and institutional objectives. The Senior Director leads a team of directors and analysts responsible for building a modern, scalable, and trusted data environment that supports fundraising effectiveness and operational excellence. Minimum & Preferred Qualifications Required Qualifications ·         Bachelor's degree required. ·         10+ years of progressive experience in analytics, business intelligence, data governance, advancement services, information management, or related field. ·         Experience leading analytics, reporting, or data strategy functions. ·         Demonstrated success leading cross-functional teams and complex organizational initiatives. ·         Strong understanding of data governance, business intelligence, and organizational performance measurement. Preferred Qualifications ·         Master's degree preferred. ·         Experience in higher education advancement, fundraising, or nonprofit environments. ·         Experience implementing enterprise analytics, governance, or AI initiatives. ·         Experience leading organizational change and innovation programs. ·         Familiarity with Salesforce, Tableau, Power BI, CRM systems, and data platforms. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities 1. Strategic Leadership & Organizational Prioritization Establish the vision, priorities, and operating model for Intelligence, Analytics & Data. Align resources and initiatives with Advancement goals and provide strategic guidance to leadership regarding analytics, reporting, governance, innovation, and technology opportunities. 2. Analytics, Reporting & Decision Support Strategy Oversee the development of reporting, business intelligence, and analytics capabilities that support fundraising performance, operational effectiveness, and leadership decision-making. Ensure stakeholders have access to trusted and actionable information. 3. Data Governance & Enterprise Information Management Provide executive oversight of Advancement data governance, data quality, stewardship, and information management practices. Establish accountability structures and governance processes that improve confidence in institutional data assets. 4. Innovation, AI & Organizational Learning Champion innovation across Advancement through the evaluation and adoption of emerging technologies, AI-enabled solutions, and organizational learning programs. Foster a culture of continuous improvement and data literacy. 5. Leadership, Talent Development & Cross-Functional Partnership Lead and develop a high-performing team of directors and analysts. Partner with Advancement leadership, Technology, Finance, and university stakeholders to advance shared goals and drive organizational effectiveness. Key Competencies ·         Strategic leader with experience building and leading analytics, reporting, and data governance functions ·         Ability to align data strategy with fundraising priorities and organizational goals ·         Strong decision-making and prioritization skills in complex environments ·         Proven ability to build high-performing, cross-functional teams ·         Executive communication and stakeholder management ·         Innovation leadership and change management ·         Enterprise data governance and analytics expertise Supervision Directly supervises: ·         Director of Intelligence & Innovation ·         Director of Data Integrity & Governance ·         Director of Reporting & Analytics Position Type Data Planning and Analysis Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 114S Expected Hiring Range: $130,945.00 - $189,868.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Program-Coordinator_R142510": {
      "title": "Program Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Program Coordinator provides technical support for one or more programs, including planning, organizing, scheduling and responding to inquiries.  Activities may include serving as a lead point of contact with minimal direction to exchange program information, planning/organizing events, budget/finance responsibilities, general program administration work and ensuring the program is administered effectively and efficiently. The Program Coordinator may also direct the work of students, temporary workers or support staff, carry out day to-day operational plans, and coordinate and/or provide input into projects, programs, or systems. Key Responsibilities Provide an array of technical support activities that require subject matter knowledge to administer the program in an efficient, effective manner (e.g., communications, recordkeeping, reports, etc.). Serves as a point of contact for the program: review inquiries, determine the course of action to take, and complete, delegate or escalate to the person best able to address the problem. Support the budget administration with forecasting, identify and resolve budget related issues, maintain expense controls, review monthly expenditures and projections, ensure appropriateness and accuracy of expenditures against guidelines. Assists in the ongoing evaluation of the program’s workflows, design, and documentation to ensure it as administer effectively and efficiently; makes minor improvements of processes, procedures, or systems within job area or team. Oversees services with vendors and resolve outstanding issues Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s Degree preferred; two to three (2-3) years of professional experience preferably in higher education and/or development required. The university is seeking a highly motivated, energetic individual who can think independently and analytically. Ability to manage and facilitate an intense workload and produce quality results in a fast-paced environment. Must be able to manage multiple priority projects at once with ease and efficiency. Must be proactive, anticipate problems, be resourceful, and work effectively with colleagues and external constituents at all levels. Strong interpersonal skills necessary for building relationships with external (donors, prospects) and internal (faculty, staff, colleagues) constituents. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Strong organizational skills. Highly collaborative in approach. Ability to exercise good judgment when dealing with confidential and sensitive material. Proficiency in Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, Outlook, and Excel Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Facilities-Customer-Service-Coordinator_R142495": {
      "title": "Facilities Customer Service Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY Facilities Customer Service Coordinators are on staff around the clock, purposely, to quickly respond to all requests/needs, especially emergencies (e.g., elevator entrapments, floods, no heat, etc.) and serve as the initial point of contact for the Facilities Division.  The Facilities Customer Service Center (FCSC) is open and staffed 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, 365 days per year. Faculty, staff, students, vendors, contractors and visitors either visit in person or contact by phone/web the FCSC for a variety of services, chief amongst those being the submission of work requests (approximately 50,000 requests per year.) During the normal university workday (M-F, 8:30 AM – 5:00 PM), all duties and responsibilities listed below may occur. Outside of these hours, only some of these duties are typically performed, and less frequently. Duties include: Greeting visitors and act on their needs, accordingly, as detailed in procedures/processes.  Fielding incoming telephone calls and routing appropriately.  Accepting, recording, distributing, and processing work requests dispatching appropriate personnel and escalating issues as procedures direct, or superiors dictate.  Operating a two-way radio, dispatching, and paging system.  Administering the sign out and return of university keys/keycards Encoding keycards and emergency Huskycard Photo ID printing. Monitoring energy management system and other emergency alarms. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS High School diploma or GED and two (2) years of customer service-related work experience, preferably in a call center, required.  Ability to effectively interact and clearly communicate with people at a variety of different levels with a focus on customer needs. Ability to accurately follow procedures and verbal directions. Possesses basic computer skills with the ability/desire to learn new software programs. Ability to effectively use NU’s Computerized Maintenance Management System (CMMS) for receiving, completing, and documenting actions on Work Orders, preferred. Accurate typing & data entry skills Proficient typing skills with the ability to accurately enter data. Willing to work as part of a team and able to work independently in a fast-paced environment. Other Requirements This position is 40 hours per week and requires a background check. Mandatory overtime and off-shift coverage. Must remain on duty until relief personnel arrives. Schedule is Tuesday through Saturday, 2:30PM until 11PM and includes an alternative holiday schedule. Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 105H Expected Hiring Range: $20.77 - $28.56 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Studio-Multimedia-Producer--Part-Time-_R142407": {
      "title": "Studio Multimedia Producer (Part-Time)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: Northeastern University’s Experiential Digital Global Education Division (EDGE) is seeking a part-time Studio Multimedia Producer (SMP) to help create innovative educational media content in support of the teaching and learning goals of our digital learning products. EDGE drives Northeastern’s growth goals and digital strategy broadly by serving two functions: 1. Providing digital learning design and development services to Northeastern’s schools and colleges 2. Works with colleges & corporate partners connecting learning to employees and professionals.   The Studio Multimedia Producer supports EDGE’s video and multimedia production operations, providing hands-on technical execution for video and audio studio recordings, live and live-to-tape productions, and post-production workflows that power Northeastern’s learning partnerships and digital learning products.   This role operates primarily within a fixed studio/control-room environment and works closely with Learning Enablement, Digital Strategy, and Program Delivery teams to deliver polished, broadcast-quality content on tight production schedules.   WORK EXPECTATIONS   Ranging from 5 to 20 hrs/week   This role is primarily Boston based, with video shoots in person, while editing and conducting meetings may be done remotely   Must be able to regularly commute to the EDGE Studio in Boston, in person days are based on Studio need   Availability for occasional evening or weekend production schedules   Key Accountabilities & Responsibilities: 1) Studio Production & Technical Execution   Operate studio production equipment including cameras, video switchers, lighting grids, teleprompters, and audio mixing consoles for recorded and live-to-tape sessions   Set up, calibrate, and strike studio configurations for a range of production formats: single-camera interviews, multi-camera panel discussions, and green-screen/virtual-set recordings   Provide technical direction during live and recorded shoots, troubleshooting signal flow, audio levels, and lighting issues in real time   Ensure all recordings meet EDGE’s technical and brand quality standards prior to delivery   2) Post-Production Support & Equipment Stewardship   Perform basic post-production tasks, including ingest, file management, rough assembly, and quality control review prior to handoff to digital media team members   Maintain studio equipment inventory, perform routine maintenance, and flag equipment needs or upgrades   Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree or equivalent education and experience   2+ years' experience in studio or broadcast production, video production, or a closely related field.   Demonstrated proficiency with professional video switchers, lighting instruments, and multi-channel audio consoles.   Working knowledge of signal flow, camera operation, and studio control-room workflows.   Familiarity with non-linear editing platforms (e.g., Adobe Premiere Pro, Final Cut Pro) for basic post-production tasks.   Adeptness in recording and editing with multiple audio/video equipment formats.   Color correction, audio effects, and other advanced editing skills.   Demonstrated flexibility and adaptability to take on new projects and assignments on short notice.   Strong organizational skills while working on multiple projects and completing high-quality work against strict and conflicting deadlines.   Proven ability to work effectively with vendors and collaborate with team members.   Ability to maintain focus on priorities while multi-tasking and thrive in an agile environment.   Positive and professional attitude; interest in learning new skills.   Ability to work autonomously as well as part of a team.   Must be committed to creating a professional collaborative work environment by demonstrating a respect for colleagues and their expertise; cultivating open communication & feedback; and inspiring a unified commitment to elevating the standard of quality of the deliverables.   Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $25-$35"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Operations-Coordinator--Residential-Life_R142412": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator- Residential Life",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Reporting to one of the Senior Assistant Directors within Residential Life at Northeastern University, the Operations Coordinator oversees the facilities and operational functions of a complex of residential communities, including daily operation and maintenance updates. Encompassing a wide range of operational duties, including building preparedness, facilities management, emergency response, student support and enhancing community living. The Coordinator is a live-off position that provides point of contact student support.   Key Responsibilities Administrative Management Partnering with Housing Operations maintains accurate records of student housing assignments, move-ins, and move-outs.  Oversee the documentation of maintenance requests, incident reports, and other relevant records.  Ensure compliance with university policies and federal regulations related to student housing.  Prepare and submit regular reports on housing occupancy, maintenance issues, and other relevant metrics.  Facilities Management Coordinate with Facilities Services to ensure timely resolution of facility-related issues.  Conduct regular inspections of residence halls to identify maintenance needs and safety concerns.  Oversee the implementation of preventative maintenance programs.  Student Support and Community Building Serve as the point of contact for community complex, to provide a welcoming environment for students and visitors   Provide point of contact support for students regarding housing, living, or facilities concerns.   Oversee student worker coverage of area offices   Manage move-in and move-out process and building preparedness for a complex of communities.    Serve as a conduct officer for health and safety violations, and low-level student handbook violations   Collaborate with other university departments to enhance student support services.  Emergency Management  Schedules can include evening operations management, including conducting rounds to ensure the safety and security of a group of residential complexes as needed.  Coordinate with campus security and emergency services as needed.  Supervision and Staff Development Supervise a team of student workers   Conduct regular meetings and training sessions for staff members.  Provide training and developmental opportunities for Resident Assistants throughout the year.  Assist in the recruitment and selection of new staff members.  Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree   Experience: Experience with higher education, facilities management, operational functions, and/or community development.   Skills:  Strong organizational and administrative skills.  Excellent interpersonal and communication skills.  Ability to handle emergencies calmly and effectively.  Proficiency in student housing management software, Maxient, and Workday.  Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106H Expected Hiring Range: $23.37 - $32.14 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant--Associate--Full-Professor---College-of-Engineering---Mechanical-and-Industrial-Engineering---Boston_R142462-1": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor - College of Engineering - Mechanical and Industrial Engineering - Boston",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Critical Infrastructure & Cyber-AI Security This theme focuses on protecting and strengthening the physical and digital systems that underpin modern society. This theme spans hardware and critical infrastructure resilience, leveraging AI, digital twins, and sensing to build secure and adaptive infrastructure; cyber-AI security, and safety, which addresses securing embodied AI/robotics, critical infrastructure systems, critical industrial control systems, security infrastructures and cryptographic techniques for AI systems; and advanced manufacturing for future semiconductor and energy materials, aligned with CHIPS-Act priorities. Anchored in Boston, the theme builds on cross-college collaboration among COE (College of Engineering), Khoury College of Computer Sciences, Mills (Mills College at Northeastern), CSSH (College of Social Sciences and Humanities), CAMD (College of Arts, Media and Design), COS (College of Science), and Law (School of Law), together with Northeastern's interdisciplinary ecosystem. It builds on the Kostas Research Institute (KRI), CHEST (Center for Hardware and Embedded Systems Security and Trust), SENTRY (Soft Target Engineering to Neutralize the Threat Reality), DIBI (Defense Industrial Base Institute), and the Center for Advanced Materials for Resiliency (CAMR), connecting cryptography, AI, physical infrastructure resilience, cyber-AI security, and advanced manufacturing to safeguard the systems the nation depends on against natural disasters and both conventional and emerging (including quantum-era) threats. About the opportunity: Advanced manufacturing for future semiconductor materials and energy storage systems Northeastern University invites applications for a tenured/tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant, Associate or Full Professor level in the broad area of advanced manufacturing for future semiconductor materials and energy storage systems, aligned with CHIPS-Act priorities in semiconductors, battery and energy-storage research under critical infrastructure. Appointments will be primarily or fully in the Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering (MIE) within Northeastern University’s College of Engineering, with opportunities for joint cross-college appointments with the College of Science. The position will be based primarily on Northeastern University’s Boston campus, providing an exceptional environment for interdisciplinary research, education, and collaboration across engineering, science, industry, and government. We are seeking outstanding faculty candidates with research interests and expertise in any of a set of broadly defined areas relevant to advanced manufacturing for future semiconductor materials and energy storage systems, which include but are not limited to: Advanced semiconductor materials processing Nanoscale and scalable manufacturing of semiconductor or energy materials Thermal interface materials for advanced packaging Heterogeneous integration of semiconductors Scalable synthesis of battery active materials Emerging solid-state battery materials Energy harvesting and conversion materials Polymers for electronic and energy system applications AI-driven discovery and manufacturing of energy storage materials Dry‑processing, solvent‑free electrode manufacturing In‑situ / operando manufacturing metrology, process‑monitoring, and quality control   Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Belonging, the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to further develop and support a thriving faculty. About Northeastern: Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Arlington; Boston; Charlotte, North Carolina; London; Miami; New York City; Oakland; Portland, Maine; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. About MIE and COE in Northeastern University: The Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering (MIE) is Northeastern's largest department, with a faculty of more than 90 members, including 65 tenured and tenure-track faculty, whose work spans AI, biomechanics, energy systems, fluids, human-technology integration, manufacturing, materials, mechanics, network and service systems, and robotics. MIE sits within Northeastern's College of Engineering (COE), a leader in experiential education and interdisciplinary research focused on innovating for global impact, anchored by Northeastern's signature cooperative education program and R1 research rating. The college enrolls more than 10,000 students pursuing BS, MS, and PhD degrees and includes more than 450 faculty, and leads four university-wide research institutes while partnering in ten more. Faculty across MIE and COE collaborate on funded research spanning advanced manufacturing, energy systems, robotics, and infrastructure resilience.    Responsibilities Faculty members at Northeastern teach courses at the graduate and undergraduate level; mentor students and postdocs in their area of research; and participate in service to the department, university, and discipline. Candidates are expected to develop independent research programs that attract external funding and generate vigorous research programs cross-cutting the fields of engineering and/or science. Requirements A Ph.D., or foreign equivalent, in Mechanical Engineering, Materials Engineering, Chemical Engineering or a closely related field by the employment start date is required.  Academic rank at the Associate Professor or Full Professor level will be commensurate with experience and qualifications reflecting a record of demonstrated teaching and scholarly excellence. Applicants at the Associate level should have at least 4 years of academic or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research) and at the Full Professor level at least 8 years of academic or equivalent experience (e.g., industry, research). Candidates may have opportunities for joint appointments and/or interdisciplinary collaboration within and across colleges. We encourage applicants from a wide range of backgrounds, including academia and industry. A complete application includes a cover letter, curriculum vitae that includes a full list of publications; a statement of research interests and objectives; a teaching statement; and at least three names for reference letters. Please see the information below for detailed information on what documents to include and what we are looking for within each statement. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. The review is expected to start immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Statement Prompts: Statement of Research Interests, Objectives, & Vision : Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, any funding you have received to support the work, the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students, with the external community, and/or with individuals from marginalized groups. Finally, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing as a faculty member.  Statement of Teaching Interests & Philosophy : Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a diverse cohort of undergraduate and graduate students.  Northeastern University is an equal opportunity employer, seeking to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff.  Northeastern values and celebrates diversity in all its forms and strives to foster an inclusive culture built on respect that affirms inter-group relations and builds cohesion. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. To learn more about Northeastern University’s commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please see: Belonging at Northeastern . Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $101,000.00 - $119,000.00 | Associate Professor: $117,000.00 - $155,000.00 | Professor: $148,000.00 - $215,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Director-of-Global-Finance-Operations_R142486": {
      "title": "Director of Global Finance Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Director of Global Operations serves as the primary operational lead for Northeastern University's international finance and compliance function, reporting to the AVP of Tax. This role is responsible for ensuring the integrity, timeliness, and accuracy of financial operations across the University's international entities in the United Kingdom, Ireland, India, and Canada, and is the central point of coordination for all cross-jurisdictional compliance obligations. The Director partners closely with the AVP and University Controller, legal, treasury, and institutional leadership at each global location to ensure that statutory, regulatory, and operational requirements are met in each jurisdiction. The role requires both technical depth across international accounting and tax disciplines and the operational discipline to manage multiple concurrent compliance cycles across diverse legal and regulatory environments.     Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Accounting, Finance, or a related field required. CPA, CA, or equivalent professional certification strongly preferred. A minimum of 7–10 years of progressive experience in international finance, tax, or accounting, with direct exposure to multi-jurisdictional compliance environments. Prior experience in higher education, nonprofit, or professional services (public accounting) is preferred. Strong working knowledge of VAT/GST regimes, transfer pricing concepts, statutory reporting requirements, and international treasury operations. Experience with Workday Finance or a comparable ERP system preferred. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple concurrent priorities across geographically distributed teams and third-party service providers. Excellent communication, organizational, and project management skills.       Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities International Tax and Other Compliance Serve as the operational lead for all international tax compliance obligations across the University's global entities, including corporate tax filings, VAT/GST returns, withholding tax obligations, and transfer pricing documentation. Coordinate with external tax advisors in each jurisdiction to ensure timely and accurate filing. Monitor regulatory developments across active jurisdictions and assess impact on the University's compliance posture and financial reporting. Support the AVP of Tax in developing and maintaining the University's global tax strategy and intercompany pricing policies.   Maintain a comprehensive global compliance calendar covering all tax, statutory, payroll, regulatory, and reporting deadlines across active jurisdictions through partnership with local consultants and stay informed about upcoming changes in requirements. Proactively escalate risks to upcoming deadlines or required changes and coordinate remediation as needed. Serve as the institutional knowledge owner for each jurisdiction's compliance obligations, ensuring continuity and consistency as the University's global footprint evolves. Support onboarding of new international locations by establishing compliance frameworks and local service provider relationships from inception.   Coordinate with the University's dedicated legal resource on ongoing corporate governance obligations for each international entity, including annual confirmation statements and Companies House filings in the UK, CRO filings in Ireland, MCA annual returns and board resolutions in India, and equivalent requirements in other active jurisdictions. Serve as the finance function's primary liaison on legal entity matters, ensuring that finance-related obligations — registered office, financial year-end, authorized signatories, and statutory accounts filing — are tracked and met in coordination with legal. Support new entity formation from a finance perspective, including opening of bank accounts, establishment of local registrations, and configuration of Workday Finance (company codes, cost centers, currency settings, intercompany eliminations) in coordination with the systems team.   Partner with the International Payroll Manager and Payroll Director to ensure that international payroll compliance obligations are met on a timely basis across all active jurisdictions, including PAYE and National Insurance in the UK, PRSI and USC in Ireland, and PF, ESI, and TDS obligations in India. The Director serves as the cross-functional coordination point between payroll, tax, and local statutory requirements — ensuring that payroll-related tax registrations, year-end filings, and employer compliance obligations are factored into the global compliance calendar and escalated appropriately.   Oversee finance-side compliance with OFAC regulations and applicable sanctions regimes for all international payments, including vendor disbursements, intercompany funding transfers, and research-related wire transfers. Coordinate with legal and the University's compliance function to ensure that payment screening processes are in place and consistently applied. Monitor regulatory developments affecting international payment flows and escalate identified exposure to the AVP of Tax and AVP & University Controller.   Ensure that finance operations across international entities comply with applicable data privacy regulations, including UK GDPR and EU GDPR, as they relate to employee payroll data, student financial data, and vendor records. Coordinate with the University's data privacy and legal functions to ensure that cross-border data transfers involving financial information are appropriately documented and controlled. Serve as the finance function's liaison on international IT systems and technology compliance matters, including ensuring that financial systems, data storage, and reporting tools used in each jurisdiction meet local regulatory requirements for data residency, retention, and access controls. Coordinate with the University's IT and information security teams to ensure that Workday Finance and related systems are configured appropriately for each international entity and that technology used by international finance staff complies with applicable local regulations. Identify and escalate technology gaps — including jurisdictions where primary University systems are not available or fully operational — and support the development of compliant interim solutions in coordination with IT, legal, and the Controller's office.   Serve as the finance function's liaison to the University's Risk Management office on all matters related to international entity coverage, ensuring that appropriate insurance is in place — including general liability, employer's liability, directors and officers, property, and lease-related coverage — prior to operations commencing in each new jurisdiction. Maintain awareness of entity-level coverage requirements as the University's international footprint evolves and coordinate with Risk Management to address gaps or changes in coverage resulting from new entity formations, expansions, changes in local regulatory requirements, or new real estate commitments.   Coordinate the finance workstream for international real estate transactions, including lease negotiations, local property registration requirements, and lease accounting under ASC 842. Ensure that new international leases are properly evaluated for accounting treatment, reflected accurately in Workday Finance, and compliant with local regulatory requirements governing commercial tenancy in each jurisdiction. Liaise with legal, facilities, and local service providers on lease execution, renewal, and exit processes, ensuring that financial obligations — including local stamp duties, lease registration fees, dilapidations provisions, and security deposit requirements — are identified and accounted for. Maintain a global lease register for all international locations and support periodic impairment and right-of-use asset reviews in coordination with the Controller's office.   In coordination with HR and the International Payroll Manager, ensure that statutory employee benefit obligations are met across all active international jurisdictions, including UK pension auto-enrollment, Irish statutory sick pay and pension obligations, India Provident Fund and Employee State Insurance contributions, and equivalent requirements in other active locations. Maintain a benefits compliance register by jurisdiction and ensure that new entity setups include a complete assessment of statutory benefit obligations prior to the commencement of employment. Coordinate with HR and local service providers to ensure benefit structures are compliant with local law and aligned with University policy.   Serve as a key operational partner for new international market entries, coordinating across legal, HR, payroll, and institutional leadership to ensure finance and compliance workstreams are complete and well-documented. Support the AVP of Tax and AVP and University Controller in preparing materials for the CFO, Board of Trustees, and external stakeholders on the University's global compliance posture.   Statutory Audit Coordination Manage the end-to-end statutory audit process for all international entities, including preparation of jurisdiction-specific financial statements, coordination of local external auditors, and resolution of audit queries. Ensure statutory accounts are filed on time in each jurisdiction in accordance with local requirements. Serve as the primary liaison between local finance staff or service providers and the Controller's office for all audit-related matters. Track audit timelines and maintain a rolling compliance calendar across all entities. Treasury & Cash Management Oversee day-to-day treasury operations for international entities, including cash flow monitoring, intercompany funding, and bank relationship management at the local level. Coordinate with the University's central treasury function on foreign currency exposure, repatriation of funds, and liquidity management across global locations. Ensure appropriate controls over international bank accounts and signatories. Support the development of cash flow forecasts for each international entity in coordination with local finance staff. Procurement & Accounts Payable Compliance Partner with the University's procurement function to ensure that international purchasing activity complies with local regulatory requirements, including VAT reclaim procedures, import/export controls, and local vendor requirements. Oversee accounts payable operations at international locations where no dedicated local finance staff exists, utilizing third-party providers as appropriate. Ensure the University's procurement policies and internal controls are consistently applied across all global sites.   In coordination with the AVP of Tax, draft and maintain intercompany service agreements governing the allocation of shared services costs — including technology, HR, finance, and administrative services — to international entities. Establish and document the policy basis for intercompany charges consistent with arm's-length principles and transfer pricing documentation. Maintain an intercompany agreement register and coordinate periodic reviews as the University's global service model evolves. Ensure intercompany balances are reconciled and eliminated appropriately in consolidation.   Financial Reporting Oversee the preparation of periodic financial reports for each international entity, ensuring accuracy and consistency with the University's chart of accounts and Workday Finance structure. Coordinate with the Accounting team to support timely consolidation and intercompany eliminations. Prepare management reporting packages for international locations and support the AVP and University Controller in presenting financial results to institutional leadership. Ensure compliance with applicable accounting standards (US GAAP for consolidation; local GAAP or IFRS for statutory purposes) in each jurisdiction.   Coordinate with business partners to help support the foundational data for managerial reporting and understand the reconciling items between managerial and statutory reporting. Proactively engage business owners on emerging financial issues, compliance deadlines, and operational changes that carry financial implications to ensure the systems in place to capture data will still appropriately capture data to facilitate statutory/managerial reporting.   Position Type Finance Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 114S Expected Hiring Range: $130,945.00 - $189,868.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Doctor-of-Law-and-Policy--Online-_R142374": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Doctor of Law and Policy (Online)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a non-tenure track part time faculty lecturer in our Doctor of Law and Policy program on our Online campus. The successful candidate will teach online graduate level courses in the Doctor of Law and Policy program, within Northeastern University's College of Professional Studies. The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Teach graduate online courses within the Doctor of Law and Policy degree program. Courses may include but are not limited to: law and legal reasoning, law and policy, public policy theory and practice, research methods, or thesis development. Teaching one or more 15-week (Fall, Spring, or Summer) online courses per academic year. Courses meet online. This appointment includes pre-term course development (including weekly readings, assignments, and multimedia lectures), and in-term commitments of monitoring online discussion boards, general course management, grading, online presence, and direct online engagement one-on-one with students. Training in Canvas (the online course platform) will be provided. Qualifications The ideal instructor will have a JD or a PhD in a relevant social science discipline and research experience. A history of successful teaching at the graduate level and experience teaching online is strongly preferred. Candidates should demonstrate familiarity with doctoral level research, adult learners, and the application of research to professional settings. Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter. Please direct questions to Charles Keckler at c.keckler@northeastern.edu. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The per credit rate is $2,789.42."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Nahant-MA/Bioinformatician---Lotterhos-Lab_R142449": {
      "title": "Bioinformatician - Lotterhos Lab",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity   About the Center for Marine Ecological Genomics The successful candidate will be based at the Lotterhos Lab and the Center for Marine Ecological Genomics at Northeastern Marine Science Center in Nahant, Massachusetts. This is an in-person position. Job Summary We are seeking a skilled Bioinformatician to join our consortium in Marine Macrogenomics, which seeks to create a database of interoperable population genomic data for 5,000 genomes from 100 species. The successful candidate will play a central role in developing and maintaining scalable computational pipelines for the analysis of large-scale genomic datasets, with a focus on short-read alignment and population-level inference. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to cutting-edge research in ecological and conservation genomics. Minimum Qualifications Required Master's degree in Biology, Ecology and Evolution, Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, Genetics, or a related field 2-5 years of hands-on experience in bioinformatics, with demonstrated work in genomics Proficiency in short-read alignment tools such as BWA, Bowtie2, and STAR Familiarity with downstream processing tools such as SAMtools, Genome Analysis Toolkit (GATK), and Picard Experience writing pipelines using workflow management systems such as Snakemake or Nextflow Solid scripting skills in Python, R, and Bash Comfort working on the command line, including in a Unix/Linux environment Familiarity with high-performance computing (HPC) and high-throughput computing (HTC) environments and job schedulers such as SLURM and HTCondor Experience using application programming interfaces (APIs) to send and receive data and/or experience using tools to transfer large amounts of data between remote servers Preferred PhD or 3 years of post-Master's experience preferred Experience with population genomic analyses and tools such as ANGSD, PLINK, ADMIXTURE, TreeMix, or similar platforms Familiarity with handling non-model organism genomes or low-coverage sequencing data Experience with version control systems such as Git and containerization technologies including Docker and Singularity/Apptainer Track record of contributing to published research Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Collaborate and coordinate with members of the network, including wet lab and computational team members, to define data standards, ensure analytical reproducibility, and assist with project goals Design, build, and maintain reproducible bioinformatics pipelines capable of scaling across many population genomic datasets Align short-read sequencing data, including whole-genome and Restriction Site Associated DNA Sequencing (RADseq) datasets, to reference genomes using standard and emerging tools, and evaluate mapping quality across diverse datasets Perform downstream population genomic analyses, including variant calling, filtering, and quality control Document pipelines and analyses clearly for internal use and publication Develop educational tutorials and assist with website development Collate publicly available data and metadata, corresponding with authors as necessary to ensure data standards Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Doctor-of-Law-and-Policy--Boston-_R142390": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Doctor of Law and Policy (Boston)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a non-tenure track part time faculty lecturer in our Doctor of Law and Policy program on the Boston campus. The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Courses may include: law and legal reasoning, law and policy, public policy theory and practice, research methods, or thesis development. Teaching one or more 15-week (Fall, Spring, or Summer) hybrid courses per academic year. Courses meet online and up to two weekends per semester at the Boston campus. This appointment includes pre-term course development (including weekly readings, assignments, and multimedia lectures) and in-term commitments such as monitoring online discussion boards, general course management, grading, online presence, and face-to-face participation at monthly course sessions. We will provide training in Canvas (the online course platform). **Courses meet online as well as up to two weekends (Fri-Sun) a semester at the Boston campus. Qualifications The ideal instructor will have a JD or a PhD in a relevant social science discipline, or both, and research experience. A history of successful teaching at the graduate level and experience teaching online is strongly preferred. Candidates should demonstrate familiarity with doctoral-level research, adult learners, and the application of research to professional settings. Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter. Please direct questions to Charles Keckler at c.keckler@northeastern.edu. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The per credit rate is $2,789.42."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Director-of-Outreach---Programming_R142454": {
      "title": "Director of Outreach & Programming",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Summary Reporting to the Assistant Vice President for Community Relations, the Director of Outreach and Programming will develop, coordinate, administer, and evaluate community programs and special projects conducted by the Office of City & Community Engagement (CCE) at Northeastern University. In collaboration with the Community Relations and Programs Manager and other CCE staff, this position will be an active and responsible contributor to our local and campus communities by fostering collaborative relationships and partnerships that connect, educate, and engage people and organizations throughout our network. The Director of Outreach and Programming will lead CCE’s programs and partnership initiatives with three focus areas: hosting residents and community groups on campus, managing small business and neighborhood association partnerships, and delivering programs and offerings open to the public, both on and off campus. The position involves collaborating with various stakeholders and cultivating existing relationships to work towards Northeastern’s vision of becoming a model institution for community engagement. This position also works closely with CCE leadership to set and meet the department’s budget and program oriented goals. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree required; Master’s degree preferred 5+ years of relevant experience in community/civic engagement, community development, program management and strategic planning for a higher education institution or nonprofit organization Be idea-driven with the ability to take creative initiative and demonstrate follow- through Be comfortable working in a fast-paced environment with different personalities Problem-solve and resolve conflicts and disputes in a professional manner Be relationship-oriented and results-driven Communicate effectively to various internal and external stakeholder Possess the ability to handle confidential information in a sensitive manner Have strong abilities in planning and running programs Have strong abilities in organization, supervision, administration, and budget management Willing and able to work occasional nights and weekends Excellent verbal and written communication skills with exceptional attention to details Have working knowledge of the Boston community with special attention to Roxbury, Mission Hill, Fenway, and the South End Key Responsibilities Generate and facilitate partnerships and programs that respond to the needs, interests, and concerns of the residents, businesses, and other community organizations surrounding Northeastern’s Boston campus. Refine existing, and/or establish new systems, processes and tools for tracking, collecting and sharing the outcomes, measurements and knowledge that is generated by those programs and partnerships. Oversee program creation and development, with a focus on establishing new and innovative pathways for internal and external collaboration. Regularly attend relevant events and other engagement activities that foster deeper engagement with the local community and other stakeholders in the community engagement space. Assist in developing an external identity for Northeastern’s community programs. Assist in developing the outreach strategy for the Northeastern Crossing and future community-specific spaces on campus. Support CCE staff in planning and implementing new & existing programs. Perform other duties as assigned. Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-Associate-Professor-Professor--Open-Rank---School-of-Law---Health-Policy-and-Law_R142405": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor (Open Rank), School of Law – Health Policy and Law",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Northeastern University School of Law is seeking candidates at all ranks for full-time tenured or tenure-track faculty positions to join our scholarly community and enhance our leadership in training lawyers who make a difference in their chosen fields. We seek candidates whose research, teaching, professional experience, and/or service have prepared them to contribute to our unwavering commitment to the rule of law. Particular focus will be paid to candidates with experience and interest in health policy and law, specifically population/public health, disability, ethics and the law; as well as a deep understanding of law and policy as it relates to Medicare, Medicaid, the Affordable Care Act, universal health insurance; and public health challenges and societal health risks influencing healthcare accessibility policy and law. Opportunities are available for candidates whose background and qualifications might allow for a faculty appointment entirely within the law school or jointly within the law school and another department in Bouvé College of Health Sciences. The School of Law is a national leader in experiential legal education, with a deep commitment to law in service of the public interest and to engaged scholarship and interdisciplinary collaboration. In making faculty appointments, the School of Law seeks to enhance its innovative curriculum, which combines experiential learning with rigorous analytical training and interdisciplinarity. The Bouvé College of Health Sciences has over 250 faculty members, with approximately 2,000 undergraduate and 2,400 graduate students. It is the leading national model for education and research in the health, psychosocial and biomedical sciences and supports the University’s mission of educating students for a life of fulfillment and accomplishment and creating and translating knowledge to meet global and societal needs.  The search committee is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute to the excellence of the academic community through their research, teaching, and/or service. We also encourage applicants to share how their background and experience will contribute to the Northeastern community. Responsibilities: Faculty duties will include teaching law courses at the Juris Doctor (JD), Master of Laws (LLM), graduate or undergraduate levels, advising students, conducting scholarly research, and providing service to the Law School, the University, and the legal profession. Qualifications: Required Qualifications: Applicants must hold a Juris Doctor (JD) or foreign equivalent from an accredited institution by the employment start date. Academic rank at the Associate Professor and Full Professor levels will be commensurate with experience and qualifications reflecting a record of demonstrated scholarly and teaching excellence. Appointments at the Associate Professor rank require a minimum of 3 years of related experience. Appointments at the Full Professor rank require a minimum of 6 years of related experience. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated scholarship, teaching, or professional experience in health policy and law, health care regulation, or public health law; experience with interdisciplinary collaboration across law, medicine, public health, or health administration; and a record of, or clear potential for, engagement with policymakers or health care institutions. Documents to submit: CV, Cover Letter, References upon request Additional Information: If you have questions, please contact the faculty appointments administrator, Stephen Evans at s.evans@northeastern.edu or Appointments Committee Chair, Professor Jeremy Paul at j.paul@northeastern.edu. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Professor: $150,000.00 - $172,000.00 | Associate Professor: $157,000.00 - $198,000.00 | Professor: $221,000.00 - $247,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Residence-Director_R142318": {
      "title": "Residence Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: Reporting to one of the Senior Assistant Directors, Residence Directors (RDs) at Northeastern University, play a critical role in fostering a vibrant, inclusive, and supportive community for all students. This position's critical emphasis is being a highly visible and strong presence in your residential communities. This position is student-facing and requires a dynamic individual with excellent communication skills, a passion for student development, and the ability to manage crises effectively. The RDs will work closely with students, staff, and faculty, to create a welcoming environment that promotes student engagement and a sense of belonging.  Due to the live-in requirements of the Residence Director position, it is required that the on-campus apartment provided must be the individual’s primary residence and that person must maintain a high visibility within their residential areas of responsibility. Offices are also located in the residence area. All RDs participate in an on-call duty rotation and require the individual to work untraditional hours, including evening hours and weekends.  The Residence Director is a 12-month, full-time position. Summer responsibilities include working on special projects, with conferences, orientation, and summer students.  The Residence Director can serve in this role for five years.  Qualifications: Education: Skills and knowledge are normally acquired through a Bachelor’s Degree. Master’s degree in Student Affairs, Social Work, Education, or a related field preferred. Experience: A minimum of 2 years of experience in student life, community engagement, and/or a related field. Student supervision experience is required.   Skills: Strong interpersonal and communication skills, crisis management abilities, event planning experience, and a commitment to belonging and inclusion.  Responsibilities Include: Organize and facilitate events, programs, and activities that foster community engagement and build a sense of belonging among students.  In conjunction with campus partners develop and implement leadership training and development programs for student leaders and mentors.  Serve as a hearing officer for Residential Life- hold administrative hearings with students regarding alleged policy violations, adjudicate cases, assign sanctions and make referrals when necessary. Serve in a scheduled on-call rotation to respond to student emergencies and crises, providing immediate support and intervention as needed.  Mediate conflicts between students, facilitating discussions to resolve disputes and promote an inclusive and supportive living environment.  Collect and analyze student feedback to continuously improve community engagement efforts and address student needs.  Supervise a team of Resident Assistants.   Assist in managing budgets for community engagement programs and events. Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106S Expected Hiring Range: $48,610.00 - $66,838.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Social-News-Producer_R142331": {
      "title": "Senior Social News Producer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Northeastern University is looking for an experienced video producer for its daily social-first news and storytelling content for TikTok, Instagram, and other vertical video platforms. The Sr. Social News Producer is responsible for creating high-impact multimedia content focused on news, information, and entertainment that is geared toward various audiences and promotes Northeastern University’s brand and academic mission. The role involves conceptualizing, shooting, and editing fast-turnaround content that showcases breaking news stories and university news in real time.   The Senior Social News Producer regularly appears on camera as part of a rotating team, and also brings the technical range to shoot longer-form content as needed, including podcast-style interviews and executive-level video projects. This is a hands-on production role, not a supervisory one.   The role reports to the Creative Director for Social Video and part of the Communications division. The position is based on the Boston campus and is fully on site, five days a week. As is the case for all members of the Communications team, after-hours work, including evenings and weekends is often required.   Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree or equivalent work experience. 3-5 years shooting and editing in a professional environment, with a strong portfolio of vertical, social-first video, including news content. Proficiency in Adobe Creative Suite and/or DaVinci Resolve. Demonstrated ability to shoot and edit longer-form video (10+ minutes), including interview-style and multi-camera setups, and condense those into consumable, social-forward content Strong on-camera presence and experience appearing in social video content. Deep, current knowledge of social platform trends, news cycles, and how to translate fast-moving stories into engaging vertical video. Ability to work with minimal direction, set priorities, and mentor less experienced team members informally. Excellent attention to detail while maintaining speed and consistency under deadline pressure. Excellent work ethic and positive attitude. Sense of humor. Great if you also have: Familiarity with social media analytical tools (Sprinklr, Google Analytics, etc.) Experience producing or shooting podcast-style video content. Familiarity with AP style. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Conceptualize, shoot, and edit daily social-first news and storytelling content, primarily for TikTok and Instagram. Set the visual and technical quality bar for the team's vertical video output. Lead production on longer-form content as needed, including podcast-style shoots and executive/leadership video projects. Appear on camera regularly as part of a rotating team. Stay ahead of platform trends and news cycles to identify timely content opportunities. Informally guide and give feedback to less experienced junior content creators on technique and editorial judgment. Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Director-of-Social-Media_R142329": {
      "title": "Director of Social Media",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary   We are hiring a Director of Social Media to drive our global social media strategy. The person in this role is an innovative and creative leader who has the skills and vision to build our brand presence across all platforms with a highly data-informed and analytical approach. The Director of Social Media leads a global team with creators in multiple cities and two countries to develop and execute platform-specific strategies that promote Northeastern by growing audience engagement around various types of content.   Higher education experience is not needed.   The Director of Social Media is a superb content creator and strategist who is on top of the latest trends and understands how to leverage platforms and content to both inform and entertain, while building engaged communities. The person in this role lives and breathes social media, a superb data analyst, and has a track record of meeting ambitious goals.   The Director of Social Media has a track record of leveraging AI-powered tools to develop and execute the most innovative strategies and track progress toward clear goals.   The Director of Social Media is a key member of the External Affairs team, works closely with its senior leaders across the university, and collaborates with colleagues across divisions and locations.   As the leader of a global team, the Director of Social Media must work across time zones and set location-specific strategies that build Northeastern’s brand presence in-market and worldwide.   In addition to creating original and innovative content tailored to each platform, the Director of Social Media coordinates and collaborates on all social-first content creation, including photo and video production, and works with the editorial, photography, video and media relations teams to translate reporting by Northeastern Global News (NGN) to social networks.   A crucial piece of the university’s social media strategy is the 24/7 monitoring and analytics across all relevant platforms, including timely alerts to the appropriate stakeholders, and regular reports capturing critical moments and trends.   The Director of Social Media helps set brand standards and governance guidelines across the global university system and is experienced in social media monitoring for brand management, including the effective use of analytics tools like Sprinklr.   As social media never sleeps, the Director of Social Media must be responsive, fast, thoughtful and creative at a moment's notice regardless of the time of day or day of the week. Crisis and opportunities don't and can't wait. After-hours work, including weekends, holidays and events is routinely required.    The position is based on the Boston campus and is fully in-person.   Minimum Qualifications: 4-6 years setting and executing social strategy for brands — listening, analytics, and content creation across formats and platforms. Experience leading creator teams across platforms and time zones. A sharp, strategic command of data and analytics Real experience using AI-powered tools to build and execute standout brand strategy and track it against ambitious goals. A proven ability to grow and sustain engagement. A track record of setting and hitting measurable goals. Writing chops — you understand tone, voice, and the difference one word makes. Deep fluency in every major platform, and genuine curiosity about what's next. A bias toward using data to make decisions. Experience designing and running audience engagement campaigns with measurable results. A positive, high-energy attitude. Excellent communication skills — up, down, and across the organization. A relentless work ethic and obsessive attention to detail. The ability to multitask at speed without ever dropping the ball. Wit.     Key Accountabilities & responsibilities : Strategy and Execution 24/7 Reporting and Analytics Global Team Leadership and Collaboration Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 111S Expected Hiring Range: $87,785.00 - $123,998.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director---Academic-Programs_R142224": {
      "title": "Assistant Director - Academic Programs",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary   The Assistant Director of Academic Programs creates and executes a strategic plan to grow and enhance the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering graduate programs. This role advises Master of Science (MS) and PhD students on their academic programs and serves as an integral partner to the Graduate and Undergraduate Faculty Committees in advancing initiatives and enhancing graduate education. Responsibilities include managing graduate admissions processes and data, serving as a liaison between the department and the Graduate School, overseeing course scheduling on a semester basis and for the department’s five-year growth plan, developing workshops and events that enhance the student experience, managing course assessment activities related to accreditation, providing guidance to faculty and PhD students regarding teaching assistant workload and support, and facilitating academic projects through collaboration with partners across and beyond the college. This position is dedicated primarily to the department’s graduate programs (MS, PlusOne, and PhD), with undergraduate-facing responsibilities managed by the Academic Coordinator supervised by this role. This position follows a hybrid schedule, with three days on site and two days remote each week. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Academic Program Operations and Recruitment Manage department policies related to student matters. Oversee course scheduling for each academic term and maintain the department’s five-year teaching plan. Oversee annual catalog submissions and catalog changes. Manage TRACE scores and mid-year and annual reviews for PhD students and faculty. Manage recruitment activities associated with MS, PlusOne, and PhD applications. Serve as the primary point of contact for program inquiries. Coordinate and administer orientation programs, welcome events, Graduate Candidates Day, and other graduate recruitment and onboarding activities. Serve as a liaison between the college and department for student-facing events and matters. Student Data Management Manage tracking processes for PhD student milestones, including qualifying examinations, work-in-progress reviews, proposal defenses, and dissertation defenses. Interact directly with graduate students and monitor timelines related to advisor assignments and reassignments, committee formation, qualifying examinations, proposals, and defense arrangements. Serve as the primary contact for MS students with questions regarding course scheduling and academic timelines. Manage student enrollment and tracking data and serve as the department contact for student-related data management. Program Development Manage processes related to the development of new MS programs, combined majors and minors, and new research concentrations for the PhD program. Administer approval processes for new courses, concentrations, and academic programs. Collaborate with faculty and administrators across departments and colleges to manage the development and implementation of new academic programs. Document program changes, approval processes, and voting outcomes associated with new programs. Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor’s degree or equivalent. Minimum of four years of experience supporting academic and administrative projects, preferably in higher education, with adult learners, or in a high customer service environment. Strong project management and organizational skills with keen attention to detail and a commitment to quality. Effective interpersonal, oral presentation, and written communication skills. Ability to balance multiple priorities and respond effectively to the needs of diverse constituencies. Ability to consistently produce high-quality work under tight deadlines. Professional presence and maturity when interacting with audiences internal and external to the university. Positive, proactive attitude with flexibility, initiative, and the ability to function effectively as a member of a team in a dynamic environment. Demonstrated responsiveness to feedback, commitment to continuous improvement, and high integrity. Preferred Experience with academic coordination. Master’s degree. Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Community-Assistant_R141964": {
      "title": "Community Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: The Community Assistant is an entry-level, live-in staff member responsible for directly supporting the physical, emotional, social, and academic needs of first-year residential students. This position serves as a vital member of the Residential Life team, creating positive and valuable experiential learning opportunities that aid students in their academic coursework, help orient them to the Bay Area, and foster community building within residential and campus life. Community Assistants work collaboratively with Student Life and campus staff to maintain a safe, inclusive, and educationally purposeful residential environment that supports student success and personal development.   CORE RESPONSIBILITIES  Student Support & Guidance  Provide direct guidance and support to first-year students navigating their transition to college life and studying away from home  Conduct regular one-on-one check-ins with assigned residents each semester to assess academic, social, and personal well-being  Respond to student concerns promptly and connect students to appropriate academic, medical, and/or mental health resources  Facilitate peer-to-peer connections and support networks within the residential community  Document student interactions and concerns through established case Collaborate with Student Life and campus staff on student development initiatives and intervention strategies  Safety & Crisis Response  Serve as part of an on-call rotation (including after hours and overnight) for student residences to respond to incidents and emergencies  Maintain the safety and well-being of the residential population through regular community walks and community presence  Respond to incidents by following established protocols and coordinating with campus public safety and other resources when needed  Act as primary point of contact for students in residences during emergencies  Write accurate and timely incident reports documenting emergency situations and policy violations  Conduct post-incident follow-up with affected students and staff as directed  Complete routine room inspections to document and address potential Maintain current knowledge of emergency procedures and campus safety resources  Community Development & Programming  Plan, coordinate, and implement diverse student programs, activities, and excursions that are educational, social, and/or recreational and enhance the residential experience. Programming may include evenings and weekends Create programming that helps students explore the Bay Area and develop cultural awareness  Facilitate community-building activities that foster inclusive and engaging residential environments  Responsibly manage programming materials, supplies, and basic budget allocations  Assess program effectiveness through participant feedback and attendance tracking  Partner with campus departments and local organizations, where appropriate, to enhance programming opportunities  Community Standards & Policy Enforcement  Monitor and uphold student behavioral and housing policies and protocols while maintaining high standards of conduct  Address policy violations through educational conversations and appropriate referrals  Document student conduct concerns and collaborate with supervisor on resolution strategies  Serve as a positive role model for community standards and expectations  Facilitate conflict resolution between residents when appropriate  Assist residents in understanding and navigating university policies and procedures  Administrative Responsibilities  Assist with day-to-day operational needs within Housing & Residential Life  Assist with residential opening and closing processes at the beginning and end of academic terms  Prepare and submit regular updates to supervisor, as requested, on student concerns, community issues, and programming outcomes  Attend staff meetings, trainings, and professional development sessions  Other duties as assigned  QUALIFICATIONS: Required Qualifications  Bachelor's degree  1-2 years of demonstrated student service experience or higher education experience in housing, residence life, or student affairs  Experience living with or working with high school or undergraduate students (residence life, student affairs, camp counselor, or similar role)  Experience working with sensitive and private information and maintaining confidentiality  Sound judgment and analytical skills for problem solving and decision-making  Ability to work under pressure and respond effectively to crisis situations  Ability to work effectively in a culturally diverse environment  Ability to work collaboratively in a team environment and build relationships with diverse stakeholders (colleagues, campus partners, students, families, etc.)  Experience managing, facilitating, and coordinating events for large groups with enthusiasm and attention to detail  Fluent in English with excellent written and verbal communication skills  Availability to work evenings, weekends, and participate in on-call rotation  Proficiency with basic office technology and computer systems (Microsoft 365)  Preferred Qualifications  Previous residential life or live-in staff experience Training in crisis intervention, mental health first aid, or conflict resolution  Experience with case management or student conduct software (Maxient)  Physical, Mental, and Environmental Demands  Work regularly involves weekend, evening, and overnight availability, and responsibilities may often extend outside traditional business hours  Work may involve exposure to distressing situations requiring emotional resilience and professional boundaries  Work often requires flexibility in terms of time, and physical demands  Work often requires walking moderate distances and climbing/descending stairs regularly  Work often includes navigating various terrains including hills, pathways, and outdoor areas in all weather conditions  Work often involves interruptions and meetings with a variety of people  Work occasionally requires sitting for long hours and using computers  Work occasionally requires physical exertion such as lifting heavy objects up to 50 pounds Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 105H Expected Hiring Range: $22.85 - $31.42 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Director--Fund-Management_R142293": {
      "title": "Senior Director, Fund Management",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Senior Director of Fund Management is a senior leadership role responsible for the oversight and integration of gift processing, donor record management, fund management, and donor due diligence within Advancement Resources. Ensures the integrity, compliance, and strategic alignment of advancement operations to support institutional fundraising goals. The Senior Director leads multiple functional teams and establishes policies, procedures, and controls to ensure adherence to IRS regulations, CASE standards, donor intent, and university policies. Oversees donor due diligence and gift acceptance practices, assessing reputational, financial, and legal risk associated with philanthropic contributions. The Senior Director partners closely with Advancement leadership, Finance, Legal, and Technology teams to ensure compliant, efficient, and scalable operations across the advancement enterprise. Minimum Qualifications Knowledge and skills required for this position are typically acquired through the completion of a Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s degree preferred (Business, Nonprofit Management, Finance, or related field) with 10+ years of progressive experience in advancement services, gift administration, fund management, or related field Demonstrated leadership experience managing multiple functional teams Deep knowledge of fundraising operations, gift processing, and donor database management (Salesforce/CRM preferred) Strong understanding of IRS regulations, CASE guidelines, and nonprofit accounting principles Experience with gift acceptance policies, donor due diligence, and compliance frameworks in higher education or nonprofit environments Proven ability to manage complex, cross-functional initiatives and institutional risk considerations Exceptional analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills Strong communication skills with the ability to advise senior leadership Key Accountabilities & Responsibilities Leadership & Oversight of Gift Administration, Records & Fund Management Provide strategic oversight of gift processing, donor record management, and fund administration. Ensure accurate fund setup and management in alignment with donor intent and institutional policies. Allocate resources and set priorities across teams to support efficient and compliant operations. Gift Processing & Compliance Management Ensure all gift transactions are processed accurately and in compliance with IRS regulations, CASE standards, and university policies. Oversee complex gift handling and maintain policies and procedures that support audit readiness and regulatory compliance. Donor Due Diligence & Gift Acceptance Oversight Lead donor due diligence and gift acceptance processes to assess reputational, financial, and legal risk. Establish protocols for evaluating complex or high-risk gifts and partner with leadership, Legal, and Prospect Development on decision-making. Data Integrity & Donor Record Management Oversee the accuracy and integrity of constituent records within the CRM. Implement constituent data governance standards, ensure proper record creation and maintenance, and support constituent data needs across advancement functions. Financial Integration, Reporting & Audit Readiness Oversee reconciliation between Advancement and Finance systems, ensuring accurate financial reporting. Monitor gift feeds, resolve discrepancies, and support audit processes and reporting requirements. Operational Integration & Process Improvement Lead efforts to standardize and improve advancement operations, including process optimization, system enhancements, and integration across campuses or units. Position Type Advancement Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 114S Expected Hiring Range: With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Administrative-Coordinator_R142275": {
      "title": "Administrative Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Reporting directly to the Dean of the School of Nursing (SON), the Administrative Coordinator provides high-level administrative support to faculty, staff, and students while working independently with minimal supervision. Responsibilities include coordinating meetings and schedules, managing meeting technology, maintaining departmental budgets, overseeing office operations and equipment, processing purchase orders, managing communications, handling departmental correspondence and records, answering department phones, and ordering office supplies. The Administrative Coordinator also supports workshops, conferences, seminars, and other School of Nursing events. This role performs varied and sometimes complex administrative and operational responsibilities that require initiative, creativity, sound judgment, and strong problem-solving skills. The Administrative Coordinator serves as a key resource for the School of Nursing, providing support to stakeholders and helping resolve issues efficiently. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Executive Administrative Support (35%) Manage the School of Nursing Dean’s calendar, including scheduling meetings, appointments, and travel arrangements. Prepare meeting agendas, materials, and minutes and ensure appropriate follow-up on action items. Draft, edit, and coordinate correspondence, reports, presentations, and other documents on behalf of the Dean. Handle confidential and sensitive information with a high degree of discretion and professionalism. General Administrative Support (20%) Provide administrative and operational support to School of Nursing faculty and students. Organize and schedule meetings and appointments. Track and maintain departmental budgets and related records. Maintain office equipment and coordinate operational support activities. Identify and implement improvements to processes, procedures, and systems within the department. Assist with budget preparation, monitoring, and reconciliation activities. Ensure departmental funds are appropriately utilized and accurately recorded. Provide budget updates and related information to the Dean of the School of Nursing. Supervision (15%) Supervise work-study students as assigned. Provide guidance, assign tasks, and monitor work quality to ensure departmental standards are met. Mentor and support students in developing skills and successfully completing assignments. Special Events (10%) Oversee event planning and execution for workshops, conferences, seminars, and other School of Nursing events. Provide leadership and guidance related to event development, communications, budgeting, logistics, and post-event evaluation. Assist with event budget preparation, monitoring, and reconciliation. Ensure event funds are utilized and recorded appropriately. Provide regular updates regarding budget status and event progress. Special Projects & Assignments (10%) Execute special assignments by assisting with planning, establishing objectives, coordinating activities, and ensuring successful completion. Collaborate with stakeholders to gain cooperation and facilitate project execution. Monitor project progress, resolve issues, and implement necessary adjustments to achieve desired outcomes. Data Gathering, Analysis & Reporting (10%) Collect, organize, and analyze data from multiple sources to support recordkeeping, reporting, and program management activities. Utilize data insights to provide analysis and recommendations. Identify and resolve issues with applications and systems to improve data accuracy and operational efficiency. Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor’s degree or equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum of 2-4 years of relevant professional experience. Advanced proficiency in creating documents, communications, presentations, spreadsheets, and ad hoc reports. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook. Experience updating online tools and managing special projects. Strong analytical skills and business knowledge with the ability to perform varied and moderately complex work. Demonstrated ability to manage competing priorities, make sound decisions, and work with minimal direction. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Excellent customer service, interpersonal, and communication skills. Ability to handle multiple responsibilities simultaneously while maintaining quality and accuracy. Ability to handle sensitive information and maintain a high level of confidentiality. Ability to establish priorities and work both independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Demonstrated initiative, persistence, professionalism, and business acumen. Required Documents Resume Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107H Expected Hiring Range: $25.78 - $36.41 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Seattle-WA/Associate-Dean-Campus-Admin_R142277": {
      "title": "Associate Dean-Campus Admin",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Located on the Seattle campus and reporting to the Dean & CEO, the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs & Campus Administration serves as a key member of the campus leadership team and is responsible for oversight and guidance of academic program delivery and innovation, compliance and regulatory matters, faculty affairs, student services, career advising, and program innovation. Working closely with the Dean, the Seattle campus team, and leaders across Northeastern’s Global University System, the Associate Dean leads the vision and implementation of initiatives designed to promote innovation in academic offerings, including co-curricular experiential learning opportunities. In partnership with college leadership and university stakeholders, the Associate Dean ensures the successful delivery of academic programs and an exceptional student experience. This role is responsible for supporting the development and implementation of new academic programs in collaboration with academic colleges and the global network, as well as leading strategic planning, implementation, and assessment efforts that advance the mission of the Seattle campus. The Seattle campus is advancing a five-year strategic initiative to launch multiple health science programs in partnership with Bouvé College of Health Sciences. This Health Hub expansion represents a major campus priority and will diversify the campus enrollment portfolio alongside its established Enterprise Technology graduate programs. The Associate Dean will play a critical role in supporting this expansion, partnering with Bouvé leadership on program readiness and ensuring health sciences programs are integrated into campus operations, student services, and the broader academic experience. The successful candidate will be an experienced leader who inspires teams, develops talent, drives strategic initiatives, and navigates ambiguity while maintaining a strong commitment to operational excellence, accountability, and student success. This position also requires strong crisis management, problem-solving, and change management capabilities. Minimum Qualifications Required Master’s degree or doctoral degree in education, leadership, or a related field. Minimum of 10 years of relevant experience in higher education. Demonstrated experience in respected leadership roles within an academic setting. Experience coaching, inspiring, mobilizing, and managing high-performing teams. Demonstrated ability to lead strategically and operationally in complex environments. Ability to successfully lead, motivate, and manage multidisciplinary staff groups and build effective teams and networks. Exceptional interpersonal and organizational skills, particularly in academic administration. Highly developed written and oral communication skills, including the ability to prepare detailed documents and memoranda. Proven track record of fostering a culture of innovation in a growing organization. Ability to thrive under pressure, exercise sound judgment, and work with minimal direction. Strong personal accountability and a self-starter mindset. Ability to function as an organizational ambassador, active listener, and facilitator. Ability to navigate difficult conversations effectively and professionally. Authentic communication style with the ability to engage individuals at all levels. Confidence balanced with humility. Preferred Proven track record of partnering with local academic institutions to deliver high-quality partnerships and events. Familiarity with health education and/or health education strategy. Prior experience with Veteran’s Administration compliance requirements and state-level regulatory approval processes. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Academic Stewardship Serve as a strategist for developing academic programs and recommending and implementing innovative curricula. Act as the primary liaison with college Deans, Associate Deans, Program Directors, academic personnel, and regional network colleagues to ensure programs align with local and regional workforce needs. Ensure faculty, staff, and students have access to the classroom resources, technology, and support necessary to deliver an outstanding learning and teaching experience. Serve as the primary point of contact and problem solver for Academic Advisors and more than 80 faculty members on the Seattle campus. Collaborate closely with counterparts across Northeastern’s Global University System to support academic excellence and program delivery. Strategic Planning Partner with the Dean and senior university leadership, including the Registrar and Enrollment Management teams, to identify strategic opportunities and priorities. Lead implementation efforts for strategic initiatives and establish appropriate success metrics. Manage the ongoing review and update of the campus strategic plan. People Management Oversee high-performing Student Services and Career Services teams to deliver an exceptional student experience. Collaborate with campus and network stakeholders to design and implement strategic plans related to student engagement, support, and success. Provide leadership, advocacy, and guidance while navigating complex situations and advancing student-centered initiatives. Faculty Partnership Convene faculty and staff to provide professional development opportunities. Foster a growth mindset focused on continuous improvement and stakeholder development. Support initiatives that elevate teaching effectiveness and enhance the student experience. Licensure, Compliance, and Accreditation Partner with stakeholders in Boston and Seattle to manage campus licensing, compliance, and accreditation requirements. Coordinate with national accreditors and state regulators to ensure compliance with applicable standards and regulations. Support ongoing regulatory and accreditation processes and related institutional requirements. Program Pre-Launch Support Support and oversee new and existing academic programs prior to launch. Partner with Boston-based colleges to ensure operational readiness for program implementation. Ensure appropriate resourcing, regulatory alignment, and adherence to institutional quality standards before program launch. Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 114S Expected Hiring Range: $130,945.00 - $209,510.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Bouv-Simulation-Center-Director_R140482": {
      "title": "Bouvé Simulation Center Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Reporting to the Assistant Dean of Clinical Skills and Simulation Education, the Simulation Center Director is responsible for the day-to-day operations of the Center including the prioritization, organization, and development of innovative lab programming and special projects. As a member of the Dean's Leadership Team, the Director will collaborate closely with the College Dean’s Office, Unit Leaders and Program Directors to design and deliver programming and optimize use of resources to meet program and College goals. The Director is responsible for operationalizing the Goldstein Simulation Center strategic plan that elevates simulation across the Bouvé College of Health Sciences in all locations of the Northeastern University global network and supports all units within the College. The position requires a high-level ability to organize priorities, work collaboratively across disciplines and campuses, and complete tasks in a timely manner. The successful candidate must be entrepreneurial, detail-oriented, and possess strong creative, leadership and organizational skills. The candidate must have in-depth simulation education knowledge and hands-on application. Qualifications : Master's degree in Healthcare, Nursing, Education, Instructional Technology, or a related field. Minimum of 5-7 years of related experience working with educators or with strong record of effective project and organizational/team management. Previous demonstrated experience in clinical care and education is preferred. Required Skill Sets: Ability to work effectively in a team environment with healthcare professionals. Knowledge of medical simulation and instructional technologies. Knowledge of medical terminology, human anatomy, physiology and clinical applications. Knowledge of project management theories, principles, practices, and processes. Computer proficiency in based software applications including Office 365 (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams, OneDrive). Strong organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Excellent written communication, verbal communication, interpersonal skills, and conflict management skills. Self-motivated and willing to learn new skills. Ability to prioritize work demands and work independently. Ability to carry out responsibilities in a timely and meticulous manner demonstrating initiative and creativity. Ability to coordinate training assignments, tasks, and schedules in a collaborative manner while ensuring timely work products and deliverables. Ability to maintain confidentiality regarding job assignments and sensitive issues. Ability to assess situations and adapt to highly dynamic environment. Ability to ask questions and course-correct when necessary. Responsibilities and Accountabilities: Goldstein Center Daily Operations (50%) Provide day-to-day operations of the Center including the organization, scheduling, development and delivery of innovative simulation programming, special projects, and research efforts Coordinate with units regarding programming, scheduling and proper use of Goldstein Center space Develop and maintain a prioritized calendar of daily/monthly activities each semester Maintain and sustain a simulation case repository, blueprint template, case review & approval processes, and simulation assessment (curricular and evaluation) in compliance with Society of Simulation in Healthcare (SSH) standards Create and maintain a system of metrics/dashboard for reporting use of the center and develop a reporting methodology to disseminate the data in annual reports as needed Maintain the Goldstein Simulation Center as a clean, safe, and operational facility and optimal learning environment Support Strategic and Programmatic Needs in the Goldstein Center (35%) Manage the Goldstein Simulation Center's operational and programmatic needs, including budgetary planning (day-to-day and annual), fiscal/business operations, resources, equipment, and personnel/team management Evaluate, implement learning management software to effectively operationalize 1) simulation development, 2) simulation offerings, 3) simulation quality assurance, 4) simulation continuous quality improvement, 5) trainee registration and participation tracking, 6) A/V archiving capabilities, and 7) simulation compliance tracking for program-specific accreditation requirements Procure appropriate simulation hardware and technology (e.g. computers/ LCDs/ cameras/ audio), patient simulators and task trainers, and related patient care equipment and supplies using a prioritization process that aligns with the Center’s strategic plan and College goals Initiate and oversee any contracts, including maintenance agreements, related to capital equipment and technologies in the Center Update and maintain website content for the Center in collaboration with MarCom, advancement, admissions, and other entities In conjunction with users, establish and enforce policies and procedures that promote the safe, effective use of the Goldstein Center and best practices in simulation education Co-Chair the Bouvé Simulation User Group to ensure 1) the Center meets the needs of the units across the network and 2) bi-directional communication is promoted between the Center and academic units Recruit, train, and support volunteers, technical staff, student workers and other Goldstein Center employees Develop appropriate policies and usage rates for research supported in the Center and use by parties outside the College  In collaboration with the Assistant Dean of Clinical Skills and Simulation Education, Ensure the Center’s strategic plan is current, aligns with Bouvé and University initiatives, and drives resource decision-making Develop and grow partnerships with other colleges and external entities to increase usage and visibility of the Center Expand AR/XR/VR, digital health, and AI application-based technologies within the Center that promote data, technological, and human literacies Achieve and maintain accreditation by the Society of Simulation in Healthcare    Support academic and research activities (15%) Develop and lead training/ professional development sessions on best practices in simulation education for faculty and the Bouvé community including, but not limited to, pre-brief and debrief best practices, scenario development and programming, utilization of simulator equipment, and updates related to simulation education Provide information to support grant submissions as requested Support active research through access to Center facilities within Bouvé, across colleges interested in collaborating, and the network In conjunction with College units and the Dean’s Office, develop procedures and guidelines for use of the facility to support new and developmental academic programs (e.g. continuing education, certificates, courses, and other simulation related activities with current or prospective students, faculty, partners, donors, alumni, etc.) Remain up to date with simulation technology and software through professional development and involvement with appropriate conferences and organizations Participate in philanthropic opportunities, enrollment management events, and visits/tours related to the Center as they arise with occasional evening/ weekend events. Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future.  Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 112S Expected Hiring Range: $99,010.00 - $143,567.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Gordon-CenSSIS-Systems-Coordinator_R141982": {
      "title": "Gordon-CenSSIS Systems Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Bernard M. Gordon Center for Subsurface Sensing and Imaging Systems (Gordon-CenSSIS) and its affiliates, including the ALERT (Awareness and Localization of Explosives-Related Threats) DHS Emeritus Center of Excellence (COE), Engineering PLUS NSF INCLUDES Alliance (Engineering PLUS) and the SENTRY (Soft-target Engineering to Neutralize the Threat Reality) DHS COE, is currently seeking a talented individual to fill the role of Systems Coordinator. As part of the Gordon-CenSSIS Computer Services Team, the Systems Coordinator will manage and maintain Gordon-CenSSIS and its affiliates research systems, computers, and network services while providing expert and friendly technical support to faculty, staff, students, and contractors. This person must be able to work both independently and collaboratively on projects; keep long-term projects on track; and meet day-to-day support needs of the center community in a professional and friendly manner. This role reports to the Senior Director of Research Computing Technical Program Development and requires an established working base of knowledge and experience, a demonstrated ability to learn and an interest in acquiring new technical skills.  The candidate must have at least 2-4 years’ experience in resolving computer hardware, software, and related system problems for business users with a focus on customer service, as well as systems administration for both Windows and Linux. They should be proficient with windows administration tools, networking fundamentals and have an excellent knowledge of computer hardware and software. Exceptional analytical and problem solving skills are required. Excellent verbal and written communication, time management and organizational skills are essential for success. Position requires that individual be able to work independently yet also possess extensive customer service relationship skills. Minimum Qualifications: A Bachelor’s degree is preferred, but not required. The ideal candidate must have a minimum of 2-4 years applicable experience in: End-user IT support in a business or academic environment. Extensive experience supporting connectivity, maintenance and troubleshooting of mobile devices, personal computers, Windows, Macintosh and Linux desktop and laptops. Microsoft Outlook and other Microsoft 365 products as well as a portfolio of software applications for both personal productivity and collaboration (Adobe Suite, Matlab, Zoom, WebEx, etc). Windows Server Administration Linux Server Administration The position requires a logical and structured approach to troubleshooting and decision making with an emphasis on superior customer service. Excellent interpersonal, communication and organizational skills are required. Ability to lift and carry objects weighing up to 25 lbs. Additional desirable qualifications include: Experience working in a multi-organizational academic research environment Experience with VMware virtualized servers and desktops Familiarity with basic TCP/IP network protocols and services: LDAP, NFS, CIFS, DNS, DHCP Working knowledge of CMSs (Wordpress, Druple), HTML, CSS Familiarity with service desk applications and/or incident management environments MCSE, Linux, or VMware certifications ITIL certifications Experience in AV media and telephony. To ensure that essential services are available to the Gordon-CenSSIS community, travel to our Burlington, MA campus and work outside of regular business hours will occasionally be required for this position.  This is a hybrid position. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: User Support - Provide effective and timely 1st, 2nd and some 3rd tier technology and telecommunications support, both virtually and in person which includes: troubleshooting and repair of operating systems, applications, and network issues, mobile device issues, answering questions regarding application use, and the installation of software to members of Gordon-CenSSIS and affiliates faculty, staff, students, contractors, and partners as needed. Help users with Northeastern and center managed services such as file sharing services, online portals and tools, user accounts, web sites, mailing lists, etc. Ensure proactive measures are taken to identify potential customer problems by reviewing customer history for open issues or trends. Determine root causes of continuing issues. Ability to determine when escalation to 3rd tier support and or vendors is required. Perform security evaluation and remediation as needed. Systems Administration and Management - Provide administration, monitoring, and maintenance for computer servers and systems; troubleshooting and emergency response in case of problems including network troubleshooting. Set up and configure new computers and peripherals. Software license management and administration. Coordinate hardware repairs with vendors and service providers when needed. Documentation and Training – Writes exact documentation for historical and support purposes. Create end-user documentation, how-to’s and training modules about computer systems, including software manuals, reference handouts, introductions to use of various technologies etc. for the center community. Develop and run training events as needed. Self-Education and Development - Utilize any resources provided by Northeastern University (educational opportunities, documentation, training courses, etc.) to improve both technical skills and professional development. Documents Required: Resume Cover Letter   Position Type Information Technology Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Social-Media-Video-Producer-Host_R142209": {
      "title": "Social Video Producer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Northeastern University is looking for an experienced Social Video Producer to create daily, social-first storytelling content for TikTok, Instagram, YouTube Shorts, and other vertical video platforms. This is a key role in Northeastern’s storytelling engine, conceptualizing, shooting, and editing fast-turnaround content that responds to trends, showcases the spirit of the university experience, and communicates news and information in creative ways. The Social Video Producer regularly appears on camera as part of a rotating team presence, and works with the team of content creators to shoot both short and longer-form content as needed, including podcast-style interviews and executive-level video projects. This is a hands-on production role that requires an abundance of creative ideas, the ability to multitask at a fast pace, and the ability to engage with people to create engaging vertical videos. Key Responsibilities Conceptualize, shoot, and edit daily social-first news and storytelling content, primarily for TikTok and Instagram. Appear on camera regularly as part of a rotating team presence. Stay ahead of platform trends and news cycles to identify timely content opportunities. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree 2-4 years shooting and editing in a professional environment, with a strong portfolio of vertical, social-first video. Proficiency in Adobe Creative Suite and/or DaVinci Resolve. Demonstrated ability to shoot and edit both short and longer-form video, including interview-style and multi-camera setups, and condense those into consumable, social-forward content. Solid news judgement. Strong on-camera presence and experience appearing in social video content. Deep, current knowledge of social platform trends, news cycles, and how to translate fast-moving stories into engaging vertical video. Ability to work with minimal direction. Excellent attention to detail while maintaining speed and consistency under deadline pressure. Strong communication skills and ability to troubleshoot. Excellent work ethic and positive attitude. Sense of humor. Great if you also have: Familiarity with social media analytical tools (Sprinklr, Google Analytics, etc.) Experience producing or shooting podcast-style video content. Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Administrative-Assistant_R142194": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the College of Social Sciences and Humanities The College of Social Sciences and Humanities  (CSSH) combines Northeastern University’s signature approach to experiential learning with liberal arts education, interdisciplinary research, and community engagement, both locally and globally.     The College   comprises   20 academic units, including two schools, eight departments, and ten interdisciplinary programs, as well as 15   research centers and institutes . Our faculty connects liberal arts education with the challenges of our time, and fosters ethical reasoning and critical thought, with attention to the enduring significance of history, literature, and culture .    CSSH promotes inquiry with global perspectives across interdisciplinary clusters of   expertise   including:   Artificial Intelligence, Resilience, Sustainability,   Health, and Equity; Cultural studies, Governance, and Globalization; and Network Science, Computational Social Science, Digital Humanities, and Data Ethics.     At the College of Social Sciences and Humanities (CSSH), we believe that a culture of belonging, where every faculty member, staff member, and student feels empowered, respected, and connected, strengthens our shared pursuit of knowledge.   Job Summary Reporting to the College Executive Assistant, t he Administrative Assistant   will join an administrative team   and   provide administrative support to   one or more units .  This role will   primarily   be assigned to support the   Office of Student Academic Affairs and will be the front-line contact for the College   regarding   undergraduate inquiries, requests for information, and appointments with advisors .  The Administrative Assistant s erves as a liaison between students and University offices to provide   accurate   information and excellent customer service in helping students navigate their way through processes and procedures across the College and Univers i ty.   This role will   w ork   with students and parents on the phone, via e-mail, and in person.    Duties include maintaining a welcoming office   atmosphere , providing immediate response to student   needs,    managing   work study students, ensuring appropriate front desk coverage, organizing and scheduling appointments and meetings,   answering phones, email communications,   m aintain ing   office operations,   ensu ring   accurate and confidential record keeping ,   inputting   data into central systems ,   and   troubleshoot ing   meeting technology     The Administrative Assistant will   assist   with events and programming.   This role is   on campus   five days per week   due to   the support needed for the team,   students,   parents , and visitors.     Key Responsibilities and Accountabilities Customer Service Serves as   a   key point of contact for inquiries about programs, policies, and processes   in one or more department/program Acquires   and applies department knowledge to   provide   information   for   routine matters and   escalate   more serious matters Provides front desk coverage and coordinates work study student schedules to ensure   appropriate coverage , customer service, and support are always available during needed hours (typically Monday-Friday, 9am – 5pm)   A dministrative and Operational Support Organizes and schedules meetings Tracks and maintains   unit   budgets Maintains office equipment, generates purchase orders, reconciles corporate credit cards, files documents, sorts mail, answers department telephones and e-mail, and orders office supplies. Data Gathering, Analysis & Reporting Gathers, organizes, and reviews data from a variety of sources for recordkeeping,   reporting,   and program management   Events Assists   with the planning of events and coordination of activities needed to hold workshops, conferences, and seminars Minimum Qualifications Skills and knowledge for this position are typically   acquired   through the completion of a   Bachelor’s   degree   or equivalent and 1-3 years’ relevant experience , preferably in a higher education setting Knowledge of office administration practices and processes; basic level of   proficiency   with MS Office products, including Microsoft Word, Excel,   PowerPoint   and Outlook Excellent computer skills with flexibility to adapt to   new technology   and various computer/software applications are essential Ability to plan,   organize,   and set priorities; time management; attention to detail;   conflict resolution Excellent interpersonal and communication skills   to provide the highest level of customer service Ability to navigate high volume periods and challenging student situations while maintaining a calm, patient, and professional demeanor Ability to carry out special assignments by completing activities,   monitoring   progress, and ensuring work is completed accurately and on time Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106H Expected Hiring Range: $23.37 - $32.14 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Reference-Librarian-SEIU_R142168": {
      "title": "Reference Librarian SEIU",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About F. W. Olin Library F. W. Olin Library at the Northeastern Oakland campus seeks a public service-oriented librarian to join the reference and instruction team as a Reference Librarian. Job Summary Reporting to the Head of Reference and Access Services, the Reference Librarian staffs the Reference Desk, plans and teaches information literacy sessions, acts as a liaison to academic departments, and participates in library outreach initiatives. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Reference Services (40%) Manage reference activities, including staffing the Reference Desk. Guide students, faculty, and staff in the use of the library’s physical and electronic collections. Meet with students and faculty individually or in groups for research appointments and assignment planning. Information Literacy (25%) Create and maintain LibGuides. Lead information literacy instruction sessions. Demonstrate effective use of the library’s physical and electronic collections. Teach research strategies and ethical research concepts based on the Association of College and Research Libraries (ACRL) Information Literacy Framework. Apply critical librarianship and pedagogy principles in instructional activities. Collection Development (15%) Select and deselect library resources within assigned subject areas to support campus curriculum and faculty research. Create thematic displays. Develop “Olin Recommends” booktalkers and booklists. Outreach (10%) Support library programming. Staff campus events and create handouts and mini-collections for activities such as Staff Wellness Fairs and First-Year Orientation. Serve as a liaison to academic departments and student groups. Administer library social media platforms and create related content. General Public Services (10%) Oversee the Circulation Desk as needed. Search for missing books. Collect fines and fees. Resolve patron issues. Provide computer and technology support. Create signage and other public service materials. Additional Responsibilities Assist with course reserves and interlibrary loan activities as needed. Record and maintain library usage and service statistics, including head counts, reference transactions, information literacy sessions, and student interactions. Serve on campus and professional committees and collaborate with other campus departments. Participate in professional development opportunities to remain informed about emerging trends, technologies, and innovations in librarianship. Minimum Qualifications Required Master’s degree in librarianship from an American Library Association (ALA)-accredited institution. One to two years of professional experience, preferably in an academic library. Effective teaching skills in both group and individual settings. Demonstrated experience using databases, electronic resources, online catalogs, web-based resources, and print resources. Enthusiasm for and commitment to library public services. Working knowledge of hardware and software commonly used in libraries, including personal computers, Apple products, and printers. Knowledge of emerging technologies and instructional methodologies. Excellent communication skills with the ability to provide and follow clear instructions and communicate effectively with library users and students. Strong planning, organizational, and analytical skills. Ability to contribute to a positive work culture that supports customer service and teamwork. Demonstrated commitment to serving and supporting the student population and learning about the communities and backgrounds represented on campus. Preferred Familiarity with an integrated library system (ILS); Ex Libris Alma preferred. Physical, Mental and Environmental Demands Ability to work one weeknight evening per week and occasional evenings and weekends as needed. Ability to work extensively at a computer screen. Ability to lift 10 pounds overhead and push up to 50 pounds on a book truck. Required Documents Resume Position Type Library Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Base Pay Rate: $83,299.26"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Campus-News-Reporter_R142208": {
      "title": "Campus News Reporter",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Northeastern Global News (NGN), Northeastern University’s award-winning, fast-paced, daily news organization is looking for a talented News Reporter to be based on Northeastern’s Boston campus.   The ideal candidate is an experienced journalist and versatile storyteller who has a finger on the pulse of societal trends, cultural shifts and touchstones, and entertainment news – and can write engaging and deeply reported articles with unique angles that showcase faculty expertise. The News Reporter should be ready to quickly jump on breaking news, providing commentary and analysis by faculty experts on timely topics that captivate national and global conversations. NGN showcases news about innovation across Northeastern's academic and research enterprise, including but not limited to stories about discoveries, innovations across multiple fields, experiential learning, human interest stories, and campus life inside labs, classrooms, and beyond.  We expect curiosity, accountability, creativity, and a commitment to accuracy and speed. This role is perfect for a journalist who thrives in a fast-paced environment, has great news judgment, and understands how to reach readers across digital platforms. NGN is part of Northeastern’s Communications team in External Affairs. Reporting to the Executive Editor of NGN, the News Reporter is part of a team of journalists who work closely with media relations, social media and multimedia teams within Communications. It is a highly data-informed organization with ambitious goals and impeccable standards. The position is based on the Boston campus and is fully on site, five days a week. Remote or hybrid work are not an option. As is the case for every member of the Communications team, after-hours work, including weekends and holidays, is routinely required.          Responsibilities Report, write, and publish breaking pop culture news, daily stories, and enterprise features for a large audience. Work closely with editors to refine pitches, shape coverage, and maintain high standards of accuracy. Develop reliable sources and maintain strong relationships across the Northeastern community and relevant stakeholder groups. Produce stories that are clear, engaging, and accessible to readers. Collaborate with media relations, social media, and multimedia teams to enhance stories with data, graphics, photos, and video. Monitor analytics and audience metrics to inform reporting and follow-up coverage. Adhere to guidelines and fact-check thoroughly. Minimum Qualifications 5 - 7 years reporting experience with a commercial daily news organization (digital or print). Demonstrated ability to handle general assignment (including pop culture) news coverage with speed and accuracy. Strong writing, interviewing, and source-building skills. Excellent news judgment. Experience interpreting public documents, filings, and data. Ability to multitask, manage deadlines, and respond quickly to developing stories. Familiarity with digital publishing tools is a plus. Good to have: Experience with multimedia or video reporting Data journalism or basic visual storytelling skills Knowledge of SEO and audience engagement strategies Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 110S Expected Hiring Range: $76,335.00 - $107,823.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Administrative-Officer--Finance-and-Personnel_R141984": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer, Finance and Personnel",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the College of Arts, Media and Design We are a vibrant community of creators, designers, technologists, and communicators applying creativity across disciplines and harnessing emerging technologies to advance human potential and solve the challenges of the future. Powered by experience-driven teaching, learning and research, CAMD demonstrates that the fields of design, communication, media, and the arts are crucial to the human experience, society, and the development of technology. We empower the next generations of students, researchers, and creative practitioners to create the future by sparking their innovative, creative, curious, and entrepreneurial spirit. Job Summary Under the supervision of the Senior Academic Program Manager within the Department of Art + Design, the Administrative Officer, Finance and Personnel serves as the department's primary resource for financial management, budgeting, personnel administration, and university business processes, ensuring compliance with university policies and stewardship of departmental resources. Serving as the primary liaison between the department and the College Finance Office, this position coordinates operating budget activities, financial transactions, personnel appointments, and annual compensation processes, including merit planning. The position oversees hiring and appointment processes for graduate fellows, part-time faculty, guest artists, and paid contractors. The role exercises a high degree of independent judgment and problem-solving in managing complex administrative and financial processes and serves as a trusted resource to Art + Design faculty, staff, and university partners. This position has no direct reports. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Financial Administration (45%) Oversee budget activities for start-up and externally funded research budgets, partnering with the College Grants team as needed to support hiring, purchasing, contracts, and grant administration Serve as the department liaison to the College Finance Office for annual merit planning, salary administration, and related budget activities Maintain expertise in university financial systems, policies, and business processes through ongoing training and implementation of new procedures Manage and review all payments and expenses for accuracy and adherence to university guidelines Manage and track professional development fund requests and other faculty and staff expense requests and approvals; coordinate with the Senior Academic Program Manager to project commitments and track budget obligations for discretionary expenses Provide training on university financial tools, including Concur, Marketplace, and Workday Finance Oversee payment of class support employees, including teaching assistants, research assistants, and visiting scholars Maintain gift funds and student grants and assist with reimbursement requests Submit and track additional compensation forms through approval pipelines Project event and program budgetary needs for the fiscal year and provide recommendations on value and reach Hiring & Personnel Administration (25%) Manage the collection and archiving of materials related to faculty administrative processes, including merit, tenure, and promotion Hire graduate fellows through the intake system Oversee appointment processes for part-time faculty, guest artists, and paid contractors Coordinate departmental memberships and event sponsorships in support of faculty professional development Partner with Department Chairs and the College Finance Office to support financial planning and funding approval for faculty hires Distributed Shared Service Support (20%) Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to develop standardized procedures and protocols for shared job functions and ensure consistency Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to implement robust data management practices that support the accessibility and integrity of financial and personnel records Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to create cross-training opportunities for departmental practices, support Department Chairs, and promote broad efficiencies across the College Collaborate with other Administrative Officers to provide office coverage during absences or scheduled paid time off (PTO) Reporting & Special Projects (10%) Maintain and report the departmental budget across faculty, staff, and student areas Collect data and prepare financial and personnel reports as needed Manage special projects and assignments related to budget and personnel data review Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor's degree Minimum of 2-3 years of administrative experience Experience with budget administration, office management, and financial processes Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite, particularly Excel Strong organizational, analytical, and interpersonal skills, with exceptional attention to detail Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a fast-paced, service-oriented environment, manage multiple priorities, and adapt to changing business needs, shifting priorities, and evolving processes Preferred Administrative experience in higher education Experience with Workday and Concur Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142120": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Gyori Lab for Computational Biomedicine The Gyori Lab for Computational Biomedicine at Northeastern University builds systems that accelerate scientific discovery by combining artificial intelligence with human expertise. The lab develops knowledge-grounded artificial intelligence, text mining and knowledge assembly, mathematical and systems modeling, and causal analysis. Research results are often deployed as widely used open-source software and services. The lab’s work is supported by a portfolio of federal and foundation grants spanning biomedical knowledge integration, global health, and artificial intelligence-assisted systems modeling. More information about the lab and its projects is available at https://gyorilab.github.io. About Khoury College We invite you to join a fast-moving, ambitious college with an underlying mission driven by the motto “CS for Everyone.” Job Summary The Gyori Lab for Computational Biomedicine is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to contribute to its portfolio of research. The Postdoctoral Research Associate will develop new computational methods in knowledge-grounded artificial intelligence, systems modeling, natural language processing, and data harmonization, with applications in biomedicine and related domains. The Postdoctoral Research Associate will collaborate closely with graduate and undergraduate students, scientific software developers, academic collaborators, and program partners. This position is based in Boston, Massachusetts, and comes with an initial two-year appointment. Renewal is possible, subject to funding and performance. Minimum Qualifications Required PhD or equivalent in computer science, computational biology, biomedical engineering, or a related field by the appointment start date Expertise and a demonstrated track record in one or more of the following areas, or a combination of these areas: Knowledge-grounded artificial intelligence and knowledge graphs, including large language model (LLM) and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) approaches Systems and mathematical modeling of biological or complex systems Natural language processing and machine learning Data harmonization and integration Record of research productivity evidenced by peer-reviewed publications, open-source software, or other scholarly outputs Excellent written and verbal communication skills Ability to work collaboratively on interdisciplinary teams Interest in mentoring junior researchers Preferred Strong scientific software development skills and a commitment to reproducible research practices Experience applying computational methods to biomedicine or related domains Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Conduct original research in knowledge-grounded artificial intelligence, systems modeling, natural language processing, data harmonization, and related research areas Lead the implementation of research methods as reusable scientific software Benchmark computational methods using real-world applications Publish research findings and present results Contribute to grant proposals Collaborate with graduate and undergraduate students, scientific software developers, academic collaborators, and program partners Mentor junior researchers Required Documents Resume Cover letter Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Administrative-Coordinator_R142134": {
      "title": "Administrative Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Administrative Coordinator is a key member of the Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology in the College of Science (COS). Reporting to the department Chair, the Administrative Coordinator works closely with the Chair, other faculty leaders, and academic support staff. The Administrative Coordinator oversees the department’s presence both on campus and virtually, ensuring that faculty, students, staff, and guests   are connected with   the right information and equipped with the basic resources needed to continue their active roles in teaching, learning, and administration. The Administrative Coordinator assists with faculty searches and promotions; manages department events; prepares and submits human resources management (HRM) related tasks in Workday; serves as the primary onboarding contact for new faculty and staff; and acts as the liaison to the undergraduate American Chemical Society (ACS) chapter. This position offers opportunities to   demonstrate   organizational and project skills in support of the department’s day-to-day operations.   Key Responsibilities and Accountabilities Department Administrative Support Manage the Department Chair’s and Associate Chairs’ calendars and oversee schedules for visiting faculty and staff candidates   Oversee travel arrangements for designated department guests, including speakers, presenters, and students   Arrange payments, honoraria, and reimbursements for guests or students without direct access to Concur; process internal travel reimbursements for students using Concur and for visitors using   Candex   Serve as the Business Manager’s designee for Concur reconciliation   Oversee office and breakroom supplies for the   main office   Manage access to department spaces; greet faculty, students, staff, and guests; provide information in response to inquiries; and direct individuals to the   appropriate person   or place   Manage and distribute incoming and outgoing mail and coordinate with facilities   regarding   building maintenance and repairs   Faculty and Staff Personnel Administration Liaise with the COS Faculty Affairs teams   regarding   personnel matters, including faculty hiring, merit reviews, tenure, and promotion   Hire, manage, and oversee student employees for the front office   Serve as the Concur and student employment backup for the department Business and Operations Manager   Submit and track new positions, including postdoctoral fellows, research and administrative staff, temporary non-students, student employees, and visiting scholars and students   Scheduling Management for Department Meetings and Events Oversee centralized schedules for all shared department spaces, including conference rooms, part-time faculty offices, and hoteling offices   Coordinate schedules for department events and other key department business   Manage department events, including fall orientation, the annual poster session, Graduate Open House, and graduation   Coordinate social events for student groups, including the Graduate Student Association and the Northeastern University Chapter of the American Chemical Society     Communications and Web Content Management Liaise with the COS Marketing and Communications team   regarding   website content postings   Manage department internal sites, distribution lists, and social media accounts   Create marketing and branded materials to advance department recruitment efforts, including newsletters, welcome packets, and announcements   Travel to scientific meetings to staff department recruitment tables, as needed     Additional Accountabilities   Complete special projects as assigned   Use a purchasing card and complete regular reporting   Manage reimbursements for students, employees, and guests   Supervise student workers   Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree  required Minimum of three years of relevant professional experience in an academic, business, or related environment   Strong organizational, communication, and time management skills   Ability to   assist   and work with faculty, students, staff, and guests in an academic or similar professional environment   Proven ability to work independently and as a member of a team in a fast-paced environment   Ability to manage confidential and sensitive information Experience with Banner, Concur,   Candex , or Workday   Applicants must include the following documentation with their application: 1. Resume 2. Cover Letter The University is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future. Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-1_R142116": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate-1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Woolston Lab The Woolston Lab at Northeastern University conducts research at the intersection of anaerobic microbiology, enzyme biochemistry, and natural products discovery. This position is funded by a National Institutes of Health (NIH) grant focused on unlocking the biosynthetic potential of Clostridial bacteria, a poorly understood but exceptionally rich source of novel bioactive compounds and unusual biosynthetic enzymes. The research program pursues two complementary directions. The first involves the biochemical and in vivo characterization of a newly discovered family of cobalamin-dependent radical S-adenosylmethionine (B12-rSAM) enzymes fused to non-ribosomal peptide synthetase (NRPS) adenylation domains. These enzymes are hypothesized to catalyze a stereoselective carbon backbone methylation reaction with no precedent in NRPS biochemistry. The second direction focuses on the discovery of novel antimicrobials from cellulolytic Clostridia through heterologous expression of biosynthetic gene clusters in a newly developed Clostridial chassis strain and activation of cryptic pathways in native producers via elicitor screening. Job Summary The Woolston Lab at Northeastern University seeks a Postdoctoral Research Associate to lead an exciting research program at the intersection of anaerobic microbiology, enzyme biochemistry, and natural products discovery. The Postdoctoral Research Associate will work closely with Prof. Woolston and a collaborative, interdisciplinary team of graduate students and undergraduate researchers. The Woolston Lab places a strong emphasis on open scientific exchange, mentorship, and a team-oriented culture in which postdoctoral researchers play a central role in shaping both the science and the intellectual environment of the group. The associate will be expected to conduct independent research, contribute to the mentorship of junior lab members, and communicate findings through publications, presentations, and reports to the funding agency. Minimum Qualifications Required PhD in microbiology, biochemistry, chemical engineering, or a related field. Demonstrated publication record of scientific findings in peer-reviewed journals. Experience investigating and characterizing natural products biosynthesis or experience purifying and functionally characterizing oxygen-sensitive enzymes (for example, iron-sulfur proteins and radical enzymes). Candidates with demonstrated expertise in both areas are especially competitive. Experience with modern bacterial genetics workflows, including cloning, genome editing, Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats (CRISPR)-based tools, and transposon mutagenesis. Experience with anaerobic microbiology techniques, including cultivation, handling, and experimental work under strictly anaerobic conditions. Bioinformatics skills relevant to genomics-driven natural products discovery, including genome mining, biosynthetic gene cluster analysis, sequence alignment, and annotation. Strong quantitative and statistical analysis skills for experimental data interpretation. Rigorous scientific documentation practices, including maintenance of detailed laboratory notebooks and research records. Excellent written and oral communication skills, with the ability to present research findings clearly to both specialist and general audiences. Demonstrated ability or strong potential to mentor undergraduate and graduate researchers. Ability to work independently and drive a research project forward while collaborating effectively within a team environment. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Conduct independent research, including developing assays, designing experiments, generating data, and analyzing results. Research plans and experiments should be discussed and developed in consultation with Prof. Woolston; however, the employee must be able to complete research activities independently. Communicate research progress in regular meetings with Prof. Woolston. Read and review scientific literature relevant to the project scope. Communicate results through laboratory group meetings, scientific conferences, peer-reviewed publications, and funding agency reports. Prepare presentations, manuscripts, patent applications, and other scholarly or research-related materials. Participate in professional development activities, including workshops, seminars, courses, and fellowship applications. Engage in career development activities and preparation for future career opportunities, as appropriate. Required Documents Resume Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/HR-Platforms-Reporting---Data-Analyst_R142147": {
      "title": "HR Platforms Reporting & Data Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary To meet the challenges of the university’s continued growth and expansion, the HR Platforms (HRIT) team is looking for a qualified candidate who will contribute to the support and data analytics of Workday HCM.  This is a hybrid position; required to be ON CAMPUS Mon, Tues, Thurs, Fri (Wed is remote).   The HR Platforms (HRIT) Reporting & Data Analyst serves as a key data and reporting resource within HR Platforms, partnering with HR functional areas, Payroll, Finance, and college/division HR stakeholders to understand complex business requirements and deliver accurate, meaningful workforce data and analytics.   This role requires a strong understanding of Workday HCM and Payroll business processes, particularly within the complexities of a decentralized higher education environment with diverse employee populations, organizational structures, and business practices.   The Reporting & Data Analyst translates business needs into effective reporting and analytical solutions, developing and maintaining Workday reports, SQL queries, and other data solutions. The role analyzes complex datasets to identify trends, discrepancies, anomalies, and data integrity issues; investigates root causes; and provides insights that support operational and strategic decision-making.   This position also partners closely with HR Platforms Business Systems Analysts and other technical and functional teams to test, validate, and resolve data and reporting issues.   Success in this role requires strong analytical and problem-solving skills, expert proficiency in Excel, proficiency in SQL, attention to detail, and a naturally curious mindset. The successful candidate will be comfortable asking questions, challenging data that does not appear correct, investigating complex issues, and translating analytical findings into clear, actionable information for both technical and non-technical audiences. The role requires sound judgment, initiative, strong customer service skills, and the ability to manage multiple priorities and competing deadlines in a high-volume environment while maintaining confidentiality and the highest standards of data integrity.   Key responsibilities include: ·         Partner with HR, Payroll, Finance, and college/division HR stakeholders to understand complex business requirements and translate data needs into accurate, sustainable reporting and analytical solutions. ·         Develop, maintain, and enhance reports and queries using Workday HCM/Payroll, Prism, SQL, Excel, and other reporting and analytical tools. ·         Analyze and reconcile complex HR and Payroll datasets to identify trends, anomalies, discrepancies, and data integrity issues and investigate root causes. ·         Collaborate with HR Platforms Business Systems Analysts and other technical and functional partners on requirements, testing, validation, system changes, and resolution of data and reporting issues.   To support the University's essential services and business operations, this position may occasionally be required to work outside of regular business hours, including during critical system activities, reporting deadlines, or other periods of operational need.   Minimum Qualifications   Bachelor’s degree in Information Systems, Computer Science, Business, Human Resources, Data Analytics, or a related field required. 3–5 years of progressive experience in HR data analytics, HR systems, reporting, or a related field, with demonstrated experience analyzing complex HR and payroll data. Strong understanding of Workday HCM and Payroll business processes, including the ability to understand how transactions, business processes, organizational structures, compensation, time, absence, payroll, and employee lifecycle events impact downstream data and reporting. Experience with Workday reporting across HCM and Payroll, including developing, maintaining, interpreting, and troubleshooting reports and identifying opportunities to improve reporting accuracy and effectiveness. Higher education HR and Payroll experience is strongly preferred, with an understanding of the unique organizational structures, academic populations, employee types, appointments, positions, grants/funding, faculty/staff populations, and other complexities common to higher education. Expert proficiency in Microsoft Excel, including advanced formulas/functions, pivot tables, lookups, data cleansing, conditional logic, data validation, reconciliation, and analysis of large and complex datasets. Experience using Excel to identify trends, anomalies, discrepancies, and data-quality issues is highly desirable. Proficiency in SQL, with the ability to query, join, filter, aggregate, and analyze complex datasets across multiple tables and data sources. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with a naturally curious mindset and the ability to recognize when data does not make sense and independently investigate the underlying issue, ask insightful questions, identify patterns and anomalies, determine root causes, and translate findings into meaningful business insights.   Demonstrated ability to work with large, complex, and sensitive HR and Payroll datasets, ensuring data integrity, accuracy, completeness, consistency, and confidentiality. Experience with data reconciliation and validation, including comparing data across Workday and downstream systems, identifying discrepancies, researching root causes, and partnering with functional and technical teams to resolve issues. Ability to translate complex HR and Payroll business requirements into meaningful reporting and data solutions, including gathering requirements, understanding the underlying business need, and recommending appropriate reporting or analytical approaches. Experience with data management, reporting governance, documentation, testing, and systems changes, including developing test plans and test cases and validating the impact of system changes on data and reporting. Workday Prism Analytics experience preferred, particularly experience working with multiple data sources and complex datasets. Experience with Cognos or other enterprise reporting/analytics tools preferred. Ability to interpret and communicate data clearly, translating complex analysis and technical findings into concise, actionable information for HR leadership, functional partners, and non-technical stakeholders. Strong attention to detail and commitment to data accuracy, with the ability to recognize when data does not make sense and independently investigate the underlying issue. Strong customer-service and consulting mindset, with the ability to understand customer needs, clarify ambiguous requests, provide thoughtful recommendations, and deliver high-quality reporting and analytical solutions. Self-starter with strong initiative and sound judgment, capable of working independently while knowing when to escalate issues or engage appropriate technical and functional partners. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a high-volume environment, organize work effectively, meet deadlines, and adapt to changing business and project requirements. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with demonstrated ability to effectively communicate with technical and non-technical audiences at all levels of the organization. Strong collaboration and relationship-building skills, with the ability to partner effectively across HR, Payroll, HR Technology, IT, and other university functions. Demonstrated ability to handle confidential and sensitive employee, HR, and Payroll information with discretion and in accordance with university policies and data-security requirements. Commitment to continuous improvement, with the ability to identify opportunities to automate, simplify, improve data quality, and enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of HR reporting and analytics. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Reporting and Analytics   Develop, maintain, and enhance Workday HCM and Payroll reports and SQL queries to meet complex HR, Payroll, operational, compliance, and business requirements. Analyze complex HR and Payroll data from Workday and other data sources to identify trends, anomalies, data-quality issues, and opportunities for process improvement. Serve as a reporting and data analytics subject matter expert for HR and Payroll, providing expertise to troubleshoot reporting issues, investigate discrepancies, and determine root causes. Partner with HR and Payroll stakeholders to understand business needs and translate requirements into effective reporting and analytical solutions, recommending the most appropriate reporting approach or data source. Provide advanced Excel-based analysis and data reconciliation, including analyzing large datasets, identifying discrepancies, validating results, and developing meaningful insights for HR leadership and business partners. Develop and support ad-hoc and standardized reporting capabilities that enable HR users and leadership to access timely, accurate, and actionable workforce information. Evaluate existing Workday reports and the report library to identify opportunities to leverage, enhance, consolidate, or retire existing reports and reduce redundant reporting. Establish and maintain reporting documentation, including report definitions, data sources, business logic, calculations, dependencies, and intended use to support consistency and knowledge sharing. Support reporting testing and validation associated with Workday releases, system changes, integrations, and business process changes, ensuring data and reporting outcomes remain accurate. Develop, analyze, and deliver recurring workforce metrics and reporting packages, including quarterly metrics and other HR leadership reporting, ensuring accuracy, consistency, and meaningful interpretation of results. Monitor and promote data quality and reporting integrity, partnering with HR, Payroll, and technical teams to research and resolve data discrepancies and reporting issues. Provide reporting guidance, training, and support to HR users, helping users understand available reporting capabilities, interpret report results, and use data appropriately for decision-making. Serve as a resource for HR staff and leadership on Workday reporting capabilities, including advising on report design, data availability, appropriate use of Workday data, and limitations of reporting outputs. Identify opportunities to improve, automate, and streamline reporting processes, leveraging Workday, SQL, Excel, Prism, Cognos, and other available technologies. Data Integrity & Security   Monitor, analyze, and validate HR and Payroll data across Workday and related systems, developing and executing audit queries to proactively identify data quality issues, anomalies, and inconsistencies. Reconcile HR and Payroll data across Workday, integrations, and downstream systems, investigating discrepancies and partnering with functional and technical teams to identify root causes and implement resolutions. Support data integrity audits, testing, and controls associated with HR systems, integrations, releases, and business process changes, ensuring accurate, complete, and reliable data. Protect the confidentiality and security of sensitive HR and Payroll data, adhering to University policies and data governance standards while identifying and escalating potential privacy, security, or access concerns. Training/Communications   Develop and deliver training to HR and Payroll users on Workday reporting, data analysis, and related reporting tools and capabilities. Develop, update, and maintain user guides, training materials, job aids, and other documentation to support effective and consistent use of Workday reporting and analytics. Develop and maintain a centralized library of standard reports and reporting documentation, including report purpose, data definitions, business logic, and intended use. Communicate reporting changes, new capabilities, and data insights to HR stakeholders and provide guidance to help users effectively interpret and utilize HR and Payroll data. Position Type Human Resources Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 111S Expected Hiring Range: $87,785.00 - $123,998.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Adjunct-Faculty_R142054": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Sociology and Anthropology - Oakland, CA",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Social Sciences and Humanities and its nine tenure units are the home of the Experiential Liberal Arts. Through its research, teaching, and engagement missions, the college collaborates across the university, the Northeastern network, and partners around the globe. Responsibilities: The Department of Sociology and Anthropology in the College of Social Sciences and Humanities seeks applicants to teach an undergraduate course in the Sociology of Sex, Gender, and Popular Culture on the Northeastern University Oakland campus.  Applicants will be expected to deliver lectures, develop assignments, grade assignments, and hold appropriate office hours. Positions are available throughout the year contingent upon enrollment and departmental need. Qualifications: M.A. required, Ph.D. or A.B.D. preferred. Preference will be given to persons experienced in teaching large and diverse groups of students. Documents to Submit: Cover letter CV Statement of teaching philosophy Sample syllabi Results of student evaluations Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,435.76."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Seattle-WA/Open-Rank-Clinical-Professor-and-Director-of-Clinical-Education--Physician-Assistant-Program--Department-of-Medical-Sciences--Seattle-campus-_R140844": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical Professor and Director of Clinical Education, Physician Assistant Program, Department of Medical Sciences (Seattle campus)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Department of Medical Sciences is recruiting a Director of Clinical Education (DCE) and Open Rank Clinical Professor in the Physician Assistant Program on our Seattle campus. The DCE and Open Rank Clinical Professor provides strategic and operational leadership and oversight of the clinical component of the Physician Assistant Program curriculum, including development and maintenance of clinical partnerships, oversight of supervised clinical practice experiences, clinical year organization and administration, and student education and mentoring. The DCE will collaborate with program leadership to maintain compliance with ARC-PA standards, advance innovative approaches to clinical education, and ensure the highest quality clinical learning experiences for students and clinical partners. The Open Rank Clinical Professor is responsible for teaching in the didactic component of the Physician Assistant Program curriculum, ensuring the high educational and medical quality of the curriculum, program organization and administration, and student education and mentoring. The position also carries expectations for scholarship, including contributions to the advancement of PA education and clinical practice, as well as service to the program, university, and broader professional community. The Open Rank Clinical Professor will assist in maintaining accreditation standards and participating in any activities necessary to maintain Northeastern at the forefront of PA education. The position is a 52-week, 1.0 FTE, non-tenure track faculty position. Minimum qualifications: Master's degree or higher in a health profession, health sciences, education, or a related field; doctoral degree preferred Current professional licensure, certification, or other credentials appropriate to the candidate's discipline. Physician assistant candidates should maintain NCCPA certification; physician candidates should hold board certification through an ABMS or AOA-recognized specialty board Licensed or eligible for licensure in the state of Washington Minimum of two (2) years of clinical practice experience in a healthcare profession Experience in physician assistant education, including service as faculty, clinical coordinator, preceptor, program administrator, or a comparable role within an accredited PA program Working knowledge of PA program accreditation and regulatory requirements, including familiarity with ARC-PA standards Strong interpersonal, organizational, problem-solving, and critical thinking skills Strong written and oral communication skills Proficiency with standard computer applications (e.g., word processing, email, spreadsheets, databases) Knowledge of legislative, regulatory, legal, and practice issues affecting clinical education, students, and the physician assistant profession Ability to travel to clinical sites Principal Faculty Responsibilities: As a principal faculty member, the Director of Clinical Education shares responsibility, alongside the Program Director and other principal faculty members, for the development, implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the PA program, including the following core functions: Developing, reviewing, and revising the program's mission statement, goals, and competencies Serve on the admissions committee, including selecting applicants for admission Coordinate, teach, and instruct courses based on areas of content and clinical expertise Provide continuous development, maintenance, and evaluation of both didactic courses and clinical rotation sites Facilitate small group discussion sessions; participate in simulation, and physical diagnosis and skills labs Monitor, evaluate, and document student performance to ensure that they successfully achieve the criteria for successful completion of courses Assist in the development and implementation of remediation plans for students in the didactic and clinical years, as needed Advise and mentor students; participate in all aspects of retention and academic progression Designing, implementing, coordinating, and evaluating the curriculum Provide continuous development, maintenance, and evaluation of both didactic courses and clinical rotation sites Participate in clinical site development Participate in clinical site monitoring, including performing site visits during clinical rotations Participating in all aspects of continuous quality improvement activities and using programmatic data to support ongoing program enhancement Participate in all aspects of Program administration Perform additional assignments at the direction of the Program Director of the Physician Assistant Program Director of Clinical Education Responsibilities: In addition to principal faculty responsibilities, the Director of Clinical Education provides leadership in the following areas: Provide strategic and operational leadership for all aspects of the clinical phase of the curriculum, including planning, implementation, assessment, and continuous improvement, with particular attention to ARC-PA standards governing supervised clinical practice experiences Provide continuous development, maintenance, and evaluation of clinical rotation sites, with an emphasis on establishing new clinical partnerships and sustaining existing relationships, ensuring an adequate number of sites that reflect quality and quantity of learning experiences (including continuity of care, common diagnoses, care across the lifespan, and varied healthcare delivery settings) Coordinate and perform site visits to PA students during clinical rotations Coordinate the completion and documentation of all clinical site requirements, including affiliation agreements, background checks, and immunization records Develop, support, and retain clinical preceptors through ongoing communication, orientation, faculty development, and recognition efforts that promote high-quality clinical teaching experiences Serve as the operational lead for the clinical education team, coordinating workflows and supporting team members in their clinical education responsibilities Develop criteria, policies, and procedures governing supervised clinical practice experiences consistent with ARC-PA standards Develop criteria and procedures for clinical site selection, utilization, and assessment consistent with ARC-PA Standards Monitor, evaluate, and document student performance during supervised clinical practice experiences Review the adequacy of diagnosis and procedure log data Review and analyze student evaluations from preceptors and clinical instructors Assist in determining final grades for clinical rotations and end-of-rotation examinations Oversee the coordination of end-of-rotation examinations and return-to-campus days Utilize clinical performance data to support continuous quality improvement and accreditation reporting Assist the Program Director with preparation of the continuous self-study, with primary responsibility for documentation and analysis of the clinical phase of training Additional Information: This position may be structured as a four-day academic schedule to accommodate one day per week of clinical practice. Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, statements addressing the prompt below, and at least three references. Successful faculty at Northeastern are dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering a sense of belonging among all members of the Northeastern community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the experiences, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your experience, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with a written statement addressing the following prompt: Teaching statement: Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the Bouvé College of Health, and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a cohort of graduate PA students. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by the law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: 52-week Salary Ranges: Assistant Clinical Professor: $114,000.00 - $139,000.00 | Associate Clinical Professor: $133,000.00 - $154,000.00 | Clinical Professor: $152,000.00 - $192,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Academic-Advisor_R142080": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Academic Advisor for Northeastern University’s College of Professional Studies (CPS) reports to the Associate Director of Academic Advising and is responsible for providing comprehensive academic advising services and support for CPS learners in bachelors’ completion and graduate and professional studies programs across the global network.  They ideate, implement and ensure best practices and policies related to CPS student advising services and support to ensure continuous improvement and learner success. The Academic Advisor will partner with an assigned caseload of students to facilitate the development of academic and professional planning that incorporates experiential learning elements and exploration, engagement with networks and communities through an appreciate advising model.  While the Academic Advisor may have primary responsibilities on their assigned campus, they will attend remote meetings and implement projects for specific position responsibilities and needs within Northeastern University, Boston. 60% Academic and Student Support Actively manage an active enrolled caseload of students from point of admission through program completion. Monitor student progress through adherence to the established communication and contact plan and use of technology to track substantive engagements and reporting Connect with students using multiple modalities and in ways tailored to their preferences for communication; provide daily availability for scheduled meeting hours; walk-in hours, etc. Engage in effective best practices in academic advising. Advocate for students while enforcing institutional policies and regulations Communicate regularly with students through various mediums to provide guidance and support in areas related to academics, experiential learning planning and through periods of academic difficulty Respond to student inquiries from point of contact to resolution; follow up on referrals. Make appropriate referrals to on campus and off campus services as appropriate to student needs Actively manage persistence and retention and progress to graduation for advisees in portfolio; proactively monitor and support students in need of academic support or well-being, monitor and support students who become academically inactive and support recovery; reach out to these students to encourage resumption of studies and reinstatement. Design and deliver programs (often in collaboration with other teams) to support the unique needs of advisees including workshops, webinars, etc. that enhance and add value to the student experience. 20% Assessment and Data Monitor, manage and report on portfolio of advisees using required processes and technology platforms. Maintain up-to-date notes and record keeping of each student in Salesforce or Navigate including tracking of student academic progress and outcomes using appropriate University systems. Track and report on substantive engagements, student meetings, availability and activities to ensure capacity meets volume. Assess, collect and report student data as they pertain to student persistence, retention, graduation rates, and satisfaction. Maintain consistency and compliance within ADA, FERPA, SEVIS, and other state, federal, University and College of Professional Studies guidelines. 20% Administration, Academic Program Support, & Professional Development Work as a member of the academic advising office to support and provide coverage, as possible and appropriate, for the ongoing operation. Process in a timely fashion official paperwork related to students' academic study including changes of major, transfer credit, petitions to amend degree requirements and petitions to graduate. Participate in and contribute to the content and delivery of new student orientation Stay informed of research and effective practices in academic and student affairs to propose ideas to better serve students. Participate as an active member of professional associations related to academic advising and career services. Participate in unit or College assessment efforts, including the development of benchmarks for progress. Liaise between the student and the University community working collegially with other offices and departments to ensure student service functions are performed in an efficient and consistent manner. Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Executive-Assistant-to-the-Senior-Vice-President_R142047": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant to the Senior Vice President",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Summary The Office of External Affairs at Northeastern University is looking for a talented, hard-working, and highly motivated candidate to serve as the Executive Assistant to the Senior Vice President (SVP). Reporting to the Director of Executive and Strategic Initiatives, this person will play an important role in providing administrative support while prioritizing and coordinating competing demands, as well as assisting in high-priority events and projects. The ideal candidate has experience in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly collaborative environment. They will proactively support all daily business administrative responsibilities for the SVP and work with the Director to ensure smooth daily operations across the division. The ideal candidate is looking for an exciting role with a wide range of responsibilities and projects, which include: Providing high-level administrative support to the SVP including maintaining a busy executive calendar and organizing and preparing for meetings. Coordinating domestic and international travel. Tracking and preparing Concur reports. Planning, coordinating, and implementing logistics for division-wide meetings, including all-staff meetings and engagement activities. Assisting the Director with projects and deliverables related to key priorities or initiatives for the division or university. This individual regularly handles confidential material and information and exercises discretion in doing so. They will perform other duties as required to ensure the smooth operation and responsiveness of the division. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required with 3-5 years of experience in an office environment. Candidate must be a mature professional who can manage and facilitate an intense workload and produce quality results in a fast-paced environment. Excellent written and verbal skills. Must be able to manage multiple priority projects at once with ease and efficiency. Must be a self-starter who is highly motivated, can think independently and analytically, is organized, and extremely detail oriented. Must be proactive, anticipate problems, and be resourceful to find workable solutions, and should work well with others at all levels. Proficiency with MS Office products, including Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook is required. Skills in using AI tools to productively and efficiently deliver quality work are a plus. Experience in higher education is not needed, nor is it desired. This role requires an in-person presence five days a week. Some evening and weekend hours may be required. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Responsibility Administrative Support Provide high-level administrative support for the Senior Vice President. Maintain a busy executive calendar, organize and prepare for meetings, coordinate domestic and international travel, track and prepare Concur reports. Plan, coordinate, and implement logistics for division-wide meetings and events. Performs other duties as required to ensure the smooth operation and responsiveness of the department. Project Management Assisting the Director with projects and deliverables related to key priorities or initiatives for the division or university. Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Director-of-Admissions_R142039": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Admissions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Assistant Director of Undergraduate Admissions will help advance Northeastern University’s efforts to attract and enroll the most qualified undergraduate students. This individual will design and implement an effective recruitment strategy within a specified domestic and/or international travel territory as well as evaluate applicants for admission into the University. The recruitment strategy will include travel within assigned territories to convey key institutional highlights in order to attract and subsequently enroll first-year and transfer students. Responsibilities include counseling and advising students and families regarding the admission process, formal and informal presentations for both small and large groups of prospective and admitted students, and maintaining relationships with school counselors as well as community based organizations while managing the territory throughout the admission cycle. The Assistant Director will work closely with various stakeholders to communicate admissions-related information and serve as an advocate of the constituency in the admissions process. Key Accountabilities & Responsibilities: Territory Management and Recruitment Design and implement an effective goal-driven recruitment strategy for an assigned geographic area. The strategy should include efficient and cost-effective travel management in these territories while meeting office and University expectations and priorities. Attend programs, presentations, and regional events. Meet with, counsel, and inform prospective students, families, and school counselors. Follow-up and evaluation expected upon return from travel. Application Evaluation and Selection Complete a review of applications using specified evaluation policies and procedures determined by the leadership team. The reviews must be thorough, accurate and consistent with careful attention to detail. Application evaluations must be completed within specified time periods. Individuals in this position are expected to work the hours required to complete application evaluations within the specified time to accomplish set goals which may include working evenings and/or weekends. Specialized Projects and Responsibilities Coordinate and manage specific project(s) that contribute to, and support the overall function of, the full admissions cycle. Work closely with assigned stakeholders to communicate key information at various points throughout the admissions cycle. Effectively manage all assigned project(s) including setting goals in collaboration with their supervisor, developing and managing timelines, achieving desired results, and meeting all set deadlines and expectations. Customer Service and Support Communicate accurate information and effectively counsel all students, parents, school counselors, and other stakeholders in a timely, professional, and positive manner during all on and off-campus interactions including school visits, information sessions, college fairs, campus tours, prospective and admitted student events, counselor programming, written correspondence, email and phone calls. Fully participate in all training and professional development opportunities. Support the overall day-to-day operations and workflow of the Admissions Visitor Center, and all other duties as assigned.  Contribute to a Positive Work Environment Be a team player with a strong work ethic, who actively contributes to the office welfare in a high volume, fast-paced environment.  Have the ability to work independently and collaboratively across units while being sensitive and responsive to the needs of diverse constituencies. Model strong communication and advocacy skills in a professional setting.   Minimum Qualifications: Excellent interpersonal and customer service skills, including the ability to communicate effectively with high school students, their families and school counselors in a variety of settings Superior written, verbal communication, and organizational skills along with the ability to speak and write clearly and persuasively Ability to interact with people from diverse backgrounds and be sensitive to multicultural and socioeconomic issues Experience making compelling presentations to small and large groups Proven ability to successfully work independently as well as part of a team Bachelor's degree or equivalent and at least one year of experience in a student-facing role in college admissions, secondary school admissions, or a related experience in another higher education setting required.  Valid U.S. driver's license and clean driving record.  The ability to lift and move recruitment materials weighing up to 30 pounds. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with recruitment programming, travel scheduling and budgeting, admissions counseling, and application evaluation Demonstrated proficiency or comfort with various technologies (such as Microsoft 365), admissions database/softwares, and analyzing data Position Type Enrollment Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106S Expected Hiring Range: $48,610.00 - $66,838.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Professor-Professor-and-Director--Northeastern-University-Biomedical-Imaging-Center_R142017": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor and Director, Northeastern University Biomedical Imaging Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Northeastern: Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Arlington; Boston; Charlotte, North Carolina; London; Miami; New York City; Oakland; Portland, Maine; San Francisco; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. The Institute for Cognitive and Brain Health (CBH) is a collaborative research community with faculty from the College of Science, Bouvé College of Health Sciences, College of Arts, Music, and Design, and the College of Engineering. It brings together a unique combination of disciplines, including neuroimaging, cognitive neuroscience, developmental and lifespan sciences, movement sciences, music and the art, communication sciences and disorders, and cognitive training. CBH is also home to the Northeastern University Biomedical Imaging Center (NUBIC), which houses our Siemens Prisma 3T MRI .  CBH harnesses neuroimaging, behavioral, and computational studies to understand how lifestyle choices and behaviors influence brain activity, structure, cognitive function and mental health across the lifespan. Through interdisciplinary research investigating factors such as physical activity, diet and nutrition, cognitive training, and creative activity (artistic engagement, including music, dance, and the arts), the Institute investigates mechanisms of brain health to maximize well-being and promote effective functioning from infancy through older adulthood. The Institute serves as a hub for multiple research labs and brings together expertise from psychology, neuroscience, physical therapy, music and the arts, and other disciplines to address critical challenges in cognitive development and healthy aging. NUBIC stands at the center of the CBH and the greater Northeastern human neuroimaging community, as a key tool in our scientific discovery. About the Opportunity: The College of Science at Northeastern University invites applications for an Associate or Full Professor in the Department of Psychology  serving as Director for the Northeastern University Biomedical Imaging Center (NUBIC) within the Institute for Cognitive and Brain Health ( https://cbh.northeastern.edu/ )  in the Interdisciplinary Science and Engineering Complex at Northeastern University ( https://www.northeastern.edu/isec/) . Applicants for this position must have an internationally recognized, well-funded program of research. We seek a Director with expertise conducting human MRI/fMRI research in one of the following disciplines: psychological science, developmental science, cognitive science, neuroscience, medical physics, biological science, biomedical engineering, biomedical science, radiology, or health science. Of key importance is leadership in building a user-base of scientists engaged in functional, diffusion, spectroscopy, and structural MRI research. Expertise in working with a range of interdisciplinary groups is strongly desired. The successful candidate will be appointed to the Department of Psychology in the College of Science as well as other appropriate departments (e.g. Health Sciences, Communication Sciences and Disorders, Bioengineering) based upon her/his research program. The successful candidate will be expected to bring vision and energy to developing the NUBIC, coordinating and managing all aspects of the MRI imaging functions, and providing scientific training to MRI users regarding data acquisition, processing and analysis. Support staff, including an experienced technical director and MRI technician, will report directly to the Director. The Director will have her/his office and shared research space in the Institute for Cognitive and Brain Health. The Siemens Prisma 3T MR suite is equipped with state-of-the-art MR-compatible visual and auditory stimulation equipment, eye-tracking, response devices, and physiological measurement hardware. The College of Science comprises six departments: Psychology, Biology, Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Marine and Environmental Sciences, Mathematics, and Physics. Cross-disciplinary research programs and appointments are strongly encouraged within the College, and across the University. The Department of Psychology is strongly interdisciplinary, with 31 tenured and tenure-track faculty, 25 non-tenure-track teaching faculty, and 5 additional faculty with joint appointments in Music, Computer Sciences, Political Science and Communication Sciences and Disorders. In addition to internationally recognized efforts in psychological, cognitive and brain health with a focus on translational and intervention investigation, research in the Department of Psychology spans numerous other disciplines that are central to human health and wellbeing across the lifespan, including developmental and aging neuroscience, brain-body interactions, animal models, language processing, reasoning, social cognition, visual processing and impairment. The Department of Psychology administers or co-administers programs in Psychology and Behavioral Neuroscience for over 2,000 undergraduates, and it trains 60 students in PhDs programs. Under new leadership, the College of Science, and the Department of Psychology are in a vibrant expansion phase. Northeastern is home to the Coastal Sustainability Institute , the Global Resilience Institute , the Barnett Institute for Chemical and Biological Analysis , the Center for Drug Discovery , the Institute for Chemical Imaging of Living Systems , the Network Science Institute , the Northeastern University Center for Renewable Energy Technology , an NSF Frontier Center in Theoretical Biological Physics , and the Center for Interdisciplinary Research on Complex Systems , providing a rich multidisciplinary intellectual environment. Northeastern University is a global university system. Opportunities to collaborate extend beyond the University. Our tenure and promotion process values collaborative research and teamwork. Hires will be mentored for success, with mentoring teams and group guidance. In addition, a strong and effective faculty development strategy is part of the Northeastern institutional mission. The ADVANCE Office of Faculty Development office works in conjunction with the Office of Research Development (ORD), the Office of Institutional Diversity and Inclusion (OIDI), the Center for Advancing Teaching and Learning Through Research (CATLR), and University Decision Support (UDS) to provide programs and trainings to further develop and support a thriving faculty. Responsibilities: Responsibilities will include serving as Director of the Northeastern University Biomedical Imaging Center (NUBIC), serving as a senior faculty member in the Institute for Cognitive and Brain Health (CBH), teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, conducting an independent, externally funded research program, and contributing to the academic mission of his/her department(s).  Interdisciplinary collaboration is supported and encouraged. Faculty members at Northeastern are expected to develop independent research programs that attract external funding; teach courses at the graduate and undergraduate level; supervise students and postdocs in their area of research; and participate in service to the department, university, and discipline. Qualified candidates must have excellence in, or a demonstrated commitment to, working with global student populations and/or in a global work and educational environment. Qualifications: The successful candidate will have a Ph.D. in neuroscience, psychology, physics, biomedical engineering, or a related field, or an M.D., and a record of research commensurate with rank. Candidates should have experience in, or be able to demonstrate a commitment to, working in a global work and educational environment. The Institute is seeking to give excellent candidates across all demographics of society the opportunity, resources, and support to establish innovative and impactful research careers. We encourage applicants from a wide range of backgrounds, including academia and industry. All applicants should have a strong record of scholarly accomplishment that demonstrates the ability to build a strong research program. Candidates seeking appointments at the Associate or Full Professor level should have substantial research productivity and an established history of grant support and academic service. Appointment to the ranks of Associate Professor and Full Professor requires experience and qualifications that reflect a demonstrated record of teaching excellence and scholarly accomplishment. A minimum of six years of relevant experience is required for appointment as Associate Professor, and a minimum of twelve years of relevant experience is required for appointment as Full Professor. Research excellence is the top-most priority. Additional Information: Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, statements addressing the prompts below, and names and contact information for at least three professional references from whom letters of reference can be solicited as needed. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community.  Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts: Research statement (no longer than 5 pages): Please describe the focus of your research, including the questions you have identified, the funding you have received to support the work (if applicable), the results you have discovered, and the products of these efforts. Highlight any cross-disciplinary research efforts you have undertaken. Please also describe any research you have undertaken with students and/or with the external community.  Finally, please outline the research directions you foresee pursuing in the College of Science, any cross-disciplinary and collaborative aspects, and the strategies you will take to address each direction. Teaching statement (1 page): Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the College of Science (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring global student populations. Leadership and Vision statement (1 page): Please summarize your experience in leadership positions and your vision for directing a human subject MRI Center. Describe your past experiences and activities and/or your future plans detailing your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice. Inquiries about the position may be directed to Professor Charles Hillman ( c.hillman@northeastern.edu) , Search Committee Chair and Director of the Institute for Cognitive and Brain Health. Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by the law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Associate Professor: $115,000.00 - $171,000.00 | Professor: $149,000.00 - $317,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/New-York-NY/Executive-Director--Global-Business--Northeastern-University---NYC_R141460-2": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Global Business, Northeastern University – NYC",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and a world leader in experiential learning. The same commitment to connecting with the world drives our use-inspired research enterprise. The university offers a comprehensive range of undergraduate and graduate programs leading to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Our Global University System consisting of fourteen campuses across North America and in the UK are platforms for undergraduate and graduate learning and collaborative research. Northeastern's signature cooperative education model integrates rigorous academic study with professional experience, producing graduates who are career-ready from day one. Summary: Reporting to the Dean of the D'Amore-McKim School of Business (DMSB), and working in close partnership with the Executive Director of US Degree Programs at Northeastern University London, the Executive Director of Global Business - NYC is the founding academic leader charged with building and scaling Northeastern's next-generation business degree: one program delivered across two continents, designed for a future in which competitive advantage comes from human capability — not technical knowledge alone. The Bachelor of Global Business is a transformative degree in which students move between New York and London as a core feature of the degree, not a study-abroad add-on, developing expertise across business, design, technology, creativity, entrepreneurship, and AI along the way. The centerpiece of the degree is a sequence of four required, progressive, cross-year Studios — Foundations, Innovation Lab, Strategy & Implementation, and Leadership & Transformation — each co-taught by one business faculty member and one design/arts faculty member, and each replacing select traditional core courses under an AACSB-compliant, competency-based assessment model. This business design/arts co-teaching model sits at the heart of every studio, and the Executive Director must bring a proven ability to lead cross-college collaboration, building shared ownership between business and design/arts faculty as they design and deliver much of the curriculum together. Beyond the studios, students pursue an I3 studio (a specialization studio, paired with two required electives) in areas such as media and entertainment, fashion and luxury marketing, fintech, global supply chain, and tech and AI leadership — taken at either campus, and serving as the degree's primary vehicle for cross-campus mobility. This is a significant program-building opportunity within Northeastern's global network: a chance to lead the design of an AI-native, studio-based, portfolio-assessed business degree from its earliest working skeleton through launch, in close and continuous partnership with Northeastern University – London. The ideal candidate is energized by building something that does not yet exist — a business education organized around real industry partners, experiential studios, and demonstrated capability rather than a conventional course list — and is comfortable operating amid genuinely open questions about curriculum, concentrations, and market positioning as the program takes shape. The Executive Director will lead the program from its current prototype stage through launch, finalizing the first so-called “I3 (Imagination/Ideation/Innovation) Studio” concentrations, building out the competency-based core curriculum, standing up the industry liaison and studio-partner infrastructure, and ensuring the degree is clearly differentiated from the BSBA in the market from day one. Qualifications: Education & Experience Doctorate or terminal degree (e.g., PhD, DBA) in business, management, entrepreneurship, or a closely related field Significant academic leadership experience, including service at the level of Program Director, Associate Dean, Department Chair, or equivalent, with demonstrated responsibility for curriculum design, faculty leadership, and budget oversight Demonstrated experience building or launching new academic programs, ideally programs involving experiential, studio-based, project-based, or competency-based pedagogy Experience operating in an international or multi-campus higher education environment, ideally including direct familiarity with both US and UK academic structures Experience navigating AACSB or comparable business accreditation processes, particularly where non-traditional curricular models (e.g., studios or labs replacing standard courses) require close attention to competency documentation Track record of building industry partnerships and employer engagement in business, technology, media, or entrepreneurial sectors Technical Knowledge & Skills Deep understanding of studio-based, project-based, and portfolio-assessed curriculum design, including how to structure competency-based course groupings in place of traditional single-subject courses Familiarity with AACSB accreditation standards and the documentation required to substitute studios or labs for required core courses Knowledge of AI-native curriculum design — embedding AI fluency, supervision, and strategic application throughout coursework rather than isolating it in a single class Understanding of experiential and industry-integrated learning models, including co-op education and alternative experiential-semester structures Familiarity with two-campus (or multi-campus) degree mobility design, including how cultural, regulatory, and market differences between locations shape a shared curriculum Understanding of the competitive landscape for innovative business degrees, and the market dynamics differentiating a next-generation program from a traditional BSBA or BSIB Leadership & Management Visionary leadership presence with the ability to articulate a compelling identity for a genuinely new kind of business degree, and to inspire faculty, staff, students, and industry partners around a shared mission Proven ability to lead cross-college collaboration — particularly the business/design-arts co-teaching model at the heart of every studio — building shared ownership between DMSB and CAMD faculty Experience building and sustaining partnerships with employers who sponsor project work, embed practitioners, and hire graduating talent Ability to work effectively in a matrixed, multi-campus environment, with close coordination with Northeastern University London leadership and alignment with Northeastern's central academic and administrative leadership Demonstrated track record of faculty recruitment, development, and retention, including staffing co-teaching faculty pairs across disciplines Strong financial acumen and experience managing program budgets tied to enrollment growth and phased program launches Exceptional communicator and relationship builder, with the interpersonal range to engage credibly with prospective students and families, faculty across two colleges and two campuses, industry partners, and Northeastern's senior leadership Demonstrated commitment to belonging efforts and to building an inclusive academic community across business, creative, and design disciplines Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: Program Vision & Studio Architecture Serve as the founding academic leader of Global Business in NYC, establishing the new bachelor’s degree as Northeastern's next-generation, AI-native business degree and the successor to the BS in International Business in New York. Own the four-studio progressive architecture (Foundations, Innovation Lab, Strategy & Implementation, Leadership & Transformation) as the centerpiece of the degree, ensuring each studio is problem-based, cross-year, co-taught by business and design/arts faculty, and builds toward a coherent student portfolio rather than a traditional transcript. Ensure AI fluency, human-centered leadership, cultural intelligence, and systems thinking are embedded throughout every studio, module, and assessment — not confined to a single course. Curriculum Development & I3 Lab Design Lead the design of the competency-grouped business core (e.g., Business Foundations, Innovation & Tech Leadership, Data-Driven Management & Strategic Decision Making, Global Business & Multinational Organizations) in place of traditional single-subject courses. Finalize and launch the initial I3 Lab concentrations (e.g., media and entertainment, fashion and luxury marketing, fintech and investments, global supply chain, tech and AI leadership, and others under consideration), each paired with two required electives. Clearly differentiate the Bachelor of Global Business from the BSBA in curriculum, student-facing materials, and admissions messaging. Cross-Campus Coordination with Northeastern University London Work in close, ongoing partnership with the Dean of US Degree Programs at Northeastern University London to ensure the degree functions as a genuine two-continent program rather than two parallel offerings. Coordinate I3 Lab availability, studio quality, and concentration equivalence across both campuses, recognizing that shared concentration names may carry a location-specific flavor (e.g., media and entertainment in London versus New York). Oversee the primary cross-campus mobility mechanism — an I3 Lab taken at the partner campus alongside Studio 3 or 4 — and ensure students can move seamlessly between New York and London without disruption to their studio progression or portfolio development. Industry Partnership & Studio Sponsorship Build and lead a portfolio of named industry and community partners who sponsor studio projects across all four years, place embedded practitioners on 6–8 week engagements, and contribute mentors, guest speakers, and 360-style feedback alongside faculty and peers. Establish and oversee a dedicated liaison role, modeled on the co-op office, to source and manage external partners each semester. Cultivate deeper-tier partnerships — co-developing curriculum, embedding tools and case studies, placing staff in the classroom, and establishing a campus presence — consistent with the program's tiered industry partner model. Experiential Semester & Co-op Leadership Oversee the competitive Experiential Semester model as the degree's primary alternative to a traditional co-op, including placements with Northeastern-affiliated startups, campus productions, venture studios, and Studio Manager or Studio Director roles managing a Studio 1 or 2 team. Maintain the Studio Manager (year 3) to Studio Director (year 4) leadership progression as a credited, non-TA role. Continue to support traditional co-op as a parallel path, and advise students on the trade-offs between paid co-op and unpaid, 16-credit Experiential Semesters. Faculty Leadership, Accreditation & Program Performance Recruit, pair, and develop the co-teaching faculty model at the heart of every studio — one business faculty member and one design/arts faculty member per studio — in partnership with the College of Arts, Media and Design (CAMD). Ensure studio-based competency assessment is documented to the standard required for AACSB accreditation, given that studios replace select required core courses. Track enrollment, retention, and post-graduation outcomes; manage the program's operating budget; and prepare regular performance updates for Northeastern's academic and administrative leadership. Benefits: Northeastern University offers benefits-eligible employees a competitive and comprehensive package designed to support your health, financial security, and overall well-being. Employees joining our New York City campus will begin with benefits equivalent to those of the institution formerly known as Marymount Manhattan College until benefits transition to Northeastern's full plans effective January 1, 2027. Northeastern will provide further information in advance of that transition. Current NYC campus benefits include: Medical and dental insurance Retirement plan Life and disability insurance Paid vacation, sick time, leave of absence, and other applicable paid time off Tuition benefits for employees, dependents, and spouses Position Type Leadership Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 115S Expected Hiring Range: $172,845.00 - $250,627.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R142007": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Shansky Lab is seeking a full-time postdoc to conduct National Institutes of Health (NIH)-funded behavioral neuroscience research investigating sex differences in cortical circuits mediating defensive responding. Responsibilities will include rodent colony management, stereotaxic surgery, behavior testing, fiber photometry protocols and data analysis, brain tissue processing for immunohistochemistry, RNAscope, and fluorescent microscopy. Depending on the postdoc’s career goals, they will be mentored in grant- and paper-writing, team management, and public speaking. Minimum Qualifications A PhD in neuroscience, psychology, or a related field and at least three years of experience in a rodent neuroscience lab is required Skills related to the techniques listed above are preferred Required Documents Resume Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Burlington-MA/Academic-Coach_R141993": {
      "title": "Academic Coach",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Description JOB SUMMARY The School of Nursing at Northeastern University seeks a dedicated and experienced Registered Nurse to serve as a full-time Nursing Academic Coach for our Accelerated Bachelor of Science in Nursing (ABSN) program. This position provides individualized and group academic support to students in our intensive accelerated nursing curriculum, helping them achieve academic success and develop strong clinical reasoning skills. The ideal candidate will have a passion for nursing education, strong clinical knowledge across multiple content areas, and the ability to support diverse learners in a fast-paced academic environment.   Compensation & Benefits Northeastern University offers a competitive salary commensurate with experience and qualifications, along with a comprehensive benefits package including health insurance, retirement contributions, tuition remission, and professional development opportunities. About Northeastern University School of Nursing   The School of Nursing at Bouvé College of Health Sciences offers innovative nursing programs across multiple campuses, combining rigorous academic preparation with extensive clinical experiences. Our ABSN program serves career-changing students seeking an accelerated pathway to nursing practice, preparing graduates to provide high-quality, evidence-based care in diverse healthcare settings.   Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit: Cover letter addressing qualifications and interest in nursing education Current curriculum vitae/resume Copy of current RN license Names and contact information for three professional references   Northeastern University is an equal opportunity employer, seeking to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. Northeastern values and celebrates diversity in all its forms and strives to foster an inclusive culture built on respect that affirms inter-group relations and builds cohesion on our campuses.   We encourage applications from individuals who bring diverse backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives to support our mission of experiential learning and global engagement. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Minimum education, experience, and skills required to perform the job successfully: Current, active, and unencumbered Registered Nurse (RN) license in [state] Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN) from an ACEN or CCNE-accredited program Minimum of 5 years of clinical nursing experience in acute care or specialty practice Demonstrated expertise in nursing fundamentals, pharmacology, pathophysiology, and clinical reasoning Strong communication skills and ability to explain complex concepts clearly Experience working with adult learners and diverse student populations Proficiency with learning management systems and educational technology Ability to work flexible hours, including evenings and weekends as needed   Preferred Qualifications: Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) or higher degree Prior teaching, tutoring, or precepting experience in nursing education Experience with accelerated or second-degree nursing programs Clinical experience in multiple specialties (medical-surgical, critical care, pediatrics, maternal health, mental health) Knowledge of NCLEX-RN examination content and test-taking strategies Certification in a nursing specialty area Experience with diverse teaching modalities including simulation and case-based learning   Work Schedule & Environment: Full-time position requiring 40 hours per week, 12 months per year Schedule includes daytime hours with flexibility for evening and weekend sessions based on student needs Hybrid work model may be available with combination of on-campus and virtual tutoring Peak demand periods coincide with examination schedules and end-of-semester periods Work environment includes individual and group tutoring spaces, computer lab access, and virtual platforms   Physical Requirements: Ability to sit for extended periods during tutoring sessions Ability to use computer and educational technology for extended periods Occasional standing for group tutoring sessions or workshops Ability to communicate effectively in person and via technology platforms KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Academic Support & Tutoring 55% Create a collaborative environment that provides opportunities for nursing students to thrive and meet full potential Provide one-on-one and small group tutoring sessions for ABSN students across all nursing courses Assist students in understanding complex nursing concepts, pharmacology, pathophysiology, and clinical applications Develop and implement individualized study plans for students requiring additional academic support Teach effective study strategies, time management skills, and test-taking techniques specific to nursing examinations Support students in developing clinical reasoning and critical thinking skills Student Assessment & Progress Monitoring 20% Assess individual student learning needs and identify knowledge gaps Monitor student progress and maintain detailed records of tutoring sessions Collaborate with faculty to identify at-risk students and develop intervention strategies Provide regular feedback to students, faculty, and program leadership regarding student progress Participate in academic success meetings and remediation planning Resource Development 15% Create supplemental learning materials including study guides, practice questions, and concept maps Develop and maintain a library of tutoring resources aligned with course content Design interactive learning activities to reinforce difficult concepts Utilize technology and educational platforms to enhance student learning Stay current with NCLEX-RN test plan and integrate relevant content into tutoring sessions Program Collaboration 5% Work closely with ABSN faculty to align tutoring services with course objectives and curriculum Attend faculty meetings and program planning sessions as appropriate Participate in program assessment and continuous quality improvement initiatives Collaborate with academic advisors, student services, and other support staff Contribute to retention and student success initiatives Professional Development 5% Maintain current nursing licensure and complete required continuing education Stay current with evidence-based nursing practice and educational best practices Participate in professional development activities related to nursing education Engage in scholarship of teaching and learning as appropriate Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Events---Activities-Coordinator--SEIU_R141920": {
      "title": "Events & Activities Coordinator, SEIU",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-28\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Summary: Reporting to the Director of the Center for Student Involvement, the Student Activities & Events Coordinator helps develop and implement comprehensive leadership development programs intended to enrich the co-curricular experience of undergraduate and graduate students. This role fosters an inclusive and vibrant campus life through student engagement and community building by creating and supporting traditional programs, large-scale campus-wide events, and club-specific events. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities 1)   Student Leadership and Training:   Support all clubs and organizations through leadership development, attendance at club events, assistance with purchasing processes, and ongoing support for advisors and students to help create and sustain the strongest versions of their organizations. Develop club leadership curriculum, recruit participants, and facilitate workshops that support clubs and organizations in ensuring they complete mid-year and end-of-year reports. May also support ad hoc or recurring leadership development trainings for student clubs andorganizations. 2) Event Coordination: Lead and coordinate various aspects of campus-wide social, traditional, and educational programming that supports the intellectual, personal, and social development of all students. Represent the Center at Division of Student Life and larger university events such as Orientation, Admitted Student Day, Friends & Family Weekend, Commencement, and other university programs. Support the department to cultivate partnerships and ongoing collaboration with relevant campus and community stakeholders. Plan and implement annual events and support the creation, assessment, and continuous improvement of yearly programming. Work in collaboration with College Events to manage the event approval process for student-led programming. Schedule vendors and service providers,including catering, security, audiovisual services, College Events, and Facilities, related to areas of responsibility. 3) Administrative Functions: Create reports and publications as needed, including campus-wide applications, website content, and other operational materials relevant to this position. Represent the department on Student Life, campus-wide, and departmental committees as assigned. Manage budgets for areas of responsibility, including purchasing, expense tracking, forecasting, and Concur submissions. Perform related duties as needed. Other duties as assigned that fall within the scope of this position. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree and 3–5 years of relevant experience required. Master’s degree preferred. Student or professional experience in belonging, inclusion, and community-building work that demonstrates the ability to foster a sense of community. Demonstrated expertise in event planning and program management. Knowledge of data collection, assessment, and tracking, ensuring platforms and systems are maintained and utilized to support data-informed decision-making. Ability to demonstrate strong interpersonal skills with students, campus communities, and members of the public. Ability to take appropriate steps to promote and sustain effective working relationships within a diverse environment. Ability to work some evenings and weekends as needed. Position Type Student Services Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Base Pay Rate: $72,318.62"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R141909": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-28\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Postdoctoral Associate – Gene Therapy and Transport Modeling Bajpayee Lab at Northeastern is seeking an exceptional Postdoctoral Associate to lead high-impact research in cartilage-targeted gene delivery within a fast-paced, NIH-funded and industry-collaborative environment. This position is designed for top-tier, self-driven scientists who can independently execute, manage multiple projects, and deliver at a high level. This work integrates gene therapy, chemical conjugation, and quantitative transport modeling to engineer charge-guided delivery systems for dense, negatively charged tissues. What You Will Do Lead multiple projects across federal and industry-funded programs Design and engineer gene delivery systems using chemical conjugation and molecular engineering Quantitatively analyze transport, partitioning, and binding kinetics in biological matrices Conduct and contribute to in vivo studies in musculoskeletal models Independently write and publish high-impact manuscripts Train and mentor students while driving projects forward Manage project budgets and presentations   Who You Are PhD with a strong first-author publication record Expertise in chemical conjugation, biomaterials, or drug/gene delivery Experience with quantitative modeling (transport, kinetics, or related) Comfortable working with animal models Highly motivated, accountable, and able to manage multiple projects independently   Ideal Candidates Experience with gene delivery (mRNA, DNA, CRISPR) Background in cartilage, ECM transport, or polyelectrolyte systems Prior exposure to translational or industry-linked research   Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Executive-Assistant_R141810": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Executive Assistant will be confident in working independently and with ambiguity, as well as being a strong team player. The position works within a wider team supporting the Office of the CIO.  The role will be required to maintain an understanding of the CIO’s strategic priorities and the strategic priorities of the University and apply this awareness to the support of the CIO and Deputy CIOs.    Under direction, this individual will oversee a variety of administrative projects, communicate matters with internal and external constituencies including senior university leadership, faculty, and staff; design and produce reports and presentations, schedule and coordinate meetings, travel coordination and prepare expense reports on behalf of the CIO and Deputy CIOs.  At Team ITS, your success matters as much as the mission. Learn more about our flexible, highly dynamic, and values-first culture at  careers.its.northeastern.edu .  This position is onsite 4 days per week, and eligible for remote work 1 day per week.   Regrettably, the university is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future.     Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree and minimum of 7 years of administrative experience required Expertise of calendar management, specifically managing multiple VPs and AVPs calendars at one time Must be able to work and make decisions independently, to exercise discretion and good judgment in areas of responsibility Must be able to communicate professionally and effectively with people at all levels of the organization as well as with external constituents High-level comfort working with technology is necessary Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite—especially Excel, Word, PowerPoint and Outlook, Teams—and OneNote In this fast-paced office, the successful candidate will be extremely adept at planning, prioritizing and possess a strong organizational background along with ability to be flexible, professional, and creative in responding to requests with short turnaround Strong proofreading, attention to detail and follow through skills are essential as well as flexibility, a positive attitude, and the ability to work efficiently in a busy environment while handling multiple priorities Experience dealing with sensitive personnel information  Preferred Qualifications   Experience with managing and executing projects on behalf of senior member of an organization Experience and willingness to work with digital workflow tools such as Microsoft To Do, Microsoft Flow, SharePoint, and ServiceNow to ensure follow through on AVP requests.   Experience working in IT, technology, or higher education organization preferred.    Key Responsibilities Calendaring and support for CIO Provide support for the CIOs calendar in coordination with Head of the OCIO & IT Operations  Track and manage agendas, projects, action items, priorities, and Microsoft Teams Execute items related to purchase requests on behalf of the CIO Complete expense reports, vendor justification forms & quotes Make purchases on behalf of the CIO Prep and/or complete spreadsheets, reports, presentations, documents and dashboards   Calendaring and Support for the Deputy CIOs Complex calendar management for both Deputy CIOs Daily briefings for Deputy CIOs using OneNote, Microsoft To Do, & Microsoft Flow Execute items related to purchase requests on behalf of the Deputy CIOs Complete expense reports, vendor justification forms & quotes, Make purchases on behalf of the Deputy CIOs Track and manage agendas, projects, action items, priorities, and Microsoft Teams Prep and/or complete spreadsheets, reports, presentations, documents and dashboards   Special Projects Onboarding & Offboarding for the direct hires of the Deputy CIOs Coordinate OCIO events, virtual gatherings, and travel for the CIO & Deputy CIOs Assist with projects and planning for the OCIO on behalf of the Head of the Office of the CIO & IT Operations Assist with special HR requests for the Deputy CIOs & Head of the Office of CIO & IT Operations   Position Type General Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-time-Lecturer--Bioinformatics--Boston-_R141933-1": {
      "title": "Part-time Lecturer, Bioinformatics (Boston)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Individuals are sought to teach graduate courses in science and technology relevant to the bioinformatics field. Courses include primarily biological science courses with minor algorithmic components and primarily programming courses with a focus on bioinformatics methods. Such graduate courses seek experienced bioinformatics, biotech, and data science professionals with a desire to teach for the Bioinformatics Professional Science Master’s (PSM) Program depending upon the programs’ needs and enrollment. Key Responsibilities Responsibilities include the following: review and learn key teaching points within existing instructor guides; be willing to support high-quality teaching and standards through preparation of lectures based on course materials provided; facilitate rich respectful debates and discussions in an in-person or online class; provide timely review and grading of assignments with appropriate coaching and feedback to the students; consistently connect with students and their performance; contribute to the program’s curriculum development efforts; and adhere to Northeastern’s policies. Part-time instructors work under the direction of the director with support from a faculty lead and will hold office hours as appropriate. Qualifications PhD in bioinformatics, biology, or a closely related field. Experience with the application of computational methods to biological problems. Either prior experience in teaching or more than 3 years of experience in course-relevant industry or academic work is required. Proven platform skills required, as in an open, supportive personal style willing to receive assistance and coaching when needed from the faculty lead. Prior experience in a diverse higher education institution preferred. Knowledge of Python and/or R programming languages is strongly recommended. Application Materials CV/resume Cover letter 3 references – provide name, title, company, phone, email, relationship (e.g. my current supervisor) Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. This position will not be sponsored for a work visa.  Note for STEM OPT Participants:   This position may not satisfy the 20-hour per week minimum required to maintain compliance with STEM OPT guidelines. For example, a four-credit course typically involves an estimated weekly commitment of 13.5 hours, which falls short of the required threshold. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,932.00."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Nahant-MA/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow--CSI-TNC-Coastal-Sustainability_R141921": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, CSI-TNC Coastal Sustainability",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Northeastern University’s Coastal Sustainability Institute (CSI) and The Nature Conservancy’s (TNC) Massachusetts and Global Science programs are partnering to offer a joint Postdoctoral Fellowship Opportunity in Coastal Sustainability. ​  ​ Northeastern University (NU) is a global leader in experiential education and research with expertise in marine and coastal ecology, environmental forecasting and climate adaptation, fisheries and ecosystem-based management, ecological engineering and restoration, environmental sociology, and sustainability science. ​ The Nature Conservancy is a world-leading conservation organization with marine priorities currently focused on sustainable fisheries, quantifying the economic benefits of coastal conservation, and the role of ecosystems in protecting societies from coastal hazards and climate change. ​  ​ NU and TNC are active partners in coastal conservation and sustainability science research and practice. As part of this ongoing partnership, NU’s CSI and TNC are excited to announce the fourth round of this Coastal Sustainability Fellows Program for postdoctoral scientists. ​  ​ The CSI-TNC Coastal Sustainability Fellows Program will foster a collaborative, intellectual environment that leverages the interdisciplinary expertise of both CSI and TNC to train postdoctoral scholars and address major challenges facing coastal communities and ecosystems. Qualifications The CSI- TNC Coastal Sustainability Fellows Program welcomes applicants from all fields of study focused on understanding and implementing solutions that promote coastal sustainability. Applicants must have earned a doctoral degree in a relevant field of natural science, social science, or engineering. The 2026 Fellowship competition will prioritize applicants with research interests related to coastal restoration, marsh migration, and community adaptation to flood risk. While not exclusive, specific areas of research connected to these priorities could include: • Addressing ecological and social barriers and enabling factors for coastal restoration, including salt marshes, oysters, and seagrasses • Understanding factors that influence land use decisions related to coastal flood risk, including floodplain protection, coastal relocation, restoration potential, and local socio-ecological dynamics • Identifying and facilitating marsh migration corridors Fellowship Details & Application Process Based on funding availability, the fellowship will provide two years of salary ($60,315 per year) and benefits plus an additional $5,000 each year to support travel for meetings and field research. Fellows will be based at NU’s CSI and Marine Science Center in Nahant, MA, and co-mentored by scientists at CSI and TNC. In addition to research, fellows will have opportunities to develop skills consistent with their goals including grant writing / fundraising, teaching, mentoring, and networking with TNC and academic partners. Initial review of applications will begin on September 21, with an anticipated start date as early as October 2026. Complete applications will include a CV, 2-4 representative publications, names and contact information for 2-3 individuals who can provide letters of recommendation, a 1-page broader impacts statement, and a 2-page research statement that identifies an academic mentor (or mentors) at CSI, and how their research will also link to TNC mentors in the Massachusetts Ocean and Climate programs. Prospective candidates should contact Alison Bowden at TNC and their potential academic mentor at CSI before applying. We are committed to promoting diversity in conservation and sustainability science and therefore strongly encourage applicants from all historically marginalized groups. The duration of this position is contingent upon the continued availability of funding from this award at its current financial level. For questions about the fellowship, please contact: Randall Hughes at rhughes@northeastern.edu and Alison Bowden at abowden@tnc.org Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Social-Media-Audience-Engagement-Specialist_R141938": {
      "title": "Social Media Audience Engagement Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Northeastern University is looking for a creative, highly motivated Social Media Audience Engagement Specialist to help lead Northeastern Global News’ (NGN) multi-platform audience engagement strategy. The person in this role will put NGN’s award-winning journalism in front of new audiences and drive audience growth and engagement through platform-native content, distribution, and steady experimentation. The Social Media Audience Engagement Specialist focuses on Reddit, Instagram, LinkedIn, Facebook, and other platforms to share owned news content and build engagement around it. The individual will partner with editorial and multimedia teams to identify and implement strategies for sharing newsworthy content across platforms that promote Northeastern's research and teaching enterprise. This role is ideal for someone who lives and breathes news and social media, including being a Reddit expert, and has a deep understanding of data analytics, strategic audience growth, and news content distribution. Higher education experience is not required. The person in this role knows social media deeply and keeps a close eye on how platforms and their audiences are evolving. They bring strong news instincts and editorial judgment, can turn complex research into content that travels, and act as a creative partner to the newsroom rather than a distribution channel. While the role focuses primarily on NGN content, it also calls for supporting Northeastern’s enterprise accounts during high-visibility moments and major university initiatives. Working alongside editorial, multimedia, and digital teams, the person in this role will use platform-native formats, social listening, analytics, and constant testing to grow the audience and deepen engagement with reporting. This role reports to the Director of Social Media within the Communications division of Northeastern’s External Affairs team. Northeastern is a global research university with 14 campuses in three countries. The university’s Communications strategy focuses on promoting the university, its faculty, students, and community by showcasing leadership and innovation across the enterprise. The position is based on the Boston campus and is fully in-person. After-hours and weekend work are occasionally required. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities NGN Audience Growth & Distribution Grow NGN’s audience and reach across social media, with Instagram and Reddit as the priority channels. Continue building NGN’s Reddit distribution strategy by expanding presence in relevant subreddits, sharing reporting in ways that respect each community’s culture and rules, facilitating faculty Ask Me Anything (AMA) sessions, and managing a two-prong account approach. Shape NGN’s journalism into platform-native formats by partnering with reporters and video producers to identify stories with high social potential and turn faculty research into content that travels. Build recurring social media franchises that encourage audience return visits, test new platforms and features, and scale successful approaches. Audience Insights, Performance & Social Listening Set measurable goals, track performance, and use analytics to strengthen content strategy. Surface audience insights that inform editorial decisions. Conduct social listening and monitoring by identifying conversations, sentiment, emerging stories, and reputational signals, and routing relevant information to appropriate stakeholders. Enterprise & Cross-Functional Support Support Northeastern’s enterprise accounts and campaigns during high-visibility moments and major university initiatives. Collaborate closely with NGN journalists, multimedia producers, media relations colleagues, and faculty colleagues to maintain cohesive messaging across platforms. Minimum Qualifications 3-4 years of social media experience, ideally in news, media, or a fast-moving brand. Strong news judgment and an instinct for stories that will travel. Demonstrated ability to grow a social media audience, with measurable follower and engagement gains. Expertise in Reddit, including familiarity with Reddit’s community culture and interest in reaching audiences there. Fluency in Instagram, with working knowledge of Reddit, LinkedIn, X, Bluesky, TikTok, YouTube, and Threads. Ability to execute a data-informed strategy against clear, measurable goals. Experience with social analytics and listening tools such as Sprinklr, Sprout Social, Meta Business Suite, or native platform insights. Exceptional writing, copyediting, and proofreading skills, with the ability to adapt voice across platforms. Strong visual judgment and the ability to refresh existing templates and graphics. Ability to independently manage NGN’s channels while collaborating across teams and supporting enterprise work as needed. Consistent attention to detail. Required Documents Resume Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_R136621-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity As a Postdoctoral Fellow, the candidate will conduct research on the fundamental theories of signal processing and explore their applications in modern communication systems. Work will emphasize the development and analysis of advanced methods in areas such as sparse signal recovery, compressed sensing, and statistical estimation, with a particular focus on their role in efficient transmission and robust communication. This work involves formulating rigorous analytical models, designing efficient algorithms, and establishing performance characterization methods that drive both theoretical progress and practical implementation in communication networks. Through these efforts, the Postdoc will generate impactful academic contributions that support the design of next-generation communication infrastructures. QUALIFICATIONS: Ph.D. in Applied Mathematics, Electrical & Computer Engineering, Computer Science, Industrial Engineering, or a closely related field by start date. Strong theoretical expertise in mathematical statistics, information theory, and the principles of signal estimation and detection, with the ability to translate these foundations into rigorous models and practical solutions for communication systems. Familiar with programming language including Python and MATLAB. Proven research and academic writing experience, including the preparation of high-quality scholarly articles for peer-reviewed journals and conferences. Experienced in mentoring and supervising students on research projects, with demonstrated leadership skills in guiding collaborative work. Excellent communication abilities, including fluent spoken English and strong presentation skills, enabling effective dissemination of research outcomes and active engagement in international academic discussions. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Sr-Electronic-Lab-Technician_R140812": {
      "title": "Sr Electronic Lab Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: The Sr. Electronic Lab Technician provides comprehensive technical and operational support for teaching, research, and student project activities within the Department of Chemical Engineering. This role supports faculty, staff, undergraduate and graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and visiting scholars by delivering expertise in design, fabrication, equipment maintenance, and laboratory operations. The position ensures that laboratory infrastructure, equipment, and instructional environments function efficiently, safely, and reliably. The Sr. Electronic Lab Technician maintains equipment, coordinates equipment acquisition, and provides technical guidance and mentoring to students. This position plays a critical role in supporting hands-on learning and research productivity within the department. Reporting to the Dir. of Operations and Business Management in the Chemical Engineering Department, the Sr Electronic Lab Technician will perform an array of duties, including: (1) Working safely with, in, and around lab equipment; (2) Assembling/installing equipment or parts; (3) Performing tasks/tests on equipment and instruments; (4) Configuring, operating and adjusting various analytical instruments; (5) Conducting troubleshooting on various electronic and mechanical equipment; (6) Troubleshooting, modifying, and repairing lab equipment and instruments (7) Supporting and, when needed, overseeing the work of students in the Chemical Engineering Capstone Design courses and (8) Providing assistance to students.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge of mechanical/electrical testing equipment. Ability to fabricate samples for mechanical/electrical testing. Knowledge of safe lab operations. Ability to work with and coordinate with the Department Safety Officer, purchasing, and NU facilities. Proficiency in programming, including Arduino, Raspberry PI, and some circuit design and sensor selection. Knowledge and skills required are normally obtained through vocational training/technical certification and 5-8 years of experience. Trade school and 5-8 years of experience Effective communication for collaborating with researchers and students. Strong mechanical and fabrication skills. Commitment to maintaining a safe work environment. Proficient in 3D printing and associated CAD software (SolidWorks and/or AutoCAD) · Experience with Data Acquisition systems · Experience with MATLAB Proficiency with MS office suite (Word, Excel, Outlook, Power Point). Additional Requirements Work is performed in laboratory and classroom environments. Regular use of equipment, tools, and laboratory instruments. May require lifting, moving, and installing equipment and materials. Work required to be done on campus, Monday through Frid   KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES: Lab Maintenance & Operations Maintain, replace, or install teaching and research lab equipment and analytical instruments to ensure safety, reliability, and continuous operation. Perform or assist with scheduled preventive maintenance, calibration, and troubleshooting in teaching and research labs. Provide safety oversight for electronic-based and mechanical-based projects, manage data acquisition systems, and troubleshoot issues. Repair - Diagnose, disassemble, and make all necessary repairs to equipment in the teaching and research labs. Collaborate with departmental staff and other Northeastern University units to support fabrication, as needed Be an active member of the Department Safety Committee. Locate Replacement Equipment & Parts- Find suitable replacements for un-repairable or outdated lab equipment and parts. Lab Space Repairs and Improvements - Work with NU facilities (submit work orders) to have electrical, plumbing and HVAC problems in teaching and research labs corrected. Contribute to and maintain teaching laboratory inventory tracking and stock management. Contribute to and maintain equipment maintenance logs and calibration records Fabrication & Programming Operations Fabrication- Use 3D printing & hand tools to support teaching lab operations. Support students with Design- Use CAD software (AutoCAD & SolidWorks) to create drawings of parts, fixtures and assemblies used by research lab personnel, teaching lab students, ChemE Car members and Capstone students. Student Supervision, Technical Consultation, Training & Mentoring Consult and mentor on student projects in the ChemE Teaching Labs, specifically regarding design, construction, testing, and troubleshooting of electronic and mechanical assemblies Assist students when needed by: Providing safety oversight for electric- and electronic-based projects, supporting hands-on learning by providing technical guidance that enhances student project outcomes and research productivity. Consult and mentor students on part design, manufacturability, tolerancing, material selection, and fabrication best practices. Train & supervise students in safe 3D printer and analytical instrument operations Procurement Support Source outside vendor for equipment and instrumentation if unable to fix in-house Order shop supplies, tooling, materials, and consumables Coordinate the acquisition of new equipment by obtaining quotes, advising on technical specifications, and supporting purchasing decisions. DOCUMENTS REQUIRED : Resume Cover Letter   Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108H Expected Hiring Range: $29.00 - $40.96 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Academic-Advisor-SEIU_R141824": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor SEIU",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Under the supervision of the Director of Global Undergraduate Academic Advising, the Academic Advisor delivers student-focused academic advising to undergraduate students enrolled in pathway programs offered across Northeastern network campuses. The Academic Advisor helps students identify their interests, abilities, and academic goals while providing general program advising across a portfolio of undergraduate degree programs. This position works in partnership with advising units from each program’s home college on the Boston campus to support student success and retention. Responsibilities include identifying and supporting at-risk students, assisting with registration and academic planning processes, maintaining student records, participating in recruitment and orientation activities, developing collaborative campus partnerships, and teaching multiple sections of the Introduction to College course. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Academic Advising and Retention (60%) Partner with academic advising units on the Boston campus and other global campuses to provide general academic advising and support for undergraduate pathway program students. Advise students in alignment with best practices in student support and success. Utilize the early alert system to identify, track, and conduct outreach to at-risk students. Maintain documentation of student interactions and accurate student records. Monitor student academic progress and provide appropriate guidance and referrals. Assist students with schedule planning, course registration, add/drop processes, withdrawals, and major declaration. Identify struggling students and make referrals to appropriate university resources and support services. Participate in advising-related committees and initiatives that support student success. Teaching (25%) Teach sections of the Introduction to College course. Collaborate with undergraduate academic advising units on the Boston campus to develop new course modules and teaching approaches as appropriate. Monitor student attendance and grades for assigned course sections. Submit progress reports as needed. Special Projects (15%) Serve as a liaison and collaborate with designated campus departments and offices, including the Registrar, the Office of Global Services, and WeCare. Participate in advising-related committees and collaborative initiatives. Support Admissions activities, events, and student recruitment efforts as needed. Develop and coordinate relationships with various campus offices to enhance services and support for students. Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor's degree. 2 to 3 years of relevant professional experience. Experience working with diverse student populations. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Understanding of educational requirements and academic advising processes. Demonstrated proficiency with a variety of software applications. Strong organizational skills. Experience working collaboratively as part of a team. Preferred Master's degree in Higher Education, Counseling, Social Work, or a related field. Required Documents Resume Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Base Pay Rate: $72,320.11"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Director-of-Admissions_R141839": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Admissions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Under the direct supervision of the Director of Admissions Enrollment Strategies, the Associate Director of Admissions collaborates with admissions leadership to manage critical components of the first-year evaluation and transfer enrollment processes. The Associate Director is responsible for identifying efficiencies and process improvements through strong project management, process design, and operational leadership. As part of the application evaluation process, the Associate Director manages 75-100 part-time seasonal application evaluators, including recruiting and hiring seasonal staff, overseeing training, coordinating daily assignments, and administering evaluator feedback processes. The Associate Director oversees transfer application review operations, including coordinating revisions to the application evaluation manual, training staff in transfer file review and customer service, monitoring reading queues, making daily assignment decisions, supervising the review of special applicant populations, conducting quality control reviews prior to decision release, running daily productivity reports, and participating in applicant selection processes. The Associate Director also oversees seasonal reading staff during the application review cycle as needed and supervises 1-4 Assistant Directors based on unit needs. This position works closely with university offices, programs, and relevant external organizations to support prospective students throughout the enrollment process. The Associate Director serves as a liaison with campus partners to provide key information that supports student enrollment decisions. As a member of the admissions leadership team, the Associate Director manages a recruitment territory, representing the University through recruitment travel, applicant evaluation, student and family counseling, presentations, interviews, and relationship management with guidance counselors and transfer advisors. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Temporary Application Evaluation Management Serve as a senior member of the application evaluation team Coordinate application review efforts and determine seasonal staffing needs to meet evaluation goals and timelines for increasing first-year and transfer applicant pools Maintain and advertise job descriptions and compensation rates for seasonal readers Screen and identify candidates to support the seasonal reading plan Manage the hiring process for seasonal application evaluators Conduct onboarding for 75-100 seasonal staff members annually Facilitate regular check-ins with seasonal staff and maintain a visible management presence to address questions and concerns Manage and document the temporary application evaluator feedback process Traditional Transfer and Contract Enrollment Serve as the primary point of contact for college partners and campus stakeholders regarding transfer processes, transfer initiatives, and prerequisite course requirements for transfer applicants and contract completers Lead transfer customer service operations, including monitoring the transfer@northeastern.edu inbox, transfer communication channels, and applicant phone inquiries Ensure transfer applicant inquiries receive timely responses Develop, maintain, and train staff on transfer review criteria Execute transfer application reading plans Serve as the primary point of contact for students offered and completing a contract for future enrollment Conduct transfer and contract support, evaluation, and selection activities Maintain awareness of population and college enrollment targets throughout the evaluation and selection process Territory Management – Recruitment Travel, Customer Service, and Application Evaluation Plan, design, and implement recruitment strategies for assigned geographic territories serving first-year and transfer students Manage recruitment travel in a cost-effective and efficient manner while meeting office and university priorities Meet with, counsel, and provide information to prospective students, families, and guidance counselors Conduct follow-up activities and evaluations after recruitment travel Read and evaluate applications from assigned recruitment territories Communicate with secondary school counselors regarding admission decisions when appropriate Counsel applicants, families, and counselors throughout the application evaluation process Develop and implement targeted recruitment programming and enrollment activities Manage assigned territories throughout the admission cycle Conduct interviews and presentations Establish, develop, and maintain relationships with guidance counselors and transfer advisors while effectively representing the University Management of Direct Reports Hire, supervise, train, and evaluate direct reports involved in recruitment and application evaluation activities Mentor staff to support successful completion of job responsibilities Provide direction, guidance, and daily operational support to direct reports Ensure compliance with university policies and procedures Admissions Leadership Team Participation and Unit Support Serve as a member of the Admissions management team Develop, communicate, and implement office policies and procedures Collaborate with management colleagues to support the achievement of institutional enrollment goals Support a positive and professional work environment Demonstrate professionalism and support institutional, divisional, and office policies and procedures Adhere to management expectations and provide high levels of customer service to students, colleagues, and internal and external stakeholders Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor's degree 4-6 years of progressively responsible experience in college admissions, higher education, or a related field Strong supervisory, interpersonal, and leadership skills Demonstrated commitment to staff development Demonstrated skill in creative thinking and problem solving Excellent written, verbal, presentation, and communication skills Emotional intelligence and professional maturity Experience developing efficient and effective policies and systems that manage high-volume operations while maintaining process quality and outcomes Ability to interpret enrollment research and apply findings to strategic planning Experience working with diverse populations Proven understanding of technical systems Strong organizational skills and attention to detail Strong customer service orientation Ability to manage relationships diplomatically with stakeholders and partners Knowledge of higher education issues related to student recruitment, admission, and enrollment Required Documents Resume Position Type Enrollment Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Strategic-Initiatives-Specialist_R141812": {
      "title": "Strategic Initiatives Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY Reporting to the Director of Executive and Strategic Initiatives, the Strategic Initiatives Specialist will assist with the management and execution of high-priority research, meeting content preparation, presentations, reports, and high visibility projects in support of key priorities for the External Affairs division. The ideal candidate has experience in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly collaborative environment, and is adept at working with leadership to translate priorities and accomplish goals efficiently. This person will also be a well-equipped communicator, able to clearly identify and translate information for stakeholders at varying levels of seniority. Skills in using AI tools to productively and efficiently deliver quality work are a plus. A never-ending curiosity and solutions focused attitude are important qualities in the right candidate. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree required with 3-5 years of relevant experience. Excellent communication skills, including strong writing, research, and editing ability. Strong strategic thinking and analytical skills and ability to synthesize information and develop creative, detail oriented, and impactful solutions. Mature professional who can manage and facilitate an intense workload, produce quality results in a fast-paced environment, and demonstrate discretion in handling sensitive and confidential information. Must be proactive, anticipate problems, and be resourceful to find workable solutions. Proficiency with MS Office products, including Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint, and Outlook, is required. Experience in higher education is not needed, nor is it desired. This role requires in-person presence five days a week. Some evening and weekend hours may be required. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Project and Content Management  Assist in research and preparation for major university events, including Commencement. Assist in research and preparation for presidential and executive priorities. Collaborate on university-wide messaging while exercising sound judgment for progress and direction on behalf of the division. Assist with planning and creating content for high-priority meetings and events, including presentations and summaries. Proactively assist with developing strategic opportunities as related to relevant projects. Evaluate and provide feedback for submissions from the departments in the division. This could involve editing documents, maintaining a current knowledge of division priorities, providing relevant responses, etc. Other duties as assigned. Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Program-Manager-of-Data---Partnerships_R141798": {
      "title": "Senior Program Manager of Data & Partnerships",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Community-Engaged Teaching and Research (CETR) is a part of Educational Innovation in the Office of the Chancellor and promotes ethical experiential learning at Northeastern. The CETR team supports service-learning courses, community-engaged research, and various experiential learning opportunities aligned with curricular objectives. Through student leadership programs, faculty development, and community partnership building, CETR facilitates meaningful experiences on and off-campus .     While this team frequently contributes to international dialogues on community-engaged practices in higher education, CETR is also recognized nationally as a top service-learning program.  Service-Learning (S-L) is a form of experiential learning for students and a teaching tool for faculty that purposefully integrates their academic coursework with service to meet classroom and community goals throughout the semester. Our Community-Engaged Teaching and Research team collaborates with over 60 faculty members incorporating service or community engagement into 100+ courses each year. Some of our signature programs and events include the S-L Teaching Assistant program, S-L Fellows program, Virtual EXPO, Request for Partnerships process and community partner recruitment, faculty development workshops, and more.   Key Responsibilities & Accountability: 1)Strategic Stakeholder Engagement:   Develop institutional relationships with over 100 community-based organizations through multiple mediums (i.e. emails, phone calls, face-to-face).    2) Events and Project Development:   Plan and support events (semester, annual, and bi-annual) to strengthen campus and community relationships and align with academic scholarship .   Update faculty and community partner training materials and communication platforms for CETR programs, ensuring relevant information is accessible.   Work with and provide support to program leadership as they plan program functions including recruitment events, information sessions, graduation celebrations, etc.   Provide administrative oversight on special projects and strategic initiatives within the CETR team. 3) Project and Operational Implementation:   Collaborate with the Associate Director to manage day-to-day operations and track faculty engagement, community partnerships, student leader participation, and course information.   Provide administrative oversight fo r and engagement with a network of 75+ faculty and 100+ community partnerships.   M onitor the CETR shared inbox for inquiries and resource requests.   Streamline processes and enhance efficiency within CETR operations. 4) Evaluation, Assessment , and Data Analysis :   Manage and implement evaluation processes for current programming, utilizing data to propose evidence-based program enhancements. Collaborate with university analytics teams to align assessment strategies with institutional priorities. Collect, track, and compile key metrics on programs as directed by Program Directors or other College leadership. Lead the organiz ation of our internal databases (ex. Airtable , Excel, Salesforce, etc.)   5) Communications and Campus Collaboration:   Oversee external (off-campus) communication efforts and collaborate on internal network storytelling.   Represent CETR at relevant campus events, collaborat e with other community engagement staff and contributing to broader team goals.   Minimum Qualifications This position plays a crucial role in the implementation and maintenance of CETR programs and operations.  Reporting directly to the Associate Director, this role will ensure the effective execution of CETR initiatives and support ethical community engagement in teaching and research partnerships.      Operationally, we are looking for someone who: Holds a bachelor’s degree ; m aster’s d egree preferred.   Holds 1-5 years of experience in community engagement at a higher education institution or related organization.   Has showcased experience in qualitative data methods, program evaluation, and assessment, with backgrounds in thematic coding and survey design strongly desired.    Has showcased experience in quantitative data methods, with a background in statistical analysis strongly desired.    Holds foundational skills in managing complex databases (e.g. Airtable), with deep background s in coding languages (in order of preference : JavaScript , HTML, and CSS) strongly desired.    Has showcased experience in managing projects that impact multiple stakeholders simultaneously ; experience in related budget or finance management a plus.    Characteristically, we are looking for someone who: Communicates effectively across verbal, written, and interpersonal contexts    W ork s collaboratively in a fast-paced, diverse, and urban environment.   Can handle shifting priorities or routinely cycling through multiple tasks    Takes ownership of projects with appropriate follow-through and initiative    Works independently while contributing effectively to team goals    Demonstrates excellent organization, time-management, and attention to detail    Combines critical and creative thinking approaches    Shows passion for social advocacy and community development    Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Director---Engineering-Design-Program_R140894": {
      "title": "Professor of the Practice & Director - Engineering Design Program",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and a world leader in experiential learning. The same commitment to connecting with the world drives our use-inspired research enterprise. The university offers a comprehensive range of undergraduate and graduate programs leading to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Our campuses across North America and in the UK are platforms for undergraduate and graduate learning and collaborative research. Northeastern's signature cooperative education model integrates rigorous academic study with professional experience, producing graduates who are career-ready from day one. Summary: Reporting to the Dean of Engineering and in close collaboration with the Dean of the Oakland campus, the role provides strategic and visionary leadership and administrative oversight for the BS in Engineering Design program at the Oakland campus. This is a founding leadership role responsible for launching, building, and scaling a first-of-its-kind undergraduate engineering program designed to produce AI-fluent, human-centered, interdisciplinary engineers who are ready to contribute from day one in industry. The ideal candidate is an innovative engineering educator who brings both deep technical expertise and a track record of reimagining how engineering is taught. This leader understands that the next generation of engineers must be more than technically proficient — they must be AI-fluent, entrepreneurially minded, and equipped to solve complex, human-centered problems across disciplinary boundaries. They are excited by the opportunity to build a program from the ground up that challenges traditional engineering education models and sets a new standard for what an undergraduate engineering degree can be. The role is responsible for creating, expanding, and managing a diverse portfolio of industry partnerships, curriculum development initiatives, and experiential learning opportunities — including co-op placements and industry-sponsored capstone projects — that bring the program's vision to life. This role operates within a self-supporting financial model and requires demonstrated ability to develop revenue-generating partnerships at multiple levels, ranging from project sponsorships and curriculum co-development to fully integrated talent pipeline and co-location arrangements. Working collaboratively across engineering faculty, academic administrators, and external industry partners, the Director builds a unified, high-impact program that integrates technical engineering fundamentals with AI fluency, human-centered design, entrepreneurial thinking, and multidisciplinary systems thinking. The Director develops and promotes marketing initiatives through strategic public relations and community outreach, and works in concert with campus leadership to strategize on program growth across the Bay Area. The position demands exceptional ability to lead interdisciplinary groups, convene diverse stakeholders, and build consensus across departments and industries to achieve the program's mission of producing engineers of the future who can not only build what is technically possible, but imagine, design, and bring to market what is genuinely needed. Qualifications: Education & Experience: PhD in engineering or a closely related field required Demonstrated experience designing, launching, or significantly reforming engineering education programs, with a focus on experiential, interdisciplinary, or AI-integrated learning models Minimum 5 years of progressively responsible experience in academic program leadership, industry partnership management, or workforce development within an engineering or technical higher education setting Experience in a co-op-based or experiential learning environment strongly preferred Record of scholarly or applied work in engineering education, curriculum innovation, or related fields is a plus Technical Knowledge & Skills: Deep understanding of engineering education pedagogy, competency frameworks, and industry workforce trends, with a particular emphasis on next-generation and AI-integrated curriculum design Familiarity with the application of AI tools — including machine learning, simulation, and data analysis — in engineering practice and education Broad knowledge across multidisciplinary engineering domains including mechanical, thermal, electrical, and data systems, sufficient to credibly lead a cross-disciplinary program Experience with learning management systems, co-op or experiential learning platforms, and CRM systems Understanding of industry accreditation standards relevant to undergraduate engineering programs (e.g., ABET) Leadership & Management: Proven track record of successful program development and revenue generation within a self-supporting business model Demonstrated ability to build and sustain partnerships with engineering firms, technology companies, and industry organizations, spanning project sponsorship, curriculum co-development, and deep talent pipeline integration Experience leading interdisciplinary teams and managing complex, multi-stakeholder initiatives Track record of innovation in program delivery, industry engagement, and business model development Budget management experience and financial acumen Established relationships with engineering and technology organizations in the Bay Area or comparable markets Ability to develop and lead strategic initiatives and follow through to completion; excellent management, organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills A high degree of self-motivation and self-direction, coupled with a proven track record of collaborative work and demonstrated success in partnership development and relationship management Ability to work in a matrixed environment to deliver against shared goals Effective communicator and collaborator with the ability to motivate others toward shared goals Ability to track activity and report results; ability to collaborate with faculty and staff across programs, schools, colleges, and departments including Deans, Associate Deans, Program Directors, and other academic administrators Organizational tact and diplomacy; superb interpersonal skills required to develop close relationships with external partners and key influencers across the university and the broader Bay Area community Demonstrated ability to build internal and external collaborations, finding solutions benefiting all parties, and focusing on long-term success over short-term results; ability to serve as an ambassador for the university in the region Demonstrated commitment to belonging efforts and building an inclusive engineering pipeline Flexibility for travel within the region and as needed Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities:                                                          Strategic Planning & Innovative Program Development (25%) Develop and execute annual strategic plans for the BS in Engineering Design program that align with Northeastern's mission and respond to engineering workforce trends. Champion and operationalize the program's innovative educational model — integrating AI-native instruction, human-centered design, and interdisciplinary project-based learning across all four years of the curriculum. Oversee the program launch in Fall 2027 and implement long-term plans to ensure growth, continuous curriculum innovation, and academic excellence. Identify and pursue opportunities to expand the program's reach and reputation within the Bay Area and beyond. Work closely with faculty to embed AI tools, real-world industry projects, and entrepreneurial frameworks throughout the student experience. Industry Partnership Development & Business Growth ( 25%) Establish and maintain a structured portfolio of industry partnerships at three levels of engagement: (1) Project and Problem Sponsors, who contribute real-world design challenges for student teams and participate in project reviews and mentorship; (2) Curriculum Partners, who co-develop course content, embed tools and case studies, and help shape the technical and professional competencies built into the degree; and (3) Integrated Partners, who employ students during co-ops, co-fund faculty roles, and may co-locate on the Oakland campus to build a sustained talent pipeline. Generate business contracts totaling established annual revenue goals through direct outreach and partnership development. Coordinate communication and provide consultation to stakeholders across engineering, technology, and business sectors. Co-op & Experiential Learning Oversight (20%) Oversee the development and management of co-op placements for two required co-ops per student, typically at the end of Year 2 and Year 4. Ensure all students have access to meaningful, industry-embedded experiences that align with program competencies and the goal of producing day-one-ready engineers. Develop processes for co-op employer onboarding, student preparation, and post-placement evaluation. Monitor co-op quality and employer satisfaction to maintain program standards and student outcomes. Program Operations & Supervision (15%)  Direct day-to-day operations of the Engineering Design program, ensuring policies, procedures, and academic standards are upheld. Oversee scheduling, enrollment, and delivery of program components, achieving enrollment targets and maintaining quality. Analyze enrollment and performance data quarterly to inform program adjustments and resource allocation. Implement and maintain technology systems that support efficient program operations, industry project management, and student tracking. Financial Management & Resource Development (10%)  Achieve annual revenue targets within a self-supporting business model. Develop and manage the annual operating budget, oversee program expenditures, and maintain fiscal responsibility achieving break-even or surplus. Identify and pursue external funding opportunities including grants, corporate sponsorships, and philanthropic support. Prepare and submit all required budget and performance reports on schedule. Maintain accurate financial tracking and reporting systems. Marketing, Outreach & Team Leadership (5%)  Lead marketing efforts to promote the program to prospective students, industry partners, and the broader Bay Area engineering community, positioning it as a national model for innovative engineering education. Supervise, evaluate, and develop direct reports, conducting annual performance reviews and providing ongoing coaching. Recruit, train, and retain qualified instructors and industry-based faculty to deliver high-quality educational programming. Lead interdisciplinary planning committees and working groups to achieve collaborative program development goals. Foster a team culture of innovation, collaboration, and continuous improvement. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Professor of the Practice: $127,000.00 - $250,000.00 | Professor of the Practice: $127,000.00 - $250,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Animal-Care-Technician_R141697": {
      "title": "Animal Care Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Animal Care Technician provides husbandry and care to laboratory animals, maintains animal inventory records and Animal Husbandry and Room Maintenance Records and performs facility sanitation in DLAM’s Animal Facilities.  This person is required to complete assigned work schedules with little to no supervision.  The technician should be able to work independently and make correct decisions in their daily routine.  The position requires weekend and holiday work hours.  The position involves daily exposure to laboratory animals and their waste products. Minimum Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalency degree.  A minimum of two years of animal care experience.  ALAT certification preferred. Job Duties: The staff member must be able to demonstrate the knowledge and skills necessary to provide appropriate animal care for his/her assigned responsibilities. With little to no supervision, follow assigned schedules for husbandry, care and housing of laboratory animals.  Technician responsible for feeding, watering, and cleaning the cage of their assigned animals. Reports all animal heath or behavioral problems to the appropriate staff. Maintains animal inventory records and Animal Husbandry and Room Maintenance Records for each assigned room. Able to cover and work in all/most areas in animal facility. Reports all facility and environmental issues to the Director. Provide guidance and on the job training to new animal technicians. Provide assistance with receiving and processing incoming animals and supplies Sanitizes and maintains the animal research facility equipment. Operates cage-wash, autoclave, and other animal facility related equipment. Assembles and prepares caging supplies for use in the animal facility. Performs euthanasia on small laboratory animals. Participates in technician meetings as scheduled by supervisory staff. Participates in continuing education training programs throughout employment as requested May be trained to provide specialized technical assistance to the DLAM Veterinary Staff and investigator staff. Required to work weekend and holidays.  Occasional overtime work is also required to cover work due to absences of other animal care technicians. Required to comply and follow DLAM and NU-IACUC policies and procedures. Performs all other duties necessary for the proper functioning of the animal facility as assigned by the supervisory staff. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $25.00 - $30.00 hourly rate"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Student-Part-Time-Lecturer---Nonprofit-Management--Boston-_R139496-1": {
      "title": "Student Part-Time Lecturer – Nonprofit Management (Boston)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a non-tenure track student part time faculty lecturer in Nonprofit Management on the Boston campus.   The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.    Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa.   Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Teach classes related to: nonprofit legal governance, nonprofit financial management, nonprofit human resources, nonprofit grant writing and nonprofit fundraising.   Qualifications Master’s degree and three or more years of teaching experience or Doctorate in related area such as nonprofit, higher education, management, or significant years of experience in the nonprofit field. On-line teaching experience desirable.   Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae.   Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter.   Please direct questions to Monica Borgida at m.borgida@northeastern.edu .   Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled.   Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $1569.00."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-or-Full-Teaching-Professor-and-Director-of-Graduate-Programs--School-of-Pharmacy-and-Pharmaceutical-Sciences_R141571": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Teaching Professor and Director of Graduate Programs- School of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Position Summary: The School of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences and Department of Pharmaceutical and Biomedical Sciences seek a highly innovative and experienced 52-week non-tenure track (NTT) faculty member to serve as Associate/ Full Teaching Professor and Director of Graduate Programs, acting as the academic and administrative leader for the School's M.S. and Ph.D. programs. In addition to the typical faculty workload of teaching and scholarship, this role’s service focus includes advancing, scaling, and modernizing graduate education (in all its modalities: on ground, online synchronous or asynchronous, hybrid; and across the global university system of network campuses), with particular emphasis on interdisciplinary areas such as biomedical sciences, AI-driven drug discovery, biopharma and biotech, translational therapeutics, and other emerging fields. The position will also provide the governance[KC1.1][CS1.2], oversight and operational rigor required to sustain high-quality curriculum, assessment, and student support/success led by experiential learning and partner-driven collaboration. The Director will chair the SOPPS M.S. and Ph.D. Committee, advancing the Committee's agenda in response to standing charges and charges assigned by the SOPPS executive committee. The Director will guide the Committee on curriculum development and assessment in alignment with institutional imperatives and the standards of the New England Commission of Higher Education (NECHE), including the development of learning outcomes and application of assessment strategies. In addition to this governance role, the successful candidate will lead strategic growth of the MS and PhD programs across domestic and internation markets, enhance student recruitment and outcomes, and develop industry-aligned curricula that position the School, Department, College, and University as a national leader. Key Responsibilities: Program Leadership, Governance & Oversight Serve as the primary academic and administrative lead for graduate programs ensuringe program alignment with institutional, accreditation, and regulatory requirements, including NECHE standards. Oversee curriculum governance, updates, and compliance processes related to MS or PhD programs in the school Manage program assessment, evaluation, and continuous curriculum improvement processes in collaboration with the M.S./Ph.D. Committee, Program Manager, and the Office of the Assistant Dean of Academic Affairs and Assessment Chair the M.S./Ph.D. Committee, advancing its agenda in response to standing and assigned chargesWork with the M.S./Ph.D. faculty and leadership at the Department and School to seek industry collaborations and funding opportunities such as industry-led PhD contracts, training grants, pre-doctoral fellowships, and other funding from industry, government and other sources Curriculum Innovation & Assessment Propose and develop new market-aligned graduate courses/programs (e.g., AI in drug discovery, cell therapy, immuno-engineering, among others) in collaboration with the strategic vision of the Department and School, and in close collaboration with the Bouvé Dean’s Office and the university’s Innovation Planning and Development and EM Product Launch units Lead modernization and scaling of existing and future programs with particular emphasis on interdisciplinary areas such as biomedical sciences, AI- and technology driven integration, biopharma and biotech, translational therapeutics, and other emerging fields Ensure appropriate curricular content delivery based on learning modalities (i.e., on ground, online synchronous or asynchronous, hybrid, etc.) and location across the global university system of network campuses Integrate experiential learning, industry partnerships, and research-based training Guide the M.S./Ph.D. Committee in developing learning outcomes and applying assessment strategies consistent with NECHE standards Direct the delivery and annual review of the M.S. and Ph.D. curricula and associated learning-assessment programs Develop systems to collect, analyze, and report assessment data drawn from students, faculty, staff, alumni, and experiential partners for accreditation and other tracking needs across the Department, School, College University. Student Success, Mentoring & Academic Integrity Oversee student progression, advising structures, and milestone achievement in collaboration with program faculty and staff. Ensure timely completion of academic and administrative milestones (e.g., candidacy, graduation) Provide graduate-student mentoring and advising Serve as an ex officio member of all MS thesis and PhD dissertation committees when not an official member Formulate, execute, and oversee remediation plans for student academic misconduct and academic probation in collaboration with the Bouvé Office of Student Services Collaborate with student services and global programs, including the Bouvé Graduate Office and the Bouvé Office of Graduate Advising and Student Services, to ensure conformity with NECHE curricular and assessment accreditation standards Student Recruitment & Enrollment Growth Partner with enrollment management to develop and execute recruitment strategies Evaluate graduate applicants and improve yield from diverse, high-quality candidate pools Expand domestic and international pipelines Collaborate with the SOPPS Assistant Director of Admissions and the PhD Program Manager to advance graduate-student recruiting efforts, including outreach campaigns, applicant evaluations, admissions offers, and offer follow-up External Engagement & Partnerships Build partnerships with industry, biotech, hospitals, research institutions, and other potential partners to strengthen educational programs for students and professional opportunities for alumni Develop co-op opportunites, advisory boards, and collaborative training programs to benefit the program Represent the program in external and internal meetings, activities, and events Faculty & Instructional Leadership Work with the Department Chair on aspects related to the chair’s determination of teaching assignments and course scheduling Work with the Department Chair on aspects related to the chair’s recruitment and oversight of oversee part-time/adjunct faculty Teach graduate courses per year based on departmental workload policies and administrative role as program director, which usually carries 1-2 course releases per year Plan for and engage appropriate offices and individuals in professional development activities for faculty and staff in curriculum, pedagogy, educational innovation, and assessment Strategic Growth, Reporting & Communication Identify new market-align program opportunities and teaching modalities (i.e., on ground, online synchronous or asynchronous, hybrid, etc.) such as MS in Biomedical AI, Cell Therapy, Digital Health) Lead grant development and educational funding initiatives to benefit graduate students in MS and PhD programs Align programs with workforce and market needs/opportunities Provide input into the School's strategic initiatives with respect to graduate programs Work with Department, School and College faculty and staff involved in curriculum, experiential learning, enrollment management, research, scholarship, and innovation to advance, scale, and modernize graduate education Compile the annual MS and PhD Committee Report and assist in preparing school-wide annual goals for the MS/PhD programs Ensure efficient communication with school and college faculty and staff regarding curriculum and assessment activities related to graduate pharmaceutical sciences courses Faculty Responsibilities All faculty are expected to engage in teaching and scholarship. Teaching workload will be discussed annually with the department chair and will be allocated based on departmental workload policy. Qualifications Required: PhD in Pharmaceutical Sciences, Biomedical Sciences, Bioengineering, or related field Significant experience in graduate education leadership or program development Demonstrated ability to design and implement innovative curricula Strong organizational and leadership skills Experience with committee governance, curriculum, programmatic assessment, and accreditation processes Preferred: Experience in interdisciplinary fields (drug development, AI/ML, immuno-oncology, cell therapy, digital health)  Industry or translational research experience  Track record of successful student recruitment and program growth  Experience managing accreditation or program evaluation, including NECHE or similar regional accreditors  Experience chairing academic or graduate committees Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: 52-week Salary range(s): Associate Teaching Professor: $118,000.00 - $134,000.00 | Teaching Professor: $126,000.00 - $163,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Assistant-Associate-Coop-Coordinator_R141616": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Coop Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity: Northeastern University is a global leader in cooperative education and experiential learning in which students alternate between academic study and work experiences.  The Office of the Chancellor is seeking a Co-op Faculty Coordinator to work with students and employer partners, with an initial focus on D’Amore-McKim School of Business undergraduate programs.  This is a full-time, non-tenure track 12-month faculty position based at the Boston campus and will observe a hybrid work schedule. You will join a team of 30+ highly motivated, professional, collaborative individuals who provide an outstanding cooperative education program to business students.  Responsibilities: Education of Students : The Co-op Coordinator educates students through their career development. They educate, advise, and coach students, developing and delivering effective teaching and coaching programs that enable students to be successful in today’s global business environment. This includes, but is not limited to, working with students one-on-one, in workshops and in the classroom. The Co-op Coordinator teaches a career education course each semester, and coaches students through group and individual meetings. Classroom preparation includes teaching interviewing skills, professional behavior and communication, workplace ethics, and as well as leadership skills and self-awareness and emotional intelligence development. The Co-op Coordinator also maintains contact with students while engaged in their co-op experience and works with students through their post-co-op reflection. Integrated Learning : Cooperative education is an integral part of the academic experience at Northeastern and our philosophy is that learning in the classroom is enriched by practical experience, and practical experience enriches classroom learning. The Coordinator collaborates with academic faculty, administration, and the co-op community to guide students in the integration of their co-op and academic experiences. The Co-op Coordinator shares knowledge of industry with academic faculty to integrate employment-based learning with curriculum-based learning. Employer Relations/Job Development: The Co-op Coordinator develops new partnerships and expands current employer programs by engaging with managers, human resources contacts, and alumni. They engage in lifelong learning to develop and maintain a current understanding of the industry, employers, and positions. Activities to support this endeavor include site visits to new and existing employers, outreach to potential new co-op employer partners, and active participation in relevant professional organizations. High-level customer-orientation, strong presentation skills, and experience in building and maintaining client relationships is essential. Prior professional experience in industry is preferred. Service : The Co-op Coordinator also engages in service to the Office of the Chancellor, D’Amore-McKim School of Business, and to Northeastern University. Service includes making presentations to prospective students and newly admitted students; participation on committees and task forces; and serving as an advisor to student organizations. Qualifications: A master’s degree in college student development, counseling, education, psychology or a business-related field, is required by the appointment start date, as well as a minimum of three years of professional work experience. Experience as a career coach, certification preferable, but not required. Teaching experience in a classroom environment or comparable experience with presentation skills in a business or workshop environment. Excellent written and oral communication skills, and comfort presenting to diverse audiences. Experience working with a varied staff and/or student population. Proficiency in computer/technology and database maintenance, with excellent time management and organizational skills. Knowledge and appreciation of cooperative education and/or experiential learning and its impact on an individual’s career development. Ability to effectively engage a diverse network of global stakeholders, across time zones. Preferred Qualifications: An academic degree in a related business profession, or experience in career development and counseling of business students. Knowledge of cooperative education and/or experiential learning. Experience working with an international student population. Experience developing employer partnerships and supporting experiential learning opportunities. Professional experience within the financial services industry is a plus Applicants should submit a resume and detailed cover letter highlighting relevant professional experiences and their desire to work with students to strengthen their professional career journeys. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: Assistant Coop Coordinator: $66,000.00 - $80,000.00 | Associate Coop Coordinator: $73,000.00 - $93,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Seattle-WA/Open-Rank-Clinical-Professor-and-Director-of-Didactic-Education---Department-of-Medical-Sciences_R141679": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical Professor and Director of Didactic Education - Department of Medical Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Department of Medical Sciences is recruiting a Director of Didactic Education (DDE) and Open Rank Clinical Professor in the Physician Assistant Program on our Seattle campus. The DDE serves as the academic leader of the didactic curriculum and is responsible for ensuring the quality, integration, assessment, and continuous improvement of all didactic educational experiences within the program. The Open Rank Clinical Professor is responsible for teaching in the didactic component of the Physician Assistant Program curriculum, ensuring the high educational and medical quality of the curriculum, program organization and administration, and student education and mentoring. The position also carries expectations for scholarship, including contributions to the advancement of PA education and clinical practice, as well as service to the program, university, and broader professional community. The Open Rank Clinical Professor will assist in maintaining accreditation standards and participating in any activities necessary to maintain Northeastern at the forefront of PA education. The position is a 52-week, 1.0 FTE, non-tenure track faculty position. Minimum Qualifications Minimum of a Master's degree in a field related to physician assistant practice or the education of physician assistant students; doctorate preferred Currently certified by the National Commission on Certification of Physician Assistants (NCCPA) Licensed or eligible for licensure in the state of Washington A minimum of three (3) years of clinical experience as a practicing clinician Experience in PA education (e.g., lecturer, preceptor, or core PA faculty member) Working knowledge of PA program accreditation and regulatory requirements, including familiarity with ARC-PA standards Strong interpersonal, organizational, problem-solving, and critical thinking skills Strong written and oral communication skills Proficiency with standard computer applications (e.g., word processing, email, spreadsheets, databases) Knowledge of legislative, regulatory, legal, and practice issues affecting PA education, students, and the PA profession Principal Faculty Responsibilities As a principal faculty member, the Director of Didactic Education shares responsibility, alongside the Program Director and other principal faculty members, for the development, implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the PA program, including the following core functions: Develop, review, and revise the program's mission statement, goals, and competencies Serve on the admissions committee, including selecting applicants for admission Coordinate, teach, and instruct courses based on areas of content and clinical expertise Provide continuous development, maintenance, and evaluation of both didactic courses and clinical rotation sites Facilitate small group discussion sessions; participate in simulation, and physical diagnosis and skills labs Monitor, evaluate, and document student performance to ensure that they successfully achieve the criteria for successful completion of courses Assist in the development and implementation of remediation plans for students in the didactic and clinical years, as needed Advise and mentor students; participate in all aspects of retention and academic progression Design implement, coordinate, and evaluate the curriculum Participate in clinical site development Participate in clinical site monitoring, including performing site visits during clinical rotations Participate in all aspects of continuous quality improvement activities and using programmatic data to support ongoing program enhancement Participate in all aspects of Program administration Perform additional assignments at the direction of the Program Director of the Physician Assistant Program Director of Didactic Education Responsibilities Provide leadership for the design, implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of the didactic curriculum Serve as the primary steward of the didactic curriculum, ensuring alignment with the program mission, competencies, graduate outcomes, and ARC-PA accreditation standards Develop, maintain, and continuously update the program's curriculum map to ensure appropriate sequencing, integration, progression, and documentation of curricular content and competencies Monitor the depth, breadth, sequencing, integration, and currency of the curriculum through ongoing review of course content, instructional methods, student outcomes, and programmatic assessment data; identify curricular gaps, redundancies, and opportunities for improvement. Chair the Academic Program Curriculum Committee and facilitate systematic curriculum review, documentation, and implementation of approved curricular changes Recruit, orient, support, and collaborate with course directors and instructional faculty to promote excellence and consistency in teaching, assessment, and course design Coordinate and oversee the didactic instructional schedule, including courses, laboratories, simulation activities, assessments, and other educational experiences. Ensure intentional integration of simulation-based education, procedural skills instruction, standardized patient experiences, and interprofessional education throughout the didactic curriculum in collaboration with faculty responsible for these educational activities Collaborate with the Director of Clinical Education to promote seamless integration between the didactic and clinical phases of the curriculum and ensure student readiness for supervised clinical practice experiences Collaborate with the Medical Director to ensure the medical accuracy, clinical relevance, and evidence-based nature of the didactic curriculum Assist with accreditation activities, including preparation of reports, documentation, and evidence related to the didactic curriculum, and participate in accreditation site visits and continuous quality improvement initiatives Promote innovation and educational excellence through the adoption of evidence-informed instructional strategies, educational technologies, and faculty development initiatives Additional Information: This position may be structured as a four-day academic schedule to accommodate one day per week of clinical practice. Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, statements addressing the prompts below, and at least three references. Successful faculty at Northeastern are dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering a sense of belonging among all members of the Northeastern community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the experiences, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your experience, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompt: Teaching statement: Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the Bouvé College of Health, and your strategies for teaching and mentoring a cohort of graduate PA students. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by the law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: 52-week Salary range(s): Assistant Clinical Professor: $114,000.00 - $139,000.00 | Associate Clinical Professor: $133,000.00 - $154,000.00 | Clinical Professor: $152,000.00 - $192,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-1_R140742": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate-1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Postdoctoral Research Associate will lead independent, cutting-edge research within the Barecka Lab, focused on the electrochemical conversion of carbon dioxide to ethanol. Supported by an externally funded grant, this role offers a unique opportunity to contribute to the development of transformative technologies. The successful candidate will take primary responsibility for designing, planning, and executing experiments that drive the project's scientific and technological progress, with a strong emphasis on timely advancement toward scale-up and commercialization. In addition, the Postdoctoral Research Associate will contribute to the preparation of quarterly progress reports, actively engage in discussions with project sponsors, and play a visible role in communicating research outcomes. Minimum Qualifications: Required Knowledge of electrochemistry fundamentals Proficiency in performing electrochemical tests Knowledge of analytical chemistry tools such as Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR), Gas Chromatography (GC), High-Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC), and Mass Spectrometry (MS) Proficiency in academic writing, time management, and communication High level of scientific independence, initiative, and accountability Ph.D., or foreign equivalent, in Chemical Engineering, Environmental Engineering, Environmental Science, Chemistry, by employment start date. Preferred Experience operating electrochemical flow reactors, preferably for CO2 reduction; candidates with proficiency in reactor modeling will also be considered in lieu of this experience Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities: Design, plan, and execute independent research experiments in consultation with Prof. Barecka; complete research independently and communicate progress in regular meetings. Generate and analyze experimental data in support of the project's scientific and technological goals. Read and review literature relevant to the project scope. Communicate results in laboratory group meetings, scientific conferences, and publications. Prepare progress reports, presentations, manuscripts, and patent applications. Participate in professional development activities including workshops, seminars, and courses. Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Charlotte-NC/Open-Rank-Clinical-Professor-and-Program-Director--Charlotte--NC----School-of-Nursing_R137273-1": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical Professor and Program Director (Charlotte, NC) - School of Nursing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Northeastern Founded in 1898, Northeastern is a global research university and the recognized leader in experience-driven lifelong learning. Our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and partners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Our locations—in Boston; Arlington, Charlotte, North Carolina; Miami; London; Portland, Maine; Oakland; San Francisco; Seattle; Silicon Valley; Toronto; Vancouver; and the Massachusetts communities of Burlington and Nahant—are nodes in our growing global university system. Through this network, we expand opportunities for flexible, student-centered learning and collaborative, solutions-focused research. Northeastern’s comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs— in a variety of on-campus and online formats—lead to degrees through the doctorate in nine colleges and schools. Among these, we offer more than 195 multi-discipline majors and degrees designed to prepare students for purposeful lives and careers. The Bouvé College of Health Sciences has over 250 faculty members, with approximately 2,000 undergraduate and 2,400 graduate students. It is the leading national model for education and research in the health, psychosocial and biomedical sciences and supports the University’s mission of educating students for a life of fulfillment and accomplishment and creating and translating knowledge to meet global and societal needs. The School of Nursing in the Bouvé College of Health Sciences at Northeastern University has developed a research-intensive/teaching/learning environment for students and faculty. Creating an environment where students thrive begins with the faculty. The School of Nursing faculty are a committed group of professionals who are highly qualified, nationally known, and characterized by a range of experiences in nursing practice, research, and education. The ultimate goal is to advance the School of Nursing’s position to one of the nation’s top research schools of nursing. The wide range of programs offered by the School of Nursing from baccalaureate through DNP and PhD degrees highlights the responsiveness of the school to the healthcare needs of individuals and communities in the state, nation, and globally.  The School of Nursing is committed to co-creating an environment conducive to incubating innovative ideas and strategies aimed at creating a more dignified patient-family-centered healthcare system. About the Opportunity The School of Nursing invites applications for a full-time Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Clinical Professor and Program Director for the Accelerated Bachelor of Science in Nursing program offered at the Charlotte, NC campus.  This position has an anticipated start date in January 2027. The candidate will have demonstrated teaching excellence and commitment to supporting faculty, staff, and student environments. The candidate should possess a terminal degree in Nursing, Education, or related field. We welcome candidates with interdisciplinary expertise who can lead and collaborate with faculty and researchers at Bouvé and across Northeastern University.   Responsibilities will include teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, engaging in scholarship, and participating in departmental, college, and university service. Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations.  Responsibilities of the Program Director include, but are not limited to, the following: Oversee and provide content needed to ensure accuracy and compliance with governance and regulatory processes including: curriculum governance/catalog updates, website updates, marketing materials, college database maintenance (e.g. programs list or accreditation information), professional licensure disclosure, accidental injury or liability insurance enrollment, DNP/PhD student funding as applicable and other program-specific consents and waivers, as applicable. Ensure timely submission of forms relating to students’ achievement of milestones or other academic needs (such as CPT approvals, DNP/PhD candidacy, thesis or dissertation milestones, responses to Office of Global Services, Co-Operative Education, etc.). Collaborate with Bouvé College Enrollment Management and Global Launch Services on strategic plans for program enrollment growth or sustainability across our global university system. Help to recruit and yield qualified students from different backgrounds and ensure that all correspondence with prospective and enrolled students is accurate and timely. In collaboration with Bouvé College Enrollment Management, evaluate applicants for admission to graduate and second-degree programs and internal transfer applicants to undergraduate programs. In partnership with Office of Student Services, monitor academic standing and progression of all students enrolled in the program and complete graduation clearance processes as requested. Supervise or provide department-based professional student services, such as program-specific advising and guidance on professional licensure as applicable. Complete faculty advisor training program offered by Office of Student Services. Initiate and foster external relationships that are beneficial to the program and/or that inform program development, including with professional associations, clinical placement and internship sites, guest lecturers, industry leaders or program advisory board members, alumni, potential donors, and partner institutions for prospective applicants to the program. Represent the program at department, college and university events that relate to the viability and success of the program such as open houses, convocation, commencement and faculty meetings. Execute and oversee program assessment and evaluation; manage any professional accreditation and/or licensing review as needed by specific program; and/or university requirement. Oversee execution of the program’s assessment plan. Forecast and manage the allocation of program and university resources in collaboration with the school Dean (when applicable) including: Staff support and faculty teaching workload (within program and in collaboration with Associate Dean of Academic Affairs as needed for interdisciplinary programs), class demand forecasting and optimal class scheduling (via the Courseleaf/CLSS system), orient new faculty hires regarding program student learning outcomes and assessment and other matters specific to instruction within the program and review part-time faculty evaluations with Unit Leader and make recommendations regarding future part-time faculty hiring. Qualifications Terminal degree in Nursing, Education, or related field by appointment start date. Evidence of an established area of research and scholarship, teaching excellence, and demonstrated excellence and commitment to supporting faculty, staff, and student environments. Licensure in the state of North Carolina Eligibility for RN licensure in Massachusetts Additional Information Interested candidates should apply here with a curriculum vita that includes a list of publications, statements addressing the prompts below, and a letter of interest indicating the position you are applying for. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as they arrive. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of research and teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community.  Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their research, pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work with written statements addressing the following prompts: Teaching statement: Please summarize your past instructional and mentorship experiences, your pedagogical philosophy, your plans/goals for teaching in the Bouvé College of Health Sciences (including existing and proposed courses), and your strategies for teaching and mentoring global student populations. Leadership statement:   Please describe your past experiences and activities and/or your future plans detailing  your leadership style, how you intend to cultivate a culture that promotes a sense of belonging, including fostering a free and open exchange of ideas and managing different perspectives to help others strengthen their practice.   All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by the law.   Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: 52-week salary ranges: Assistant Clinical Professor: $84,000.00 - $102,000.00 | Associate Clinical Professor: $92,000.00 - $112,000.00 | Clinical Professor: $101,000.00 - $123,000.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Director---Contracts_R141705": {
      "title": "Associate Director - Contracts",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Associate Director of Contracts plays a key role in enabling successful technology commercialization at Northeastern University. The role has a primary focus on drafting, negotiating, and managing legal technology transfer and commercialization agreements that facilitate partnerships between the University, faculty inventors, and external partners. The Associate Director leads the contracts function for the unit, providing strategic oversight, operational leadership, and subject matter expertise across all aspects of research and related agreements. The position is responsible for ensuring efficient contract execution, mitigating institutional risk, and advancing the continuous improvement of contracting processes and systems. The Associate Director of Contracts supports the Center for Research Innovation (CRI) through effective leadership of the technology transfer contracts function and works closely with the Commercialization Team to advance successful commercialization of the University's intellectual property. This position reports to the Executive Director and works closely with the Office of General Counsel (OGC) to accurately represent the University's legal position on matters related to complex intellectual property (IP) and research agreement terms. This position requires strong managerial capability combined with an entrepreneurial mindset to optimize the technology transfer contracts function of the Center for Research Innovation. Organized and timely communication, efficient time management, and demonstrated prioritization skills are essential for success in this role. This position directly supervises one full-time Contracts Administrator. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Contract Management and Agreement Execution (60%) Draft, negotiate, and finalize technology transfer and commercialization agreements, including exclusive licenses, non-exclusive licenses, field-of-use licenses, option agreements, inter-institutional agreements, material transfer agreements, non-disclosure agreements, and amendments Evaluate contractual risk and develop appropriate mitigation strategies while exercising independent judgment and serving as a point of escalation for complex agreements Collaborate with the Office of General Counsel to ensure all agreements comply with university policies and legal requirements Provide guidance, mentorship, and direction to staff involved in contract review and administration, supporting professional development and fostering a collaborative, high-performing team environment Lead and oversee agreement deliverables related to licensing revenue, compliance reporting, and grant requirement adherence Develop and maintain frameworks that promote contract quality, accuracy, and consistency in contract management procedures Oversee the day-to-day operations of the contracts function, including prioritizing workload, monitoring performance, and ensuring timely and consistent service delivery Maintain accountability for the performance, effectiveness, and continuous advancement of the contracts function Identify and implement process improvements, enhance systems and workflows, and contribute to the long-term strategy for contracts management services Licensing Support and Commercialization (25%) Collaborate with the Commercialization Team to provide specialized expertise related to contract terms, intellectual property rights, and licensing options Evaluate the risks and rewards of proposed licensing arrangements and guide decision-making to maximize commercial value while mitigating institutional risk Work with internal and external parties to resolve conflicts and explain contract terms Monitor contract compliance and track contract-related deliverables for financial reporting and compliance purposes, ensuring alignment with grant requirements and iEdison reporting standards Support technology evaluation and licensing strategy development Research Collaboration and Support (10%) Collaborate with Northeastern University Research Enterprise Services (NU-RES) and draft, negotiate, and finalize intellectual property provisions in sponsored research and collaborative research agreements Address complex intellectual property issues and negotiate favorable agreement terms Respond to inquiries related to encumbrances on university intellectual property and provide guidance regarding licensing and ownership rights Provide guidance to researchers and university faculty on intellectual property matters Support the identification and evaluation of intellectual property assets generated through university research for potential commercialization opportunities Professional Development and Relationship Management (5%) Maintain professional relationships with the Office of General Counsel, Center for Research Innovation leadership, and external partners Stay current on developments in technology licensing and commercialization practices Conduct training on licensing agreements and contract processes for internal stakeholders Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor's degree Minimum of 3-5 years of experience in technology licensing, contract negotiation, or commercialization Experience in technology licensing, contract negotiation, and commercialization Experience negotiating and drafting technology transfer and commercialization agreements Demonstrated knowledge of legally binding agreement terms and considerations, including intellectual property rights, indemnification and insurance requirements, publication restrictions, ownership of research data, and export control issues Strong knowledge of technology licensing and commercialization principles Knowledge of licensing and business principles Strong negotiation and agreement drafting skills Understanding of key issues faced by academic institutions when negotiating contracts with industry, particularly in the areas of intellectual property management, publications, data ownership, and data rights Demonstrated organizational and analytical skills with the ability to establish priorities and drive projects to completion Ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously and successfully execute competing priorities Excellent written, verbal, interpersonal, and communication skills Ability to interact effectively with colleagues, licensees, inventors, and external partners Ability to work independently and collaboratively Ability to proactively identify and develop solutions to complex problems Demonstrated integrity, professionalism, courtesy, and a collaborative approach to relationship building Demonstrated ability to work cooperatively and collaboratively with internal and external stakeholders Preferred Juris Doctor (J.D.) degree Master's degree in Law or Business Technology transfer experience Required Documents Resume Position Type Legal and Regulatory Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 112S Expected Hiring Range: $99,010.00 - $143,567.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Temporary-Assistant-Coach-Diving-Pool-Manager_R141766": {
      "title": "Temporary Assistant Coach Diving/Pool Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Summary: The Temporary Assistant Coach Diving will assist the head coach in maintaining a highly competitive Division I program to include recruitment of prospective student-athletes, practice and game coaching, fundraising, community service, off-season conditioning, and administrative tasks. This temporary role also oversees the day-to-day operation, maintenance, and administration of Barletta Pool; oversees the hiring, training, testing, and certification of lifeguards; ensures that all appropriate water health and safety standards are maintained. Responsible for the continued assessment and upkeep of the pool; required to work on pre-determined weekends, mornings, and evenings to cover events throughout the year. There is potential for a full-time position for the right candidate. Qualifications: Assistant Coach Diving: A bachelor’s degree is required. Candidate must have two to four years of diving coaching and recruiting experience in Division I Women’s Swimming & Diving; a thorough knowledge of recruiting practices and NCAA regulations; good organizational, communication, and time management skills; and the ability to take on multiple tasks and work independently or in a team setting. Must be willing to work nights and weekends as well as be available for travel depending on the team schedules. Previous collegiate competition experience is preferred. Pool Manager: Currently hold/willing to obtain: Certification/Licensure American Red Cross Lifeguard Instructor Certification, American Red Cross Professional Rescuer, CPR/AED Instructor Certification, and National Swimming Pool Foundation Certified Pool Operator Certification. Ability to recruit, supervise, and train employees, including organizing, prioritizing, and scheduling work assignments. Knowledge of swimming pool water chemistry, temperature, and cleanliness standards. Ability to inspect facility for risk management or maintenance concerns and identify resolutions. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $30/hour"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Student-Recruitment---Retention-Specialist_R141761": {
      "title": "Student Recruitment & Retention Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About Mills Institute @ Northeastern University Upward Bound Upward Bound (UB) is a federally funded college preparatory program that offers assistance to low-income and first-generation college-bound students. The focus of Upward Bound is to develop skills needed for a college education. UB is dedicated to helping participating students graduate from high school with a solid foundation, including completion of required coursework and a strong GPA, necessary for entering college. Upward Bound continues to support participants to ensure continued college enrollment and, ultimately, graduation. The Mills Institute @ Northeastern University Upward Bound program has played an integral role in the Oakland Unified School District for the last 50 years and is now beginning a relationship with the West Contra Costa Unified School District to continue building and expanding close relationships with eligible students and their families. The program’s ultimate goal is to equip students with the skills and motivation necessary for college graduation. Job Summary The Student Recruitment & Retention Specialist provides outreach, recruitment, and administrative support for the Upward Bound program. Reporting to the Director, Associate Director, and/or Office Manager, this position develops and manages comprehensive recruitment initiatives designed to increase participation among students from historically low college enrollment communities. The role works directly with students, families, partner schools, and community organizations to support the student application and onboarding process, facilitate student engagement activities, and assist with program operations. This is a temporary position through April 2027 with the possibility of extension or renewal for the 2027-2028 academic year. The position may require travel. Compensation is $20 per hour for up to 25 hours per week. Reports to: Director, Associate Director, and/or Office Manager Supervises: None Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Recruitment of Potential Upward Bound Scholars Develop and manage comprehensive and strategic student recruitment initiatives to increase the number of students from historically low college enrollment communities Conduct recruitment presentations at feeder school sites and various classes Recruit prospective students at open house events and community outreach activities Use data to identify students who may be eligible for the Upward Bound program Support prospective students and families with completion of the Upward Bound student application Conduct initial student and parent interviews to determine program eligibility and assess program fit Process applications in a timely manner Develop program marketing materials Maintain detailed files for program applicants Supporting Student Retention Activities Facilitate workshops for newly admitted students during bi-monthly Saturday College sessions Facilitate workshops for newly admitted middle school students during Saturday College Conduct outreach via email, text, and telephone to recently admitted students Support Upward Bound staff during Saturday College and Wednesday events Communicate regularly with students, parents, and community partners to support the application and enrollment process Program Outreach and Event Support Collaborate with staff to plan and implement recruitment and engagement activities Conduct outreach to area high school students Plan and support recruitment events General Office Support Support administrative tasks as assigned by the Program Director and/or Office Manager Provide general program and office support as needed Perform other duties as assigned by the Program Director Minimum Qualifications Required High School Diploma Demonstrated proficiency in a related field Strong written and verbal communication skills Inspirational and culturally aware, with demonstrated knowledge of challenges facing low-income and first-generation students Knowledge of child and adolescent development and different learning styles Ability to effectively work with students from diverse academic, socioeconomic, cultural, linguistic, and ethnic backgrounds Ability to manage multiple activities, services, deadlines, and priorities while maintaining attention to detail and accuracy Clean driving record and reliable transportation Ability to travel to multiple school sites Ability to lift up to 50 pounds Required Documents Resume Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $20/hour"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Executive-Director---Career-Futures_R141744": {
      "title": "Executive Director - Career Futures",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary Reporting to the Assistant Vice President of Leadership Relations and Campaign Strategy, the Executive Director will build and lead Northeastern's Career Futures platform, creating a university-wide and globally connected capability that brings together workforce intelligence, employer insight, alumni and parent engagement, industry partnerships, and lifelong learning into a cohesive ecosystem supporting professional success throughout the career lifecycle. Working collaboratively across Leadership Relations and Campaign Strategy, Employer Engagement & Career Design, Global Enterprise Initiatives, Education Innovation, the Co-op ecosystem, colleges and schools, academic leadership, Northeastern's global campus network, and external partners, the Executive Director will serve as Northeastern's institutional connector to the evolving global talent marketplace. Bringing current, working knowledge of talent dynamics, career pathways, hiring trends, and emerging opportunities, this leader will translate workforce intelligence into scalable engagement strategies, thought leadership, programming, and partnerships that create meaningful value for alumni, parents, students, employers, and the university. As a newly created role, the Executive Director will help shape both the vision and operating model for Career Futures. Building on successful pilots and emerging initiatives across the university, this leader will establish the infrastructure, partnerships, governance, and scalable operating model needed to transform innovation into a sustainable institutional platform.   Minimum Qualifications Minimum of 10 years of progressive leadership experience in workforce strategy, talent, employer engagement, alumni engagement, executive search, human capital, organizational development, or a related field. Demonstrated knowledge of labor market trends, workforce dynamics, talent ecosystems, and the future of work, with the ability to translate insight into scalable programs, partnerships, and engagement strategies. Experience building new initiatives, operational infrastructure, partnerships, or platforms within complex organizations. Ability to engage alumni and parents as active contributors and strategic partners, not solely as audiences. An entrepreneurial, first-mover mindset paired with the global fluency and nimbleness to work effectively across Northeastern's global network of campuses. Outstanding communication, relationship-building, and executive presence, with the ability to convene diverse stakeholders and influence across organizational boundaries. Strong analytical orientation and experience using workforce intelligence, engagement metrics, and outcomes data to guide strategy and measure impact. PREFERRED EXPERIENCE Experience working within higher education, employer engagement, workforce development, talent strategy, or another highly matrixed organization. Familiarity with AI-enabled workforce technologies, labor market intelligence platforms, and digital engagement tools. Established professional credibility and networks within one or more industry sectors, with the expectation that Career Futures will expand strategically over time across additional sectors and disciplines. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Build & Scale the Career Futures Platform Own the Career Futures platform, working with cross-university collaborators (employer engagement, co-op, employer partners, alumni and parent relations, advancement, etc.) to align around shared workforce thought leadership. Serve as the scaling point for proven pilots emerging from Global Enterprise Initiatives and network-market testing, building the durable infrastructure to run them as an ongoing part of Career Futures. Lead Workforce Intelligence The workforce intelligence function will look beyond domestic labor market trends to understand how talent, skills, industries, and career pathways are evolving across the markets in which Northeastern's students, alumni, employers, and campuses are located. Create a workforce intelligence function that monitors talent trends, employer priorities, skills gaps, technology, etc. and translates those into ongoing lifelong career value. Activate the Career Futures Network Northeastern's global network creates a distinctive opportunity to connect alumni and parents not only within industries, but across markets—creating pathways for mentorship, learning, entrepreneurship, and professional opportunity that transcend geography. Connect alumni and adjacent audiences with the people, industries, and opportunities shaping the future of work, while activating Northeastern's global alumni and parent network as a source of mentorship, career intelligence, and pre- and post-graduate professional opportunity. Engage alumni and parents as coaches and cohort leaders, and offer credentialing programs aligned with industry needs, providing both vertical depth within sectors and horizontal skills across industries, with particular attention to supporting alumni founders and entrepreneurs. Build this network as a two-way relationship, where alumni and parents gain access to Northeastern's workforce intelligence and opportunities while contributing the insight and mentorship that make the network valuable in the first place. Prioritize sectors where Northeastern alumni and parents are most concentrated and where workforce disruption creates the greatest opportunity, launching with industries offering strong existing C-suite alumni and parent relationships and expanding from there, positioning Northeastern as industry's strategic partner in talent development, with an eye on global labor markets. Advance Northeastern’s Voice Position Northeastern as a trusted voice on workforce transformation, lifelong learning, and career mobility through content, partnerships, and engagement that Northeastern is uniquely able to deliver. Offer candid, substantive perspective on where the field is headed, reaching senior alumni and parent audiences and cultivating high-value relationship s and future collaboration. Position Type Advancement Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 114S Expected Hiring Range: $130,945.00 - $189,868.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Editor_R141745": {
      "title": "Senior Editor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Senior Editor - Northeastern Global News Northeastern Global News (NGN), Northeastern University's award-winning, fast-paced, daily news organization is looking for a talented Senior Editor to be based on Northeastern's Boston campus. The ideal candidate is an adaptable and creative journalist with experience in top-tier media organizations, who will help drive innovative and timely storytelling, set news agenda and manage content production. The Senior Editor must have several years of experience working for daily commercial news organizations. The experience should include meticulous line-editing often under tight deadlines, factchecking, ensuring accuracy and adherence to ethical standards, and helping reporters to conceptualize and shape high-impact stories. NGN showcases news about innovation across Northeastern's academic and research enterprise, including but not limited to stories about discoveries, innovations across multiple areas, such as artificial intelligence, health, engineering, computer science, data science, and the humanities, to name a few. NGN covers newsworthy stories across Northeastern's global system of campuses in the US, UK, and Canada. Stories focus on examples of leadership and innovation throughout the university in areas including experiential learning programs, research, teaching, and campus life inside labs, classrooms, and beyond. NGN also provides commentary and analysis by faculty experts on timely topics that captivate national and global conversations. It is a highly data-informed organization with ambitious goals and impeccable standards. The Senior Editor must be capable of delivering accurate and timely edits across multiple platforms. We expect curiosity, accountability, creativity, along with a commitment to precision and speed. This role is perfect for a journalist who is well-versed in a plethora of topics from science, technology and AI to health and wellbeing, sports and pop culture. The person in this role thrives in a fast-paced environment, has great news judgment, and understands how to mold copy to reach readers across digital platforms. The Senior Editor will be adept at crafting compelling, SEO-friendly headlines and captions. NGN is part of Northeastern's Communications team in External Affairs. Reporting to the Executive Editor of NGN, the Senior Editor is part of a team of journalists who work closely with media relations, social media and multimedia teams within Communications.  The position is based on the Boston campus and is fully on site, five days a week. Remote or hybrid work are not an option. As is the case for every member of the Communications team, after-hours work, including weekends and holidays, is routinely required. Key Responsibilities Edit a variety of stories including global breaking news, features, enterprise pieces, and articles focused on artificial intelligence, innovation, experiential learning programs, research, teaching and campus life, among other areas. Edit stories to ensure they are clear, engaging, and accessible to readers.  Craft compelling, SEO-friendly headlines and captions. Work closely with reporters to evaluate and refine story pitches, shape coverage, and maintain high standards of accuracy. Help reporters develop reliable sources and maintain strong relationships across the Northeastern community and relevant stakeholder groups. Collaborate with media relations, social media, and multimedia teams to enhance stories with data, graphics, photos, and video. Monitor analytics and audience metrics to inform reporting and follow-up coverage.  Adhere to guidelines and fact-check thoroughly. Minimum Qualifications 5 - 7 years editing experience with a commercial daily news organization (digital or print). Demonstrated ability to edit a wide range of stories with speed and accuracy. Experience assigning stories and managing content production. Strong source-building skills. Experience using AI tools. Excellent news judgment. Experience interpreting public documents, filings, and data. Ability to multitask, manage deadlines, and respond quickly to developing stories. Familiarity with digital publishing tools is a plus. Good to have Experience with multimedia or video reporting Data journalism or basic visual storytelling skills Knowledge of SEO and audience engagement strategies Position Type Marketing and Communications Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Adjunct-Faculty---Educational-Leadership--Oakland-_R141756": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Educational Leadership (Oakland)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The College of Professional Studies at Northeastern University invites applications for a non-tenure track part time faculty lecturer in the Graduate School of Education on the Oakland campus. The successful candidate will teach graduate level courses in the Master of Arts in Educational Leadership program at Northeastern University’s Oakland campus. The program prepares current and aspiring educational leaders with the knowledge and skills needed for leadership in K through 12 educational settings. The curriculum emphasizes the integration of research and practice, reflective leadership, educational equity, and the application of theory to real world educational settings. The College of Professional Studies is one of ten colleges of Northeastern University, a nationally ranked private research university. Founded in 1960, the College provides lifelong experiential learning that unleashes the capacities of aspiring individuals in all stages and walks of life. The College teaches undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students on campus and online in more than 90 programs.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Responsibilities Qualified candidates must be prepared to work with global student populations. Teach graduate courses within the Master of Arts in Educational Leadership program. Courses may include Working with Families and the Community and other courses across the Educational Leadership curriculum that align with the candidate’s areas of expertise.** Responsibilities include preparing and teaching course sessions, developing and assessing assignments, maintaining course materials in Canvas, facilitating graduate level discussion and applied learning, grading and providing feedback on student work, meeting with students as needed, and supporting students in connecting research, theory, policy, and professional practice. Qualifications Doctorate degree in education, educational leadership, or a related field required. Candidates should have relevant professional experience and demonstrated expertise in one or more areas represented in the Educational Leadership curriculum. Experience teaching graduate students and connecting academic knowledge with educational leadership practice is preferred. For courses focused on working with families and communities, substantial professional or educational experience in family and community engagement, educational leadership, school and community partnerships or related work is preferred. Experience working with diverse communities and addressing educational equity through collaborative and inclusive approaches is also preferred. Application Materials Applicants should submit materials including a cover letter and vitae. Successful faculty at Northeastern will be dynamic and innovative scholars with a record of teaching excellence and a commitment to fostering belonging in the university community. Thus, strong candidates for this faculty position will have the expertise, knowledge, and skills to build their pedagogy, and curriculum in ways that reflect and enhance this commitment. Please indicate how your expertise, knowledge, and skills have prepared you to contribute to this work in your cover letter. Please direct questions to Dana Wright at da.wright@northeastern.edu. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The per credit rate is $2,435.76."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Enterprise-Product-Lead--ServiceNow_R141743": {
      "title": "Enterprise Product Lead- ServiceNow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY ServiceNow Product Lead will own our organization's ServiceNow strategy design and execution, ensuring it effectively supports business processes and objectives across ITSM, CSM, HAM/SAM and SPM, with a growing footprint in IRM. The role involves the execution of strategic planning, leading implementation initiatives and continuous improvement of ServiceNow solutions to optimize data integrity, operational efficiency and business outcome delivery. The position requires a strong technical background in the ServiceNow platform, project/program management, and an ability to collaborate with cross-functional teams to align ServiceNow capabilities with business needs, manage vendor relationships and lead support teams that will help the organization achieve and maintain a stable and responsive platform. It is expected that this leader will help our organization keep up with the latest trends in the ServiceNow market, including (but not limited to) portal experiences, AI powered virtual agent and chat capabilities, and workflow automation and orchestration. The ServiceNow-focused Product Lead will need to help manage costs and prioritize initiatives with business stakeholders, driving measurable value from the early to the late stages of such initiatives This role is critical to ensuring that our business technology solutions align with our university strategy, enhance operational efficiencies and provide competitive advantage. As a key member of the senior IT management team, the Product Lead will collaborate closely with other leaders in IT and business units to spearhead digital transformation initiatives, optimize platform value, and ensure the security and compliance of our ServiceNow environment. This role requires occasional availability outside of traditional working hours to address urgent business needs as they arise, including, but not limited to, responding to security incidents, supporting software deployments, resolving software issues or system breaks, and addressing other critical operational requirements. The ideal candidate must be proactive and adaptable, ensuring minimal disruption to business operations by promptly addressing any issues, regardless of time or day. Flexibility and a strong sense of urgency are essential for success in this position. This role is hybrid and in the office a minimum of three days a week to facilitate collaboration and teamwork. In-office presence is an essential part of our on-campus culture and allows for engaging directly with staff and students, sharing ideas, and contributing to a dynamic work environment. Being on-site allows for stronger connections, more effective problem-solving, and enhanced team synergy, all of which are key to achieving our collective goals and driving success. This role requires flexibility in performing duties outside of the primary responsibilities to support evolving business needs. The ideal candidate must be adaptable and willing to take on additional tasks or projects as required, ensuring smooth operations across the organization. This may include stepping in to assist with cross-functional teams, handling unexpected challenges, or contributing to initiatives that support business growth and success. A proactive mindset and the ability to pivot quickly are essential for thriving in this dynamic environment. **Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The University is unable to work sponsor for this role, now or in the future. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Minimum of 10 years’ experience in IT, with at least five years in a leadership role managing enterprise applications. Proven track record of leading digital transformation initiatives and implementing large-scale IT projects. Extensive experience with the ServiceNow platform, preferably in an environment that deployed 3+ modules such as ITSM, CSM, HAM/SAM, SPM or IRM. Demonstrated experience with ServiceNow portals, AI chat or virtual agent capabilities, and workflow automation and orchestration. Demonstrated success in vendor and stakeholder management. Experience in higher education or healthcare concepts such as service management, asset management, or risk and compliance are a plus. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Strategic leadership: Develop and implement a comprehensive strategy for the ServiceNow platform that supports our organization’s business goals and objectives. In collaboration with the software engineering team, carry out buy-build-customize analyses and develop plans to operationalize the results. Own, maintain and promote awareness of the platform strategic roadmap. Leadership of digital transformation efforts: Identify opportunities for innovation and improvement, while overseeing ongoing implementation of multiple, overlapping platform initiatives across ITSM, CSM, HAM/SAM, SPM and IRM. Team leadership and development: Lead, mentor and develop a high-performance team of IT professionals supporting the ServiceNow platform. Foster a culture of innovation, excellence and ongoing improvement. Business engagement: Develop productive relationships with business leaders across our organization to influence how the platform can enable new sources of value. Conduct regular surveys of stakeholders’ satisfaction with the platform, publish the results and, where necessary, implement action plans to improve satisfaction. Portfolio management: Oversee the management of our ServiceNow portfolio across ITSM, CSM, HAM/SAM, SPM and IRM, ensuring it meets current and future business requirements. Evaluate and prioritize initiatives based on strategic alignment, return on investment and risk. Perform ongoing buy-build-customization analyses for current and future components of the portfolio, including portals, AI chat and virtual agent capabilities, and automation and orchestration. Identify opportunities for system modernization, integration, automation and rationalization. Operational excellence: Safeguard the reliability, availability and performance of the ServiceNow platform, including portals, AI chat and virtual agent tools, and automation and orchestration workflows. Implement best practices for application life cycle management, including development, deployment, maintenance and support. Manage master data across the platform. Vendor management: Establish and maintain strong relationships with ServiceNow and key implementation partners. Develop a nuanced understanding of the vendor’s roadmap and how it will support the company’s business objectives. Collaborate with IT sourcing and vendor management teams to strengthen the company’s position when negotiating with software and service vendors. Risk management and compliance: Work with our internal governance team to ensure the ServiceNow platform, including IRM capabilities, complies with all relevant laws, regulations and industry standards. Oversee risk management processes to identify, assess and mitigate risks associated with the platform and connected systems. Budget management: Develop and manage the budget for the ServiceNow platform, ensuring optimal allocation of resources and cost-effectiveness. Competencies & Key Behaviors Technical expertise: In-depth knowledge of the ServiceNow platform, cloud computing and platform architecture. Ability to use strategic planning to translate business goals into technology solutions. At least one of the following certifications required: ServiceNow Certified System Administrator (CSA), Certified Implementation Specialist in ITSM, Certified Implementation Specialist in CSM, Certified Implementation Specialist in HAM/SAM, Certified Implementation Specialist in SPM, or Certified Implementation Specialist in Risk and Compliance (IRM) Communication and collaboration: Exceptional communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to collaborate simultaneously with multiple executives from different parts of the organization, stakeholders and teams across the organization, and to influence people to achieve common goals. Experience partnering with Higher Education or student and employee service delivery functions desired Project management: Strong project management skills, including experience with agile methodologies and change management. Analytical skills: Excellent analytical and problem-solving abilities; proficiency in data analysis and performance metrics. Position Type Information Technology Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 115S Expected Hiring Range: $157,130.00 - $227,840.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---MS-in-Cybersecurity--Khoury-College-of-Computer-Sciences_R141718": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer - MS in Cybersecurity- Khoury College of Computer Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity   The Khoury College of Computer Sciences invites applications for the position of Part-time teaching faculty for the MS in Cybersecurity program.     Responsibilities:     Deliver and design online or in-person cybersecurity courses in topics such as AI and Machine Learning in Cybersecurity, Threat Hunting & Incident Response and Data Privacy & Protection.     Qualifications:     Master’s degree required . Ph.D. in Cybersecurity related fields and/or industry experience is preferred.   Documents to Submit :    CV and Cover Letter   About Khoury College:    We invite you to join a fast-moving, ambitious college with an underlying mission that is driven by our motto of “CS for Everyone.”   Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The per credit rate for an instructor with a PhD is $4,924.50. The per credit rate for an instructor with an MS degree is $3,939.50."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Oakland-CA/Asst-Dir---Undergraduate-Academic-Advising_R141750": {
      "title": "Asst Dir - Undergraduate Academic Advising",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity This job description is intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by people assigned to this classification. It is not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties and skills required of personnel so classified. JOB SUMMARY The Assistant Director of Undergraduate Academic Advising (Oakland campus) is part of a leadership team that is responsible for supporting the development, implementation and assessment of student advising services on the Northeastern University Oakland campus. The Assistant Director works in partnership with the Director of Global Undergraduate Academic Advising in hiring, supervising and training staff; supports implementation of advising-related technology; provides academic advisement to students; coordinates special advising related projects to support student retention and university goals, and best practices in advising. It is critical that the Assistant Director works to foster a welcoming, nurturing, and belonging environment for all students.  MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Master’s Degree in Higher Education, Counseling, Social Work, or other relevant fields required, with 2-3 years relevant advising experience, including supervisory experience; experience working with diverse student populations; excellent oral and written communication skills; understanding of educational requirements and advising processes; demonstrated technology proficiency with various software applications; strong organizational skills; experience working as part of a team. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES Maintain a caseload of student to provide overall guidance to students on their academic goals. Interpret and explain academic policies and procedures to students; monitor students’ academic progress and performance to ensure students are on track for timely completion of degree requirements; make referrals to faculty, staff and support offices.  Provide day-to-day management of the Oakland Advising team. Hire, supervise, mentor and train Academic Advisors. Support duties include maintaining student records and updating appropriate advising platforms. Assist with orientation. Represent Oakland campus advising on relevant committees. Help advising team troubleshoot issues relating to students and parents.  Collaborate with the Director while working to increase the quality of student support, and to identify and develop retention strategies that support university goals. Maintain strong relationships with campus and university departments such as the Registrar, Student Financial Services, WeCare, University Health and Counselling. Implement strategic advising plans based on assessment and evaluation of data.  Other: Teach introductory course(s). Collaborate with team members to develop syllabi and course content. Grade all material submitted by students, keep attendance record and issue final grades.   *Candidates must submit a cover letter and resume to be considered. Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $74,640.00 - $105,427.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Senior-Assistant-Director-of-Student-Accounts--Customer-Experience_R141673": {
      "title": "Senior Assistant Director of Student Accounts, Customer Experience",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary The Senior Assistant Director of Student Accounts promotes positive customer experiences within the Student Accounts unit and the Enrollment Management division. This position monitors service levels and partners with Student Financial Services (SFS) management and the Enrollment Management Contact Center (EMCC) to ensure established service standards are met. The Senior Assistant Director communicates daily with staff to ensure team members have the information needed to assist customers and manages escalated and special cases through to resolution. The Senior Assistant Director develops, maintains, and monitors service metrics to assess the impact of key initiatives and establishes and refines service expectations around response times, communication, and issue resolution. This position also optimizes the ServiceNow platform to understand customer engagement and behavior, anticipate customer needs, and develop solutions that respond promptly to emerging service trends. This position directly supervises a team of student account representatives responsible for frontline service delivery and payment processing, with regular oversight of case management, quality assurance, transaction processing, batch reconciliation, and resolution of payment discrepancies to maintain the integrity of student account records. The Senior Assistant Director also monitors staffing resources and workload distribution to ensure the team is appropriately scaled to meet cyclical demands, including peak billing, payment, and enrollment periods. In collaboration with the Associate Director of Communications and Training, the Senior Assistant Director helps create and deliver communications that provide students, families, and campus partners with timely information and foster a culture of self-service. This includes developing, coordinating, and maintaining Student Accounts communications and content, such as website updates and knowledge articles; collaborating with Enrollment Management Marketing on messaging and collateral; and partnering with SFS Leadership, Student Accounts Management, SFS Customer Service, the EMCC, and others to deliver targeted communications to unique populations while ensuring alignment with marketing best practices and compliance requirements. Key Responsibilities & Accountabilities Monitor service levels and partner with Student Financial Services management and the Enrollment Management Contact Center to ensure established service standards are met Communicate daily with staff to ensure team members have the information needed to assist customers effectively Manage escalated and special cases through to resolution Develop, maintain, and monitor service metrics to assess the impact of key initiatives Establish and refine service expectations related to response times, communication, and issue resolution Optimize the ServiceNow platform to better understand customer engagement and behavior, anticipate customer needs, and develop solutions that address emerging service trends Supervise a team of student account representatives responsible for frontline service delivery and payment processing Provide oversight of case management, quality assurance, transaction processing, batch reconciliation, and resolution of payment discrepancies to maintain the integrity of student account records Monitor staffing resources and workload distribution to ensure appropriate coverage during peak billing, payment, and enrollment periods Collaborate with the Associate Director of Communications and Training to develop and deliver communications for students, families, and campus partners Develop, coordinate, and maintain Student Accounts communications and content, including website updates and knowledge articles Collaborate with Enrollment Management Marketing on messaging and collateral development Partner with SFS Leadership, Student Accounts Management, SFS Customer Service, the Enrollment Management Contact Center, and other stakeholders to deliver targeted communications to unique populations while maintaining compliance and alignment with marketing best practices Minimum Qualifications Required Bachelor's degree 3–5 years of progressively responsible experience in student accounts, customer service operations within higher education, or a related financial services environment Strong analytical skills with the ability to interpret data and translate findings into actionable service improvements Experience developing and motivating staff, including performance management, coaching, and training Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively across departments and with diverse stakeholder groups Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including experience developing content for diverse audiences Ability to multitask, work independently, manage time effectively, and deliver quality outcomes under pressure and on deadline Commitment to excellence in student service, with sound judgment and discretion in handling escalated, sensitive, or complex cases Ability to learn and effectively navigate multiple technology systems and platforms, integrating information across systems to support service delivery and decision-making Preferred Experience using PowerFAIDS, ServiceNow, and Banner Position Type Enrollment Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 108S Expected Hiring Range: $60,315.00 - $85,192.50 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Career-Advisor----Temporary_R141641": {
      "title": "Career Advisor -- Temporary",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity Job Summary: The Career Assistant will report to an Associate Director, in the Career Development department, and provide career coaching, program management and support for students, employers and alumni, on the Boston Campus.  They will provide individualized support to students, deliver career development workshops, and participate in the department’s efforts to support students’ career exploration and job seeking.  This is a temporary position. Key Accountabilities & Responsibilities: Career Advising & Student Coaching   Provide individualized and small group career advising and coaching to students and recent graduates. Guide students through job search strategies, resume, cover letter and portfolio development, interview preparation, and offer evaluation.   Programming Design & Delivery   Support the design, delivery, and continuous improvement of programming that addresses the full arc of the job search: industry exploration, networking, mock interviews, career fairs and employer showcases, and salary negotiation. Develop programming in partnership with career development team, faculty, student organizations and employer partners. Capture and leverage data to evaluate program effectiveness to iterate accordingly.  Departmental Initiatives   Assist with career education special projects and initiatives including, but not limited to, career fairs, employer visits, networking events, and engagements with prospective, incoming, current and graduating Northeastern students. Support the ideation, planning, logistics and delivery of both small and large-scale events.  Minimum Qualifications: A bachelor's degree is required with a minimum of 1 years of experience in career services, recruiting, workforce development, or higher education. Experience working with or advising preferred.  Strong interpersonal skills.  Strong organizational with careful attention to detail.  Demonstrated ability to build authentic relationships across diverse cultures, backgrounds and identities, and genuine commitment to inclusive practice.  Proficiency with career management platforms (e.g., Handshake), labor market intelligence tools (e.g., Lightcast, LinkedIn Talent Insights), and AI-enabled career development tools, a plus. Position Type Temporary Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This job is for a current or anticipated job vacancy. Pay Rate: $30.00"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Academic-Advisor_R141496": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity About the Opportunity: The Academic Advisor in the D’Amore-McKim School of Business provides student- centered support and guidance to undergraduate Business majors regarding college and university policies, course selection, preregistration, registration, and co-op scheduling. The Advisor will provide support to enhance student engagement, academic progression and retention. The Advisor serves as a liaison between university administrators, students, parents, faculty, staff and others regarding students’ academic success. This individual will be in frequent communication with offices across the University to support student success. The Advisor utilizes a variety of university systems to advise the student. Due to the complex and sensitive issues that can arise, high emphasis is placed on confidentiality and privacy. The Advisor needs to be familiar with and adhere to applicable guidelines laid out by ADA, FERPA, SEVIS and other state, federal, University and College regulations. About the D’Amore-McKim School of Business: Founded in 1922, the D’Amore-McKim School of Business at Northeastern University prepares the leaders that the times demand. We deliver a curriculum that combines knowledge in technology and data analytics with uniquely human skills, such as critical thinking, creativity, and an entrepreneurial mindset. From undergraduate to MBA-level programs—online, on-site, or hybrid, full- or part-time, our world-renowned experiential approach empowers our students, faculty, alumni, and p artners to create impact far beyond the confines of discipline, degree, and campus. Responsibilities: Academic Advisors are responsible for an assigned caseload of students and work in a team-based model. The Advisor is expected to successfully manage student demand during high traffic times. The Undergraduate Programs Office and the D’Amore-McKim School of Business as well as Northeastern are fast-paced, innovative environments. The Advisor will assist with new student orientation sessions and participate in recruitment activities as needed. Supporting and facilitating events and workshops that contribute to the student experience is an expectation of the Academic Advisor. The Academic Advisor will be involved with special events hosted by the Undergraduate Programs Office. Work hours include some evenings and weekends. Travel may be necessary for certain events or tasks. Qualifications: Master’s degree required, preferably in counseling, human services or higher education (or equivalent experience advising in a college/university setting.) A minimum of 3 to 4 years of work or educational experience; preferably in higher education. Must enjoy working with students and help guide them through their academics to graduation. Experience working with the following systems is a plus: Course Catalog, Degree Audit Reporting System (DARS), Workday, SalesForce, EAB Navigate. The successful candidate will: • Have excellent computer (Excel and Word), written and oral communication skills. • Have demonstrated counseling ability utilizing development theory to create a student-centered academic environment. • Be detail-oriented and have the ability to work with precision. • Have the ability to keep calm under pressure. • Be able to communicate and interact effectively with diverse backgrounds and across multiple levels. • Possess good public speaking and presentation skills. • Demonstrate qualities that facilitate collaboration in a team-oriented environment. • Value and provide confidentiality around sensitive issues and have the ability to determine appropriate levels of communication around sensitive information. • Effectively set goals, multitask and manage time. • Display self-motivation and initiative. • Be motivated to gain a broad knowledge base of the portfolio of academic programs offered. Documents to Submit: Resume Cover Letter Position Type Academic Administration Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 107S Expected Hiring Range: $53,615.00 - $75,728.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer---Architecture_R140252": {
      "title": "Part Time Lecturer - Architecture",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nPart time\n\nAbout the Opportunity The Lecturer will teach introductory courses in architectural drawing, sketching, studio design, computer modeling, architectural history, technology, or project case studies; as well as other undergraduate and graduate courses depending on expertise, enrollment, funding, and departmental needs. Qualifications: Master of Architecture, PhD or equivalent preferred. Please note: applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis.  Northeastern University will not provide H-1B, TN, O-1, E-3, or any other type of employment visa sponsorship for the successful applicant to this position, now or in the future. Furthermore, the successful applicant must be able to maintain valid work authorization in the United States throughout the entire appointment without Northeastern University’s sponsorship for a visa. Position Type Academic Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Pay Range: The rate per credit is $2,085"
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Associate-Research-Scientist_R141382-2": {
      "title": "Associate Research Scientist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: The Associate Research Scientist will conduct integrated experimental and theoretical research on the mechano-electrochemical behavior of battery materials, electrodes, and cells. The primary function of the position is to advance new knowledge about the materials science and engineering for energy materials and devices, particularly Li-ion batteries and direct recycling of electrodes. The scientist will also lead a research project in collaboration with Saint-Gobain, funded by ARPA-E. The role combines hands-on battery fabrication and characterization with rigorous data analysis, AI, and new material development.   MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge of electrochemistry, battery materials, and electrochemical testing methods; ability to fabricate and assemble battery cells (e.g., coin, pouch, cylindrical) and conduct cycling and diagnostic tests; knowledge of chemo-mechanics and coupled electrochemical–mechanical phenomena in electrodes and active particles; ability to design and execute experiments, including optical or in situ characterization techniques for deformation measurements; ability to analyze and interpret experimental data and develop physics-based models (e.g., porous electrode theory and single-particle models); and familiarity with computational tools for data analysis and/or simulation. These qualifications are normally acquired through a Ph.D. in mechanical engineering, chemical engineering, materials science, or a closely related field, with 0–3 years of relevant research experience in battery systems or electrochemical energy storage.   KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES: Lead the ARPA-E project to collaborate with Saint-Gobain on battery lifetime extension Develop new technologies for lithium extraction and direct recycling of battery electrode Write new proposals with the PI for NSF, DOE, and other funding agencies Act as lab manager for lab safety and basic functioning; train students and other group member with battery manufacturing skills. Document and disseminate research findings through publications, reports, and presentations, and contribute to collaborative research efforts DOCUMENT REQUIRED: Resume Cover letter Position Type Research Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 109S Expected Hiring Range: $67,855.00 - $95,845.00 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
    },
    "https://northeastern.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/careers/job/Boston-MA-Main-Campus/Graduate--Financial-Aid-Counselor_R141055": {
      "title": "Graduate  Financial Aid Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nFull time\n\nAbout the Opportunity JOB SUMMARY: The Graduate Financial Aid Counselor will primarily focus on graduate students, advise and counsel full and part-time students/families on the availability of financial aid, the delivery process for receiving it, and the reconciliation processes required to ensure that financial aid funds are transferred to student account.  Responsibilities include reviewing student applications, performing verification procedures, reviewing/evaluating appeals, and interceding for students with participating departments (School administrators, Student Accounts, Registrar, and Co-op). This position will have a hybrid work arrangement.  RESPONSIBILITIES INCLUDE: Counsel and advise students and parents on financial aid process, NU billing procedures, and financing options. Administer federal, state and Northeastern-based financial assistance. Review student applications and perform federal needs analysis to determine eligibility. Verify information according to federal process. Perform the per term audit of federal eligibility: adjusting aid and monitor satisfactory academic progress. Represent the Financial Aid Office at Open Houses, student meetings, workshops, and part-time evening programs to overview and answer questions regarding the financial aid process. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelors Degree preferred or relevant experience Experience in student personnel administration, preferably in Financial Aid or Student Accounts Strong communication, interpersonal and organizational skills Ability to communicate well by telephone and email, in addition to face-to-face interactions Willingness and ability to give presentations to large groups of students and their families Working knowledge of ServiceNow, PowerFAIDS, Banner, Microsoft Teams, Outlook, Word, and Excel preferred Experience working with non-traditional student populations and adult learners preferred Demonstrated ability to work with a diverse student population Strong analytical, decision-making, and problem-solving skills Position Type Enrollment Additional Information Northeastern University considers factors such as candidate work experience, education and skills when extending an offer.   Northeastern has a comprehensive benefits package for benefit eligible employees. This includes medical, vision, dental, paid time off, tuition assistance, wellness & life, retirement- as well as commuting & transportation. Visit https://hr.northeastern.edu/benefits/ for more information.    All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply and will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, color, national origin, age, sex, sexual orientation, disability status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Compensation Grade/Pay Type: 106S Expected Hiring Range: $48,610.00 - $66,838.75 With the pay range(s) shown above, the starting salary will depend on several factors, which may include your education, experience, location, knowledge and expertise, and skills as well as a pay comparison to similarly-situated employees already in the role. Salary ranges are reviewed regularly and are subject to change."
